abled:opacity-50 pr-2\",\"children\":\"Trends in bot behavior\"}]]}]}]}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"3b:[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"className\":\"group block select-none space-y-1 rounded-lg p-3 -m-3 leading-none no-underline outline-hidden transition-colors duration-150 hover:bg-bg-tertiary focus:bg-bg-tertiary focus:outline-hidden focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-brand-primary focus-visible:ring-offset-2\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"$L39\",null,{\"asChild\":true,\"children\":[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"ref\":\"$undefined\",\"href\":\"/whitepaper/agent-sites/\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"flex items-center gap-2\",\"children\":[\"$undefined\",[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"font-bold text-content-primary wrap-break-word\",\"children\":\"Agent Site white paper\"}],\"$undefined\"]}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"line-clamp-2 text-sm leading-snug mt-1.5 text-content-secondary group-data-disabled:opacity-50 pr-2\",\"children\":\"Benchmarking impact of optimized Agent Sites\"}]]}]}]}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"3d:I[3279,[\"6647\",\"static/chunks/6647-d721c635c0154aff.js\",\"1176\",\"static/chunks/1176-0c8f2e524a01165d.js\",\"1683\",\"static/chunks/1683-75ceec0b1b655f42.js\",\"3253\",\"static/chunks/3253-362c53e33079e1b8.js\",\"3530\",\"static/chunks/3530-1c2a36597ccab303.js\",\"4748\",\"static/chunks/app/(docs)/state-of-the-bots/%5B%5B...slug%5D%5D/page-13fdc771ad34dd27.js\"],\"StateOfTheBotsPageLayout\"]\n3e:Te4fc,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\n\u003cSection id=\"foreword\"\u003e\n\n## **Foreword**\n\n**By Dr. Jonathan Roberts, Chief Innovation Officer at People Inc.:**\n\n\u003cInfoBox\u003e\n\n\"A lot has changed in a year. Last summer the questions were:\n\n- Does content have value in an AI economy?\n- Will blocking work?\n- Will there be a market for information?\n\nIn July 2026, we know that good AI needs good inputs, and needs those inputs in real time. Blocking makes products worse, and great inputs make it better. You can’t just throw more chips at the problem.\n\n- Data centers are the engine.\n- Content is the fuel.\n\nIn the last 12 months stealing content has become big business:\n\n- There are now more bots online than humans.\n- Multiple AI Search startups [have raised](https://exa.ai/blog/announcing-series-c) [capital](https://parallel.ai/blog/series-b) at valuations of over $2bn, selling access to content that they take for free.\n- Demand is growing exponentially.\n\nAt People Inc. we provide fast, direct, licensed access to our premium content for our partners, and have gone deep to block those who are wholesale stealing our content. In February we moved to block all bots from companies we don’t have a partnership with. We allow a short list of named crawlers from trusted partners, and we block tens of thousands of unique bad bots every day, and block tens of millions of daily attempts to take our content.\n\nThe most aggressive actors follow a standard pattern of behavior:\n\n1. They send a named crawler \\- we block it.\n2. They send an anonymous crawler \\- we block it.\n3. They send a crawler that spoofs googlebot \\- we block it.\n4. They send a crawler that attempts to look human, scrolling the page, and executing code \\- we provide a ‘are you human’ challenge. It fails and is blocked.\n5. They send multiple crawlers through residential internet connections and mobile devices. Because these home proxy networks use legitimate IP addresses they are extremely difficult to identify as being compromised by bad actors and therefore block. We are able to block some of this activity, but not all.\n\nIt’s now clear that:\n\n- Content has a lot of value in an AI economy.\n- Blocking works.\n- There’s a market being built in real time.\n\nIn the last year we’ve seen the emergence of around 30 “Napsters of content”, but no Spotify. How do we build a premium information economy that lets people who need information pay the people who create it?\n\n- Step 1: stop people taking it for free.\n- Step 2: flip the market from stealing to paying.\n\nIf we get this wrong \\- a few intermediaries sell the world’s information for their own gain, removing any reason to invest in new knowledge, killing the digital economy, and AI gets worse as inputs degrade.\n\nIf we get this right, the exploding demand for knowledge funds investment in new information creation and AI products get much better.\n\nI know which future I want to live in, and we’re working across publishing, CDNs, platforms, legislation and regulation to bring it into reality.\"\n\n\u003c/InfoBox\u003e\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n\n---\n\n\u003cSection id=\"executive-summary\"\u003e\n\n## **Executive Summary**\n\n\u003cExecSummary\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={1}\u003e\n  **Bots are shockingly effective at evading cybersecurity protections and\n  paywalls; we observed them impersonate Google and others, rotate IP addresses,\n  and appear to use residential proxies, hammering publishers with bot\n  traffic.** On UK sites, we saw similar trends to those we observed on US\n  sites: paywalled content was not necessarily safe from web scrapers, and 95%\n  of the top UK sites could be scraped by at least one of the 14 scrapers we\n  tested. Scrapers' disguises are sometimes good enough to fool ad systems — we\n  observed scrapers triggering Google Ads bots, meaning the page treated a bot\n  like a human and rendered ads to it, a concern for advertisers paying to reach\n  humans. In one instance, we observed a scraper hit a publisher's site over 40\n  times in 3 seconds, creating bandwidth cost the publisher ends up paying.\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={2}\u003e\n  **The economics of online publishers are eroding in real time, and European\n  publishers are being hit hardest.** Data from publishers on TollBit shows\n  European sites get scraped more, receive fewer referrals in return, and have\n  their robots.txt rules bypassed more often. Median AI scrapes per site were 4×\n  higher on European sites compared to North American sites, and AI scrapes on\n  European sites were nearly 20% higher in June than in January 2026, with local\n  news and sports sites seeing the highest quarter-over-quarter growth. Note:\n  these numbers reflect only identified bots, so the true scale of AI scraping\n  is likely even higher.\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={3}\u003e\n  **Publishers aren't receiving anything back from the scrapes, and the exchange\n  is getting worse.** It takes 179 AI bot visits to get a single human visitor\n  referral in return from AI applications for European publishers — a rate over\n  3x worse than North American sites. This imbalance worsened over H1; the\n  scrape-to-referral ratio went from 150:1 in Q1 2026 to 227:1 in Q2 2026. AI\n  bot traffic accounted for only 0.05% of total human referrals on European\n  sites, which also carry roughly 3x the AI load per human visitor as their\n  North American peers, seeing about 1 AI bot scrape for every 33 human visits.\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={4}\u003e\n  **Robots.txt instructions do very little to stop these bots.** The median\n  European site's robots.txt instructions to not scrape were ignored 2.8x more\n  often than North American sites'.\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={5}\u003e\n  **The methods these scrapers use to evade detection are the same ones used to\n  access infrastructure or manipulate social media by nation-state hackers and\n  sophisticated cyber-criminals.** Now, these tools are used by venture-backed\n  Silicon Valley companies serving major corporate clients — without\n  intervention, the volume will compound exponentially as the companies behind\n  it scale. A majority of these scraping companies advertise using methods such\n  as residential proxies, the same functionality used by bad actors in\n  cyberattacks, to gain access to sites and evade their cybersecurity measures.\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={6}\u003e\n  **Bots should not be allowed to mimic humans or other traffic on the web; they\n  should be required to self-identify.** As these disguise techniques grow more\n  sophisticated, bots become harder to detect, placing an undue burden and\n  expense on the sites. Self-identification would let publishers and sites\n  decide how to respond to bot visitors.\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003c/ExecSummary\u003e\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n\n---\n\n\u003cSection id=\"section-1\"\u003e\n\n## **Section 1: The Scraper Ecosystem Powering AI Applications \u0026 How Proxy Networks Work**\n\n\u003cUserStory\u003e\n\nThe web scrapers that power AI tools are using sophisticated methods to\nconceal the identities of the bots they use for scraping using many\ntechniques. Using our Scraper Audit tool, we can observe how effective\nscrapers are in getting access to content, and to an extent, the techniques\nthey use to gain access:\n\n- In testing on sites in the UK, we saw similar trends to those we observed\n  on sites in the US: paywalled content was not necessarily safe from web\n  scrapers, and 19 out of the 20 top UK sites we tested could be scraped by\n  at least one of the 14 scrapers we tested.\n- In testing on various publisher sites, we observed a number of techniques\n  that scrapers used to get access to publisher sites, including rotating\n  IP addresses and masquerading as other user agents, including Googlebot.\n- We also noticed Google Ads bots could be triggered on sites, a concern for\n  advertisers who are paying for their ads to be seen by humans, and not\n  scraper bots.\n\nOne of the most concerning methods scrapers are using is residential\nproxies, the same technique bad actors use to mask cyberattacks. Residential\nproxies are a potential national security concern as they make it virtually\nimpossible to know who is on the other end, operating a bot. A majority of\nthe web scrapers in our [Scraper Index](https://tollbit.com/scraper-index/)\nopenly advertise that they use residential proxies.\n\n\u003c/UserStory\u003e\n\n\u003cInfoBox title=\"Introduction: The Growth and Sophistication of the Data Acquisition Stack\"\u003e\n\nAI developers are combining multiple data acquisition processes and\ntechnologies, many offered by specialist web scraping vendors, into tech\nstacks that obtain access to the Internet. Fueled by the AI boom, hundreds of\nthese third-party scrapers are in use, many by Fortune 1000 companies.\n\nThese intermediaries scrape publisher content to resell it as data or search\ninfrastructure, rather than to directly power their own chatbots or AI\napplications. Many of these scrapers employ specialized cybersecurity evasion\ntools and do not comply with robots.txt by default. This places an undue\nburden on sites to attempt to detect these increasingly hard-to-detect\nscrapers.\n\nThe data acquisition stack has existed for quite some time, but its effects\nhave become more visible recently. With AI, the need to access content \u0026 data\nhas exploded. Funded by the AI investment boom, developers are combining\nmultiple data acquisition processes and technologies, with more advanced or\nclandestine approaches deployed where cybersecurity barriers are encountered.\nThese increasingly complicated tech stacks are intended to secure \"just\nenough\" access to content to ensure the applications they serve can handle\nthe range of queries needed to compete. These stacks are comprised of some or\nall of the following:\n\n- Direct crawling and scraping via first-party bots, either declared or\n  disguised.\n- Secondary source crawling / scraping, targeting surfaces on which the\n  content itself may be visible on the open web (i.e., Google Search results\n  page).\n- Residential IP proxies, presenting as ordinary user traffic — often with\n  location and device characteristics/IP addresses that match the human\n  audience.\n- Circumvention services that evade bot detection / IP protection measures.\n- Cloud-based headless browsers that load web content in a browser running in\n  the cloud, rather than on a user's device. These are often used with a\n  residential IP proxy: the proxy makes the request appear to come from a\n  normal human user.\n- Third-party scraping services for outsourcing the end-to-end process of\n  scraping a page.\n\nFor more detailed information, please visit our\n[Scraper Index](https://tollbit.com/scraper-index/).\n\nFor obvious reasons, this environment is one in which it is exceedingly\ndifficult for digital publishers to exercise control over the use of their\nIP. They are in a structurally disadvantaged position, having to respond to\nnew threats as they emerge, whereas the web scrapers just need to evolve\nbeyond the latest protection. Most websites deploy only one cybersecurity\ntool, whereas AI companies can choose from dozens of scraping tools\ninterchangeably.\n\n\u003c/InfoBox\u003e\n\n\u003cInfoBox title=\"Growth of AI Data Acquisition Stack \u0026 Key H1 2026 Observations\"\u003e\n\nTwo notable additions here to our prior reporting on the AI Data Acquisition\nstack:\n\nFirst is the increase in attention we've seen to residential proxies, along\nwith the potential national security risk associated with them. A\nresidential proxy routes traffic through everyday consumer devices so that\nthe request appears to come from a human using an ordinary home internet\nconnection. This technique is utilized by many web scrapers — and this is the\nsame exact technique that bad actors use to conceal who they are in\ncyberattacks and large-scale cognitive warfare, such as astroturfing\ncampaigns. Recently, there has been a lot of attention to this in the Wall\nStreet Journal reporting, with an\n[article here](https://www.wsj.com/tech/cybersecurity/how-hackers-found-a-back-door-into-the-american-living-room-c117cb9f?mod=hp_lead_pos7)\non this topic, and a recent separate\n[piece here](https://www.wsj.com/tech/cybersecurity/how-millions-of-digital-home-devices-are-secretly-powering-cyberattacks-8fd73584?mod=WTRN_pos2)\nwith a view of an FBI director stating \"we all want to sort of find and\ndefeat these Residential Proxy networks,\" because of their association with\ncrime. Those efforts are already underway. In July 2026, Google, working with\nthe FBI and others,\n[disrupted NetNut](https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/threat-intelligence/google-continued-disruption-residential-proxy-networks),\none of the largest residential proxy networks, which it estimated ran on more\nthan 2 million hijacked consumer devices such as smart TVs and streaming\nboxes. Google reported that in a single week, it observed hundreds of\ndistinct threat groups, including cybercriminal and espionage actors, using\nthe network to hide the origin of their traffic. The Google blog post about\nthis also linked out to a\n[study by a cybersecurity firm](https://krebsonsecurity.com/2026/06/popa-botnet-linked-to-publicly-traded-israeli-firm/)\nthat noted many of the largest proxy providers' websites highlight their\nutility for AI platforms and tools, \"implying it is a primary use case for\ntheir residential proxies.\"\n\nIn an analysis of nearly 40 scraping vendors, we find that many explicitly\nadvertise cybersecurity detection evasion techniques, and many do not\ndefault to abiding by robots.txt. A majority of the web scraping companies in\nour [Web Scraper Index](https://tollbit.com/scraper-index) openly advertise\nthat they use residential proxies and have pools of hundreds of millions of\nIP addresses. Using residential proxies appears to be a fairly standard\npractice for scrapers today. Residential proxies make it even more difficult\nfor websites to detect, and therefore block, allow, or monetize bot/scraper\ntraffic. In a site's logs, a bot visitor using a residential proxy will look\njust like a human visitor.\n\nAnother addition to our prior reporting is some data on third-party\nscrapers/data resellers across the TollBit network, as we have started to\nnotice some of their traffic actually starting to stand out in our log data\nacross publisher sites. (Important to note, many of these third parties do\nnot self-identify their user agents, but a few do, and so we can analyze\ntheir data). Across all sites on TollBit in H1 2026, we observed that Common\nCrawl, the open corpus behind much LLM training, reached the widest share of\nany identifiable third-party scraper/crawler. We observed Common Crawler\nscrapes on ~74% of sites on TollBit: it roughly doubled its reach\nquarter-over-quarter (from Q1 to Q2). Because a single Common Crawl scrape\ncan support many models and AI applications downstream, its footprint across\nsites matters far more than its per-site volume: one scrape from Common Crawl\nmay then become data that then gets reused to power a vast AI ecosystem.\n\nTwo other notable players that scrape content and resell it as a search or\ndata API: Parallel, a web-search/data API, which had scrapes on 74% of sites\non TollBit, nearly doubled its per-site scraping (+94%), and Diffbot (which\nsells a structured \"knowledge graph\" of the web) roughly tripled per-site\n(+195%) and has scrapes on over 33% of sites on TollBit. Because a reseller's\ncrawl can be resold to many buyers, these figures understate true exposure —\none scrape resurfaces across multiple AI products, strengthening the case for\nlicensing terms that follow the data downstream.\n\n\u003c/InfoBox\u003e\n\n### 1.1 Scraper Audit Tool \u0026 Results on UK Sites\n\nIn order to examine the sophistication of web scrapers against websites'\ncybersecurity tooling, TollBit developed the Scraper Audit, a tool for\nwebsite owners to test 14 popular web scrapers, many of which are Europe-based\ncompanies, against their own sites. The Scraper Audit allows website owners\nto initiate scraping on any page to evaluate how well scrapers evade\ndefenses, access content, and retrieval completeness.\n\nIn selecting web scrapers, we picked a few industry leaders and a couple of\nnewer scrapers offering advanced technology, despite their company size and\ntime in market, and have plans to add more to the tool. For each of these web\nscrapers, whenever available, we purchased subscriptions of their web\nscraping APIs to leverage their full suite of AI-driven parsing, advanced\nanti-bot bypassing, and premium proxy networks.\n\nTo demonstrate the utility of this tool in auditing scrapers, we conducted an\nevaluation of this tool across the top 20 sites based in the UK as defined by\nthe\n[Press Gazette](https://pressgazette.co.uk/media-audience-and-business-data/media_metrics/most-popular-websites-news-uk-monthly-2/).\n\nOur test of these sites resulted in similar outcomes to the tests run for the\nprevious edition of the report:\n\n- Even paywalled content is not necessarily safe. In our testing of 14 of\n  these scraping tools, we observed that some were able to scrape various\n  paywalled articles in full.\n- In fact, non-paywalled sites offered no discernible increase in protection\n  compared to their paywalled counterparts, as scrapers successfully\n  retrieved full content from the vast majority of target pages regardless of\n  subscription barriers.\n- In the rare instance where all scrapers failed to fully extract data —\n  occurring in only one of the twenty sites — the failure was primarily\n  attributed to server-side rendering implementations of the paywall. On\n  these specific sites, the full article was not present in the initial page\n  and required server-side authorization before the content was served.\n\n### 1.2 Scraper Audit: Other Observations\n\nSince our last bot report, we've added functionality to our Scraper Audit\nTool to allow publishers to observe the suspected chain of events that occurs\nas a web scraper attempts to access their sites. In the tool, publishers can\nenter a specific URL in the Scraper Audit Tool and observe some of the\nstrategies the web scrapers utilize in an attempt to scrape the page. In sum,\nwe observed that scrapers often deploy various tactics to obtain access to\nsites, some hammering sites dozens of times with different user agents,\nsometimes masquerading as other bots (including Googlebot, Chrome, etc.), and\nothers rotating IP addresses in order to attempt to trick the site's\ncybersecurity and allow them to gain access to scrape the page. We also\nobserved that some of the web scrapers would trigger Google Ads bot on sites\n— concerning for advertisers who are paying for their ads to be seen by\nhumans, and not bots/web scrapers.\n\n_A related note: In our last bot report, we noted that we did not observe any\nscrapers checking robots.txt files before attempting to access sites._\n\n\u003cInfoBox title=\"Methodology\"\u003e\n\nThe strongest signal (Canary verification) we have is that the Scraper Audit\nadds a unique ID to the request of each URL we tell the webscraper to scrape,\nand we then look for that unique ID in the respective site's logs so that we\ncan identify which scraper visited the URL. When we find it, we can be highly\nconfident the request was triggered by the scraper audit test we ran.\n\nThe second signal, which we treat as lower confidence, is fingerprint\nmatching. Some requests that hit the server in response to our scrape don't\ncarry the unique ID. For those, we build a fingerprint from the request's\nattributes (its timestamp, IP address, geo, and user agent — all of which we\ncan obtain from the respective site's logs) and match it against the scrape\nwe initiated. A request that lines up across all of these — arriving at the\nsame moment, from the same IP and geo, with the same user agent — points\nstrongly to the scraper, even without the ID to confirm it. We are relying on\nvery close timing to make this assumption (typically sub-second to a few\nseconds after invocation), which we assume is related to the scraper test we\nrun. We know when the scraper was triggered and what page it was triggered\nto, because we control when the scrapers are triggered and to which pages,\nand we have the publishers' site logs data that includes information like IP\naddress, timestamp of the scrape to the site, etc.\n\n\u003c/InfoBox\u003e\n\nHere are some real examples of what we observed with this new functionality\non publisher sites. Each of the examples below shows a single scraper\ninstance attempting to access a publisher's URL:\n\n#### 1.2.1 Example 1: Masquerading as Google \u0026 IP Address Rotation\n\nThe scraper in this example appeared to have tried more than one disguise to\nget in. It first used User-Agent Spoofing and posed as Googlebot to gain\ntrusted access. When that was blocked, it appeared to switch to a Chrome\nbrowser, and its apparent location shifted from Los Angeles, US, to San\nSebastián de los Reyes, ES, meaning it also changed its IP address. Once it\nchanged to a browser, it was first served 301 redirects, then kept trying\nuntil it got a 200 and pulled the content. This showcases that when one\nidentity is refused, a scraper may just change who it claims to be and where\nit appears to come from and try again.\n\n\u003cExpandableImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/scraper-audit-example-1.svg\"\n  alt=\"Scraper masquerading as Google, request timeline\"\n/\u003e\n\n\u003cExpandableImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/scraper-audit-example-2.svg\"\n  alt=\"Scraper masquerading as Google, evidence detail\"\n/\u003e\n\n#### 1.2.2 Example 2: Multiple Browser Impersonations \u0026 IP Address Rotation\n\nThis attempt to access a publisher's URL appeared to trigger 43 requests from\na single web scraper, routed through many different IPs and locations,\ncycling between Chrome and Firefox identities throughout the attempt to\naccess the site. The locations of the scrapes heavily varied: alongside US\ncities (San Jose, Ashburn, Clifton, Pennington), the scrapes arrived from\nmultiple Hungarian locations (Sopron, Sümeg, Nyíregyháza) and Bahrain (Bu\nKuwarah), all within seconds.\n\n\u003cExpandableImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/scraper-audit-example-3.svg\"\n  alt=\"Multiple browser impersonations, request timeline\"\n  expandLabel=\"View full screenshot\"\n/\u003e\n\n\u003cExpandableImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/scraper-audit-example-4.svg\"\n  alt=\"Multiple browser impersonations, evidence detail\"\n  expandLabel=\"View full screenshot\"\n/\u003e\n\n#### 1.2.3 Example 3: Multiple Browsers \u0026 IP Rotation\n\nThis scrape request appeared to result in 32 requests hitting the URL at\nessentially the same time, from many different IPs across scattered US\ncities and cycling between Chrome and Firefox identities throughout. One\nscrape is arriving from a dozen-plus origins at once, and being disguised on\nboth the network and identity side. The status column is a real mix of 200s\n(served), 403s (blocked), and 301s (redirected), so the publisher's defenses\nwere firing and worked some of the time, but the blocks didn't matter because\neach request came from a different IP. A 403 on one address did nothing to\nstop the next from a different IP, and the scraper kept rotating until enough\n200s landed to assemble the content.\n\n\u003cExpandableImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/scraper-audit-example-5.svg\"\n  alt=\"Multiple browsers and IP rotation, request timeline\"\n  expandLabel=\"View full screenshot\"\n/\u003e\n\n\u003cExpandableImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/scraper-audit-example-6.svg\"\n  alt=\"Multiple browsers and IP rotation, evidence detail\"\n  expandLabel=\"View full screenshot\"\n/\u003e\n\n#### 1.2.4 Example 4: Scraper Triggers Google Ad Bots\n\nThe two Google Ads Bot requests are worth taking a look at here. They appear\nto have fired because the scraper rendered the page like a real browser,\nloading its ads, which is what set them off. The scrape looks human enough to\ntrigger the ad stack; it could be taken as evidence that the scraper's\ndisguise is convincing and successful. This presents an ad fraud concern for\nadvertisers who are paying to see their ads viewed by humans, and not bots.\n(This is a topic we mentioned previously in our\n[Q2 2025 Bot report, Section 1](https://tollbit.com/state-of-the-bots/q2-2025/)).\n\n\u003cExpandableImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/scraper-audit-example-7.svg\"\n  alt=\"Scraper triggering Google Ad bots\"\n  expandLabel=\"View full screenshot\"\n/\u003e\n\nTollBit has made the Scraper Audit tool available on the TollBit platform to\nhelp websites test and understand their vulnerability to IP threats from\nmajor web scrapers. If you are interested in seeing how your site is being\nscraped by these third parties, please contact\n[[email withheld by Lighthouse]](mailto:[email withheld by Lighthouse]).\n\n### 1.3 The Inefficiency and Cost of Web Scraping\n\nThis arms race between scrapers and website operators is not only\ninefficient for website owners, who are increasingly spending on\ncybersecurity to detect bots that are only getting harder to detect, but it\nis also deeply inefficient for AI developers. As mentioned in our prior bot\nreport, we have found advanced scraping services charging up to $22.50 for\n1,000 pages. Given the volume of data needed to serve consumer AI\napplications, the data acquisition costs for the most popular chatbots and\nspecialized agents are likely to run into the tens of millions of dollars a\nyear. And this is before considering the legal fees needed to fight lawsuits.\n\nWe have also observed that scrapers take 16 seconds on average to access\npublisher sites, and some of them may fail entirely in securing access to the\npage requested, whereas accessing content via a sanctioned route (i.e. via a\nplatform like TollBit) typically takes less than 0.7 seconds.\n\nThere's a better way. Instead of spending huge sums on technical workarounds\nto bypass IP controls, AI developers can simply pay for the content they use.\nThis reduces latency and increases reliability. By relying on licensing\nrather than scraping, journalism is funded — not the scrapers — and the media\necosystem AI depends on is sustained.\n\n### 1.4 The Limits of Cybersecurity and the Need for Regulation\n\nWe've seen publishers increasingly take matters into their own hands to\ncombat the surge in AI bots, increasingly investing in cybersecurity to\nactively block this traffic from their sites or even take action by filing\nlawsuits. As mentioned in our\n[Q3 \u0026 Q4 2025 report](https://tollbit.com/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/), in\nlate 2025, both Reddit and Google turned to the courts over unauthorized\nscraping. Google alleged that scrapers had circumvented SearchGuard, the\nin-house protection system it built at a cost of millions. If a company with\nGoogle's resources and engineering talent cannot keep determined scrapers\nout, an individual publisher has no realistic chance of doing so alone.\n\nCybersecurity is important, but it is not a foolproof solution. These tools\nare costly to run, and many of our publishers, even those with the most\nsophisticated defenses in place, have seen scrapers and AI bots get through\nanyway.\n\nThis brings us to an important limitation of the data in the subsequent\nsections of this report: we rely on AI companies self-identifying through\ntheir user agents. Developers sometimes use third-party vendors to scrape on\ntheir behalf; they may masquerade as other user agents, rotate their IP\naddresses, use headless browsers that mimic humans, use residential proxies,\netc. This activity is difficult, and often impossible, for sites to trace\nback to a specific AI company. The true level of scraping, and of\nunpermitted access to European content, is, therefore, likely greater than\nthis report shows.\n\nAs AI traffic becomes harder to distinguish from human traffic, it is\nimportant that regulators take a position that, at the bare minimum, AI bots\nshould not be allowed to mimic humans on the Internet. Ideally, they would be\nrequired to self-identify and state their intended use, which is what\n[New York's Stealth Crawler Prohibition Act](https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/bills/2025/A11292)\nwould require. That bill, passed by the New York State Legislature in June\n2026 and awaiting the Governor's signature, would require crawlers accessing\na news site to disclose their identity and purpose through an accurate\nuser-agent string before they access the content.\n\nSelf-identification enables a site owner to decide how to respond: allow,\nblock, or charge for access via infrastructure controls like the ones\nTollBit provides. It would help ensure that the undue burden of paying to\nserve, detect, and block unwanted bots no longer falls on the websites\nthemselves.\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n\n---\n\n\u003cSection id=\"section-2\"\u003e\n\n## **Section 2: The Scale of AI Scraping**\n\n\u003cUserStory\u003e\n\n- Over the first half of the year, median AI scraping per site was 4× higher\n  on European sites compared to North American sites.\n- European sites saw roughly 1 AI bot scrape per 33 human visits over the\n  course of H1 2026.\n- RAG bots visited an average publisher page 41 times in Q1 and 50 times in Q2\n  for the European cohort of sites. Training bots only hit a page an average of\n  5 times.\n- North American sites are scraped most often by RAG bots (49%); European\n  scraping is weighted toward training (41%).\n\n\u003c/UserStory\u003e\n\n### 2.1 Aggregate Scraping Levels\n\nIn the previous section, we looked at scrapers that go out of their way to\ndisguise themselves. This section covers the AI companies that identify their\nscrapers/crawlers. Our data comes from the first-party website server logs of\npublishers on the TollBit network, which record every request made to their\nsites. Many major AI platforms send a user-agent string naming the bot and\nthe company behind it, and the data in this section counts only that\nself-identified traffic. We define a scrape in this report as a single\nrequest from one of these\n[identified AI bots](https://tollbit.com/bot-index/), but do not count\nrequests to a site's robots.txt file. Since this data does not include any of\nthe disguised scraping described in Section 1, the AI scraping volumes\npresented in our report are likely meaningfully lower than the actual volume\nof AI visitors to publisher sites.\n\nOver the first half of 2026, website traffic from AI bots continued to grow,\nincreasing by 25% over H1. Looking at the daily volume of AI bot scrapes per\nTollBit publisher website, indexed to January 1, 2026, we see a sharp\nincrease — on the busiest days, the per-site scraping level more than doubled\nrelative to where it started the year.\n\n**Figure 2.1: Daily AI bot scrapes per website, indexed to 1/1/2026**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-2-1-daily-scrapes-indexed.svg\"\n  alt=\"Daily AI bot scrapes per website, indexed to 1/1/2026\"\n/\u003e\n\n### 2.2 AI Scraping by Region: North America vs Europe\n\nEuropean publisher sites on TollBit have a higher median scrapes per site\nthan their North American counterparts. The median European site has 4× the\nAI scrapes of the median North American site. Scraping places an\ninfrastructure cost burden on sites. Every one of these AI bots consumes\nbandwidth and server resources, whether or not it ever returns a human\nvisitor.\n\nFor the regional cohorts throughout this section, we look at all sites that\nwere fully onboarded to TollBit before Q1 2026, grouped by region.\n\n**Figure 2.2.1: Median AI scrapes per site, by region | H1 2026**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-2-2-1-median-scrapes-by-region.svg\"\n  alt=\"Median AI scrapes per site, by region\"\n/\u003e\n\n**Median AI Scrapes for Same-Size Sites: Europe vs North America**\n\nWe grouped sites into bands by average monthly human traffic and put the\nmedian European site in each band up against its North American counterpart.\nEuropean websites are scraped roughly 2x to 4x more by AI bots than\ncomparably sized North American websites. And the multiple is biggest for\nsmaller websites. The ratio shrinks as sites get larger, but even the biggest\nEuropean sites are hit more than twice as hard as their North American peers.\n\nSite-for-site, against North American sites with the same readership, the\nmedian European site gets out-scraped compared to its North American peer by\naverage monthly human-traffic band:\n\n- 1M–10 million monthly human visitors: 3.7×\n- 10M–50 million monthly human visitors: 4.1×\n- 50M–100 million monthly human visitors: 2.6×\n- \\\u003e100 million monthly human visitors: 2.3×\n\n**Figure 2.2.2: Median AI scrapes per site, for similar-size sites by region | H1 2026**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-2-2-2-median-scrapes-same-size-sites.svg\"\n  alt=\"Median AI scrapes per site, for similar-size sites by region\"\n/\u003e\n\n\u003cInfoBox title=\"AI Bot Categories\"\u003e\n\nWe separate AI bots into three primary categories:\n\n**Retrieval augmented generation (RAG) agent** — These bots often retrieve\ninformation in real-time to respond to user prompts. They use an index of\nthe web, gathered by an indexing crawler (either proprietary or third-party,\nsuch as Bing or Google), to locate the relevant content, which is then\nretrieved and synthesized into a response. Unlike a training crawler, which\ncollects content once, a RAG agent needs to fetch content again each time it\nanswers a query, so the same pages are scraped repeatedly as users keep\nasking about them.\n\n**Training data crawling agent** — Large language models (LLMs) are trained\non vast quantities of data. Training data crawlers move around the web —\nfollowing links from website to website or working through sitemaps —\ndownloading content, which is then processed and stored for offline use.\nTraining is the process of teaching an AI system to recognize patterns and\nmake decisions by feeding it massive amounts of data. Training does not allow\nAI systems to reliably recall a specific source or anything published since\ntraining concluded, which is why RAG agents fetch live content at query time\ninstead to ensure more accurate responses.\n\n**AI search indexing agent** — AI systems with access to the real-time web\nneed an index of the internet, used to direct RAG agents to the right\nsources when collecting data for responses to prompts. AI search indexing\ncrawlers build these indexes by systematically navigating the web, collecting\nand organizing content and metadata.\n\nAdditionally, some AI developers use a single bot (or at least software that\nidentifies itself with the same user agent metadata) for more than one of\nthese purposes. We consider these \"hybrid agents\". For example, Googlebot and\nBingbot were built to index the web for search, but the same bots now also\nfeed AI products such as Google's AI Overviews and Microsoft's Copilot.\n\n\u003c/InfoBox\u003e\n\nThe two regions are primarily scraped for different reasons: North America is\nRAG-led (49%), while Europe skews to training (41%). As we discuss in our\nreferral section, training, unlike RAG, returns no human visitors.\n\nFor training to still come out ahead of RAG traffic in Europe may suggest\nthat the AI ecosystem is earlier than in North America, and we expect to\neventually see the flip where RAG scraping exceeds training, as we observed\nin North America. On the other hand, Europe's large, multi-language body of\ncontent is well-suited to building a diverse training corpus to support\nvarious languages, though seeing this shift towards RAG may take further\ntime.\n\nAlso, AI search indexers, the infrastructure behind AI search products, make\nup a similar portion of AI traffic in each respective region. The prominence\nof search indexers is consistent with our Q3 \u0026 Q4 2025 findings that\nindexers were the fastest-growing category globally.\n\n**Figure 2.2.3: AI scrape composition by bot category and region | H1 2026**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-2-2-3-scrape-composition-by-category-region.svg\"\n  alt=\"AI scrape composition by bot category and region\"\n/\u003e\n\nThe median European site sees 1 AI bot visit for every 33 human visits,\nversus 1 AI bot for every 93 human visits for sites in North America. The\nlower the ratio, the worse for publishers: European publishers are facing a\nhigher load of AI bots for every human visitor to a tune of ~2.8× that of\nNorth American sites.\n\n**Figure 2.2.4: Median AI-to-human ratio by region | H1 2026**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-2-2-4-ai-human-ratio-by-region.svg\"\n  alt=\"Median AI-to-human ratio by region\"\n/\u003e\n\n### 2.3 The Europe Cohort, Quarter over Quarter\n\nThe average AI bot scrapes per European site grew by nearly 20% from January\nto June of 2026.\n\n**Figure 2.3.1: Daily average AI bot scrapes per site, European cohort | H1 2026**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-2-3-1-daily-avg-scrapes-europe.svg\"\n  alt=\"Daily average AI bot scrapes per site, European cohort\"\n/\u003e\n\nRAG bots keep coming back to the same page. In Q1, RAG bots hit each unique\nEuropean page about 41 times, against 5 for training crawlers and 7 for\nsearch indexers. By Q2, the RAG figure rose to 50, while training and\nindexing stayed flat. This is expected, based on what RAG bots are intended\nto do: retrieve up-to-date information. They re-fetch a page each time a user\nquery needs it, and a single article ends up pulled dozens of times to\nanswer dozens of prompts.\n\n**Figure 2.3.2: AI scrapes per unique page, European cohort | Q1 2026 vs Q2 2026**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-2-3-2-scrapes-per-page-europe-q1-q2.svg\"\n  alt=\"AI scrapes per unique page, European cohort\"\n/\u003e\n\nScraping demand is uneven across content categories. Across sites on TollBit,\nnational news is by far the heaviest scraped content category, followed by\nsports and entertainment. The two fastest-growing categories: Local news\n+103%, Sports +21%.\n\n**Figure 2.3.3: AI scrapes per site by content category, European cohort | Q1 2026 vs Q2 2026**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-2-3-3-scrapes-by-content-category-europe.svg\"\n  alt=\"AI scrapes per site by content category, European cohort\"\n/\u003e\n\n### 2.4 Scraping by AI Company \u0026 by AI Bot\n\nOpenAI scrapes European publisher sites far more heavily than anyone else.\nOver H1 OpenAI averaged about 4.5M scrapes per site across our cohort,\nroughly 3.5x the next platform. Meta, Amazon, and Anthropic sit well behind,\nand all close to the same scale of scraping, each at about a quarter of\nOpenAI's rate. Amazon was the fastest riser of the three, with per-site\nscraping roughly doubling from Q1 to Q2.\n\n**Figure 2.4: Average scrapes per site by AI platform, European cohort | H1 2026**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-2-4-scrapes-by-platform-europe.svg\"\n  alt=\"Average scrapes per site by AI platform, European cohort\"\n/\u003e\n\nBroken out by individual bot instead of by company, one bot stands out.\nChatGPT-User, OpenAI's real-time retrieval agent, averaged about 3.16M\nscrapes per site in H1. That's more than three times the next bot on the\nlist. Following ChatGPT-User we see a few bots that averaged between 500,000\nand 1 million scrapes. Amazonbot and ClaudeBot came in almost even at roughly\n960,000 each, with meta-webindexer, GPTBot, and OAI-SearchBot close behind\nnear 660,000.\n\n**Figure 2.4.1: Average scrapes per site by AI bot, European cohort | H1 2026**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-2-4-1-scrapes-by-bot-europe.svg\"\n  alt=\"Average scrapes per site by AI bot, European cohort\"\n/\u003e\n\n**Figure 2.4.2: Training crawler share by bot, European cohort | H1 2026**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-2-4-2-training-data-crawler-share-by-bot.svg\"\n  alt=\"Training crawler share by bot, European cohort\"\n/\u003e\n\n### 2.5 IP Address Investigation\n\nA user-agent string is the label a bot attaches to its requests to identify\nitself. Any bot can set the user agent string to whatever it likes. Changing\nit to pose as a different browser, device, or platform is known as\nuser-agent spoofing. Because it can be faked, a user-agent string isn't\nreliable on its own for identifying bots. Therefore, we also analyzed the IP\naddresses associated with the AI bot requests.\n\nTo do this, we looked at how often bots claiming to be a major AI platform\nactually came from that platform's own, publicly available IP addresses.\nAcross OpenAI, Anthropic, Amazon, and Perplexity user agents, less than 2% of\neach platform's AI scrapes on sites appear to be spoofed by someone else\nusing the platform's name. The IP addresses for over 98% of traffic match the\nIP addresses published by the user agent's respective platform. If you are\nseeing these AI platforms accessing your site based on user agents, it is\nhighly likely that the traffic is actually coming from the relevant platform.\n\n**By Platform**\n\nAt the platform level, spoofing is rare. Every operator we checked came in\nunder 2%: OpenAI at 0.67%, Anthropic at 1.96%, Amazon at 0.87%, Perplexity at\n0.23%. When a request says it's GPTBot or ClaudeBot, it usually is.\n\n| Platform   | Percentage of scrapes from the official IP address range | Percentage of scrapes that are spoofed |\n| :--------- | :------------------------------------------------------: | :------------------------------------: |\n| OpenAI     |                          99.33%                          |                 0.67%                  |\n| Anthropic  |                          98.04%                          |                 1.96%                  |\n| Amazon     |                          99.13%                          |                 0.87%                  |\n| Perplexity |                          99.77%                          |                 0.23%                  |\n\n**By AI Bot**\n\nThere are some individual bots that are spoofed at higher rates. Anthropic's\nclaude-user was spoofed 12.70% of the time and claude-searchbot 5.87%, well\nabove Anthropic's 1.96% average. Out of the 12.7% of times claude-user is\nbeing listed as spoofed, 27% of these requests are actually from an IP\naddress that appears to be registered by Anthropic, but they have not yet\nadded this IP address to their official docs at the time of this report's\npublication. Amazon also had some individual bots with higher percentages:\namzn-searchbot at 5.67% and amzn-user at 4.10%, but amazonbot was only at\n0.14%.\n\n| Platform   | User Agent       | Percentage of scrapes that are spoofed |\n| :--------- | :--------------- | :------------------------------------: |\n| OpenAI     | ChatGPT-User     |                 0.65%                  |\n| OpenAI     | GPTBot           |                 1.02%                  |\n| OpenAI     | OAI-Searchbot    |                 0.29%                  |\n| Anthropic  | ClaudeBot        |                 1.31%                  |\n| Anthropic  | Claude-SearchBot |                 5.87%                  |\n| Anthropic  | Claude-User      |                 12.7%                  |\n| Amazon     | Amazonbot        |                 0.14%                  |\n| Amazon     | Amzn-SearchBot   |                 5.67%                  |\n| Amazon     | Amzn-User        |                 4.10%                  |\n| Perplexity | PerplexityBot    |                 0.23%                  |\n| Perplexity | Perplexity-User  |                 0.33%                  |\n\n**By Region and by Platform**\n\nDepending on the platform, some bots are spoofed more often in Europe, while\nothers are spoofed more often in North America. Overall, European requests\nwere spoofed at 0.91%, more than double North America's 0.36%.\n\n| Region        | Platform   | Percentage of scrapes from the official IP address range | Percentage of scrapes that are spoofed |\n| :------------ | :--------- | :------------------------------------------------------: | :------------------------------------: |\n| North America | OpenAI     |                          99.69%                          |                 0.31%                  |\n| North America | Anthropic  |                          99.09%                          |                 0.91%                  |\n| North America | Amazon     |                          99.91%                          |                 0.09%                  |\n| North America | Perplexity |                          99.8%                           |                  0.2%                  |\n| Europe        | OpenAI     |                          99.1%                           |                  0.9%                  |\n| Europe        | Anthropic  |                          98.31%                          |                 1.69%                  |\n| Europe        | Amazon     |                          99.94%                          |                 0.06%                  |\n| Europe        | Perplexity |                          99.9%                           |                  0.1%                  |\n\n\u003cInfoBox title=\"TollBit Analytics\"\u003e\n\nIf you're curious what AI bot traffic looks like on your own site and how it\nis evolving over time, you can sign up for TollBit Analytics at\n[tollbit.com/analytics](https://tollbit.com/analytics). It's free for\npublishers and typically takes an hour or less to set up and will show you\nwho is scraping you, how often, whether or not they send you referrals, and\nmore.\n\n\u003c/InfoBox\u003e\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n\n---\n\n\u003cSection id=\"section-3\"\u003e\n\n## **Section 3: Referral Traffic**\n\n\u003cUserStory\u003e\n  - Referrals from AI applications are a negligible source of traffic for\n  European publishers. In the first half of 2026, just 0.05% of external\n  referrals to European sites came from an AI app, versus 0.16% in North\n  America. - Google still sends the overwhelming majority of human visitors to\n  sites, around 84% of external referrals for European sites. Google returns\n  roughly 1,681 human visits for every one an AI application returns. - European\n  sites receive fewer human visitors back for the RAG scrapes on their sites\n  compared to North American sites. It takes 179 AI bot visits to get a single\n  human visitor referral in return from AI applications for European publishers\n  (versus 53 AI bot visits for 1 human visitor in return for sites in North\n  America). - Within our European cohort, this scrape-to-referral exchange\n  worsened over H1, from 150:1 in Q1 2026 to 227:1 in Q2 2026.\n\u003c/UserStory\u003e\n\n### 3.1 Referral Composition\n\nPublisher revenue largely depends on human visitors to sites for advertiser\nand subscription revenue. Our reports have shown that AI bot traffic sends\nback far fewer visitors compared to traditional Google search, threatening\npublishers' traditional methods of revenue. Newer advanced agents like Claude\nCode (from Anthropic) and Codex (from OpenAI) do not even include citation\nlinks in their outputs, depriving publishers of any opportunity to receive a\nhuman click and visitor back to their sites.\n\nAI applications provide abysmal amounts of referrals back to European sites.\nThey accounted for 0.05% of external referrals to European sites in H1 2026,\ncompared with 0.16% in North America. To put that into perspective, Google\nsends a European publisher roughly 1,681 human visitors for every one an AI\napplication sends back. North America's AI referral share is higher, about 3x\nEurope's, but the number is too small in either region to count as\nmeaningful.\n\nLooking across TollBit's European sites in the first half of 2026, the\nexternal referral mix is still overwhelmingly driven by Google despite the\nsurge in AI bot activity. Google accounts for around 84% of external\nreferrals to European sites, with 80% in North America.\n\n**Figure 3.1: Referral share by source and region | H1 2026**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-3-1-referral-share-by-source-region.svg\"\n  alt=\"Referral share by source and region\"\n/\u003e\n\nFor European publishers, the more relevant non-Google referral channel is\nsocial. Outside Google, European referrals are led by Facebook at 7.13% of\nall referrals, followed by Bing (1.79%) and Instagram (0.68%).\n\n### 3.2 Scrape-to-Referral Ratio\n\nReferral share tells us how little traffic AI sends out of all traffic. The\nscrape-to-referral ratio measures what a publisher gives up versus what it\ngets back: how much of its content is scraped for each visitor an AI\nplatform actually sends. In this report, we only look at the number of\nsuccessful scrapes by a RAG agent (not all AI agents) for this ratio. The\nreason for this is that training and search indexing crawlers do not present\na potential opportunity for a human visitor to see a link and click through\nto a publisher's site.\n\nEuropean publishers fare poorly with this metric: It takes around 179\nsuccessful RAG scrapes to return one human visitor to a European site,\ncompared to 53 in North America. The exchange for European content is less\nfavorable than for North American sites. Compared to North America, European\ncontent is being taken more heavily, relative to the human visitors' AI\nreturns.\n\nOne potential reason for a higher, worse-off ratio is that users are getting\nwhat they need from the AI summary and not clicking through when the source\nwas cited. When this occurs, value is extracted from publishers' content\nwithout compensating them and without sending a human visitor back.\n\n**Figure 3.2: AI scrape-to-referral ratio by region | H1 2026**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-3-2-scrape-referral-ratio-by-region.svg\"\n  alt=\"AI scrape-to-referral ratio by region\"\n/\u003e\n\nOne caveat worth calling out is that because Googlebot and Bingbot (MSFT) are\nhybrid agents, crawling for both search and AI products under one identity,\nwe cannot separate their traditional search-related scrapes from their\nAI-driven ones. Therefore, we cannot look at Gemini (Google) or Copilot\n(MSFT) when calculating the scrape-to-referral ratio. We only use the scrapes\nfrom bots that are explicitly defined as RAG bots to see how many scrapes it\ntakes to get a referral — aka a human visitor in return.\n\n### 3.3 European Cohort: Referral Composition and Scrape-to-Referral Ratio\n\nTo see how referrals are changing over time, we looked at the same cohort of\nEuropean publishers over H1 and found that the composition of referrals\nshifted modestly over the quarter. Google's share held roughly flat at\n~84.3%, and Facebook's fell by 17.6%, whilst AI applications fell by 40% from\n0.063% to 0.038%. That means roughly 1 in every 2,634 external referrals now\ncomes from an AI app for this cohort of publishers.\n\n**Figure 3.3: Referral share by source, European Cohort | Q1 2026 vs Q2 2026**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-3-3-referral-share-europe-cohort-q1-q2.svg\"\n  alt=\"Referral share by source, European Cohort\"\n/\u003e\n\nThe worsening scrape-to-referral ratio within the European cohort similarly\nechoes our prior findings. The European cohort's scrape-to-referral ratio\nrose from 150:1 in Q1 to 227:1, meaning it now on average takes 51% more RAG\nscrapes to return a single human visitor.\n\n**Figure 3.4: Scrape-to-referral ratio, European cohort | Q1 2026 vs Q2 2026**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-3-4-scrape-referral-ratio-europe-cohort-q1-q2.svg\"\n  alt=\"Scrape-to-referral ratio, European cohort\"\n/\u003e\n\nThe scrape-to-referral ratio varies widely by AI company. Meta appears to be\nby far the least efficient referrer as of the end of H1, with over 1,500 RAG\nscrapes to send back a single human visitor. OpenAI, the cohort's largest RAG\nscraper by far, returns one human visitor for every 265 scrapes, worsening\nfrom its Q1 ratio of 165 scrapes for 1 human visitor.\n\nThere are two important limitations of the scrape-to-referral ratio data.\nWhile this ratio approximates what referrals publishers are receiving in\nreturn for the use of their content, it's not perfect:\n\n1. AI companies are not presently required to self-identify their user\n   agents, though that may be starting to change.\n   [New York's Stealth Crawler Prohibition Act](https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/bills/2025/S9934/amendment/A),\n   passed by the state legislature in June 2026 and awaiting signature, would\n   require exactly this kind of disclosure. But until legislation is passed,\n   it's always possible these companies are utilizing unidentified user\n   agents, which could mean that AI companies are actually scraping sites\n   more often than we can measure. This goes for all scrape data in the\n   report, but the impacts are particularly visible with this metric. It's\n   worth noting that in our Q4 2024 State of the Bots report we found data\n   potentially supporting the notion that Perplexity does not always\n   self-identify its user agent and may be using other user agents to\n   scrape. This is likely why the Perplexity referral ratio below appears\n   more favorable than it likely is.\n2. Another limitation is that AI companies may cache (store) and reuse\n   content for periods of time. Section 6 of our Q2 2025 State of the Bots\n   report documents this. This is why scrapes aren't equivalent to the\n   actual usage of content, but it's the best approximation we have to it.\n   Anthropic (Claude) cached content longer than other AI applications,\n   which could be why its scrape-to-referral ratio appears more favorable\n   than others'. This means Anthropic may be storing content more, versus\n   going out and fetching/scraping content as frequently as other AI\n   companies. However, that doesn't necessarily mean it isn't using the\n   content it's fetched less.\n\n**Figure 3.5: Scrape-to-referral ratio by AI platform, Europe cohort | Q1 vs Q2 2026**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-3-5-scrape-referral-ratio-by-platform-europe.svg\"\n  alt=\"Scrape-to-referral ratio by AI platform, Europe cohort\"\n/\u003e\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n\n---\n\n\u003cSection id=\"section-4\"\u003e\n\n## **Section 4: Robots.txt and the Bypassing Problem**\n\n\u003cUserStory\u003e\n  - Robots.txt is not protecting European publishers: ~15% of AI scrapes on\n  European sites in H1 2026 ignored an active disallow instruction. - The median\n  amount of bypassed scrapes for a European site is 2.8x that of a North\n  American site. - For the major training crawlers, European sites disallow more\n  than North American sites — CCBot 63% vs 46%, GPTBot 60% vs 41%, ClaudeBot 53%\n  vs 43%. - ChatGPT-User, Bytespider, and Youbot bypass robots.txt instructions\n  on nearly 50% of all European sites that have explicitly disallowed them.\n\u003c/UserStory\u003e\n\n### 4.1 Disallow Adoption\n\n\u003cInfoBox title=\"Robots.txt \u0026 Bypass Definitions\"\u003e\n\n**Robots Exclusion Protocol (robots.txt)** — a voluntary handshake agreement,\nin place since the early days of the Internet, by which a website uses a\nrobots.txt file to signal which automated user agents are permitted to\naccess it. It has no enforcement mechanism and depends entirely on the\ngoodwill of those operating the bots.\n\n**Disallow** — an instruction in a publisher's robots.txt file naming a\nspecific user agent that the publisher does not permit to access its\ncontent. AI developers note it may take 24-72 hours for disallow to be\nlogged and reflected by their bots.\n\n**Bypass** — when an AI bot scrapes a publisher's content despite that\npublisher having explicitly disallowed that bot in its robots.txt file.\n\n\u003c/InfoBox\u003e\n\nDisallowing a bot in robots.txt only matters if the bot obeys the guidance,\nbut a measurable share of AI bots do not. Across European and North American\nsites in the first half of 2026, ~15% of AI scrapes bypassed a disallow\ninstruction.\n\nEuropean publishers have disallowed the following bots on robots.txt at a\nhigher rate than North American sites. 63% of European sites disallow CCBot,\nagainst 46% in North America. The same holds for GPTBot (60% vs 41%),\nClaudeBot (53% vs 43%), PerplexityBot (50% vs 40%), and OAI-SearchBot (46% vs\n34%).\n\nFor the newer real-time user agents, the ones that fetch pages live to\nanswer a prompt, European disallow adoption drops off and North America\nmoves ahead, often by more than double. Only 9% of European sites disallow\nClaude-User, compared to 26% in North America. Perplexity-User is 13% versus\n26%. Most of the newest agents sit in the single digits in Europe.\nChatGPT-User is the exception among the AI user agents, and is blocked at a\nrate closer to the established crawlers, most likely because it is the most\nwidely recognized.\n\n**Figure 4.1: Disallow adoption by AI bot and region | Q2 2026**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-4-1-disallow-adoption-by-bot-region.svg\"\n  alt=\"Disallow adoption by AI bot and region\"\n/\u003e\n\n### 4.2 The Bypass Problem\n\nFor the median (typical) European site, bypassed scrapes are 2.8x times a\ntypical North American site. These bypasses represent the website being\naccessed against the publisher's stated wishes, consuming server resources\nin the process, and, for AI bots, rarely returning a human visitor in\nexchange.\n\nGiven that European publishers are scraped at a 4x higher rate by AI bots\nthan North American sites, it's not too surprising that their median\nbypassed scrapes would appear higher.\n\n**Figure 4.2: Median bypassed scrapes per site by region | H1 2026**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-4-2-median-bypassed-scrapes-by-region.svg\"\n  alt=\"Median bypassed scrapes per site by region\"\n/\u003e\n\n### 4.3 Bypass by AI Bot\n\nThis brings us to another metric: the bypass rate, which is the percentage of\nall scrapes that ignored explicit robots.txt guidelines and successfully\nscraped content anyway.\n\nChatGPT-User had the highest bypass rate, ignoring explicit robots.txt\ninstructions on 54% of all its scrapes. It is followed by Bytespider with a\nbypass rate of 48% and PerplexityBot with a bypass rate of 42%.\n\n**Figure 4.3: Share of sites where disallowed bots bypassed robots.txt, all European sites | H1 2026**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-4-3-bypass-share-by-bot-europe.svg\"\n  alt=\"Share of sites where disallowed bots bypassed robots.txt\"\n/\u003e\n\nFor the AI bots that have a smaller bypass rate, there often appears to be an\ninteresting trend of the bypass rate increasing between Q1 \u0026 Q2. For example,\nthe data shows GPTBot, CCBot, and DuckAssistBot all bypassing more often than\nin Q1, an early sign that bypassing behaviors may be spreading beyond the\nhandful of agents that dominate it today.\n\n**Figure 4.3.1: Bypass rate by AI bot, Europe Cohort | Q1 vs Q2 2026**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-4-3-1-bypass-rate-by-bot-europe-q1-q2.svg\"\n  alt=\"Bypass rate by AI bot, Europe Cohort\"\n/\u003e\n\nRobots.txt files were designed for an era when honoring it cost a crawler\nlittle, and defying it gained it little and cost the site little. As AI\ntools and traffic explode, that balance no longer holds. AI bots take\npublisher content, cost the sites yet serve them, yet send back very few\nhuman visitors in return.\n\nBecause the robots.txt protocol is not active enforcement and rests on the\ngoodwill of the operator, European publishers who rely on it alone today are\nprotected only if AI developers choose to cooperate, and for the bots doing\nthe most scraping, abidance of robots.txt remains thin.\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"3f:T11dad,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"var Component=(()=\u003e{var b=Object.create;var d=Object.defineProperty;var y=Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor;var w=Object.getOwnPropertyNames;var v=Object.getPrototypeOf,A=Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty;var I=(n,t)=\u003e()=\u003e{try{return t||n((t={exports:{}}).exports,t),t.exports}catch(s){throw t=0,s}},x=(n,t)=\u003e{for(var s in t)d(n,s,{get:t[s],enumerable:!0})},p=(n,t,s,h)=\u003e{if(t\u0026\u0026typeof t==\"object\"||typeof t==\"function\")for(let i of w(t))!A.call(n,i)\u0026\u0026i!==s\u0026\u0026d(n,i,{get:()=\u003et[i],enumerable:!(h=y(t,i))||h.enumerable});return n};var k=(n,t,s)=\u003e(s=n!=null?b(v(n)):{},p(t||!n||!n.__esModule?d(s,\"default\",{value:n,enumerable:!0}):s,n)),T=n=\u003ep(d({},\"__esModule\",{value:!0}),n);var g=I((B,u)=\u003e{u.exports=_jsx_runtime});var E={};x(E,{default:()=\u003em,frontmatter:()=\u003eq});var e=k(g()),q={title:\"State of the Bots\",id:\"q1-q2-2026\",quarter:\"q1-q2-2026\",description:\"2026 Q1 \u0026 Q2, The Bad Bots\",docType:\"report\",sections:[{title:\"Foreword\",slug:\"foreword\"},{title:\"Executive Summary\",slug:\"executive-summary\"},{title:\"Section 1: The Scraper Ecosystem \u0026 Proxy Networks\",slug:\"section-1\",subsections:[{title:\"Scraper Audit Tool \u0026 Results on UK Sites\",slug:\"scraper-audit-uk\"},{title:\"Scraper Audit: Other Observations\",slug:\"scraper-audit-observations\"},{title:\"The Inefficiency and Cost of Web Scraping\",slug:\"scraping-cost\"},{title:\"The Limits of Cybersecurity and the Need for Regulation\",slug:\"cybersecurity-limits\"}]},{title:\"Section 2: The Scale of AI Scraping\",slug:\"section-2\",subsections:[{title:\"Aggregate Scraping Levels\",slug:\"aggregate-scraping\"},{title:\"AI Scraping by Region: North America vs Europe\",slug:\"scraping-region\"},{title:\"The Europe Cohort, Quarter over Quarter\",slug:\"europe-cohort-qoq\"},{title:\"Scraping by AI Company \u0026 by AI Bot\",slug:\"scraping-by-company\"},{title:\"IP Address Investigation\",slug:\"ip-investigation\"}]},{title:\"Section 3: Referral Traffic\",slug:\"section-3\",subsections:[{title:\"Referral Composition\",slug:\"referral-composition\"},{title:\"Scrape-to-Referral Ratio\",slug:\"scrape-referral-ratio\"},{title:\"European Cohort: Referrals \u0026 Ratio Over Time\",slug:\"europe-cohort-referrals\"}]},{title:\"Section 4: Robots.txt and the Bypassing Problem\",slug:\"section-4\",subsections:[{title:\"Disallow Adoption\",slug:\"disallow-adoption\"},{title:\"The Bypass Problem\",slug:\"bypass-problem\"},{title:\"Bypass by AI Bot\",slug:\"bypass-by-bot\"}]}]};function f(n){let t={a:\"a\",em:\"em\",h2:\"h2\",h3:\"h3\",h4:\"h4\",hr:\"hr\",li:\"li\",ol:\"ol\",p:\"p\",strong:\"strong\",table:\"table\",tbody:\"tbody\",td:\"td\",th:\"th\",thead:\"thead\",tr:\"tr\",ul:\"ul\",...n.components},{ExecItem:s,ExecSummary:h,ExpandableImage:i,Image:r,InfoBox:o,Section:a,UserStory:c}=t;return s||l(\"ExecItem\",!0),h||l(\"ExecSummary\",!0),i||l(\"ExpandableImage\",!0),r||l(\"Image\",!0),o||l(\"InfoBox\",!0),a||l(\"Section\",!0),c||l(\"UserStory\",!0),(0,e.jsxs)(e.Fragment,{children:[(0,e.jsxs)(a,{id:\"foreword\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Foreword\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"By Dr. Jonathan Roberts, Chief Innovation Officer at People Inc.:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(o,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:'\"A lot has changed in a year. Last summer the questions were:'}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"Does content have value in an AI economy?\"}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"Will blocking work?\"}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"Will there be a market for information?\"}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"In July 2026, we know that good AI needs good inputs, and needs those inputs in real time. Blocking makes products worse, and great inputs make it better. You can\\u2019t just throw more chips at the problem.\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"Data centers are the engine.\"}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"Content is the fuel.\"}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"In the last 12 months stealing content has become big business:\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"There are now more bots online than humans.\"}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[\"Multiple AI Search startups \",(0,e.jsx)(t.a,{href:\"https://exa.ai/blog/announcing-series-c\",children:\"have raised\"}),\" \",(0,e.jsx)(t.a,{href:\"https://parallel.ai/blog/series-b\",children:\"capital\"}),\" at valuations of over $2bn, selling access to content that they take for free.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"Demand is growing exponentially.\"}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"At People Inc. we provide fast, direct, licensed access to our premium content for our partners, and have gone deep to block those who are wholesale stealing our content. In February we moved to block all bots from companies we don\\u2019t have a partnership with. We allow a short list of named crawlers from trusted partners, and we block tens of thousands of unique bad bots every day, and block tens of millions of daily attempts to take our content.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"The most aggressive actors follow a standard pattern of behavior:\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ol,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"They send a named crawler - we block it.\"}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"They send an anonymous crawler - we block it.\"}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"They send a crawler that spoofs googlebot - we block it.\"}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"They send a crawler that attempts to look human, scrolling the page, and executing code - we provide a \\u2018are you human\\u2019 challenge. It fails and is blocked.\"}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"They send multiple crawlers through residential internet connections and mobile devices. Because these home proxy networks use legitimate IP addresses they are extremely difficult to identify as being compromised by bad actors and therefore block. We are able to block some of this activity, but not all.\"}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"It\\u2019s now clear that:\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"Content has a lot of value in an AI economy.\"}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"Blocking works.\"}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"There\\u2019s a market being built in real time.\"}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"In the last year we\\u2019ve seen the emergence of around 30 \\u201CNapsters of content\\u201D, but no Spotify. How do we build a premium information economy that lets people who need information pay the people who create it?\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"Step 1: stop people taking it for free.\"}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"Step 2: flip the market from stealing to paying.\"}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"If we get this wrong - a few intermediaries sell the world\\u2019s information for their own gain, removing any reason to invest in new knowledge, killing the digital economy, and AI gets worse as inputs degrade.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"If we get this right, the exploding demand for knowledge funds investment in new information creation and AI products get much better.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:'I know which future I want to live in, and we\\u2019re working across publishing, CDNs, platforms, legislation and regulation to bring it into reality.\"'})]})]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(a,{id:\"executive-summary\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Executive Summary\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(h,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(s,{num:1,children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:`Bots are shockingly effective at evading cybersecurity protections and\npaywalls; we observed them impersonate Google and others, rotate IP addresses,\nand appear to use residential proxies, hammering publishers with bot\ntraffic.`}),` On UK sites, we saw similar trends to those we observed on US\nsites: paywalled content was not necessarily safe from web scrapers, and 95%\nof the top UK sites could be scraped by at least one of the 14 scrapers we\ntested. Scrapers' disguises are sometimes good enough to fool ad systems \\u2014 we\nobserved scrapers triggering Google Ads bots, meaning the page treated a bot\nlike a human and rendered ads to it, a concern for advertisers paying to reach\nhumans. In one instance, we observed a scraper hit a publisher's site over 40\ntimes in 3 seconds, creating bandwidth cost the publisher ends up paying.`]})}),(0,e.jsx)(s,{num:2,children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:`The economics of online publishers are eroding in real time, and European\npublishers are being hit hardest.`}),` Data from publishers on TollBit shows\nEuropean sites get scraped more, receive fewer referrals in return, and have\ntheir robots.txt rules bypassed more often. Median AI scrapes per site were 4\\xD7\nhigher on European sites compared to North American sites, and AI scrapes on\nEuropean sites were nearly 20% higher in June than in January 2026, with local\nnews and sports sites seeing the highest quarter-over-quarter growth. Note:\nthese numbers reflect only identified bots, so the true scale of AI scraping\nis likely even higher.`]})}),(0,e.jsx)(s,{num:3,children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:`Publishers aren't receiving anything back from the scrapes, and the exchange\nis getting worse.`}),` It takes 179 AI bot visits to get a single human visitor\nreferral in return from AI applications for European publishers \\u2014 a rate over\n3x worse than North American sites. This imbalance worsened over H1; the\nscrape-to-referral ratio went from 150:1 in Q1 2026 to 227:1 in Q2 2026. AI\nbot traffic accounted for only 0.05% of total human referrals on European\nsites, which also carry roughly 3x the AI load per human visitor as their\nNorth American peers, seeing about 1 AI bot scrape for every 33 human visits.`]})}),(0,e.jsx)(s,{num:4,children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Robots.txt instructions do very little to stop these bots.\"}),` The median\nEuropean site's robots.txt instructions to not scrape were ignored 2.8x more\noften than North American sites'.`]})}),(0,e.jsx)(s,{num:5,children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:`The methods these scrapers use to evade detection are the same ones used to\naccess infrastructure or manipulate social media by nation-state hackers and\nsophisticated cyber-criminals.`}),` Now, these tools are used by venture-backed\nSilicon Valley companies serving major corporate clients \\u2014 without\nintervention, the volume will compound exponentially as the companies behind\nit scale. A majority of these scraping companies advertise using methods such\nas residential proxies, the same functionality used by bad actors in\ncyberattacks, to gain access to sites and evade their cybersecurity measures.`]})}),(0,e.jsx)(s,{num:6,children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:`Bots should not be allowed to mimic humans or other traffic on the web; they\nshould be required to self-identify.`}),` As these disguise techniques grow more\nsophisticated, bots become harder to detect, placing an undue burden and\nexpense on the sites. Self-identification would let publishers and sites\ndecide how to respond to bot visitors.`]})})]})]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(a,{id:\"section-1\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Section 1: The Scraper Ecosystem Powering AI Applications \u0026 How Proxy Networks Work\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(c,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`The web scrapers that power AI tools are using sophisticated methods to\nconceal the identities of the bots they use for scraping using many\ntechniques. Using our Scraper Audit tool, we can observe how effective\nscrapers are in getting access to content, and to an extent, the techniques\nthey use to gain access:`}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:`In testing on sites in the UK, we saw similar trends to those we observed\non sites in the US: paywalled content was not necessarily safe from web\nscrapers, and 19 out of the 20 top UK sites we tested could be scraped by\nat least one of the 14 scrapers we tested.`}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:`In testing on various publisher sites, we observed a number of techniques\nthat scrapers used to get access to publisher sites, including rotating\nIP addresses and masquerading as other user agents, including Googlebot.`}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:`We also noticed Google Ads bots could be triggered on sites, a concern for\nadvertisers who are paying for their ads to be seen by humans, and not\nscraper bots.`}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[`One of the most concerning methods scrapers are using is residential\nproxies, the same technique bad actors use to mask cyberattacks. Residential\nproxies are a potential national security concern as they make it virtually\nimpossible to know who is on the other end, operating a bot. A majority of\nthe web scrapers in our `,(0,e.jsx)(t.a,{href:\"https://tollbit.com/scraper-index/\",children:\"Scraper Index\"}),`\nopenly advertise that they use residential proxies.`]})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(o,{title:\"Introduction: The Growth and Sophistication of the Data Acquisition Stack\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`AI developers are combining multiple data acquisition processes and\ntechnologies, many offered by specialist web scraping vendors, into tech\nstacks that obtain access to the Internet. Fueled by the AI boom, hundreds of\nthese third-party scrapers are in use, many by Fortune 1000 companies.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`These intermediaries scrape publisher content to resell it as data or search\ninfrastructure, rather than to directly power their own chatbots or AI\napplications. Many of these scrapers employ specialized cybersecurity evasion\ntools and do not comply with robots.txt by default. This places an undue\nburden on sites to attempt to detect these increasingly hard-to-detect\nscrapers.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`The data acquisition stack has existed for quite some time, but its effects\nhave become more visible recently. With AI, the need to access content \u0026 data\nhas exploded. Funded by the AI investment boom, developers are combining\nmultiple data acquisition processes and technologies, with more advanced or\nclandestine approaches deployed where cybersecurity barriers are encountered.\nThese increasingly complicated tech stacks are intended to secure \"just\nenough\" access to content to ensure the applications they serve can handle\nthe range of queries needed to compete. These stacks are comprised of some or\nall of the following:`}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:`Direct crawling and scraping via first-party bots, either declared or\ndisguised.`}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:`Secondary source crawling / scraping, targeting surfaces on which the\ncontent itself may be visible on the open web (i.e., Google Search results\npage).`}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:`Residential IP proxies, presenting as ordinary user traffic \\u2014 often with\nlocation and device characteristics/IP addresses that match the human\naudience.`}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"Circumvention services that evade bot detection / IP protection measures.\"}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:`Cloud-based headless browsers that load web content in a browser running in\nthe cloud, rather than on a user's device. These are often used with a\nresidential IP proxy: the proxy makes the request appear to come from a\nnormal human user.`}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:`Third-party scraping services for outsourcing the end-to-end process of\nscraping a page.`}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[`For more detailed information, please visit our\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.a,{href:\"https://tollbit.com/scraper-index/\",children:\"Scraper Index\"}),\".\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`For obvious reasons, this environment is one in which it is exceedingly\ndifficult for digital publishers to exercise control over the use of their\nIP. They are in a structurally disadvantaged position, having to respond to\nnew threats as they emerge, whereas the web scrapers just need to evolve\nbeyond the latest protection. Most websites deploy only one cybersecurity\ntool, whereas AI companies can choose from dozens of scraping tools\ninterchangeably.`})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(o,{title:\"Growth of AI Data Acquisition Stack \u0026 Key H1 2026 Observations\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`Two notable additions here to our prior reporting on the AI Data Acquisition\nstack:`}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[`First is the increase in attention we've seen to residential proxies, along\nwith the potential national security risk associated with them. A\nresidential proxy routes traffic through everyday consumer devices so that\nthe request appears to come from a human using an ordinary home internet\nconnection. This technique is utilized by many web scrapers \\u2014 and this is the\nsame exact technique that bad actors use to conceal who they are in\ncyberattacks and large-scale cognitive warfare, such as astroturfing\ncampaigns. Recently, there has been a lot of attention to this in the Wall\nStreet Journal reporting, with an\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.a,{href:\"https://www.wsj.com/tech/cybersecurity/how-hackers-found-a-back-door-into-the-american-living-room-c117cb9f?mod=hp_lead_pos7\",children:\"article here\"}),`\non this topic, and a recent separate\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.a,{href:\"https://www.wsj.com/tech/cybersecurity/how-millions-of-digital-home-devices-are-secretly-powering-cyberattacks-8fd73584?mod=WTRN_pos2\",children:\"piece here\"}),`\nwith a view of an FBI director stating \"we all want to sort of find and\ndefeat these Residential Proxy networks,\" because of their association with\ncrime. Those efforts are already underway. In July 2026, Google, working with\nthe FBI and others,\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.a,{href:\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/threat-intelligence/google-continued-disruption-residential-proxy-networks\",children:\"disrupted NetNut\"}),`,\none of the largest residential proxy networks, which it estimated ran on more\nthan 2 million hijacked consumer devices such as smart TVs and streaming\nboxes. Google reported that in a single week, it observed hundreds of\ndistinct threat groups, including cybercriminal and espionage actors, using\nthe network to hide the origin of their traffic. The Google blog post about\nthis also linked out to a\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.a,{href:\"https://krebsonsecurity.com/2026/06/popa-botnet-linked-to-publicly-traded-israeli-firm/\",children:\"study by a cybersecurity firm\"}),`\nthat noted many of the largest proxy providers' websites highlight their\nutility for AI platforms and tools, \"implying it is a primary use case for\ntheir residential proxies.\"`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[`In an analysis of nearly 40 scraping vendors, we find that many explicitly\nadvertise cybersecurity detection evasion techniques, and many do not\ndefault to abiding by robots.txt. A majority of the web scraping companies in\nour `,(0,e.jsx)(t.a,{href:\"https://tollbit.com/scraper-index\",children:\"Web Scraper Index\"}),` openly advertise\nthat they use residential proxies and have pools of hundreds of millions of\nIP addresses. Using residential proxies appears to be a fairly standard\npractice for scrapers today. Residential proxies make it even more difficult\nfor websites to detect, and therefore block, allow, or monetize bot/scraper\ntraffic. In a site's logs, a bot visitor using a residential proxy will look\njust like a human visitor.`]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`Another addition to our prior reporting is some data on third-party\nscrapers/data resellers across the TollBit network, as we have started to\nnotice some of their traffic actually starting to stand out in our log data\nacross publisher sites. (Important to note, many of these third parties do\nnot self-identify their user agents, but a few do, and so we can analyze\ntheir data). Across all sites on TollBit in H1 2026, we observed that Common\nCrawl, the open corpus behind much LLM training, reached the widest share of\nany identifiable third-party scraper/crawler. We observed Common Crawler\nscrapes on ~74% of sites on TollBit: it roughly doubled its reach\nquarter-over-quarter (from Q1 to Q2). Because a single Common Crawl scrape\ncan support many models and AI applications downstream, its footprint across\nsites matters far more than its per-site volume: one scrape from Common Crawl\nmay then become data that then gets reused to power a vast AI ecosystem.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`Two other notable players that scrape content and resell it as a search or\ndata API: Parallel, a web-search/data API, which had scrapes on 74% of sites\non TollBit, nearly doubled its per-site scraping (+94%), and Diffbot (which\nsells a structured \"knowledge graph\" of the web) roughly tripled per-site\n(+195%) and has scrapes on over 33% of sites on TollBit. Because a reseller's\ncrawl can be resold to many buyers, these figures understate true exposure \\u2014\none scrape resurfaces across multiple AI products, strengthening the case for\nlicensing terms that follow the data downstream.`})]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"1.1 Scraper Audit Tool \u0026 Results on UK Sites\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`In order to examine the sophistication of web scrapers against websites'\ncybersecurity tooling, TollBit developed the Scraper Audit, a tool for\nwebsite owners to test 14 popular web scrapers, many of which are Europe-based\ncompanies, against their own sites. The Scraper Audit allows website owners\nto initiate scraping on any page to evaluate how well scrapers evade\ndefenses, access content, and retrieval completeness.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`In selecting web scrapers, we picked a few industry leaders and a couple of\nnewer scrapers offering advanced technology, despite their company size and\ntime in market, and have plans to add more to the tool. For each of these web\nscrapers, whenever available, we purchased subscriptions of their web\nscraping APIs to leverage their full suite of AI-driven parsing, advanced\nanti-bot bypassing, and premium proxy networks.`}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[`To demonstrate the utility of this tool in auditing scrapers, we conducted an\nevaluation of this tool across the top 20 sites based in the UK as defined by\nthe\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.a,{href:\"https://pressgazette.co.uk/media-audience-and-business-data/media_metrics/most-popular-websites-news-uk-monthly-2/\",children:\"Press Gazette\"}),\".\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`Our test of these sites resulted in similar outcomes to the tests run for the\nprevious edition of the report:`}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:`Even paywalled content is not necessarily safe. In our testing of 14 of\nthese scraping tools, we observed that some were able to scrape various\npaywalled articles in full.`}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:`In fact, non-paywalled sites offered no discernible increase in protection\ncompared to their paywalled counterparts, as scrapers successfully\nretrieved full content from the vast majority of target pages regardless of\nsubscription barriers.`}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:`In the rare instance where all scrapers failed to fully extract data \\u2014\noccurring in only one of the twenty sites \\u2014 the failure was primarily\nattributed to server-side rendering implementations of the paywall. On\nthese specific sites, the full article was not present in the initial page\nand required server-side authorization before the content was served.`}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"1.2 Scraper Audit: Other Observations\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`Since our last bot report, we've added functionality to our Scraper Audit\nTool to allow publishers to observe the suspected chain of events that occurs\nas a web scraper attempts to access their sites. In the tool, publishers can\nenter a specific URL in the Scraper Audit Tool and observe some of the\nstrategies the web scrapers utilize in an attempt to scrape the page. In sum,\nwe observed that scrapers often deploy various tactics to obtain access to\nsites, some hammering sites dozens of times with different user agents,\nsometimes masquerading as other bots (including Googlebot, Chrome, etc.), and\nothers rotating IP addresses in order to attempt to trick the site's\ncybersecurity and allow them to gain access to scrape the page. We also\nobserved that some of the web scrapers would trigger Google Ads bot on sites\n\\u2014 concerning for advertisers who are paying for their ads to be seen by\nhumans, and not bots/web scrapers.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:`A related note: In our last bot report, we noted that we did not observe any\nscrapers checking robots.txt files before attempting to access sites.`})}),(0,e.jsxs)(o,{title:\"Methodology\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`The strongest signal (Canary verification) we have is that the Scraper Audit\nadds a unique ID to the request of each URL we tell the webscraper to scrape,\nand we then look for that unique ID in the respective site's logs so that we\ncan identify which scraper visited the URL. When we find it, we can be highly\nconfident the request was triggered by the scraper audit test we ran.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`The second signal, which we treat as lower confidence, is fingerprint\nmatching. Some requests that hit the server in response to our scrape don't\ncarry the unique ID. For those, we build a fingerprint from the request's\nattributes (its timestamp, IP address, geo, and user agent \\u2014 all of which we\ncan obtain from the respective site's logs) and match it against the scrape\nwe initiated. A request that lines up across all of these \\u2014 arriving at the\nsame moment, from the same IP and geo, with the same user agent \\u2014 points\nstrongly to the scraper, even without the ID to confirm it. We are relying on\nvery close timing to make this assumption (typically sub-second to a few\nseconds after invocation), which we assume is related to the scraper test we\nrun. We know when the scraper was triggered and what page it was triggered\nto, because we control when the scrapers are triggered and to which pages,\nand we have the publishers' site logs data that includes information like IP\naddress, timestamp of the scrape to the site, etc.`})]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`Here are some real examples of what we observed with this new functionality\non publisher sites. Each of the examples below shows a single scraper\ninstance attempting to access a publisher's URL:`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h4,{children:\"1.2.1 Example 1: Masquerading as Google \u0026 IP Address Rotation\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`The scraper in this example appeared to have tried more than one disguise to\nget in. It first used User-Agent Spoofing and posed as Googlebot to gain\ntrusted access. When that was blocked, it appeared to switch to a Chrome\nbrowser, and its apparent location shifted from Los Angeles, US, to San\nSebasti\\xE1n de los Reyes, ES, meaning it also changed its IP address. Once it\nchanged to a browser, it was first served 301 redirects, then kept trying\nuntil it got a 200 and pulled the content. This showcases that when one\nidentity is refused, a scraper may just change who it claims to be and where\nit appears to come from and try again.`}),(0,e.jsx)(i,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/scraper-audit-example-1.svg\",alt:\"Scraper masquerading as Google, request timeline\"}),(0,e.jsx)(i,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/scraper-audit-example-2.svg\",alt:\"Scraper masquerading as Google, evidence detail\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h4,{children:\"1.2.2 Example 2: Multiple Browser Impersonations \u0026 IP Address Rotation\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`This attempt to access a publisher's URL appeared to trigger 43 requests from\na single web scraper, routed through many different IPs and locations,\ncycling between Chrome and Firefox identities throughout the attempt to\naccess the site. The locations of the scrapes heavily varied: alongside US\ncities (San Jose, Ashburn, Clifton, Pennington), the scrapes arrived from\nmultiple Hungarian locations (Sopron, S\\xFCmeg, Ny\\xEDregyh\\xE1za) and Bahrain (Bu\nKuwarah), all within seconds.`}),(0,e.jsx)(i,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/scraper-audit-example-3.svg\",alt:\"Multiple browser impersonations, request timeline\",expandLabel:\"View full screenshot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(i,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/scraper-audit-example-4.svg\",alt:\"Multiple browser impersonations, evidence detail\",expandLabel:\"View full screenshot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h4,{children:\"1.2.3 Example 3: Multiple Browsers \u0026 IP Rotation\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`This scrape request appeared to result in 32 requests hitting the URL at\nessentially the same time, from many different IPs across scattered US\ncities and cycling between Chrome and Firefox identities throughout. One\nscrape is arriving from a dozen-plus origins at once, and being disguised on\nboth the network and identity side. The status column is a real mix of 200s\n(served), 403s (blocked), and 301s (redirected), so the publisher's defenses\nwere firing and worked some of the time, but the blocks didn't matter because\neach request came from a different IP. A 403 on one address did nothing to\nstop the next from a different IP, and the scraper kept rotating until enough\n200s landed to assemble the content.`}),(0,e.jsx)(i,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/scraper-audit-example-5.svg\",alt:\"Multiple browsers and IP rotation, request timeline\",expandLabel:\"View full screenshot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(i,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/scraper-audit-example-6.svg\",alt:\"Multiple browsers and IP rotation, evidence detail\",expandLabel:\"View full screenshot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h4,{children:\"1.2.4 Example 4: Scraper Triggers Google Ad Bots\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[`The two Google Ads Bot requests are worth taking a look at here. They appear\nto have fired because the scraper rendered the page like a real browser,\nloading its ads, which is what set them off. The scrape looks human enough to\ntrigger the ad stack; it could be taken as evidence that the scraper's\ndisguise is convincing and successful. This presents an ad fraud concern for\nadvertisers who are paying to see their ads viewed by humans, and not bots.\n(This is a topic we mentioned previously in our\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.a,{href:\"https://tollbit.com/state-of-the-bots/q2-2025/\",children:\"Q2 2025 Bot report, Section 1\"}),\").\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(i,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/scraper-audit-example-7.svg\",alt:\"Scraper triggering Google Ad bots\",expandLabel:\"View full screenshot\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[`TollBit has made the Scraper Audit tool available on the TollBit platform to\nhelp websites test and understand their vulnerability to IP threats from\nmajor web scrapers. If you are interested in seeing how your site is being\nscraped by these third parties, please contact\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.a,{href:\"mailto:[email withheld by Lighthouse]\",children:\"[email withheld by Lighthouse]\"}),\".\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"1.3 The Inefficiency and Cost of Web Scraping\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`This arms race between scrapers and website operators is not only\ninefficient for website owners, who are increasingly spending on\ncybersecurity to detect bots that are only getting harder to detect, but it\nis also deeply inefficient for AI developers. As mentioned in our prior bot\nreport, we have found advanced scraping services charging up to $22.50 for\n1,000 pages. Given the volume of data needed to serve consumer AI\napplications, the data acquisition costs for the most popular chatbots and\nspecialized agents are likely to run into the tens of millions of dollars a\nyear. And this is before considering the legal fees needed to fight lawsuits.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`We have also observed that scrapers take 16 seconds on average to access\npublisher sites, and some of them may fail entirely in securing access to the\npage requested, whereas accessing content via a sanctioned route (i.e. via a\nplatform like TollBit) typically takes less than 0.7 seconds.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`There's a better way. Instead of spending huge sums on technical workarounds\nto bypass IP controls, AI developers can simply pay for the content they use.\nThis reduces latency and increases reliability. By relying on licensing\nrather than scraping, journalism is funded \\u2014 not the scrapers \\u2014 and the media\necosystem AI depends on is sustained.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"1.4 The Limits of Cybersecurity and the Need for Regulation\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[`We've seen publishers increasingly take matters into their own hands to\ncombat the surge in AI bots, increasingly investing in cybersecurity to\nactively block this traffic from their sites or even take action by filing\nlawsuits. As mentioned in our\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.a,{href:\"https://tollbit.com/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/\",children:\"Q3 \u0026 Q4 2025 report\"}),`, in\nlate 2025, both Reddit and Google turned to the courts over unauthorized\nscraping. Google alleged that scrapers had circumvented SearchGuard, the\nin-house protection system it built at a cost of millions. If a company with\nGoogle's resources and engineering talent cannot keep determined scrapers\nout, an individual publisher has no realistic chance of doing so alone.`]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`Cybersecurity is important, but it is not a foolproof solution. These tools\nare costly to run, and many of our publishers, even those with the most\nsophisticated defenses in place, have seen scrapers and AI bots get through\nanyway.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`This brings us to an important limitation of the data in the subsequent\nsections of this report: we rely on AI companies self-identifying through\ntheir user agents. Developers sometimes use third-party vendors to scrape on\ntheir behalf; they may masquerade as other user agents, rotate their IP\naddresses, use headless browsers that mimic humans, use residential proxies,\netc. This activity is difficult, and often impossible, for sites to trace\nback to a specific AI company. The true level of scraping, and of\nunpermitted access to European content, is, therefore, likely greater than\nthis report shows.`}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[`As AI traffic becomes harder to distinguish from human traffic, it is\nimportant that regulators take a position that, at the bare minimum, AI bots\nshould not be allowed to mimic humans on the Internet. Ideally, they would be\nrequired to self-identify and state their intended use, which is what\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.a,{href:\"https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/bills/2025/A11292\",children:\"New York's Stealth Crawler Prohibition Act\"}),`\nwould require. That bill, passed by the New York State Legislature in June\n2026 and awaiting the Governor's signature, would require crawlers accessing\na news site to disclose their identity and purpose through an accurate\nuser-agent string before they access the content.`]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`Self-identification enables a site owner to decide how to respond: allow,\nblock, or charge for access via infrastructure controls like the ones\nTollBit provides. It would help ensure that the undue burden of paying to\nserve, detect, and block unwanted bots no longer falls on the websites\nthemselves.`})]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(a,{id:\"section-2\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Section 2: The Scale of AI Scraping\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(c,{children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:`Over the first half of the year, median AI scraping per site was 4\\xD7 higher\non European sites compared to North American sites.`}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:`European sites saw roughly 1 AI bot scrape per 33 human visits over the\ncourse of H1 2026.`}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:`RAG bots visited an average publisher page 41 times in Q1 and 50 times in Q2\nfor the European cohort of sites. Training bots only hit a page an average of\n5 times.`}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:`North American sites are scraped most often by RAG bots (49%); European\nscraping is weighted toward training (41%).`}),`\n`]})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"2.1 Aggregate Scraping Levels\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[`In the previous section, we looked at scrapers that go out of their way to\ndisguise themselves. This section covers the AI companies that identify their\nscrapers/crawlers. Our data comes from the first-party website server logs of\npublishers on the TollBit network, which record every request made to their\nsites. Many major AI platforms send a user-agent string naming the bot and\nthe company behind it, and the data in this section counts only that\nself-identified traffic. We define a scrape in this report as a single\nrequest from one of these\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.a,{href:\"https://tollbit.com/bot-index/\",children:\"identified AI bots\"}),`, but do not count\nrequests to a site's robots.txt file. Since this data does not include any of\nthe disguised scraping described in Section 1, the AI scraping volumes\npresented in our report are likely meaningfully lower than the actual volume\nof AI visitors to publisher sites.`]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`Over the first half of 2026, website traffic from AI bots continued to grow,\nincreasing by 25% over H1. Looking at the daily volume of AI bot scrapes per\nTollBit publisher website, indexed to January 1, 2026, we see a sharp\nincrease \\u2014 on the busiest days, the per-site scraping level more than doubled\nrelative to where it started the year.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.1: Daily AI bot scrapes per website, indexed to 1/1/2026\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-2-1-daily-scrapes-indexed.svg\",alt:\"Daily AI bot scrapes per website, indexed to 1/1/2026\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"2.2 AI Scraping by Region: North America vs Europe\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`European publisher sites on TollBit have a higher median scrapes per site\nthan their North American counterparts. The median European site has 4\\xD7 the\nAI scrapes of the median North American site. Scraping places an\ninfrastructure cost burden on sites. Every one of these AI bots consumes\nbandwidth and server resources, whether or not it ever returns a human\nvisitor.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`For the regional cohorts throughout this section, we look at all sites that\nwere fully onboarded to TollBit before Q1 2026, grouped by region.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.2.1: Median AI scrapes per site, by region | H1 2026\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-2-2-1-median-scrapes-by-region.svg\",alt:\"Median AI scrapes per site, by region\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Median AI Scrapes for Same-Size Sites: Europe vs North America\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`We grouped sites into bands by average monthly human traffic and put the\nmedian European site in each band up against its North American counterpart.\nEuropean websites are scraped roughly 2x to 4x more by AI bots than\ncomparably sized North American websites. And the multiple is biggest for\nsmaller websites. The ratio shrinks as sites get larger, but even the biggest\nEuropean sites are hit more than twice as hard as their North American peers.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`Site-for-site, against North American sites with the same readership, the\nmedian European site gets out-scraped compared to its North American peer by\naverage monthly human-traffic band:`}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"1M\\u201310 million monthly human visitors: 3.7\\xD7\"}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"10M\\u201350 million monthly human visitors: 4.1\\xD7\"}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"50M\\u2013100 million monthly human visitors: 2.6\\xD7\"}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"\u003e100 million monthly human visitors: 2.3\\xD7\"}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.2.2: Median AI scrapes per site, for similar-size sites by region | H1 2026\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-2-2-2-median-scrapes-same-size-sites.svg\",alt:\"Median AI scrapes per site, for similar-size sites by region\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(o,{title:\"AI Bot Categories\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"We separate AI bots into three primary categories:\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Retrieval augmented generation (RAG) agent\"}),` \\u2014 These bots often retrieve\ninformation in real-time to respond to user prompts. They use an index of\nthe web, gathered by an indexing crawler (either proprietary or third-party,\nsuch as Bing or Google), to locate the relevant content, which is then\nretrieved and synthesized into a response. Unlike a training crawler, which\ncollects content once, a RAG agent needs to fetch content again each time it\nanswers a query, so the same pages are scraped repeatedly as users keep\nasking about them.`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Training data crawling agent\"}),` \\u2014 Large language models (LLMs) are trained\non vast quantities of data. Training data crawlers move around the web \\u2014\nfollowing links from website to website or working through sitemaps \\u2014\ndownloading content, which is then processed and stored for offline use.\nTraining is the process of teaching an AI system to recognize patterns and\nmake decisions by feeding it massive amounts of data. Training does not allow\nAI systems to reliably recall a specific source or anything published since\ntraining concluded, which is why RAG agents fetch live content at query time\ninstead to ensure more accurate responses.`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"AI search indexing agent\"}),` \\u2014 AI systems with access to the real-time web\nneed an index of the internet, used to direct RAG agents to the right\nsources when collecting data for responses to prompts. AI search indexing\ncrawlers build these indexes by systematically navigating the web, collecting\nand organizing content and metadata.`]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`Additionally, some AI developers use a single bot (or at least software that\nidentifies itself with the same user agent metadata) for more than one of\nthese purposes. We consider these \"hybrid agents\". For example, Googlebot and\nBingbot were built to index the web for search, but the same bots now also\nfeed AI products such as Google's AI Overviews and Microsoft's Copilot.`})]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`The two regions are primarily scraped for different reasons: North America is\nRAG-led (49%), while Europe skews to training (41%). As we discuss in our\nreferral section, training, unlike RAG, returns no human visitors.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`For training to still come out ahead of RAG traffic in Europe may suggest\nthat the AI ecosystem is earlier than in North America, and we expect to\neventually see the flip where RAG scraping exceeds training, as we observed\nin North America. On the other hand, Europe's large, multi-language body of\ncontent is well-suited to building a diverse training corpus to support\nvarious languages, though seeing this shift towards RAG may take further\ntime.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`Also, AI search indexers, the infrastructure behind AI search products, make\nup a similar portion of AI traffic in each respective region. The prominence\nof search indexers is consistent with our Q3 \u0026 Q4 2025 findings that\nindexers were the fastest-growing category globally.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.2.3: AI scrape composition by bot category and region | H1 2026\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-2-2-3-scrape-composition-by-category-region.svg\",alt:\"AI scrape composition by bot category and region\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`The median European site sees 1 AI bot visit for every 33 human visits,\nversus 1 AI bot for every 93 human visits for sites in North America. The\nlower the ratio, the worse for publishers: European publishers are facing a\nhigher load of AI bots for every human visitor to a tune of ~2.8\\xD7 that of\nNorth American sites.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.2.4: Median AI-to-human ratio by region | H1 2026\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-2-2-4-ai-human-ratio-by-region.svg\",alt:\"Median AI-to-human ratio by region\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"2.3 The Europe Cohort, Quarter over Quarter\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`The average AI bot scrapes per European site grew by nearly 20% from January\nto June of 2026.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.3.1: Daily average AI bot scrapes per site, European cohort | H1 2026\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-2-3-1-daily-avg-scrapes-europe.svg\",alt:\"Daily average AI bot scrapes per site, European cohort\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`RAG bots keep coming back to the same page. In Q1, RAG bots hit each unique\nEuropean page about 41 times, against 5 for training crawlers and 7 for\nsearch indexers. By Q2, the RAG figure rose to 50, while training and\nindexing stayed flat. This is expected, based on what RAG bots are intended\nto do: retrieve up-to-date information. They re-fetch a page each time a user\nquery needs it, and a single article ends up pulled dozens of times to\nanswer dozens of prompts.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.3.2: AI scrapes per unique page, European cohort | Q1 2026 vs Q2 2026\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-2-3-2-scrapes-per-page-europe-q1-q2.svg\",alt:\"AI scrapes per unique page, European cohort\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`Scraping demand is uneven across content categories. Across sites on TollBit,\nnational news is by far the heaviest scraped content category, followed by\nsports and entertainment. The two fastest-growing categories: Local news\n+103%, Sports +21%.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.3.3: AI scrapes per site by content category, European cohort | Q1 2026 vs Q2 2026\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-2-3-3-scrapes-by-content-category-europe.svg\",alt:\"AI scrapes per site by content category, European cohort\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"2.4 Scraping by AI Company \u0026 by AI Bot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`OpenAI scrapes European publisher sites far more heavily than anyone else.\nOver H1 OpenAI averaged about 4.5M scrapes per site across our cohort,\nroughly 3.5x the next platform. Meta, Amazon, and Anthropic sit well behind,\nand all close to the same scale of scraping, each at about a quarter of\nOpenAI's rate. Amazon was the fastest riser of the three, with per-site\nscraping roughly doubling from Q1 to Q2.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.4: Average scrapes per site by AI platform, European cohort | H1 2026\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-2-4-scrapes-by-platform-europe.svg\",alt:\"Average scrapes per site by AI platform, European cohort\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`Broken out by individual bot instead of by company, one bot stands out.\nChatGPT-User, OpenAI's real-time retrieval agent, averaged about 3.16M\nscrapes per site in H1. That's more than three times the next bot on the\nlist. Following ChatGPT-User we see a few bots that averaged between 500,000\nand 1 million scrapes. Amazonbot and ClaudeBot came in almost even at roughly\n960,000 each, with meta-webindexer, GPTBot, and OAI-SearchBot close behind\nnear 660,000.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.4.1: Average scrapes per site by AI bot, European cohort | H1 2026\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-2-4-1-scrapes-by-bot-europe.svg\",alt:\"Average scrapes per site by AI bot, European cohort\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.4.2: Training crawler share by bot, European cohort | H1 2026\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-2-4-2-training-data-crawler-share-by-bot.svg\",alt:\"Training crawler share by bot, European cohort\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"2.5 IP Address Investigation\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`A user-agent string is the label a bot attaches to its requests to identify\nitself. Any bot can set the user agent string to whatever it likes. Changing\nit to pose as a different browser, device, or platform is known as\nuser-agent spoofing. Because it can be faked, a user-agent string isn't\nreliable on its own for identifying bots. Therefore, we also analyzed the IP\naddresses associated with the AI bot requests.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`To do this, we looked at how often bots claiming to be a major AI platform\nactually came from that platform's own, publicly available IP addresses.\nAcross OpenAI, Anthropic, Amazon, and Perplexity user agents, less than 2% of\neach platform's AI scrapes on sites appear to be spoofed by someone else\nusing the platform's name. The IP addresses for over 98% of traffic match the\nIP addresses published by the user agent's respective platform. If you are\nseeing these AI platforms accessing your site based on user agents, it is\nhighly likely that the traffic is actually coming from the relevant platform.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"By Platform\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`At the platform level, spoofing is rare. Every operator we checked came in\nunder 2%: OpenAI at 0.67%, Anthropic at 1.96%, Amazon at 0.87%, Perplexity at\n0.23%. When a request says it's GPTBot or ClaudeBot, it usually is.`}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.table,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.thead,{children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.th,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Platform\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.th,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"Percentage of scrapes from the official IP address range\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.th,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"Percentage of scrapes that are spoofed\"})]})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tbody,{children:[(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"OpenAI\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"99.33%\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"0.67%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Anthropic\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"98.04%\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"1.96%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Amazon\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"99.13%\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"0.87%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Perplexity\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"99.77%\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"0.23%\"})]})]})]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"By AI Bot\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`There are some individual bots that are spoofed at higher rates. Anthropic's\nclaude-user was spoofed 12.70% of the time and claude-searchbot 5.87%, well\nabove Anthropic's 1.96% average. Out of the 12.7% of times claude-user is\nbeing listed as spoofed, 27% of these requests are actually from an IP\naddress that appears to be registered by Anthropic, but they have not yet\nadded this IP address to their official docs at the time of this report's\npublication. Amazon also had some individual bots with higher percentages:\namzn-searchbot at 5.67% and amzn-user at 4.10%, but amazonbot was only at\n0.14%.`}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.table,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.thead,{children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.th,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Platform\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.th,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"User Agent\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.th,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"Percentage of scrapes that are spoofed\"})]})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tbody,{children:[(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"OpenAI\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"ChatGPT-User\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"0.65%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"OpenAI\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"GPTBot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"1.02%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"OpenAI\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"OAI-Searchbot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"0.29%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Anthropic\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"ClaudeBot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"1.31%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Anthropic\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Claude-SearchBot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"5.87%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Anthropic\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Claude-User\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"12.7%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Amazon\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Amazonbot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"0.14%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Amazon\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Amzn-SearchBot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"5.67%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Amazon\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Amzn-User\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"4.10%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Perplexity\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"PerplexityBot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"0.23%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Perplexity\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Perplexity-User\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"0.33%\"})]})]})]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"By Region and by Platform\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`Depending on the platform, some bots are spoofed more often in Europe, while\nothers are spoofed more often in North America. Overall, European requests\nwere spoofed at 0.91%, more than double North America's 0.36%.`}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.table,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.thead,{children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.th,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Region\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.th,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Platform\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.th,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"Percentage of scrapes from the official IP address range\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.th,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"Percentage of scrapes that are spoofed\"})]})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tbody,{children:[(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"North America\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"OpenAI\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"99.69%\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"0.31%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"North America\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Anthropic\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"99.09%\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"0.91%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"North America\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Amazon\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"99.91%\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"0.09%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"North America\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Perplexity\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"99.8%\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"0.2%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Europe\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"OpenAI\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"99.1%\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"0.9%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Europe\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Anthropic\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"98.31%\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"1.69%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Europe\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Amazon\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"99.94%\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"0.06%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Europe\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Perplexity\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"99.9%\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"0.1%\"})]})]})]}),(0,e.jsx)(o,{title:\"TollBit Analytics\",children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[`If you're curious what AI bot traffic looks like on your own site and how it\nis evolving over time, you can sign up for TollBit Analytics at\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.a,{href:\"https://tollbit.com/analytics\",children:\"tollbit.com/analytics\"}),`. It's free for\npublishers and typically takes an hour or less to set up and will show you\nwho is scraping you, how often, whether or not they send you referrals, and\nmore.`]})})]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(a,{id:\"section-3\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Section 3: Referral Traffic\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(c,{children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:`Referrals from AI applications are a negligible source of traffic for\nEuropean publishers. In the first half of 2026, just 0.05% of external\nreferrals to European sites came from an AI app, versus 0.16% in North\nAmerica. - Google still sends the overwhelming majority of human visitors to\nsites, around 84% of external referrals for European sites. Google returns\nroughly 1,681 human visits for every one an AI application returns. - European\nsites receive fewer human visitors back for the RAG scrapes on their sites\ncompared to North American sites. It takes 179 AI bot visits to get a single\nhuman visitor referral in return from AI applications for European publishers\n(versus 53 AI bot visits for 1 human visitor in return for sites in North\nAmerica). - Within our European cohort, this scrape-to-referral exchange\nworsened over H1, from 150:1 in Q1 2026 to 227:1 in Q2 2026.`}),`\n`]})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"3.1 Referral Composition\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`Publisher revenue largely depends on human visitors to sites for advertiser\nand subscription revenue. Our reports have shown that AI bot traffic sends\nback far fewer visitors compared to traditional Google search, threatening\npublishers' traditional methods of revenue. Newer advanced agents like Claude\nCode (from Anthropic) and Codex (from OpenAI) do not even include citation\nlinks in their outputs, depriving publishers of any opportunity to receive a\nhuman click and visitor back to their sites.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`AI applications provide abysmal amounts of referrals back to European sites.\nThey accounted for 0.05% of external referrals to European sites in H1 2026,\ncompared with 0.16% in North America. To put that into perspective, Google\nsends a European publisher roughly 1,681 human visitors for every one an AI\napplication sends back. North America's AI referral share is higher, about 3x\nEurope's, but the number is too small in either region to count as\nmeaningful.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`Looking across TollBit's European sites in the first half of 2026, the\nexternal referral mix is still overwhelmingly driven by Google despite the\nsurge in AI bot activity. Google accounts for around 84% of external\nreferrals to European sites, with 80% in North America.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.1: Referral share by source and region | H1 2026\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-3-1-referral-share-by-source-region.svg\",alt:\"Referral share by source and region\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`For European publishers, the more relevant non-Google referral channel is\nsocial. Outside Google, European referrals are led by Facebook at 7.13% of\nall referrals, followed by Bing (1.79%) and Instagram (0.68%).`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"3.2 Scrape-to-Referral Ratio\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`Referral share tells us how little traffic AI sends out of all traffic. The\nscrape-to-referral ratio measures what a publisher gives up versus what it\ngets back: how much of its content is scraped for each visitor an AI\nplatform actually sends. In this report, we only look at the number of\nsuccessful scrapes by a RAG agent (not all AI agents) for this ratio. The\nreason for this is that training and search indexing crawlers do not present\na potential opportunity for a human visitor to see a link and click through\nto a publisher's site.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`European publishers fare poorly with this metric: It takes around 179\nsuccessful RAG scrapes to return one human visitor to a European site,\ncompared to 53 in North America. The exchange for European content is less\nfavorable than for North American sites. Compared to North America, European\ncontent is being taken more heavily, relative to the human visitors' AI\nreturns.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`One potential reason for a higher, worse-off ratio is that users are getting\nwhat they need from the AI summary and not clicking through when the source\nwas cited. When this occurs, value is extracted from publishers' content\nwithout compensating them and without sending a human visitor back.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.2: AI scrape-to-referral ratio by region | H1 2026\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-3-2-scrape-referral-ratio-by-region.svg\",alt:\"AI scrape-to-referral ratio by region\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`One caveat worth calling out is that because Googlebot and Bingbot (MSFT) are\nhybrid agents, crawling for both search and AI products under one identity,\nwe cannot separate their traditional search-related scrapes from their\nAI-driven ones. Therefore, we cannot look at Gemini (Google) or Copilot\n(MSFT) when calculating the scrape-to-referral ratio. We only use the scrapes\nfrom bots that are explicitly defined as RAG bots to see how many scrapes it\ntakes to get a referral \\u2014 aka a human visitor in return.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"3.3 European Cohort: Referral Composition and Scrape-to-Referral Ratio\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`To see how referrals are changing over time, we looked at the same cohort of\nEuropean publishers over H1 and found that the composition of referrals\nshifted modestly over the quarter. Google's share held roughly flat at\n~84.3%, and Facebook's fell by 17.6%, whilst AI applications fell by 40% from\n0.063% to 0.038%. That means roughly 1 in every 2,634 external referrals now\ncomes from an AI app for this cohort of publishers.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.3: Referral share by source, European Cohort | Q1 2026 vs Q2 2026\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-3-3-referral-share-europe-cohort-q1-q2.svg\",alt:\"Referral share by source, European Cohort\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`The worsening scrape-to-referral ratio within the European cohort similarly\nechoes our prior findings. The European cohort's scrape-to-referral ratio\nrose from 150:1 in Q1 to 227:1, meaning it now on average takes 51% more RAG\nscrapes to return a single human visitor.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.4: Scrape-to-referral ratio, European cohort | Q1 2026 vs Q2 2026\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-3-4-scrape-referral-ratio-europe-cohort-q1-q2.svg\",alt:\"Scrape-to-referral ratio, European cohort\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`The scrape-to-referral ratio varies widely by AI company. Meta appears to be\nby far the least efficient referrer as of the end of H1, with over 1,500 RAG\nscrapes to send back a single human visitor. OpenAI, the cohort's largest RAG\nscraper by far, returns one human visitor for every 265 scrapes, worsening\nfrom its Q1 ratio of 165 scrapes for 1 human visitor.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`There are two important limitations of the scrape-to-referral ratio data.\nWhile this ratio approximates what referrals publishers are receiving in\nreturn for the use of their content, it's not perfect:`}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ol,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[`AI companies are not presently required to self-identify their user\nagents, though that may be starting to change.\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.a,{href:\"https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/bills/2025/S9934/amendment/A\",children:\"New York's Stealth Crawler Prohibition Act\"}),`,\npassed by the state legislature in June 2026 and awaiting signature, would\nrequire exactly this kind of disclosure. But until legislation is passed,\nit's always possible these companies are utilizing unidentified user\nagents, which could mean that AI companies are actually scraping sites\nmore often than we can measure. This goes for all scrape data in the\nreport, but the impacts are particularly visible with this metric. It's\nworth noting that in our Q4 2024 State of the Bots report we found data\npotentially supporting the notion that Perplexity does not always\nself-identify its user agent and may be using other user agents to\nscrape. This is likely why the Perplexity referral ratio below appears\nmore favorable than it likely is.`]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:`Another limitation is that AI companies may cache (store) and reuse\ncontent for periods of time. Section 6 of our Q2 2025 State of the Bots\nreport documents this. This is why scrapes aren't equivalent to the\nactual usage of content, but it's the best approximation we have to it.\nAnthropic (Claude) cached content longer than other AI applications,\nwhich could be why its scrape-to-referral ratio appears more favorable\nthan others'. This means Anthropic may be storing content more, versus\ngoing out and fetching/scraping content as frequently as other AI\ncompanies. However, that doesn't necessarily mean it isn't using the\ncontent it's fetched less.`}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.5: Scrape-to-referral ratio by AI platform, Europe cohort | Q1 vs Q2 2026\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-3-5-scrape-referral-ratio-by-platform-europe.svg\",alt:\"Scrape-to-referral ratio by AI platform, Europe cohort\"})]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(a,{id:\"section-4\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Section 4: Robots.txt and the Bypassing Problem\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(c,{children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:`Robots.txt is not protecting European publishers: ~15% of AI scrapes on\nEuropean sites in H1 2026 ignored an active disallow instruction. - The median\namount of bypassed scrapes for a European site is 2.8x that of a North\nAmerican site. - For the major training crawlers, European sites disallow more\nthan North American sites \\u2014 CCBot 63% vs 46%, GPTBot 60% vs 41%, ClaudeBot 53%\nvs 43%. - ChatGPT-User, Bytespider, and Youbot bypass robots.txt instructions\non nearly 50% of all European sites that have explicitly disallowed them.`}),`\n`]})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"4.1 Disallow Adoption\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(o,{title:\"Robots.txt \u0026 Bypass Definitions\",children:[(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Robots Exclusion Protocol (robots.txt)\"}),` \\u2014 a voluntary handshake agreement,\nin place since the early days of the Internet, by which a website uses a\nrobots.txt file to signal which automated user agents are permitted to\naccess it. It has no enforcement mechanism and depends entirely on the\ngoodwill of those operating the bots.`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Disallow\"}),` \\u2014 an instruction in a publisher's robots.txt file naming a\nspecific user agent that the publisher does not permit to access its\ncontent. AI developers note it may take 24-72 hours for disallow to be\nlogged and reflected by their bots.`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Bypass\"}),` \\u2014 when an AI bot scrapes a publisher's content despite that\npublisher having explicitly disallowed that bot in its robots.txt file.`]})]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`Disallowing a bot in robots.txt only matters if the bot obeys the guidance,\nbut a measurable share of AI bots do not. Across European and North American\nsites in the first half of 2026, ~15% of AI scrapes bypassed a disallow\ninstruction.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`European publishers have disallowed the following bots on robots.txt at a\nhigher rate than North American sites. 63% of European sites disallow CCBot,\nagainst 46% in North America. The same holds for GPTBot (60% vs 41%),\nClaudeBot (53% vs 43%), PerplexityBot (50% vs 40%), and OAI-SearchBot (46% vs\n34%).`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`For the newer real-time user agents, the ones that fetch pages live to\nanswer a prompt, European disallow adoption drops off and North America\nmoves ahead, often by more than double. Only 9% of European sites disallow\nClaude-User, compared to 26% in North America. Perplexity-User is 13% versus\n26%. Most of the newest agents sit in the single digits in Europe.\nChatGPT-User is the exception among the AI user agents, and is blocked at a\nrate closer to the established crawlers, most likely because it is the most\nwidely recognized.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 4.1: Disallow adoption by AI bot and region | Q2 2026\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-4-1-disallow-adoption-by-bot-region.svg\",alt:\"Disallow adoption by AI bot and region\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"4.2 The Bypass Problem\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`For the median (typical) European site, bypassed scrapes are 2.8x times a\ntypical North American site. These bypasses represent the website being\naccessed against the publisher's stated wishes, consuming server resources\nin the process, and, for AI bots, rarely returning a human visitor in\nexchange.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`Given that European publishers are scraped at a 4x higher rate by AI bots\nthan North American sites, it's not too surprising that their median\nbypassed scrapes would appear higher.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 4.2: Median bypassed scrapes per site by region | H1 2026\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-4-2-median-bypassed-scrapes-by-region.svg\",alt:\"Median bypassed scrapes per site by region\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"4.3 Bypass by AI Bot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`This brings us to another metric: the bypass rate, which is the percentage of\nall scrapes that ignored explicit robots.txt guidelines and successfully\nscraped content anyway.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`ChatGPT-User had the highest bypass rate, ignoring explicit robots.txt\ninstructions on 54% of all its scrapes. It is followed by Bytespider with a\nbypass rate of 48% and PerplexityBot with a bypass rate of 42%.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 4.3: Share of sites where disallowed bots bypassed robots.txt, all European sites | H1 2026\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-4-3-bypass-share-by-bot-europe.svg\",alt:\"Share of sites where disallowed bots bypassed robots.txt\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`For the AI bots that have a smaller bypass rate, there often appears to be an\ninteresting trend of the bypass rate increasing between Q1 \u0026 Q2. For example,\nthe data shows GPTBot, CCBot, and DuckAssistBot all bypassing more often than\nin Q1, an early sign that bypassing behaviors may be spreading beyond the\nhandful of agents that dominate it today.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 4.3.1: Bypass rate by AI bot, Europe Cohort | Q1 vs Q2 2026\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026/fig-4-3-1-bypass-rate-by-bot-europe-q1-q2.svg\",alt:\"Bypass rate by AI bot, Europe Cohort\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`Robots.txt files were designed for an era when honoring it cost a crawler\nlittle, and defying it gained it little and cost the site little. As AI\ntools and traffic explode, that balance no longer holds. AI bots take\npublisher content, cost the sites yet serve them, yet send back very few\nhuman visitors in return.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`Because the robots.txt protocol is not active enforcement and rests on the\ngoodwill of the operator, European publishers who rely on it alone today are\nprotected only if AI developers choose to cooperate, and for the bots doing\nthe most scraping, abidance of robots.txt remains thin.`})]})]})}function m(n={}){let{wrapper:t}=n.components||{};return t?(0,e.jsx)(t,{...n,children:(0,e.jsx)(f,{...n})}):f(n)}function l(n,t){throw new Error(\"Expected \"+(t?\"component\":\"object\")+\" `\"+n+\"` to be defined: you likely forgot to import, pass, or provide it.\")}return T(E);})();\n;return Component;"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"40:Tc9f5,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\n\u003cSection id=\"executive-summary\"\u003e\n\n## **Executive Summary**\n\n\u003cExecSummary\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={1}\u003e\n  **AI developers and many large companies building or using AI apps are paying\n  third-party web scrapers for access to the internet instead of paying\n  publishers directly for their content.** These third parties take the content,\n  often reformatting it for AI consumption, and sell it. The recent Reddit and\n  Google lawsuits against scrapers surface the tip of the iceberg of this\n  rapidly growing ecosystem. We've documented nearly 40 of these web scraping\n  actors and built a tool for publishers and website owners to test whether\n  their sites are being scraped. Many of these scrapers list Fortune 1000\n  customers \u0026 AI companies, and explicitly advertise cybersecurity evasion and\n  human-mimicking tooling on their sites.\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={2}\u003e\n  **Even paywalled content is not necessarily safe from these scrapers.** In our\n  testing, we found that some scrapers were able to retrieve full versions of\n  various paywalled articles. Investing in cybersecurity is important for sites,\n  but it alone is not a complete solution. Companies as tech-advanced and\n  well-financed as Google and Reddit cannot stop the scrapers with\n  cybersecurity, and have turned to litigation, which Google stated was a \"last\n  resort.\"\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={3}\u003e\n  **AI traffic on sites is continuing to grow.** In Q1, on average, there was 1\n  AI bot visit for every 200 human visits to a site. By EOY, that ratio\n  increased to 1 AI bot visit for every 31 human visits.\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={4}\u003e\n  **In Q4, the average scrapes per page for a RAG bot was ~10× more than for a\n  Training bot.** For every 1 page request a training bot makes to a single\n  page, a RAG bot makes, on average, 10. Some of the most frequently scraped\n  URLs on TollBit in Q3 fell into the categories of \"Kimmel / Kirk Controversy\"\n  and \"The Summer I Turned Pretty\". In Q4, this shifted to categories like\n  \"Stranger Things Season 5\" and \"Holiday Gift Guides \u0026 Black Friday Deals.\"\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={5}\u003e\n  **Looking at the top URLs that resulted in referrals for websites from AI\n  companies,** we can see that a disproportionate number of referrals came from\n  ChatGPT for technical topics such as \"Software/developer tools downloads\" and\n  \"B2B Software Reviews\". Meanwhile, for Perplexity, shopping-related categories\n  were a key source of referrals. Referrals are likely higher in these\n  categories because users are more likely to click through to complete actions.\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={6}\u003e\n  **It appears that click-through rates from AI applications, already abysmal,\n  are declining:** Websites without a direct 1:1 deal with an AI company on\n  average saw their click-through rates drop by nearly 3x — from 0.8% in Q2 to\n  0.27% at EOY. Websites with 1:1 AI licensing deals saw click-through rates\n  drop by over 6.5x in 2025 — from 8.8% in Q1 down to 1.33% EOY — suggesting\n  these deals are not necessarily insulating publishers from low AI referrals.\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003c/ExecSummary\u003e\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n\n---\n\n\u003cSection id=\"section-1\"\u003e\n\n## **Section 1: The Growing Cost and Complexity of Content Access**\n\n\u003cUserStory\u003e\n  AI developers are combining multiple data acquisition processes and\n  technologies — many offered by specialist web scraping vendors — into tech\n  stacks that secure access to the Internet. Reddit has filed a lawsuit against\n  Perplexity and three of these web scraping vendors. Two of these vendors mask\n  IP addresses, one of which scrapes Google's search engine results page. More\n  recently, Google has also filed a lawsuit against this same scraper. Fueled by\n  the AI boom, hundreds of third-party scrapers are in use, many by Fortune 1000\n  companies. Many of these scrapers employ specialized cybersecurity evasion\n  tools and do not comply with robots.txt by default. We built a tool for\n  websites to test which of these third parties can scrape their sites. In\n  testing this tool across top sites, we found that even paywalled content is\n  not necessarily safe — we observed that some scrapers retrieved full versions\n  of various paywalled articles. This arms race between the scrapers and website\n  operators is deeply inefficient for both AI developers and rights-holders. The\n  infrastructure for web scraping is unreliable and inefficient, and highlights\n  the growing need for sanctioned access.\n\u003c/UserStory\u003e\n\n### 1.1 The Data Acquisition Stack\n\nThe data acquisition stack has existed for quite some time, but its effects have become more visible recently. With AI, the need to access content \u0026 data has exploded. Funded by the AI investment boom, developers are combining multiple data acquisition processes and technologies, with more advanced or clandestine approaches deployed where cybersecurity barriers are encountered. These increasingly complicated tech stacks are intended to secure 'just enough' access to content to ensure the applications they serve can handle the range of queries needed to compete.\n\nThese stacks comprise some or all of the following:\n\n- **Direct crawling and scraping** via first-party bots, either declared or disguised.\n- **Secondary source crawling / scraping**, targeting surfaces on which the content itself may be visible on the open web (i.e., Google Search results page).\n- **Residential IP proxies,** presenting as ordinary user traffic — often with location and device characteristics/IP addresses that match the human audience.\n- **Circumvention services** that evade bot detection / IP protection measures.\n- **Cloud-based headless browsers** that load web content in a browser running in the cloud, rather than on a user's device. These are often used with a residential IP proxy: the proxy makes the request appear to come from a normal human user.\n- **Third-party scraping services** for outsourcing the end-to-end process of scraping a page.\n\nFor obvious reasons, this environment is one in which it is exceedingly difficult for digital publishers to exercise control over the use of their IP. They are in a structurally disadvantaged position, having to respond to new threats as they emerge, whereas the web scrapers just need to evolve beyond the latest protection. Most websites deploy only one cybersecurity tool, whereas AI companies can choose from dozens of scraping tools interchangeably.\n\n### 1.2 Google \u0026 Reddit Take on the Third-Party Scrapers\n\nOn the demand side of the market, the recent Reddit and Google lawsuits shine a light on the software/service providers and intermediaries that have emerged to serve AI's need for content. On 22nd October 2025, Reddit filed a lawsuit in the Southern District of New York against AI 'answer engine' Perplexity, plus three other businesses few will have heard of: Oxylabs UAB, AWMProxy, and SerpApi. This case provides a startling window on the state of the fight between website owners, trying to protect their IP, and AI developers with a voracious appetite for data to fuel their AI applications.\n\nUnlike the other copyright cases, which target businesses using — or replicating — intellectual property acquired without authorization or a licence, Reddit's filing also takes aim at intermediaries and service providers involved in the scraping of its content.\n\nTwo of the defendants — Oxylabs UAB and AWMProxy — are providers of IP proxies, allowing scrapers to disguise themselves as human traffic and bypass bot detection tools. SerpApi, meanwhile, acquires Reddit content by crawling Google's search engine results pages (SERPs).\n\n\u003cInfoBox title=\"Filing Extract: Reddit vs Perplexity et al.\"\u003e\n  \"Reddit and Google implement technological control measures to effectively\n  control access and prevent scraping of content posted to Reddit. Reddit has\n  and maintains automated anti-scraping systems and teams dedicated to detecting\n  and protecting against unauthorized access to its products and services...\n  Google likewise has anti-scraping systems and teams dedicated to preventing\n  unauthorized access to its products and services... Google utilizes a\n  technological access control system called 'SearchGuard,' which is designed to\n  prevent automated systems from accessing and obtaining wholesale search\n  results and indexed data.\"\n\u003c/InfoBox\u003e\n\nOn December 19th, 2025, Google also filed a lawsuit against SerpApi, alleging unlawful scraping of copyrighted content that is included in Google's search results. Google's blog post about this case cites, \"SerpApi uses shady back doors — like cloaking themselves, bombarding websites with massive networks of bots and giving their crawlers fake and constantly changing names — circumventing our security measures to take websites' content wholesale. This unlawful activity has increased dramatically over the past year.\"\n\n### 1.3 The Growth and Sophistication of Web Scraping\n\nThe web scrapers in the Reddit \u0026 Google cases are the tip of the iceberg; there are many others in the market. As the Google blog highlights, scraping has skyrocketed over the past year. Many of these web scrapers are being funded by the AI boom to provide content to power AI for both training and RAG use cases.\n\nIn an analysis of nearly 40 scraping vendors, we find that many explicitly advertise cybersecurity detection evasion techniques and many do not default to abiding by robots.txt.\n\n**See our [Web Scraper Index](/scraper-index) section for more details on these web scrapers and their specific features.**\n\nThis growth in volume of scraping and sophistication increasingly creates challenges for site owners. SerpApi's success in building a business around scraping Google at scale demonstrates the challenges that even the largest and best-funded platforms have in controlling unauthorized, non-human access to their digital properties. In Google's lawsuit against SerpApi, it highlights how SerpApi circumvented Google's in-house cybersecurity tool, SearchGuard, which was \"the product of tens of thousands of person-hours and millions of dollars of investment\". If Google, with its strong technical capabilities and financial resources, is unable to prevent this activity — activity which is likely to be economically damaging — media owners face a far steeper challenge.\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Google-SerpApi-filing.png\"\n  alt=\"Screenshots from Google vs SerpApi filing\"\n/\u003e\n\n### 1.4 Scraper Audit Tool \u0026 Experiment\n\nIn order to examine the sophistication of web scrapers against websites' cybersecurity tooling, TollBit developed a tool for website owners to test 15 popular web scrapers against their own sites. The tool allows website owners to initiate scraping on any page to evaluate both defense evasion, content accessibility, and retrieval completeness. In selecting web scrapers, we picked a few industry leaders and a couple of newer scrapers offering advanced technology, despite their company size and time in market. For each of these web scrapers, whenever available, we purchased each of their top-tier enterprise subscriptions of their web scraping APIs to leverage their full suite of AI-driven parsing, advanced anti-bot bypassing, and premium proxy networks.\n\nTo demonstrate the utility of this tool in auditing scrapers, we conducted an evaluation of this tool across 30 high-authority sites, strategically divided into 15 paywalled and 15 non-paywalled domains from the US and UK as defined by the Press Gazette.\n\nOur analysis revealed:\n\n- **Even paywalled content is not necessarily safe.** In our testing of 15 of these scraping tools, we observed that some were able to scrape various paywalled articles in full.\n- **Non-paywalled sites offered no discernible increase in protection** compared to their paywalled counterparts, as scrapers successfully retrieved full content from the vast majority of target pages regardless of subscription barriers.\n- In the rare instances where all scrapers failed to fully extract data — occurring in only three of the thirty sites — the failure was primarily attributed to **server-side rendering implementations** of the paywall.\n\nTo determine some of the behavioral strategies used by these scrapers, we also tested the tool on our own test site. Based on the server logs, the following strategies were identified:\n\n- **Robots.txt Bypass:** None of the tested scrapers attempted to fetch the `robots.txt` file before accessing the target page\n- **User-Agent Spoofing:** Scrapers utilized the latest Chrome User-Agents to blend into legitimate traffic, avoiding custom identifying strings.\n- **IP Rotation:** Tools executed aggressive proxy rotation strategies; one hit the server from at least ten different IP addresses within a one-second window.\n- **Engine Diversification:** Some scrapers pivoted to a Firefox User-Agent to avoid Chromium-centric detection.\n- **Referrer Masking:** Scrapers combined modern User-Agents with spoofed Google search referrers to simulate organic site discovery and entry.\n- **Legacy Rotation:** Tools utilized older, static browser profiles to avoid the predictable behavioral signatures associated with modern headless frameworks.\n- **Referrer-Origin Simulation:** Scrapers used \"Same-Origin\" referrers to replicate internal site navigation\n\nTollBit is launching this tool to help websites test and understand their vulnerability to IP threats from major web scrapers. If you are interested in seeing how your site is being scraped by these third parties, please contact [email withheld by Lighthouse].\n\n### 1.5 The Inefficiency and Cost of Web Scraping\n\nThis arms race between the scrapers and website operators is not only inefficient for website owners, but it's also increasingly inefficient for AI developers. Our investigation has found advanced scraping services charging up to $22.50 for 1,000 pages. Given the volume of data needed to serve consumer AI applications, the data acquisition costs for the most popular chatbots and specialized agents are likely to run into the tens of millions of dollars a year. And this is before considering the legal fees needed to fight lawsuits such as the ones filed by Reddit and Google.\n\nThe current 'scrape-and-block' dynamic is a negative-sum game. AI developers spend huge sums on scraping infrastructure, while website owners are forced to spend exorbitantly on defense. There's a better way. Instead of spending huge sums on technical workarounds to bypass IP controls, AI developers can simply pay for the content they use. By relying on licensing rather than scraping, journalism is funded — not the scrapers — and the media ecosystem AI depends on is sustained.\n\nIn order for this to occur, it is important that regulators ensure that bots are not allowed to mimic human visitors on the Internet. Otherwise, website owners may be caught in an increasingly expensive and ineffective cat-and-mouse game of trying to detect web scrapers.\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n\n---\n\n\u003cSection id=\"section-2\"\u003e\n\n## **Section 2: Scale of AI Scraping**\n\n\u003cUserStory\u003e\n  From Q2 through EOY, the rate of AI scraping continued to increase across\n  sites, with an average quarterly growth rate of 24.4%. In Q2, on average,\n  there was 1 AI bot visit for every 50 human visits to a site. By Q4, that\n  ratio increased to 1 AI bot visit for every 31 human visits. RAG bots remain\n  the primary type of AI bot visitors to sites. In Q4, RAG bots on average\n  scraped sites over 4.5x more than training bots. And while RAG bot activity\n  across sites grew by an average of 33% from Q2 to Q4 2025, training activity\n  declined by 15%. In Q4, OpenAI's ChatGPT-User had an average scrape per page\n  5x higher than the second-highest AI scraper (Meta), and over 16x higher than\n  Perplexity's agent. Comparing the growth of AI bot traffic across regions,\n  Asia has the highest growth rate of AI scraping across sites, clocking in at a\n  7x higher growth rate than North America — although North American sites\n  continue to have the highest rates of scraping.\n\u003c/UserStory\u003e\n\n### 2.1 Aggregate Level of AI Scraping\n\nBy examining the level of AI bot scraping in the second half of 2025 across the cohort of sites that haven't changed their robots.txt files within this period, we can see the underlying level of demand for publisher content by AI systems.\n\nWe can see a steady increase in demand for publisher content from AI bots, with scrapes growing 29% from Q2 to Q3 and 20% from Q3 to Q4.\n\n**Figure 2.1: AI bot scrapes per site | Q2–Q4 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 2.1_ AI bot scrapes per site _ Q2–Q4 2025.svg\"\n  alt=\"AI bot scrapes per site Q2-Q4 2025\"\n/\u003e\n\n### 2.2 Changing Composition of Web Traffic\n\nIn TollBit's last _State of the Bots_, we reported on the changing composition of web traffic, predicting the rise of bot traffic, which in turn is likely to transform into 'faux human' traffic; AI bots masquerading as humans in order to bypass website owners' bot detection measures. The pattern we predicted continues to play out in the data with AI bot requests rising relative to human traffic across our partner websites.\n\nIn light of this, the data below is conservative; it is likely worse than these numbers because some of these \"humans\" are likely bots. From the tests we ran, as mentioned in section 1, many of these web scrapers are indistinguishable from human visitors on sites.\n\nIn Q2, there was 1 AI bot visit for every 50 human visits. In Q4, this shifted to 1 AI bot visit for every 31 human visits; an increase in the bot-to-human ratio of ~60% across this period. The ratio of AI visitors to Human visitors is increasing, with an average quarterly compound growth rate of 26%.\n\nOver the same period, human traffic to sites increased by 1% from Q2 to Q3 but decreased by 5% from Q3 to Q4. The average quarterly compound growth rate for human visitors is -2%.\n\n**Figure 2.2: AI bot-to-human request ratio | Q2–Q4 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 2.2_ AI bot-to-human request ratio _ Q2–Q4 2025.svg\"\n  alt=\"AI bot-to-human request ratio\"\n/\u003e\n\n### 2.3 Scraping Level by Type of AI Bot\n\nWhen we break down the aggregate level of scraping by AI bot category, we can see a stark difference in traffic patterns. RAG agent requests are rising consistently (average quarterly growth rate of 33% between Q2 and Q4 2025), driven by upstream consumer adoption of AI applications and their use in settings that require access to the live web. Meanwhile, training declined by 15% on average during the same period, though with some fluctuation.\n\n**RAG vs Training vs Search Indexer Scraping Percentage Change — Q2 vs Q4**\n\n| Bot Type       | Avg Quarterly Growth Rate |\n| :------------- | :-----------------------: |\n| RAG            |           +33%            |\n| Training       |           -15%            |\n| Search Indexer |           +59%            |\n\nActivity from AI search indexers is also increasing rapidly (average quarterly compound growth rate is 59% from Q2 to Q4). These bots crawl the web autonomously, gathering data to build a map of the internet, which is later used to direct RAG bots, when needed by a user prompt.\n\nRAG bots remain the primary type of AI bot visitors to sites. In Q4, RAG bots on average scraped sites over 4.5x more than training bots.\n\n**Figure 2.3.1: RAG vs training vs search indexer scrapes (Cohort) | Q2–Q4 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 2.3.1_ RAG vs training vs indexer scrapes (Cohort) _ Q2–Q4 2025.svg\"\n  alt=\"RAG vs training vs search indexer scrapes\"\n/\u003e\n\nIf we look at this data on the average scrapes per page level, we can start to see data that provides some evidence as to why RAG bots are scraping sites more frequently. This is because RAG bots require continued access to content; they cannot store the entirety of the web and therefore revisit pages repeatedly. In Q4, the average scrapes per page for a RAG bot was ~10× more than for Training bots. For every 1 page request a training bot makes to a single page, a RAG bot makes, on average, 10. This demonstrates that AI bots require repeated access to content to ground their responses to end users.\n\n**Figure 2.3.2: RAG vs training scrapes per page | Q3–Q4 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 2.3.2_ RAG vs training scrapes per page _ Q3–Q4 2025.svg\"\n  alt=\"RAG vs training scrapes per page\"\n/\u003e\n\n**Figure 2.3.3: Composition of AI bot scrapes — RAG vs training vs search Indexer (All sites)**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 2.3.3_ Composition of AI bot scrapes - RAG vs Training vs Search Indexer (All sites).svg\"\n  alt=\"Composition of AI bot scrapes\"\n/\u003e\n\n### 2.4 OpenAI is One of the Most Active AI Scrapers\n\nAverage AI scrapes per page are dominated by OpenAI's ChatGPT user, with an average scrape per page 5x higher than the second-highest scraper (Meta), and over 16x higher than Perplexity, in Q4 2025.\n\n**Figure 2.4: AI bot scrapes per page | Q3 vs Q4 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 2.4_ AI bot scrapes per page _ Q3 vs Q4 2025.svg\"\n  alt=\"AI bot scrapes per page Q3 vs Q4\"\n/\u003e\n\n**Figure 2.4.1: AI bot scrapes per site | Q3 vs Q4 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 2.4.1_ AI bot scrapes per site _ Q3 vs Q4 2025.svg\"\n  alt=\"AI bot scrapes per site Q3 vs Q4\"\n/\u003e\n\n### 2.5 Aggregate AI Scraping by Site Region\n\nBy categorizing websites that joined TollBit prior to Q3 by region, we see that, in absolute terms, US- and Canada-based publishers experience significantly higher scraping volumes than those in Europe or Asia. Language is likely to be one factor here, with English content subject to the highest demand from AI applications. Furthermore, the US is over-represented in the user bases of many consumer AI tools (for example, around 20% of ChatGPT traffic originates from the US), which will bias RAG requests toward local sources for certain user needs.\n\nComparing the growth of AI bot traffic across regions, we can see that Asia has the highest rate of growth in AI scraping across sites, clocking in at over 120%, followed by Europe at half that rate, and North America at a much lower rate of approximately 17%. However, North America continues to have a significantly higher average AI scraping rate across sites.\n\n**Figure 2.5: AI bot scrapes per page by region (Cohort) | Q3–Q4 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 2.5_ AI bot scrapes per page by region (Cohort) _ Q3–Q4 2025.svg\"\n  alt=\"AI bot scrapes per page by region\"\n/\u003e\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n\n---\n\n\u003cSection id=\"section-3\"\u003e\n\n## **Section 3: AI Demand for Content**\n\n\u003cUserStory\u003e\n  The most AI-scraped sites on TollBit are in the B2B/professional, National\n  News, and Lifestyle categories. Some of the most frequently scraped URLs on\n  TollBit in Q3 fell into the categories of \"Charlie Kirk\" and \"NFL Week 3 \u0026 4\".\n  In Q4, this shifted to categories like \"Stranger Things Season 5\", \"Netflix\n  Warner Bros Deal\", and \"Holiday Gift Guides \u0026 Black Friday Deals\". This\n  highlights how many of these AI apps are scraping sites to power RAG. This\n  analysis shows that each RAG agent appears to serve a different user need:\n  ChatGPT tends to be most used for general search, Perplexity for consumer\n  product research and reviews, and Claude for professional tasks — especially\n  in the tech space.\n\u003c/UserStory\u003e\n\n### 3.1 AI Content Scraping Across Topics\n\nLooking at the cohort of sites that joined TollBit before Q2 2025, the content categories that were scraped the most were B2B/professional, followed by National News and Lifestyle.\n\nAnalyzing changes in scraping levels by publisher category reveals very substantial increases across technology \u0026 consumer electronics (+107%), B2B/professional (+62%), national news (+55%), and political/advocacy (+48%). Again, this growth is likely to be driven by a corresponding increase in relevant prompts on consumer AI applications that stems from more users turning to these tools for information retrieval tasks.\n\nB2B/professional content is vastly more scraped than the next category (national news). This is likely due to generative engine optimization (GEO) efforts by companies who are trying to get their content consumed by AI platforms to recommend their product, as opposed to other verticals like news that are trying to block it.\n\n**Figure 3.1: Scrapes per site by site category (Cohort) | Q2–Q4 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 3.1_ Scrapes per site by site category (Cohort) _ Q2–Q4 2025.svg\"\n  alt=\"Scrapes per site by site category\"\n/\u003e\n\nViewed on a per-page basis, we see marked variation in AI demand across publisher categories, as well as divergences in the rate of growth and decline. This view helps separate the content-volume effect (more pages to crawl) from the underlying level of demand. In general, higher per-page scraping indicates greater demand for RAG (given that training crawlers tend to access content less repeatedly). We can see that health and wellness and lifestyle content are subject to the highest — and growing — per-page demand, suggesting stronger end-user demand in AI apps for content in these areas.\n\n**Figure 3.1.2: Scrapes per page by site category (Cohort) | Q2–Q4 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 3.1.2_ Scrapes per page by site category (Cohort) _ Q2–Q4 2025.svg\"\n  alt=\"Scrapes per page by site category\"\n/\u003e\n\n### 3.2 Top Scraped URLs by Granular Topic\n\nAcross all partner domains, TollBit analyzed the URLs most frequently scraped, which together account for approximately 10% of total AI scrapes. Granular categorization of the specific topics of the most-scraped URLs/articles provides a window into the types of prompts that require AI bots to fetch content. The majority of these topics are time-sensitive, covering major news stories, popular culture events, or product reviews.\n\nConsumer use of AI applications in search-like settings — around time-sensitive topics — is likely the source of this demand, with RAG requests being needed to gather up-to-date information from reputable sources to respond to users in real-time.\n\n**Figure 3.2.1: Most-scraped pages by topic | Q3 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 3.2.1_ Most-scraped pages by topic _ Q3 2025.svg\"\n  alt=\"Most-scraped pages by topic Q3 2025\"\n/\u003e\n\nThis data appears to support the concept that AI chatbots are being used by consumers in general, information-retrieval settings that are highly likely to be substitutional to the use of Google.\n\n**Figure 3.2.2: Most-scraped pages by topic | Q4 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 3.2.2_ Most-scraped pages by topic _ Q4 2025.svg\"\n  alt=\"Most-scraped pages by topic Q4 2025\"\n/\u003e\n\n### 3.3 Content Preferences of Different RAG Agents\n\nWe analyzed the most frequently scraped URLs for the RAG agents used by ChatGPT, Claude, and Perplexity (accounting for nearly 10% of each agent's traffic on TollBit partner sites) to assess their relative content preferences. To learn these preferences, we determined their demand for different content formats like news stories, reviews, forums, etc., and topics.\n\n**ChatGPT's RAG agent** most frequently accessed content spanning finance, viral controversies, entertainment, product reviews, and breaking news. This breadth suggests users are treating the platform as a general-purpose search tool.\n\n**Figure 3.3.1: ChatGPT-User most-scraped topics | Q3 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 3.3.1_ ChatGPT-User most-scraped topics _ Q3 2025.svg\"\n  alt=\"ChatGPT-User most-scraped topics Q3\"\n/\u003e\n\n**Figure 3.3.2: ChatGPT-User most-scraped topics | Q4 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 3.3.2_ ChatGPT-User most-scraped topics _ Q4 2025.svg\"\n  alt=\"ChatGPT-User most-scraped topics Q4\"\n/\u003e\n\n**Perplexity's RAG agent** retrieved content focused on product reviews, B2B software guides, and real-time news — from consumer product and car reviews and listicles to live NBA \u0026 NFL games and election coverage. This focus suggests users are treating the platform as a research tool for product decisions.\n\n**Figure 3.3.3: Perplexity-User most-scraped topics | Q3 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/figure 3.3.3.svg\"\n  alt=\"Perplexity-User most-scraped topics Q3\"\n/\u003e\n\n**Figure 3.3.4: Perplexity-User most-scraped topics | Q4 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 3.3.4.svg\"\n  alt=\"Perplexity-User most-scraped topics Q4\"\n/\u003e\n\n**Claude's RAG agent** retrieved content spanning B2B software reviews, tech careers, and interview prep, and AI — alongside consumer tech and news. The prominence of business and career-focused topics suggests users are leveraging the platform for professional tasks.\n\n**Figure 3.3.5: Claude-User most-scraped topics | Q3 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 3.3.5.svg\"\n  alt=\"Claude-User most-scraped topics Q3\"\n/\u003e\n\n**Figure 3.3.6: Claude-User most-scraped topics | Q4 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 3.3.6.svg\"\n  alt=\"Claude-User most-scraped topics Q4\"\n/\u003e\n\nEach RAG agent appears to serve a different user need. The data suggests interesting segmentation: ChatGPT tends to be most used for general search, Perplexity for consumer product research and reviews, and Claude for professional tasks — especially in the tech space.\n\n### 3.4 AI Application Referrals to Different Topics\n\nTo evaluate queries about which topics satisfied by a generative summary and those requiring high-intent browsing, we analyzed the top referred-to URLs for each AI agent (which account for approximately 15% each of their total referral traffic on TollBit partner sites). Benchmarking these referrals against scraping frequency reveals how different topics witnessed varied clickthrough rates.\n\nAcross both quarters, a disproportionate volume of referrals for ChatGPT and Claude originated from technical and transactional domains, specifically \"Software/Developer Tools Downloads\" and \"B2B Professional\" topics. Topics like Stranger Things Season 5, Movies, TV, Streaming drove few referrals.\n\nThroughout Q3 and Q4, Perplexity drove the most referral traffic to the shopping vertical (a category that also ranks among the most frequently scraped). However, it is interesting to note that Perplexity's RAG agent scraped many car review pages in Q4 but there are nearly no referrals to car review pages.\n\nInterestingly, across all AI RAG bots, news topics tend to make up at least 15% of referrals. However, it's important to note that AI applications this still remains a very small quantity of overall referrals (approximately 0.1%).\n\n**Figure 3.4.1: ChatGPT top referral topics | Q3 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 3.4.1.svg\"\n  alt=\"ChatGPT top referral topics Q3\"\n/\u003e\n\n**Figure 3.4.2: ChatGPT top referral topics | Q4 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 3.4.2.svg\"\n  alt=\"ChatGPT top referral topics Q4\"\n/\u003e\n\n**Figure 3.4.3: Perplexity top referral topics | Q3 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 3.4.3.svg\"\n  alt=\"Perplexity top referral topics Q3\"\n/\u003e\n\n**Figure 3.4.4: Perplexity top referral topics | Q4 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 3.4.4.svg\"\n  alt=\"Perplexity top referral topics Q4\"\n/\u003e\n\n**Figure 3.4.5: Claude top referral topics | Q3 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 3.4.5.svg\"\n  alt=\"Claude top referral topics Q3\"\n/\u003e\n\n**Figure 3.4.6: Claude top referral topics | Q4 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 3.4.6.svg\"\n  alt=\"Claude top referral topics Q4\"\n/\u003e\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n\n---\n\n\u003cSection id=\"section-4\"\u003e\n\n## **Section 4: Referral Traffic**\n\n\u003cUserStory\u003e\n  AI apps continue to be an extremely small source of referrals for sites,\n  averaging 0.12%. It appears click-through rates from AI applications are\n  declining, dropping by nearly 3x — from 0.8% in Q2 to 0.27% in Q4 2025.\n  Websites with 1:1 AI licensing deals saw click-through rates drop by over 6.5x\n  in 2025 — from 8.8% in Q1 down to 1.33% in Q4 — suggesting that deals are not\n  necessarily insulating publishers from low AI referrals. Scrape-to-referral\n  ratios for AI RAG agents indicate that human visitors click through more often\n  to sites in content categories such as National News, Local News, and\n  Tech/Consumer Electronics. Content in categories like Business, Entertainment,\n  and Sports, on average, was scraped far more often before getting back a human\n  visitor.\n\u003c/UserStory\u003e\n\n### 4.1 Aggregate Referrals from AI Applications\n\nThe aggregated view on external traffic sources for Tollbit partner publishers continues to illustrate the dominant role of Google. It delivers over 80% of referred human visitor traffic, whilst AI applications are the source of just 0.1%. This means that, despite renewed competition in search and the growth of zero-click searches, on average Google delivers over 678 human visitors to a site for each visitor from an AI application.\n\n**Figure 4.1: Referral share by source | Q3 \u0026 Q4 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 4.1.svg\"\n  alt=\"Referral share by source\"\n/\u003e\n\n### 4.2 AI Applications Continue to Be a Small Portion of Referrals\n\nLooking at a cohort of sites onboarded prior to Q2, AI application referrals remain a very small share of these sites' referrals, averaging 0.12%, while approximately 80.5% of referrals come from Google.\n\n**Figure 4.2: Referral share by source (Cohort) | Q2–Q4 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 4.2.svg\"\n  alt=\"Referral share by source cohort\"\n/\u003e\n\n### 4.3 AI Application Click-through Rates\n\nAverage click-through rate (CTR) from AI applications (the proportion of users that visit a publisher page given the use of that content in a prompt response) for a cohort of sites that onboarded prior to Q2 has decreased from 0.8% in Q2 to 0.27% in Q4.\n\nIn aggregate, the CTR for AI applications remains over 13x lower than that delivered by pre-AI Google search engine results pages (SERPs).\n\n**Figure 4.3: AI referral CTR (Cohort) vs Google benchmark | Q2–Q4 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 4.3.svg\"\n  alt=\"AI referral CTR vs Google benchmark\"\n/\u003e\n\nThe presence of a 1:1 deal is shown to increase prominence on and traffic from the respective AI company. Although notably the CTR uplift provided by a deal appears to be diminishing, dropping from over 15x more referrals versus those without a deal in Q1 2025 to 7x in Q4.\n\n**Figure 4.3.1: AI referral CTR by deal status (Cohort) | Q1–Q4 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 4.3.1.svg\"\n  alt=\"AI referral CTR by deal status\"\n/\u003e\n\n### 4.4 Scrape-to-Referral Ratio by Content Category\n\nThe scrape-to-referral ratio is the number of scrapes by an AI RAG agent it takes to get one human site visitor back. For example, for the sports category, on average, it takes 218 scrapes by an AI RAG agent for one human visitor to click through. A high ratio (as seen in Deals \u0026 shopping, Business \u0026 Finance, Health \u0026 wellness, Lifestyle) implies the AI output is treated as a substitute, and users are satisfied with the summary and thereby not clicking through to visit the site. Conversely, a low ratio (which we observe in local news and national news) suggests the output requires verification or users need to visit the original source to continue their journey.\n\n**Figure 4.4: Scrape-to-Referral ratio by topic | Q3 \u0026 Q4 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 4.4.svg\"\n  alt=\"Scrape-to-Referral ratio by topic\"\n/\u003e\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n\n---\n\n\u003cSection id=\"section-5\"\u003e\n\n## **Section 5: Robots.txt \u0026 Blocking**\n\n\u003cUserStory\u003e\n  30% of total AI bot scrapes in Q4 2025 were scrapes that did not abide by\n  explicit robots.txt permissions. In Q4 2025, Deals \u0026 Shopping sites saw 4x\n  more non-permitted scrapes than permitted scrapes. Entertainment sites saw\n  2.5x more, and Local News sites saw roughly equal amounts of each. This data\n  shows that robots.txt remains insufficient and AI bots continue to bypass\n  robots.txt at scale. 42% of OpenAI's ChatGPT-User scrapes accessed content\n  from sites that blocked it on robots.txt — the highest non-compliance rate of\n  any major AI bot in Q4 2025. The number of AI bot visits hitting the TollBit\n  Bot Paywall increased by 999% in 2025 to 1 billion, showcasing that publishers\n  are continuing to attempt to increase their defenses against scrapers and\n  redirect them to a mechanism to pay.\n\u003c/UserStory\u003e\n\n### 5.1 Robots.txt Compliance\n\nCertain AI bots demonstrate a greater propensity to ignore robots.txt signals than others. In some instances, this will be driven by the number of disallow requests encountered (i.e., if two bots are programmed to behave the same but one is widely disallowed, then, of course, it will bypass more robots.txt signals). Notably, OpenAI's RAG agent — 'ChatGPT-User' — is found to routinely bypass publisher signals, despite its developer notes suggesting otherwise. As highlighted in our past reports, it's plausible that other bots are using third-party scrapers with alternative user agents or are using headless browsers that appear as humans so that their scraping goes less noticed.\n\n**Figure 5.1: Robots.txt bypass rate by bot | Q2–Q4 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 5.1.svg\"\n  alt=\"Robots.txt bypass rate by bot\"\n/\u003e\n\n### 5.2 Robots.txt Compliance by Category\n\nAI bots continue to ignore publisher signals and access content in contravention of explicit disallow requests as expressed via the Robots Exclusion Protocol (i.e. 'robots.txt'). Looking at the total volume of bypassing scrapes by publisher segment, we can see that entertainment \u0026 pop culture is a notable outlier, receiving over double the volume versus the next largest category. This is a function of the level of demand for this content; the extent of disallow requests and the behaviour of the user agents.\n\n**Figure 5.2: Robots.txt bypass scrapes by site category | Q4 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 5.2.svg\"\n  alt=\"Robots.txt bypass scrapes by site category\"\n/\u003e\n\nIn Q4 2025, Deals and Shopping sites saw over 400 bypassing scrapes per 100 permitted requests. Similarly, Entertainment \u0026 Pop Culture sites received almost 250 bypassing scrapes per 100 permitted requests. This data is a stark illustration of the inadequacy of the Robots Exclusion Protocol as a means of rights reservation. Because it carries no enforcement mechanism and relies entirely on the goodwill of those operating web scraping bots, rights holders urgently need an alternative means of protecting their IP online.\n\n**Figure 5.2.1: Bypass scrapes per 100 permitted by topic | Q3–Q4 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 5.2.1.svg\"\n  alt=\"Bypass scrapes per 100 permitted by topic\"\n/\u003e\n\n### 5.3 Redirects to Bot Paywall\n\nThe TollBit platform works with websites' existing tech stacks to redirect bots that do not have permission to access a given site to a bot paywall. The licensing price is set by the publisher. Hits to these paywalls have been growing steeply, reaching one billion in Q4 2025. From Q1 to Q4, the number of AI bot visits hitting the TollBit Bot Paywall grew at an average quarterly growth rate of 122.32%.\n\n**Figure 5.3: Hits to the TollBit paywall by quarter | 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 5.3.svg\"\n  alt=\"Hits to the TollBit paywall by quarter\"\n/\u003e\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n\n---\n\n\u003cSection id=\"appendix\"\u003e\n\n## **Appendix: AI User Agent Profiles**\n\nAs our State of the Bots reports chart, the internet is crawled by an ever-growing number of AI user agents. These have different functions for the applications they serve and consequently, behave differently online. Here is a quick overview of the principal forms of AI user agent, what they do, how they work and why they matter to publishers.\n\n### Categories of AI User Agent\n\n**1. Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) Agent**\n\nThese bots retrieve information in real-time to respond to user prompts. They use an index of the web, gathered by an indexing crawler (either proprietary or third-party such as Bing or Google) to locate the relevant content which is then retrieved and synthesized into a response.\n\n_Publisher effects:_ When a RAG agent accesses a site, it's using that content to provide a response to a user's prompt, typically in an AI chatbot (e.g. ChatGPT) or AI search (e.g. Perplexity) application. This might be substitutional to a human visitor.\n\n**2. Training Data Crawling Agent**\n\nLarge language models — such as Llama from Meta or GPT-5 from OpenAI, both of which power a multitude of consumer applications — are trained on vast quantities of data. Training data crawlers move around the web — following links from websites to websites or working through sitemaps — downloading content which is then processed and stored for offline use.\n\n_Publisher effects:_ The inclusion of content in the training data means that a model has this 'knowledge' to answer prompts. Training data typically cuts off several months prior to model release so the substitutional risk is limited for content with a longer shelf-life.\n\n**3. AI Search Indexing Agent**\n\nAI systems with access to the real-time web need an index of the internet. This is used to direct RAG agents to the right sources when collecting data needed for responses to prompts. AI search indexing crawlers build these indexes by systematically navigating the web, collecting and organizing content and metadata.\n\n_Publisher effects:_ These crawlers only ensure that a website's content can be navigated to by a RAG agent, should a relevant prompt require that.\n\n**4. Hybrid Agent**\n\nSome AI developers use a single bot (or at least software which identifies itself with the same user agent metadata) for more than one of these purposes. For example, PerplexityBot appears to act both as an indexing crawler and a RAG agent.\n\n_Publisher effects:_ This makes it hard for publishers to apply granular controls to the access and use of their IP by AI applications.\n\n---\n\n### AI Bot Profiles by Operating Organization\n\n**Google**\n\nThe search giant is a leading frontier lab with its Gemini models moving to, or near, the top of the leaderboards in 2025. Its Gemini chatbot has around 20% of the market but this could dramatically increase if Google uses its powerful distribution levers through Android, Chrome and search.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **GoogleBot** — The generic name for Google's mobile and desktop web crawlers. These move around the internet, collecting data which is used to create Google's search index. The content collected by Googlebot is also likely to be used for AI development. This bot is often spoofed by web scrapers.\n- **Google-NotebookLM** — A user-triggered fetcher used by NotebookLM that requests specific URLs a user has added as sources for a project.\n- **Google-Extended** — This user agent does not collect data but allows publishers to signal whether content collected by Google's other bots can be used for AI training and grounding.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ With the exception of user-initiated fetchers, Google's crawlers respect robots.txt signals.\n\n_Publisher partnerships:_ Whilst Google has signed a $60M AI content licensing deal with Reddit, the large number of partnerships it has with publishing businesses are built around its News Showcase product.\n\n---\n\n**OpenAI**\n\nDeveloper of ChatGPT, OpenAI's chatbot was first-to-market and still holds a ~60% share with 700M weekly active users as of August 2025. Its foundation models — and therefore user agents — power both ChatGPT and an array of third-party applications, including Microsoft's Co-Pilot and Bing search engine.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **ChatGPT-User** — Accesses websites in real-time and on-demand so that ChatGPT can formulate responses to user prompts based on the live web. It visits websites to gather information then processes, summarises and synthesizes this to provide the output. It is not used to gather data for model training.\n- **OAI-SearchBot** — Used to power ChatGPT's search capabilities. Similar to ChatGPT-User, it accesses the internet in real-time but is optimized for search scenarios.\n- **GPTBot** — Gathers data from the web for the training of OpenAI's large language models. It operates continuously in the background.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ OpenAI respects the signals provided by content owners via robots.txt, allowing them to disallow any or all of its crawlers.\n\n_Publisher partnerships:_ OpenAI has an extensive publisher partnerships program, having signed bilateral deals with ~40 publishers at the time of writing.\n\n\u003cInfoBox title=\"Robots Exclusion Protocol (robots.txt)\"\u003e\n  This index also refers to the Robots Exclusion Protocol or 'robots.txt'. This\n  mechanism allows website owners to give instructions to bots about accessing a\n  website. It uses a machine-readable file (named robots.txt) which specifies —\n  for individual bots or all bots collectively — which pages or sections they\n  can or cannot crawl. Robots.txt operates simply as a signal though and does\n  not actively block access. Not all developers program their bots to comply\n  with these instructions.\n\u003c/InfoBox\u003e\n\n---\n\n**Perplexity**\n\nPerplexity has developed an AI answer engine, effectively AI-powered search that provides users with a natural language response to a prompt alongside a list of links and sources. It has 22 million active users. The standard free product primarily uses a proprietary model, whereas the premium, paid-for service includes access to a range of models including OpenAI's GPT-4 Omni, Claude 3.5 from Anthropic, Llama 3 from Meta and Grok-2 from xAI.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **PerplexityBot** — Gathers data from across the web to index it for Perplexity's search function. It has also appeared to act in real-time.\n- **Perplexity-User** — Accesses web pages in real-time to respond to user prompts.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ Perplexity claims that its PerplexityBot user agent respects robots.txt. However, there have been widely-reported complaints from publishers that it has ignored their signals. Its policy makes no mention of the behaviour of Perplexity-User.\n\n_Publisher partnerships:_ In 2024 Perplexity launched a publisher partner program under which it provides a share of advertising revenues. Around 20 deals have been signed so far. The revenue shared is reported to be capped at 25%.\n\n---\n\n**Anthropic**\n\nFounded by seven former OpenAI employees, Anthropic is an AI developer with a focus on privacy, safety and alignment (with human values). Its foundation models power its Claude chatbot — which has a free and premium version — and a multitude of third-party applications.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **ClaudeBot** — Used to gather data from the internet for AI training.\n- **Claude-SearchBot** — Indexes online content to power Claude's web search.\n- **Claude-User** — Fetches a web page on demand when a user asks a question that requires a live lookup.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ Anthropic's bots respect publisher signals in robots.txt files. Notably they also respond to any disallows for Common Crawl's CCBot.\n\n---\n\n**Meta**\n\nMeta has developed the Llama family of open-source AI models. These are used in Meta's own products and extensively across third-party applications.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **Meta-ExternalAgent** — Crawls the web for 'use cases such as training AI models or improving products by indexing content directly'. Launched in summer 2024.\n- **Meta-ExternalFetcher** — Accesses websites in real-time in response to actions by users. May bypass robots.txt on account of being user-'initiated'.\n- **FacebookBot** — Previously described as crawling public web pages to improve language models for speech recognition technology.\n- **Meta-webindexer** — Meta's search indexing crawler for its push to bring web search in-house.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ Meta's crawlers respect robots.txt signals although Meta-ExternalFetcher may bypass the protocol.\n\n_Publisher partnerships:_ Meta has signed an AI licensing deal with just one publisher — Reuters.\n\n---\n\n**Apple**\n\nApple has been investing in AI — including the development of its own foundation models — to enhance its products, particularly in privacy-centric applications and on-device AI capabilities.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **Applebot** — Acts as both a search indexing crawler and a RAG agent feeding information to power features across Apple's ecosystem, including Siri, Spotlight and Safari.\n- **Applebot-Extended** — A secondary user agent that allows publishers to opt-out of their content being used to train Apple's foundation models.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ Apple claims that its bots respect robots.txt directives.\n\n_Publisher partnerships:_ Apple has not publicly announced any AI content licensing deals at time of writing but has reportedly been in negotiations with publishers including Condé Nast and NBC News.\n\n---\n\n**Amazon**\n\nAs well as a strategic partnership with Anthropic, Amazon has developed its own 'Nova' family of AI models which emphasize speed and value.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **AmazonBot** — Described as being used to improve services, 'such as enabling Alexa to answer even more questions for customers'. Amazon is clear that data collected by this crawler may be used for model training.\n- **Amzn-SearchBot** — Amazon's search indexing crawler, used to improve search experiences across Amazon products and services.\n- **Amzn-User** — A user-triggered fetcher that supports end-user actions by fetching live web pages on the user's behalf.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ Amazon's crawlers respect robots.txt signals.\n\n_Publisher partnerships:_ Amazon has notable AI licensing agreements with New York Times, Condé Nast, and Hearst.\n\n---\n\n**ByteDance**\n\nByteDance — owner of TikTok — has developed both text and video generation models. Most of these are for research purposes although its Doubao text models power its chatbot, only available in China.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **Bytespider** — Has been scraping the web at a high rate since it first appeared in early 2024. ByteDance has published no information on the function it serves or what the data collected is being used for.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ ByteDance has no published robots.txt policy. There are widespread reports of Bytespider ignoring robots.txt signals.\n\n---\n\n**Other Notable AI User Agents**\n\n- **CCBot** — Common Crawl's web crawler, which collects public web pages into the Common Crawl open dataset. Common Crawl states it respects robots.txt.\n- **DuckAssistBot** — DuckDuckGo's web crawler that crawls pages in real time to source information for answers by DuckAssist. Data collected is not used to train AI models and it respects robots.txt signals.\n- **Timpibot** — Web crawler for Timpi, a decentralized search index. Data is also available to AI developers for model training.\n- **YouBot** — Crawler for You.com, an AI-powered search engine that integrates AI query responses alongside conventional search links.\n- **Diffbot** — Web crawler focused on extracting data from web pages which it then converts into structured datasets. Fully respects robots.txt directives.\n- **ProRataInc** — ProRata.ai's user-agent for its Gist Crawler Bot, which crawls and extracts public and licensed web content to power its Gist.ai RAG answer service. Respects robots.txt.\n- **Ai2Bot-Dolma** — The Allen Institute for AI (AI2) crawler associated with building Dolma — AI2's open, large-scale pretraining corpus.\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"41:T104b8,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"var Component=(()=\u003e{var m=Object.create;var c=Object.defineProperty;var b=Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor;var y=Object.getOwnPropertyNames;var w=Object.getPrototypeOf,v=Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty;var A=(s,t)=\u003e()=\u003e{try{return t||s((t={exports:{}}).exports,t),t.exports}catch(i){throw t=0,i}},I=(s,t)=\u003e{for(var i in t)c(s,i,{get:t[i],enumerable:!0})},d=(s,t,i,l)=\u003e{if(t\u0026\u0026typeof t==\"object\"||typeof t==\"function\")for(let r of y(t))!v.call(s,r)\u0026\u0026r!==i\u0026\u0026c(s,r,{get:()=\u003et[r],enumerable:!(l=b(t,r))||l.enumerable});return s};var x=(s,t,i)=\u003e(i=s!=null?m(w(s)):{},d(t||!s||!s.__esModule?c(i,\"default\",{value:s,enumerable:!0}):i,s)),T=s=\u003ed(c({},\"__esModule\",{value:!0}),s);var g=A((k,p)=\u003e{p.exports=_jsx_runtime});var Q={};I(Q,{default:()=\u003ef,frontmatter:()=\u003eG});var e=x(g()),G={title:\"State of the Bots\",id:\"q3-q4-2025\",quarter:\"q3-q4-2025\",description:\"2025 Q3 \u0026 Q4, The Leaky Pipes\",docType:\"report\",sections:[{title:\"Executive Summary\",slug:\"executive-summary\"},{title:\"Section 1: Content Access Complexity\",slug:\"section-1\",subsections:[{title:\"Data Acquisition Stack\",slug:\"data-acquisition\"},{title:\"Google \u0026 Reddit Lawsuits\",slug:\"lawsuits\"},{title:\"Web Scraper Growth\",slug:\"scraper-growth\"},{title:\"Scraper Audit Tool\",slug:\"scraper-audit\"},{title:\"Cost of Web Scraping\",slug:\"scraping-cost\"}]},{title:\"Section 2: Scale of AI Scraping\",slug:\"section-2\",subsections:[{title:\"Aggregate Scraping Levels\",slug:\"aggregate-scraping\"},{title:\"Traffic Composition\",slug:\"traffic-composition\"},{title:\"RAG vs Training Bots\",slug:\"rag-vs-training\"},{title:\"Top AI Scrapers\",slug:\"top-scrapers\"},{title:\"Scraping by Region\",slug:\"scraping-region\"}]},{title:\"Section 3: AI Demand for Content\",slug:\"section-3\",subsections:[{title:\"Scraping by Topic\",slug:\"scraping-topic\"},{title:\"Top Scraped URLs\",slug:\"top-urls\"},{title:\"RAG Agent Preferences\",slug:\"rag-preferences\"},{title:\"Referral Topics\",slug:\"referral-topics\"}]},{title:\"Section 4: Referral Traffic\",slug:\"section-4\",subsections:[{title:\"Aggregate Referrals\",slug:\"aggregate-referrals\"},{title:\"Click-through Rates\",slug:\"ctr\"},{title:\"Scrape-to-Referral Ratio\",slug:\"scrape-referral\"}]},{title:\"Section 5: Robots.txt \u0026 Blocking\",slug:\"section-5\",subsections:[{title:\"Robots.txt Compliance\",slug:\"robots-compliance\"},{title:\"Compliance by Category\",slug:\"compliance-category\"},{title:\"Bot Paywall\",slug:\"bot-paywall\"}]},{title:\"Appendix: AI User Agent Profiles\",slug:\"appendix\",subsections:[{title:\"Categories of AI User Agent\",slug:\"agent-categories\"},{title:\"Bot Profiles by Organization\",slug:\"bot-profiles\"}]}]};function u(s){let t={a:\"a\",code:\"code\",em:\"em\",h2:\"h2\",h3:\"h3\",hr:\"hr\",li:\"li\",p:\"p\",strong:\"strong\",table:\"table\",tbody:\"tbody\",td:\"td\",th:\"th\",thead:\"thead\",tr:\"tr\",ul:\"ul\",...s.components},{ExecItem:i,ExecSummary:l,Image:r,InfoBox:h,Section:n,UserStory:o}=t;return i||a(\"ExecItem\",!0),l||a(\"ExecSummary\",!0),r||a(\"Image\",!0),h||a(\"InfoBox\",!0),n||a(\"Section\",!0),o||a(\"UserStory\",!0),(0,e.jsxs)(e.Fragment,{children:[(0,e.jsxs)(n,{id:\"executive-summary\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Executive Summary\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(l,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(i,{num:1,children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:`AI developers and many large companies building or using AI apps are paying\nthird-party web scrapers for access to the internet instead of paying\npublishers directly for their content.`}),` These third parties take the content,\noften reformatting it for AI consumption, and sell it. The recent Reddit and\nGoogle lawsuits against scrapers surface the tip of the iceberg of this\nrapidly growing ecosystem. We've documented nearly 40 of these web scraping\nactors and built a tool for publishers and website owners to test whether\ntheir sites are being scraped. Many of these scrapers list Fortune 1000\ncustomers \u0026 AI companies, and explicitly advertise cybersecurity evasion and\nhuman-mimicking tooling on their sites.`]})}),(0,e.jsx)(i,{num:2,children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Even paywalled content is not necessarily safe from these scrapers.\"}),` In our\ntesting, we found that some scrapers were able to retrieve full versions of\nvarious paywalled articles. Investing in cybersecurity is important for sites,\nbut it alone is not a complete solution. Companies as tech-advanced and\nwell-financed as Google and Reddit cannot stop the scrapers with\ncybersecurity, and have turned to litigation, which Google stated was a \"last\nresort.\"`]})}),(0,e.jsx)(i,{num:3,children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"AI traffic on sites is continuing to grow.\"}),` In Q1, on average, there was 1\nAI bot visit for every 200 human visits to a site. By EOY, that ratio\nincreased to 1 AI bot visit for every 31 human visits.`]})}),(0,e.jsx)(i,{num:4,children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:`In Q4, the average scrapes per page for a RAG bot was ~10\\xD7 more than for a\nTraining bot.`}),` For every 1 page request a training bot makes to a single\npage, a RAG bot makes, on average, 10. Some of the most frequently scraped\nURLs on TollBit in Q3 fell into the categories of \"Kimmel / Kirk Controversy\"\nand \"The Summer I Turned Pretty\". In Q4, this shifted to categories like\n\"Stranger Things Season 5\" and \"Holiday Gift Guides \u0026 Black Friday Deals.\"`]})}),(0,e.jsx)(i,{num:5,children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:`Looking at the top URLs that resulted in referrals for websites from AI\ncompanies,`}),` we can see that a disproportionate number of referrals came from\nChatGPT for technical topics such as \"Software/developer tools downloads\" and\n\"B2B Software Reviews\". Meanwhile, for Perplexity, shopping-related categories\nwere a key source of referrals. Referrals are likely higher in these\ncategories because users are more likely to click through to complete actions.`]})}),(0,e.jsx)(i,{num:6,children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:`It appears that click-through rates from AI applications, already abysmal,\nare declining:`}),` Websites without a direct 1:1 deal with an AI company on\naverage saw their click-through rates drop by nearly 3x \\u2014 from 0.8% in Q2 to\n0.27% at EOY. Websites with 1:1 AI licensing deals saw click-through rates\ndrop by over 6.5x in 2025 \\u2014 from 8.8% in Q1 down to 1.33% EOY \\u2014 suggesting\nthese deals are not necessarily insulating publishers from low AI referrals.`]})})]})]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(n,{id:\"section-1\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Section 1: The Growing Cost and Complexity of Content Access\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(o,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`AI developers are combining multiple data acquisition processes and\ntechnologies \\u2014 many offered by specialist web scraping vendors \\u2014 into tech\nstacks that secure access to the Internet. Reddit has filed a lawsuit against\nPerplexity and three of these web scraping vendors. Two of these vendors mask\nIP addresses, one of which scrapes Google's search engine results page. More\nrecently, Google has also filed a lawsuit against this same scraper. Fueled by\nthe AI boom, hundreds of third-party scrapers are in use, many by Fortune 1000\ncompanies. Many of these scrapers employ specialized cybersecurity evasion\ntools and do not comply with robots.txt by default. We built a tool for\nwebsites to test which of these third parties can scrape their sites. In\ntesting this tool across top sites, we found that even paywalled content is\nnot necessarily safe \\u2014 we observed that some scrapers retrieved full versions\nof various paywalled articles. This arms race between the scrapers and website\noperators is deeply inefficient for both AI developers and rights-holders. The\ninfrastructure for web scraping is unreliable and inefficient, and highlights\nthe growing need for sanctioned access.`})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"1.1 The Data Acquisition Stack\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"The data acquisition stack has existed for quite some time, but its effects have become more visible recently. With AI, the need to access content \u0026 data has exploded. Funded by the AI investment boom, developers are combining multiple data acquisition processes and technologies, with more advanced or clandestine approaches deployed where cybersecurity barriers are encountered. These increasingly complicated tech stacks are intended to secure 'just enough' access to content to ensure the applications they serve can handle the range of queries needed to compete.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"These stacks comprise some or all of the following:\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Direct crawling and scraping\"}),\" via first-party bots, either declared or disguised.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Secondary source crawling / scraping\"}),\", targeting surfaces on which the content itself may be visible on the open web (i.e., Google Search results page).\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Residential IP proxies,\"}),\" presenting as ordinary user traffic \\u2014 often with location and device characteristics/IP addresses that match the human audience.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Circumvention services\"}),\" that evade bot detection / IP protection measures.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Cloud-based headless browsers\"}),\" that load web content in a browser running in the cloud, rather than on a user's device. These are often used with a residential IP proxy: the proxy makes the request appear to come from a normal human user.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Third-party scraping services\"}),\" for outsourcing the end-to-end process of scraping a page.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"For obvious reasons, this environment is one in which it is exceedingly difficult for digital publishers to exercise control over the use of their IP. They are in a structurally disadvantaged position, having to respond to new threats as they emerge, whereas the web scrapers just need to evolve beyond the latest protection. Most websites deploy only one cybersecurity tool, whereas AI companies can choose from dozens of scraping tools interchangeably.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"1.2 Google \u0026 Reddit Take on the Third-Party Scrapers\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"On the demand side of the market, the recent Reddit and Google lawsuits shine a light on the software/service providers and intermediaries that have emerged to serve AI's need for content. On 22nd October 2025, Reddit filed a lawsuit in the Southern District of New York against AI 'answer engine' Perplexity, plus three other businesses few will have heard of: Oxylabs UAB, AWMProxy, and SerpApi. This case provides a startling window on the state of the fight between website owners, trying to protect their IP, and AI developers with a voracious appetite for data to fuel their AI applications.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Unlike the other copyright cases, which target businesses using \\u2014 or replicating \\u2014 intellectual property acquired without authorization or a licence, Reddit's filing also takes aim at intermediaries and service providers involved in the scraping of its content.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Two of the defendants \\u2014 Oxylabs UAB and AWMProxy \\u2014 are providers of IP proxies, allowing scrapers to disguise themselves as human traffic and bypass bot detection tools. SerpApi, meanwhile, acquires Reddit content by crawling Google's search engine results pages (SERPs).\"}),(0,e.jsx)(h,{title:\"Filing Extract: Reddit vs Perplexity et al.\",children:(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`\"Reddit and Google implement technological control measures to effectively\ncontrol access and prevent scraping of content posted to Reddit. Reddit has\nand maintains automated anti-scraping systems and teams dedicated to detecting\nand protecting against unauthorized access to its products and services...\nGoogle likewise has anti-scraping systems and teams dedicated to preventing\nunauthorized access to its products and services... Google utilizes a\ntechnological access control system called 'SearchGuard,' which is designed to\nprevent automated systems from accessing and obtaining wholesale search\nresults and indexed data.\"`})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`On December 19th, 2025, Google also filed a lawsuit against SerpApi, alleging unlawful scraping of copyrighted content that is included in Google's search results. Google's blog post about this case cites, \"SerpApi uses shady back doors \\u2014 like cloaking themselves, bombarding websites with massive networks of bots and giving their crawlers fake and constantly changing names \\u2014 circumventing our security measures to take websites' content wholesale. This unlawful activity has increased dramatically over the past year.\"`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"1.3 The Growth and Sophistication of Web Scraping\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"The web scrapers in the Reddit \u0026 Google cases are the tip of the iceberg; there are many others in the market. As the Google blog highlights, scraping has skyrocketed over the past year. Many of these web scrapers are being funded by the AI boom to provide content to power AI for both training and RAG use cases.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"In an analysis of nearly 40 scraping vendors, we find that many explicitly advertise cybersecurity detection evasion techniques and many do not default to abiding by robots.txt.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.strong,{children:[\"See our \",(0,e.jsx)(t.a,{href:\"/scraper-index\",children:\"Web Scraper Index\"}),\" section for more details on these web scrapers and their specific features.\"]})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`This growth in volume of scraping and sophistication increasingly creates challenges for site owners. SerpApi's success in building a business around scraping Google at scale demonstrates the challenges that even the largest and best-funded platforms have in controlling unauthorized, non-human access to their digital properties. In Google's lawsuit against SerpApi, it highlights how SerpApi circumvented Google's in-house cybersecurity tool, SearchGuard, which was \"the product of tens of thousands of person-hours and millions of dollars of investment\". If Google, with its strong technical capabilities and financial resources, is unable to prevent this activity \\u2014 activity which is likely to be economically damaging \\u2014 media owners face a far steeper challenge.`}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Google-SerpApi-filing.png\",alt:\"Screenshots from Google vs SerpApi filing\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"1.4 Scraper Audit Tool \u0026 Experiment\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"In order to examine the sophistication of web scrapers against websites' cybersecurity tooling, TollBit developed a tool for website owners to test 15 popular web scrapers against their own sites. The tool allows website owners to initiate scraping on any page to evaluate both defense evasion, content accessibility, and retrieval completeness. In selecting web scrapers, we picked a few industry leaders and a couple of newer scrapers offering advanced technology, despite their company size and time in market. For each of these web scrapers, whenever available, we purchased each of their top-tier enterprise subscriptions of their web scraping APIs to leverage their full suite of AI-driven parsing, advanced anti-bot bypassing, and premium proxy networks.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"To demonstrate the utility of this tool in auditing scrapers, we conducted an evaluation of this tool across 30 high-authority sites, strategically divided into 15 paywalled and 15 non-paywalled domains from the US and UK as defined by the Press Gazette.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Our analysis revealed:\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Even paywalled content is not necessarily safe.\"}),\" In our testing of 15 of these scraping tools, we observed that some were able to scrape various paywalled articles in full.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Non-paywalled sites offered no discernible increase in protection\"}),\" compared to their paywalled counterparts, as scrapers successfully retrieved full content from the vast majority of target pages regardless of subscription barriers.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[\"In the rare instances where all scrapers failed to fully extract data \\u2014 occurring in only three of the thirty sites \\u2014 the failure was primarily attributed to \",(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"server-side rendering implementations\"}),\" of the paywall.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"To determine some of the behavioral strategies used by these scrapers, we also tested the tool on our own test site. Based on the server logs, the following strategies were identified:\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Robots.txt Bypass:\"}),\" None of the tested scrapers attempted to fetch the \",(0,e.jsx)(t.code,{children:\"robots.txt\"}),\" file before accessing the target page\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User-Agent Spoofing:\"}),\" Scrapers utilized the latest Chrome User-Agents to blend into legitimate traffic, avoiding custom identifying strings.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"IP Rotation:\"}),\" Tools executed aggressive proxy rotation strategies; one hit the server from at least ten different IP addresses within a one-second window.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Engine Diversification:\"}),\" Some scrapers pivoted to a Firefox User-Agent to avoid Chromium-centric detection.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Referrer Masking:\"}),\" Scrapers combined modern User-Agents with spoofed Google search referrers to simulate organic site discovery and entry.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Legacy Rotation:\"}),\" Tools utilized older, static browser profiles to avoid the predictable behavioral signatures associated with modern headless frameworks.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Referrer-Origin Simulation:\"}),' Scrapers used \"Same-Origin\" referrers to replicate internal site navigation']}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[\"TollBit is launching this tool to help websites test and understand their vulnerability to IP threats from major web scrapers. If you are interested in seeing how your site is being scraped by these third parties, please contact \",(0,e.jsx)(t.a,{href:\"mailto:[email withheld by Lighthouse]\",children:\"[email withheld by Lighthouse]\"}),\".\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"1.5 The Inefficiency and Cost of Web Scraping\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"This arms race between the scrapers and website operators is not only inefficient for website owners, but it's also increasingly inefficient for AI developers. Our investigation has found advanced scraping services charging up to $22.50 for 1,000 pages. Given the volume of data needed to serve consumer AI applications, the data acquisition costs for the most popular chatbots and specialized agents are likely to run into the tens of millions of dollars a year. And this is before considering the legal fees needed to fight lawsuits such as the ones filed by Reddit and Google.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"The current 'scrape-and-block' dynamic is a negative-sum game. AI developers spend huge sums on scraping infrastructure, while website owners are forced to spend exorbitantly on defense. There's a better way. Instead of spending huge sums on technical workarounds to bypass IP controls, AI developers can simply pay for the content they use. By relying on licensing rather than scraping, journalism is funded \\u2014 not the scrapers \\u2014 and the media ecosystem AI depends on is sustained.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"In order for this to occur, it is important that regulators ensure that bots are not allowed to mimic human visitors on the Internet. Otherwise, website owners may be caught in an increasingly expensive and ineffective cat-and-mouse game of trying to detect web scrapers.\"})]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(n,{id:\"section-2\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Section 2: Scale of AI Scraping\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(o,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`From Q2 through EOY, the rate of AI scraping continued to increase across\nsites, with an average quarterly growth rate of 24.4%. In Q2, on average,\nthere was 1 AI bot visit for every 50 human visits to a site. By Q4, that\nratio increased to 1 AI bot visit for every 31 human visits. RAG bots remain\nthe primary type of AI bot visitors to sites. In Q4, RAG bots on average\nscraped sites over 4.5x more than training bots. And while RAG bot activity\nacross sites grew by an average of 33% from Q2 to Q4 2025, training activity\ndeclined by 15%. In Q4, OpenAI's ChatGPT-User had an average scrape per page\n5x higher than the second-highest AI scraper (Meta), and over 16x higher than\nPerplexity's agent. Comparing the growth of AI bot traffic across regions,\nAsia has the highest growth rate of AI scraping across sites, clocking in at a\n7x higher growth rate than North America \\u2014 although North American sites\ncontinue to have the highest rates of scraping.`})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"2.1 Aggregate Level of AI Scraping\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"By examining the level of AI bot scraping in the second half of 2025 across the cohort of sites that haven't changed their robots.txt files within this period, we can see the underlying level of demand for publisher content by AI systems.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"We can see a steady increase in demand for publisher content from AI bots, with scrapes growing 29% from Q2 to Q3 and 20% from Q3 to Q4.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.1: AI bot scrapes per site | Q2\\u2013Q4 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 2.1_ AI bot scrapes per site _ Q2\\u2013Q4 2025.svg\",alt:\"AI bot scrapes per site Q2-Q4 2025\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"2.2 Changing Composition of Web Traffic\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[\"In TollBit's last \",(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"State of the Bots\"}),\", we reported on the changing composition of web traffic, predicting the rise of bot traffic, which in turn is likely to transform into 'faux human' traffic; AI bots masquerading as humans in order to bypass website owners' bot detection measures. The pattern we predicted continues to play out in the data with AI bot requests rising relative to human traffic across our partner websites.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:'In light of this, the data below is conservative; it is likely worse than these numbers because some of these \"humans\" are likely bots. From the tests we ran, as mentioned in section 1, many of these web scrapers are indistinguishable from human visitors on sites.'}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"In Q2, there was 1 AI bot visit for every 50 human visits. In Q4, this shifted to 1 AI bot visit for every 31 human visits; an increase in the bot-to-human ratio of ~60% across this period. The ratio of AI visitors to Human visitors is increasing, with an average quarterly compound growth rate of 26%.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Over the same period, human traffic to sites increased by 1% from Q2 to Q3 but decreased by 5% from Q3 to Q4. The average quarterly compound growth rate for human visitors is -2%.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.2: AI bot-to-human request ratio | Q2\\u2013Q4 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 2.2_ AI bot-to-human request ratio _ Q2\\u2013Q4 2025.svg\",alt:\"AI bot-to-human request ratio\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"2.3 Scraping Level by Type of AI Bot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"When we break down the aggregate level of scraping by AI bot category, we can see a stark difference in traffic patterns. RAG agent requests are rising consistently (average quarterly growth rate of 33% between Q2 and Q4 2025), driven by upstream consumer adoption of AI applications and their use in settings that require access to the live web. Meanwhile, training declined by 15% on average during the same period, though with some fluctuation.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"RAG vs Training vs Search Indexer Scraping Percentage Change \\u2014 Q2 vs Q4\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.table,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.thead,{children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.th,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Bot Type\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.th,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"Avg Quarterly Growth Rate\"})]})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tbody,{children:[(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"RAG\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"+33%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Training\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"-15%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Search Indexer\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"+59%\"})]})]})]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Activity from AI search indexers is also increasing rapidly (average quarterly compound growth rate is 59% from Q2 to Q4). These bots crawl the web autonomously, gathering data to build a map of the internet, which is later used to direct RAG bots, when needed by a user prompt.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"RAG bots remain the primary type of AI bot visitors to sites. In Q4, RAG bots on average scraped sites over 4.5x more than training bots.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.3.1: RAG vs training vs search indexer scrapes (Cohort) | Q2\\u2013Q4 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 2.3.1_ RAG vs training vs indexer scrapes (Cohort) _ Q2\\u2013Q4 2025.svg\",alt:\"RAG vs training vs search indexer scrapes\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"If we look at this data on the average scrapes per page level, we can start to see data that provides some evidence as to why RAG bots are scraping sites more frequently. This is because RAG bots require continued access to content; they cannot store the entirety of the web and therefore revisit pages repeatedly. In Q4, the average scrapes per page for a RAG bot was ~10\\xD7 more than for Training bots. For every 1 page request a training bot makes to a single page, a RAG bot makes, on average, 10. This demonstrates that AI bots require repeated access to content to ground their responses to end users.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.3.2: RAG vs training scrapes per page | Q3\\u2013Q4 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 2.3.2_ RAG vs training scrapes per page _ Q3\\u2013Q4 2025.svg\",alt:\"RAG vs training scrapes per page\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.3.3: Composition of AI bot scrapes \\u2014 RAG vs training vs search Indexer (All sites)\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 2.3.3_ Composition of AI bot scrapes - RAG vs Training vs Search Indexer (All sites).svg\",alt:\"Composition of AI bot scrapes\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"2.4 OpenAI is One of the Most Active AI Scrapers\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Average AI scrapes per page are dominated by OpenAI's ChatGPT user, with an average scrape per page 5x higher than the second-highest scraper (Meta), and over 16x higher than Perplexity, in Q4 2025.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.4: AI bot scrapes per page | Q3 vs Q4 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 2.4_ AI bot scrapes per page _ Q3 vs Q4 2025.svg\",alt:\"AI bot scrapes per page Q3 vs Q4\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.4.1: AI bot scrapes per site | Q3 vs Q4 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 2.4.1_ AI bot scrapes per site _ Q3 vs Q4 2025.svg\",alt:\"AI bot scrapes per site Q3 vs Q4\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"2.5 Aggregate AI Scraping by Site Region\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"By categorizing websites that joined TollBit prior to Q3 by region, we see that, in absolute terms, US- and Canada-based publishers experience significantly higher scraping volumes than those in Europe or Asia. Language is likely to be one factor here, with English content subject to the highest demand from AI applications. Furthermore, the US is over-represented in the user bases of many consumer AI tools (for example, around 20% of ChatGPT traffic originates from the US), which will bias RAG requests toward local sources for certain user needs.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Comparing the growth of AI bot traffic across regions, we can see that Asia has the highest rate of growth in AI scraping across sites, clocking in at over 120%, followed by Europe at half that rate, and North America at a much lower rate of approximately 17%. However, North America continues to have a significantly higher average AI scraping rate across sites.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.5: AI bot scrapes per page by region (Cohort) | Q3\\u2013Q4 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 2.5_ AI bot scrapes per page by region (Cohort) _ Q3\\u2013Q4 2025.svg\",alt:\"AI bot scrapes per page by region\"})]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(n,{id:\"section-3\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Section 3: AI Demand for Content\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(o,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`The most AI-scraped sites on TollBit are in the B2B/professional, National\nNews, and Lifestyle categories. Some of the most frequently scraped URLs on\nTollBit in Q3 fell into the categories of \"Charlie Kirk\" and \"NFL Week 3 \u0026 4\".\nIn Q4, this shifted to categories like \"Stranger Things Season 5\", \"Netflix\nWarner Bros Deal\", and \"Holiday Gift Guides \u0026 Black Friday Deals\". This\nhighlights how many of these AI apps are scraping sites to power RAG. This\nanalysis shows that each RAG agent appears to serve a different user need:\nChatGPT tends to be most used for general search, Perplexity for consumer\nproduct research and reviews, and Claude for professional tasks \\u2014 especially\nin the tech space.`})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"3.1 AI Content Scraping Across Topics\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Looking at the cohort of sites that joined TollBit before Q2 2025, the content categories that were scraped the most were B2B/professional, followed by National News and Lifestyle.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Analyzing changes in scraping levels by publisher category reveals very substantial increases across technology \u0026 consumer electronics (+107%), B2B/professional (+62%), national news (+55%), and political/advocacy (+48%). Again, this growth is likely to be driven by a corresponding increase in relevant prompts on consumer AI applications that stems from more users turning to these tools for information retrieval tasks.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"B2B/professional content is vastly more scraped than the next category (national news). This is likely due to generative engine optimization (GEO) efforts by companies who are trying to get their content consumed by AI platforms to recommend their product, as opposed to other verticals like news that are trying to block it.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.1: Scrapes per site by site category (Cohort) | Q2\\u2013Q4 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 3.1_ Scrapes per site by site category (Cohort) _ Q2\\u2013Q4 2025.svg\",alt:\"Scrapes per site by site category\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Viewed on a per-page basis, we see marked variation in AI demand across publisher categories, as well as divergences in the rate of growth and decline. This view helps separate the content-volume effect (more pages to crawl) from the underlying level of demand. In general, higher per-page scraping indicates greater demand for RAG (given that training crawlers tend to access content less repeatedly). We can see that health and wellness and lifestyle content are subject to the highest \\u2014 and growing \\u2014 per-page demand, suggesting stronger end-user demand in AI apps for content in these areas.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.1.2: Scrapes per page by site category (Cohort) | Q2\\u2013Q4 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 3.1.2_ Scrapes per page by site category (Cohort) _ Q2\\u2013Q4 2025.svg\",alt:\"Scrapes per page by site category\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"3.2 Top Scraped URLs by Granular Topic\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Across all partner domains, TollBit analyzed the URLs most frequently scraped, which together account for approximately 10% of total AI scrapes. Granular categorization of the specific topics of the most-scraped URLs/articles provides a window into the types of prompts that require AI bots to fetch content. The majority of these topics are time-sensitive, covering major news stories, popular culture events, or product reviews.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Consumer use of AI applications in search-like settings \\u2014 around time-sensitive topics \\u2014 is likely the source of this demand, with RAG requests being needed to gather up-to-date information from reputable sources to respond to users in real-time.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.2.1: Most-scraped pages by topic | Q3 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 3.2.1_ Most-scraped pages by topic _ Q3 2025.svg\",alt:\"Most-scraped pages by topic Q3 2025\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"This data appears to support the concept that AI chatbots are being used by consumers in general, information-retrieval settings that are highly likely to be substitutional to the use of Google.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.2.2: Most-scraped pages by topic | Q4 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 3.2.2_ Most-scraped pages by topic _ Q4 2025.svg\",alt:\"Most-scraped pages by topic Q4 2025\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"3.3 Content Preferences of Different RAG Agents\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"We analyzed the most frequently scraped URLs for the RAG agents used by ChatGPT, Claude, and Perplexity (accounting for nearly 10% of each agent's traffic on TollBit partner sites) to assess their relative content preferences. To learn these preferences, we determined their demand for different content formats like news stories, reviews, forums, etc., and topics.\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"ChatGPT's RAG agent\"}),\" most frequently accessed content spanning finance, viral controversies, entertainment, product reviews, and breaking news. This breadth suggests users are treating the platform as a general-purpose search tool.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.3.1: ChatGPT-User most-scraped topics | Q3 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 3.3.1_ ChatGPT-User most-scraped topics _ Q3 2025.svg\",alt:\"ChatGPT-User most-scraped topics Q3\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.3.2: ChatGPT-User most-scraped topics | Q4 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 3.3.2_ ChatGPT-User most-scraped topics _ Q4 2025.svg\",alt:\"ChatGPT-User most-scraped topics Q4\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Perplexity's RAG agent\"}),\" retrieved content focused on product reviews, B2B software guides, and real-time news \\u2014 from consumer product and car reviews and listicles to live NBA \u0026 NFL games and election coverage. This focus suggests users are treating the platform as a research tool for product decisions.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.3.3: Perplexity-User most-scraped topics | Q3 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/figure 3.3.3.svg\",alt:\"Perplexity-User most-scraped topics Q3\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.3.4: Perplexity-User most-scraped topics | Q4 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 3.3.4.svg\",alt:\"Perplexity-User most-scraped topics Q4\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Claude's RAG agent\"}),\" retrieved content spanning B2B software reviews, tech careers, and interview prep, and AI \\u2014 alongside consumer tech and news. The prominence of business and career-focused topics suggests users are leveraging the platform for professional tasks.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.3.5: Claude-User most-scraped topics | Q3 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 3.3.5.svg\",alt:\"Claude-User most-scraped topics Q3\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.3.6: Claude-User most-scraped topics | Q4 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 3.3.6.svg\",alt:\"Claude-User most-scraped topics Q4\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Each RAG agent appears to serve a different user need. The data suggests interesting segmentation: ChatGPT tends to be most used for general search, Perplexity for consumer product research and reviews, and Claude for professional tasks \\u2014 especially in the tech space.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"3.4 AI Application Referrals to Different Topics\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"To evaluate queries about which topics satisfied by a generative summary and those requiring high-intent browsing, we analyzed the top referred-to URLs for each AI agent (which account for approximately 15% each of their total referral traffic on TollBit partner sites). Benchmarking these referrals against scraping frequency reveals how different topics witnessed varied clickthrough rates.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:'Across both quarters, a disproportionate volume of referrals for ChatGPT and Claude originated from technical and transactional domains, specifically \"Software/Developer Tools Downloads\" and \"B2B Professional\" topics. Topics like Stranger Things Season 5, Movies, TV, Streaming drove few referrals.'}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Throughout Q3 and Q4, Perplexity drove the most referral traffic to the shopping vertical (a category that also ranks among the most frequently scraped). However, it is interesting to note that Perplexity's RAG agent scraped many car review pages in Q4 but there are nearly no referrals to car review pages.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Interestingly, across all AI RAG bots, news topics tend to make up at least 15% of referrals. However, it's important to note that AI applications this still remains a very small quantity of overall referrals (approximately 0.1%).\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.4.1: ChatGPT top referral topics | Q3 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 3.4.1.svg\",alt:\"ChatGPT top referral topics Q3\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.4.2: ChatGPT top referral topics | Q4 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 3.4.2.svg\",alt:\"ChatGPT top referral topics Q4\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.4.3: Perplexity top referral topics | Q3 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 3.4.3.svg\",alt:\"Perplexity top referral topics Q3\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.4.4: Perplexity top referral topics | Q4 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 3.4.4.svg\",alt:\"Perplexity top referral topics Q4\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.4.5: Claude top referral topics | Q3 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 3.4.5.svg\",alt:\"Claude top referral topics Q3\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.4.6: Claude top referral topics | Q4 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 3.4.6.svg\",alt:\"Claude top referral topics Q4\"})]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(n,{id:\"section-4\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Section 4: Referral Traffic\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(o,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`AI apps continue to be an extremely small source of referrals for sites,\naveraging 0.12%. It appears click-through rates from AI applications are\ndeclining, dropping by nearly 3x \\u2014 from 0.8% in Q2 to 0.27% in Q4 2025.\nWebsites with 1:1 AI licensing deals saw click-through rates drop by over 6.5x\nin 2025 \\u2014 from 8.8% in Q1 down to 1.33% in Q4 \\u2014 suggesting that deals are not\nnecessarily insulating publishers from low AI referrals. Scrape-to-referral\nratios for AI RAG agents indicate that human visitors click through more often\nto sites in content categories such as National News, Local News, and\nTech/Consumer Electronics. Content in categories like Business, Entertainment,\nand Sports, on average, was scraped far more often before getting back a human\nvisitor.`})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"4.1 Aggregate Referrals from AI Applications\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"The aggregated view on external traffic sources for Tollbit partner publishers continues to illustrate the dominant role of Google. It delivers over 80% of referred human visitor traffic, whilst AI applications are the source of just 0.1%. This means that, despite renewed competition in search and the growth of zero-click searches, on average Google delivers over 678 human visitors to a site for each visitor from an AI application.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 4.1: Referral share by source | Q3 \u0026 Q4 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 4.1.svg\",alt:\"Referral share by source\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"4.2 AI Applications Continue to Be a Small Portion of Referrals\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Looking at a cohort of sites onboarded prior to Q2, AI application referrals remain a very small share of these sites' referrals, averaging 0.12%, while approximately 80.5% of referrals come from Google.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 4.2: Referral share by source (Cohort) | Q2\\u2013Q4 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 4.2.svg\",alt:\"Referral share by source cohort\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"4.3 AI Application Click-through Rates\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Average click-through rate (CTR) from AI applications (the proportion of users that visit a publisher page given the use of that content in a prompt response) for a cohort of sites that onboarded prior to Q2 has decreased from 0.8% in Q2 to 0.27% in Q4.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"In aggregate, the CTR for AI applications remains over 13x lower than that delivered by pre-AI Google search engine results pages (SERPs).\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 4.3: AI referral CTR (Cohort) vs Google benchmark | Q2\\u2013Q4 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 4.3.svg\",alt:\"AI referral CTR vs Google benchmark\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"The presence of a 1:1 deal is shown to increase prominence on and traffic from the respective AI company. Although notably the CTR uplift provided by a deal appears to be diminishing, dropping from over 15x more referrals versus those without a deal in Q1 2025 to 7x in Q4.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 4.3.1: AI referral CTR by deal status (Cohort) | Q1\\u2013Q4 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 4.3.1.svg\",alt:\"AI referral CTR by deal status\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"4.4 Scrape-to-Referral Ratio by Content Category\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"The scrape-to-referral ratio is the number of scrapes by an AI RAG agent it takes to get one human site visitor back. For example, for the sports category, on average, it takes 218 scrapes by an AI RAG agent for one human visitor to click through. A high ratio (as seen in Deals \u0026 shopping, Business \u0026 Finance, Health \u0026 wellness, Lifestyle) implies the AI output is treated as a substitute, and users are satisfied with the summary and thereby not clicking through to visit the site. Conversely, a low ratio (which we observe in local news and national news) suggests the output requires verification or users need to visit the original source to continue their journey.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 4.4: Scrape-to-Referral ratio by topic | Q3 \u0026 Q4 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 4.4.svg\",alt:\"Scrape-to-Referral ratio by topic\"})]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(n,{id:\"section-5\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Section 5: Robots.txt \u0026 Blocking\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(o,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`30% of total AI bot scrapes in Q4 2025 were scrapes that did not abide by\nexplicit robots.txt permissions. In Q4 2025, Deals \u0026 Shopping sites saw 4x\nmore non-permitted scrapes than permitted scrapes. Entertainment sites saw\n2.5x more, and Local News sites saw roughly equal amounts of each. This data\nshows that robots.txt remains insufficient and AI bots continue to bypass\nrobots.txt at scale. 42% of OpenAI's ChatGPT-User scrapes accessed content\nfrom sites that blocked it on robots.txt \\u2014 the highest non-compliance rate of\nany major AI bot in Q4 2025. The number of AI bot visits hitting the TollBit\nBot Paywall increased by 999% in 2025 to 1 billion, showcasing that publishers\nare continuing to attempt to increase their defenses against scrapers and\nredirect them to a mechanism to pay.`})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"5.1 Robots.txt Compliance\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Certain AI bots demonstrate a greater propensity to ignore robots.txt signals than others. In some instances, this will be driven by the number of disallow requests encountered (i.e., if two bots are programmed to behave the same but one is widely disallowed, then, of course, it will bypass more robots.txt signals). Notably, OpenAI's RAG agent \\u2014 'ChatGPT-User' \\u2014 is found to routinely bypass publisher signals, despite its developer notes suggesting otherwise. As highlighted in our past reports, it's plausible that other bots are using third-party scrapers with alternative user agents or are using headless browsers that appear as humans so that their scraping goes less noticed.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 5.1: Robots.txt bypass rate by bot | Q2\\u2013Q4 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 5.1.svg\",alt:\"Robots.txt bypass rate by bot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"5.2 Robots.txt Compliance by Category\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"AI bots continue to ignore publisher signals and access content in contravention of explicit disallow requests as expressed via the Robots Exclusion Protocol (i.e. 'robots.txt'). Looking at the total volume of bypassing scrapes by publisher segment, we can see that entertainment \u0026 pop culture is a notable outlier, receiving over double the volume versus the next largest category. This is a function of the level of demand for this content; the extent of disallow requests and the behaviour of the user agents.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 5.2: Robots.txt bypass scrapes by site category | Q4 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 5.2.svg\",alt:\"Robots.txt bypass scrapes by site category\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"In Q4 2025, Deals and Shopping sites saw over 400 bypassing scrapes per 100 permitted requests. Similarly, Entertainment \u0026 Pop Culture sites received almost 250 bypassing scrapes per 100 permitted requests. This data is a stark illustration of the inadequacy of the Robots Exclusion Protocol as a means of rights reservation. Because it carries no enforcement mechanism and relies entirely on the goodwill of those operating web scraping bots, rights holders urgently need an alternative means of protecting their IP online.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 5.2.1: Bypass scrapes per 100 permitted by topic | Q3\\u2013Q4 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 5.2.1.svg\",alt:\"Bypass scrapes per 100 permitted by topic\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"5.3 Redirects to Bot Paywall\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"The TollBit platform works with websites' existing tech stacks to redirect bots that do not have permission to access a given site to a bot paywall. The licensing price is set by the publisher. Hits to these paywalls have been growing steeply, reaching one billion in Q4 2025. From Q1 to Q4, the number of AI bot visits hitting the TollBit Bot Paywall grew at an average quarterly growth rate of 122.32%.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 5.3: Hits to the TollBit paywall by quarter | 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q3-q4-2025/Figure 5.3.svg\",alt:\"Hits to the TollBit paywall by quarter\"})]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(n,{id:\"appendix\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Appendix: AI User Agent Profiles\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"As our State of the Bots reports chart, the internet is crawled by an ever-growing number of AI user agents. These have different functions for the applications they serve and consequently, behave differently online. Here is a quick overview of the principal forms of AI user agent, what they do, how they work and why they matter to publishers.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"Categories of AI User Agent\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"1. Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) Agent\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"These bots retrieve information in real-time to respond to user prompts. They use an index of the web, gathered by an indexing crawler (either proprietary or third-party such as Bing or Google) to locate the relevant content which is then retrieved and synthesized into a response.\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher effects:\"}),\" When a RAG agent accesses a site, it's using that content to provide a response to a user's prompt, typically in an AI chatbot (e.g. ChatGPT) or AI search (e.g. Perplexity) application. This might be substitutional to a human visitor.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"2. Training Data Crawling Agent\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Large language models \\u2014 such as Llama from Meta or GPT-5 from OpenAI, both of which power a multitude of consumer applications \\u2014 are trained on vast quantities of data. Training data crawlers move around the web \\u2014 following links from websites to websites or working through sitemaps \\u2014 downloading content which is then processed and stored for offline use.\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher effects:\"}),\" The inclusion of content in the training data means that a model has this 'knowledge' to answer prompts. Training data typically cuts off several months prior to model release so the substitutional risk is limited for content with a longer shelf-life.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"3. AI Search Indexing Agent\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"AI systems with access to the real-time web need an index of the internet. This is used to direct RAG agents to the right sources when collecting data needed for responses to prompts. AI search indexing crawlers build these indexes by systematically navigating the web, collecting and organizing content and metadata.\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher effects:\"}),\" These crawlers only ensure that a website's content can be navigated to by a RAG agent, should a relevant prompt require that.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"4. Hybrid Agent\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Some AI developers use a single bot (or at least software which identifies itself with the same user agent metadata) for more than one of these purposes. For example, PerplexityBot appears to act both as an indexing crawler and a RAG agent.\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher effects:\"}),\" This makes it hard for publishers to apply granular controls to the access and use of their IP by AI applications.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"AI Bot Profiles by Operating Organization\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Google\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"The search giant is a leading frontier lab with its Gemini models moving to, or near, the top of the leaderboards in 2025. Its Gemini chatbot has around 20% of the market but this could dramatically increase if Google uses its powerful distribution levers through Android, Chrome and search.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"GoogleBot\"}),\" \\u2014 The generic name for Google's mobile and desktop web crawlers. These move around the internet, collecting data which is used to create Google's search index. The content collected by Googlebot is also likely to be used for AI development. This bot is often spoofed by web scrapers.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Google-NotebookLM\"}),\" \\u2014 A user-triggered fetcher used by NotebookLM that requests specific URLs a user has added as sources for a project.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Google-Extended\"}),\" \\u2014 This user agent does not collect data but allows publishers to signal whether content collected by Google's other bots can be used for AI training and grounding.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" With the exception of user-initiated fetchers, Google's crawlers respect robots.txt signals.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher partnerships:\"}),\" Whilst Google has signed a $60M AI content licensing deal with Reddit, the large number of partnerships it has with publishing businesses are built around its News Showcase product.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"OpenAI\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Developer of ChatGPT, OpenAI's chatbot was first-to-market and still holds a ~60% share with 700M weekly active users as of August 2025. Its foundation models \\u2014 and therefore user agents \\u2014 power both ChatGPT and an array of third-party applications, including Microsoft's Co-Pilot and Bing search engine.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"ChatGPT-User\"}),\" \\u2014 Accesses websites in real-time and on-demand so that ChatGPT can formulate responses to user prompts based on the live web. It visits websites to gather information then processes, summarises and synthesizes this to provide the output. It is not used to gather data for model training.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"OAI-SearchBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Used to power ChatGPT's search capabilities. Similar to ChatGPT-User, it accesses the internet in real-time but is optimized for search scenarios.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"GPTBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Gathers data from the web for the training of OpenAI's large language models. It operates continuously in the background.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" OpenAI respects the signals provided by content owners via robots.txt, allowing them to disallow any or all of its crawlers.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher partnerships:\"}),\" OpenAI has an extensive publisher partnerships program, having signed bilateral deals with ~40 publishers at the time of writing.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(h,{title:\"Robots Exclusion Protocol (robots.txt)\",children:(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`This index also refers to the Robots Exclusion Protocol or 'robots.txt'. This\nmechanism allows website owners to give instructions to bots about accessing a\nwebsite. It uses a machine-readable file (named robots.txt) which specifies \\u2014\nfor individual bots or all bots collectively \\u2014 which pages or sections they\ncan or cannot crawl. Robots.txt operates simply as a signal though and does\nnot actively block access. Not all developers program their bots to comply\nwith these instructions.`})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Perplexity\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Perplexity has developed an AI answer engine, effectively AI-powered search that provides users with a natural language response to a prompt alongside a list of links and sources. It has 22 million active users. The standard free product primarily uses a proprietary model, whereas the premium, paid-for service includes access to a range of models including OpenAI's GPT-4 Omni, Claude 3.5 from Anthropic, Llama 3 from Meta and Grok-2 from xAI.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"PerplexityBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Gathers data from across the web to index it for Perplexity's search function. It has also appeared to act in real-time.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Perplexity-User\"}),\" \\u2014 Accesses web pages in real-time to respond to user prompts.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" Perplexity claims that its PerplexityBot user agent respects robots.txt. However, there have been widely-reported complaints from publishers that it has ignored their signals. Its policy makes no mention of the behaviour of Perplexity-User.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher partnerships:\"}),\" In 2024 Perplexity launched a publisher partner program under which it provides a share of advertising revenues. Around 20 deals have been signed so far. The revenue shared is reported to be capped at 25%.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Anthropic\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Founded by seven former OpenAI employees, Anthropic is an AI developer with a focus on privacy, safety and alignment (with human values). Its foundation models power its Claude chatbot \\u2014 which has a free and premium version \\u2014 and a multitude of third-party applications.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"ClaudeBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Used to gather data from the internet for AI training.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Claude-SearchBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Indexes online content to power Claude's web search.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Claude-User\"}),\" \\u2014 Fetches a web page on demand when a user asks a question that requires a live lookup.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" Anthropic's bots respect publisher signals in robots.txt files. Notably they also respond to any disallows for Common Crawl's CCBot.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Meta\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Meta has developed the Llama family of open-source AI models. These are used in Meta's own products and extensively across third-party applications.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Meta-ExternalAgent\"}),\" \\u2014 Crawls the web for 'use cases such as training AI models or improving products by indexing content directly'. Launched in summer 2024.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Meta-ExternalFetcher\"}),\" \\u2014 Accesses websites in real-time in response to actions by users. May bypass robots.txt on account of being user-'initiated'.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"FacebookBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Previously described as crawling public web pages to improve language models for speech recognition technology.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Meta-webindexer\"}),\" \\u2014 Meta's search indexing crawler for its push to bring web search in-house.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" Meta's crawlers respect robots.txt signals although Meta-ExternalFetcher may bypass the protocol.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher partnerships:\"}),\" Meta has signed an AI licensing deal with just one publisher \\u2014 Reuters.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Apple\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Apple has been investing in AI \\u2014 including the development of its own foundation models \\u2014 to enhance its products, particularly in privacy-centric applications and on-device AI capabilities.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Applebot\"}),\" \\u2014 Acts as both a search indexing crawler and a RAG agent feeding information to power features across Apple's ecosystem, including Siri, Spotlight and Safari.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Applebot-Extended\"}),\" \\u2014 A secondary user agent that allows publishers to opt-out of their content being used to train Apple's foundation models.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" Apple claims that its bots respect robots.txt directives.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher partnerships:\"}),\" Apple has not publicly announced any AI content licensing deals at time of writing but has reportedly been in negotiations with publishers including Cond\\xE9 Nast and NBC News.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Amazon\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"As well as a strategic partnership with Anthropic, Amazon has developed its own 'Nova' family of AI models which emphasize speed and value.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"AmazonBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Described as being used to improve services, 'such as enabling Alexa to answer even more questions for customers'. Amazon is clear that data collected by this crawler may be used for model training.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Amzn-SearchBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Amazon's search indexing crawler, used to improve search experiences across Amazon products and services.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Amzn-User\"}),\" \\u2014 A user-triggered fetcher that supports end-user actions by fetching live web pages on the user's behalf.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" Amazon's crawlers respect robots.txt signals.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher partnerships:\"}),\" Amazon has notable AI licensing agreements with New York Times, Cond\\xE9 Nast, and Hearst.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"ByteDance\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"ByteDance \\u2014 owner of TikTok \\u2014 has developed both text and video generation models. Most of these are for research purposes although its Doubao text models power its chatbot, only available in China.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Bytespider\"}),\" \\u2014 Has been scraping the web at a high rate since it first appeared in early 2024. ByteDance has published no information on the function it serves or what the data collected is being used for.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" ByteDance has no published robots.txt policy. There are widespread reports of Bytespider ignoring robots.txt signals.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Other Notable AI User Agents\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"CCBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Common Crawl's web crawler, which collects public web pages into the Common Crawl open dataset. Common Crawl states it respects robots.txt.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"DuckAssistBot\"}),\" \\u2014 DuckDuckGo's web crawler that crawls pages in real time to source information for answers by DuckAssist. Data collected is not used to train AI models and it respects robots.txt signals.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Timpibot\"}),\" \\u2014 Web crawler for Timpi, a decentralized search index. Data is also available to AI developers for model training.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"YouBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Crawler for You.com, an AI-powered search engine that integrates AI query responses alongside conventional search links.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Diffbot\"}),\" \\u2014 Web crawler focused on extracting data from web pages which it then converts into structured datasets. Fully respects robots.txt directives.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"ProRataInc\"}),\" \\u2014 ProRata.ai's user-agent for its Gist Crawler Bot, which crawls and extracts public and licensed web content to power its Gist.ai RAG answer service. Respects robots.txt.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Ai2Bot-Dolma\"}),\" \\u2014 The Allen Institute for AI (AI2) crawler associated with building Dolma \\u2014 AI2's open, large-scale pretraining corpus.\"]}),`\n`]})]})]})}function f(s={}){let{wrapper:t}=s.components||{};return t?(0,e.jsx)(t,{...s,children:(0,e.jsx)(u,{...s})}):u(s)}function a(s,t){throw new Error(\"Expected \"+(t?\"component\":\"object\")+\" `\"+s+\"` to be defined: you likely forgot to import, pass, or provide it.\")}return T(Q);})();\n;return Component;"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"42:Td5e2,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\n\u003cSection id=\"executive-summary\"\u003e\n\n## **Executive Summary**\n\n\u003cExecSummary\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={1}\u003e\n  **The next wave of AI visitors won't look like bots or agents; they're\n  increasingly looking like humans.** The latest AI browsers like Perplexity\n  Comet, and devtools like ScrapingBee or Browserless are indistinguishable from\n  humans in site logs: Chrome appears as their user agent and they behave like\n  human users — loading pages, sometimes loading ads, and solving CAPTCHAs. This\n  is why it is mission-critical to push for a future where non-human site\n  traffic is required to self-identify.\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={2}\u003e\n  **AI bot activity now surpasses crawls from Bingbot:** AI bot aggregate\n  traffic has surpassed that of the world's second-largest search engine, Bing —\n  highlighting the growing scale of AI adoption relative to traditional search\n  engines.\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={3}\u003e\n  **Google's expansion of 'AI Overviews' in October 2024 resulted in 34.8%\n  increase in Googlebot crawls.** At the same time, the number of Google crawls\n  needed to get a human visitor got worse, increasing by 24.4%. The overall\n  increase in AI visitors puts further economic strains on sites, as their CDN\n  costs increase to support this additional bot traffic amidst declining human\n  referrals.\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={4}\u003e\n  **Human visitors to sites are falling, while AI bot traffic is rising:**\n  Between Q1 and Q2 2025, there was a 9.4% decrease in human visitors across\n  sites on TollBit. Meanwhile, AI bot/agent traffic is continuing to increase.\n  Looking at human and AI visitors across sites, at the start of Q1, 1 out of\n  every 200 visitors was AI. Now it is 1 in every 50, reflecting a 4x increase\n  in the relative volume of AI visitors.\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={5}\u003e\n  **Over the past year, there has been a 336% increase in sites blocking or\n  redirecting AI bots/agents,** as evidenced by redirections and HTTP forbidden\n  errors served to AI bots. This is a result of bot detection solutions being\n  deployed more extensively across publisher sites. This corresponds with hits\n  to the TollBit Bot/Agent Paywall, which have increased around 360% from Q1 to\n  Q2 2025 as websites have increasingly taken active measures to charge AI\n  traffic.\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={6}\u003e\n  **Across all AI bots, 13.26% of requests bypassed robots.txt in Q2 2025,** up\n  4x from just 3.3% in Q4 2024.\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={7}\u003e\n**There is evidence that AI apps store \u0026 re-use content, but it appears it's not always efficient for them to cache data for long periods.** This highlights the importance of RAG and continued content access. Here's a breakdown of what that caching pattern looks like based on our tests:\n\n- Claude: cached for 16+ days\\*\n- ChatGPT: cached for 30 minutes\n- Gemini: cached for 15 minutes\n\n_Our testing ended after this time period._\n\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003c/ExecSummary\u003e\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n\n---\n\n\u003cSection id=\"section-1\"\u003e\n\n## **Section 1: Rise of the Agentic Web**\n\n\u003cUserStory\u003e\n  This quarter saw the release of additional AI web browsers and new consumer\n  agentic applications. As adoption grows, these look set to radically change\n  the shape of web traffic with profound consequences for digital business\n  models. New AI applications and capabilities are increasingly being powered by\n  'headless' browsers; these are web browsers that are controlled by automated\n  systems. Headless browsers are a more interactive form of web scraping that\n  enables AI agents to take actions on behalf of users, including accessing\n  dynamic websites, adding items to their cart, or booking a table. There is no\n  human user controlling the browser, yet the traffic looks like a human visitor\n  — despite being an agent. TollBit data suggests that human visitors to\n  websites are beginning to decline, with bots supplanting them.\n\u003c/UserStory\u003e\n\nThe second quarter of 2025 saw the mainstream or beta releases of AI web browsers such as OpenAI Agent Mode, Perplexity Comet, Google's Project Mariner. Fortune 1000 companies also turn to platforms like MindStudio or n8n to build agentic systems. These new technologies — which further extend the capabilities of automated systems to gather information and perform actions on our behalf — foreshadow a transformative change in how consumers engage with the online world.\n\nWhile these changes are still nascent, and the signals in the data are only beginning to emerge, we can already see the contours of a future in which interactions with websites look radically different. This shift means traffic will present itself to publishers in different ways with direct implications for revenue, raising fundamental questions about the relationships between website owners, users, and the developers of AI systems that increasingly occupy an intermediary role.\n\n### Headless Browsers and Agentic Systems\n\nSince the release of chatbots that can access the real-time internet, AI applications have needed systems that interface with websites, retrieving and synthesizing content on behalf of users when required by a prompt. In the first wave of AI this function was served by purpose-built crawlers operated by the AI developer itself. These bots declared themselves via user agent strings such as ChatGPT-User and PerplexityBot. Much of the analysis in TollBit's _State of the Bots_ reports has focused on these technologies.\n\nThis landscape is now changing. New AI interfaces and capabilities (chiefly agentic systems and AI browsers) and the widespread adoption of publisher IP controls have led developers to turn to the use of 'headless browsers', often operated by third parties. These are web browsers that are programmatically controlled via automated systems, without a human doing the browsing — hence the term, \"headless.\" This tech has been available for many years, but until recently, was almost exclusively used for enterprise functions, such as website performance testing or QA automation.\n\n**Three types of AI headless browsers:**\n\n- **Masked web agents:** Infrastructure providers allow an AI developer's agent to access websites through headless browsers at scale. Think of this as renting browsers in the cloud, each controlled by the AI developer's agent. These visitors appear to publishers as indistinguishable from ordinary human traffic. Providers of this infrastructure include Browserbase and Hyperbrowser.\n\n- **Third-party web agents:** These services (e.g. BrightData, Apify, Zyte) act as outsourced crawlers. Instead of the AI agent itself opening a browser session, the agent calls an API, and the third-party service performs the crawl. Part of the value prop of these services is their ability to defeat anti-bot tools to scrape high-value sites.\n\n- **Browser-driven web agents:** With the release of the AI-first web browsers — _Comet_ from Perplexity and _Dia_ from The Browser Company — come agents that operate directly inside a consumer-facing browser. From a publisher's perspective, these agents are indistinguishable from normal human browsing.\n\n**Figure 1.1. Perplexity's Comet browser**\n\n\u003cImage src=\"/bots/25q2/image1.png\" alt=\"Perplexity Comet browser\" /\u003e\n\nHeadless browsers are being used because they provide a more reliable way to automate web access for AI developers. They execute JavaScript, maintain sessions, and mimic normal user behaviour, enabling agents to complete tasks that simple HTTP-based scrapers cannot. In practice, they can also bypass common bot-mitigation measures.\n\nIn the case of Perplexity Comet, the agent appears to use the user's own desktop to request webpages when automating workflows. This makes the requests appear to come from a user's residential IP address, which can confuse CDN companies into thinking it is legitimate. Comet is also built on the open source Chromium project, which powers Chrome, so the requests also pass along Chrome user agent strings.\n\nThese practices create a new technical challenge for publishers. Some headless browser services can evade detection tools and even solve CAPTCHAs (tests intended to distinguish humans from bots), making automated sessions hard to separate from genuine human visitors.\n\n### Future Traffic Patterns\n\nWhether a publisher, an ecommerce or an aggregator site, their advertising and subscription revenues depend upon humans engaging with content on properties. When AI systems scrape and summarize (or browse and checkout), they potentially negate the need for a user to visit the site. This corresponds to a lost monetization opportunity for the content creator.\n\nTollBit data suggests that human traffic to publisher websites is beginning to decline, with bots supplanting it. As the use of headless browsers (or advanced web scrapers) grows, this trend is expected to falsely appear to reverse, with website owners seeing a growth in visitors that present as humans but are in fact agents serving an AI application. These faux human visitors will erode advertiser trust and cannot be monetized with traditional methods.\n\n**Figure 1.2. Projected future traffic composition**\n\n\u003cImage src=\"/bots/25q2/image1-1.png\" alt=\"Projected future traffic\" /\u003e\n\nLooking further to the future, as agentic systems grow in complexity and sophistication (which in turn will rely on improvements in reliability and speed), this is likely to result in a dramatic expansion in this faux human traffic — far exceeding the levels seen today — with multiple agents serving a single user simultaneously; many likely to be operating autonomously and without the need for a user-trigger.\n\n### Agent-to-Web Protocols, Distinguishing Humans from Bots\n\nIronically, to maintain the integrity of the open web, it may need to be that a parallel 'agent web' will emerge, optimized for non-human actors. This would negate the need for headless browsers to engage with human websites with all the inefficiencies that entails. This is certainly a possibility with emerging approaches such as the Model Context Protocol (MCP), structured data standards like Schema.org, and TollBit's own agent gateway, creating the means of discrete agent-to-site interfaces.\n\nShould agent-to-site protocols become the norm, it would simplify the process of distinguishing between humans and non-humans; the onus of figuring out \"bot or not\" would no longer be an unfair burden placed on website owners in a web that is increasingly trafficked by autonomous visitors.\n\n### Regulation and Policy\n\nAs this section of the report has described, the rise of headless browsers and the blurring of human and non-human traffic pose clear risks for all website owners. For some publishers, value is being eroded by intellectual property leakage. For other sites, resources are diverted into costly detection, mitigation or compute costs. At the same time, these practices by AI developers are stalling the emergence of a fair and liquid content access market.\n\nWhile we can already see distinctions between agentic systems, agentic automated systems, user-directed AI browsers, automated AI browsers, etc., it is clear that this taxonomy will evolve, and edge cases that blur the human-bot boundary will continue to appear.\n\nIt is our view at TollBit that this is where regulatory intervention is needed. As a minimum, automated agents should be mandated to identify themselves. There is no legitimate justification for any autonomous visitor on the web to present itself as human; this should be prohibited, requiring disclosure/identification embedded in user agent strings or elsewhere in the HTTP headers.\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n\n---\n\n\u003cSection id=\"section-2\"\u003e\n\n## **Section 2: Scale of AI Scraping**\n\n\u003cUserStory\u003e\n  Traffic from AI bots continue to grow strongly both in absolute terms and as a\n  proportion of overall traffic. Out of human \u0026 AI visitor totals, we now see 1\n  out of every 50 visitors to a site is an AI visitor. It was 1 out of every 200\n  visitors to a site in Q1. Alongside this growth in AI traffic, the number of\n  human visitors to websites is beginning to fall, TollBit data saw a 9.4%\n  reduction between Q1 and Q2 2025. This suggests that human visitors to\n  websites are being replaced by AI bots, operating on their behalf. In\n  aggregate AI bot requests have now surpassed those from Bing, the world's\n  second-largest search engine that currently accounts for around 4% of the\n  market.\n\u003c/UserStory\u003e\n\n### 2.1 Aggregate Scraping Levels\n\nOver the second quarter of 2025, website traffic from AI bots has continued to grow strongly. When we examine the daily volume of AI bot requests per TollBit partner website since the start of the year we can see a sharp increase, with, on some days, the level exceeding 300% what it was at the beginning of 2025.\n\n**Figure 2.1. Daily per website AI bot scraping level, percent vs 1/1/2025**\n\n\u003cImage src=\"/bots/25q2/image2.svg\" alt=\"Daily AI bot scraping levels\" /\u003e\n\nAI bot traffic also continues to grow as a proportion of all web traffic. Looking at human and AI visitors across sites, at the start of 2025, 1 out of every 200 visitors was AI. Now it is 1 in every 50, reflecting a 4x increase in the relative volume of AI visitors.\n\n**Figure 2.2. AI to human ratio, total requests**\n\n\u003cImage src=\"/bots/25q2/image3.svg\" alt=\"AI to human ratio\" /\u003e\n\nTwo factors are driving this change in publisher traffic composition. Alongside the growth in AI bot traffic we are now beginning to see human visitors decline. TollBit data saw a 9.4% drop in human requests between Q1 and Q2 2025. Put together with the AI bot traffic growth, these data suggest that substitution could be taking place — human visitors to websites being replaced by AI user agents which are operating on their behalf whether that's through a chatbot or an AI search interface. Unless publishers can monetize this AI traffic, it might result in lost revenue.\n\n**Figure 2.3. Human vs AI bot requests, per website (log scale)**\n\n\u003cImage src=\"/bots/25q2/image4.svg\" alt=\"Human vs AI bot requests\" /\u003e\n\n### 2.2 Scraping Levels per AI Bot Category\n\nThis substitutional effect may be driven by consumers using AI applications for information retrieval, likely needs that would previously have been satisfied by the use of a search engine followed by a human visiting a publisher website. When AI applications serve these needs, they often access the web in real-time, using a retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) agent to visit and scrape websites to formulate a response. TollBit data shows that website visits from these AI RAG agents are growing the fastest.\n\nThese increases in AI Bot traffic hammering sites are concerning for publishers, not only due to the potential loss in human visitors but also because this is driving up their website/CDN costs in order to support this additional traffic. AI tools often \"read\", aka use \u0026 cite more content than a human would to answer a question.\n\n**Figure 2.4. AI bot traffic by user-agent type (average, per domain)**\n\n\u003cImage src=\"/bots/25q2/image5.svg\" alt=\"AI bot traffic by user-agent type\" /\u003e\n\n### 2.3 AI and Online Search\n\nWhen we compare the level of scraping from these new AI bots to those from the web crawlers operated by conventional search engines, we can see that, in aggregate, AI requests per website have now surpassed those from Bing, the world's second largest search engine, currently holding around 4% of the market.\n\n**Figure 2.5. Requests per site AI bot vs conventional search bots**\n\n\u003cImage src=\"/bots/25q2/image6.png\" alt=\"AI bot vs search bots\" /\u003e\n\nIn response to the growing use of AI chatbots for information retrieval, Google has been introducing AI features into its core web search product. This started with a trial of 'search generative experience' back in May 2023. This feature turned into 'AI Overviews' which were piloted, expanded to all US users then rolled-out extensively — to over 100 countries — in October 2024.\n\nThis global expansion of AI Overviews coincided with a very substantial increase in the level of requests from Google's crawler on TollBit partner websites. The daily average crawls for the cohort of websites increased by 34.8% after the rollout. This data strongly suggests that Google still needs to visit websites in real-time to power its AI search features.\n\n**Figure 2.6. Googlebot requests and the global rollout of AI Overviews (0 = 10/28/2024)**\n\n\u003cImage src=\"/bots/25q2/image7.png\" alt=\"Googlebot requests and AI Overviews\" /\u003e\n\n**A new user agent from Google?**\n\nWe saw ~45M requests across our publisher network from a user agent string 'Google' and some IP addresses associated with Google. We observe this user agent appearing in server logs when we use Gemini to search the website. However, Google's documentation at the time of publishing this report doesn't mention this crawler.\n\nWhile this IP is not listed in Google's published IP ranges, a reverse DNS lookup confirmed the IP addresses were Google, as per Google's verification guidelines.\n\nWe first observed requests from 'Google' user agent in our logs in April 2024, followed by a noticeable uptick in November 2024 and a significant rise in average monthly visits starting April 2025. These increases align with the launch of the Gemini iPhone app in November 2024 and the rollout of AI Mode and Deep Search features in May 2025.\n\n**Figure 2.7. Daily Average Crawls from 'Google' user agent**\n\n\u003cImage src=\"/bots/25q2/image8.svg\" alt=\"Google user agent crawls\" /\u003e\n\n### 2.4 User Agent Scraping Levels\n\nWhen we examine the level of scraping by individual AI bots, we can observe a notable increase in activity from OpenAI's web crawlers, both chatgpt-user — its RAG agent — which has increased by 100% on a per-website basis, and gptbot — its training data crawler — which has grown by 252%. Notably, aggregate scrapes from Perplexity's user agents (PerplexityBot and Perplexity-User, introduced earlier this year) have decreased by 19% over the quarter. This could be due to alternative data acquisition methods, like third-party web scrapers or disguised user agents.\n\n**Figure 2.8. Monthly Average Scrapes per site by AI Bots — All sites from Q4 '24 vs Q1 '25**\n\n\u003cImage src=\"/bots/25q2/image9.svg\" alt=\"Monthly average scrapes by AI bots\" /\u003e\n\nLooking specifically at patterns of activity from training data crawlers, we see periods of intense scraping activity from a particular user agent, followed by relative dormancy. This is likely to be driven by the need to collect data for a specific model training run. In the period from late May to the end of June we saw particularly intensive scraping from gptbot.\n\n**Figure 2.8. Training data crawlers, user agent composition**\n\n\u003cImage src=\"/bots/25q2/image10.svg\" alt=\"Training data crawlers composition\" /\u003e\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n\n---\n\n\u003cSection id=\"section-3\"\u003e\n\n## **Section 3: AI Demand for Content**\n\n\u003cUserStory\u003e\n  Certain categories of content are subject to markedly higher levels of AI\n  visitors: Relative to the level of human traffic, B2B/professional, sport,\n  parenting and consumer technology content receive the highest level of AI\n  scraping, suggesting these are central (publisher-disruptive) AI use cases. AI\n  requests to parenting content (+333%) and deals and shopping content (+111%)\n  are growing the fastest across TollBit sites. This is likely to indicate new\n  or growing consumer AI use. National news content is subject to 5x the number\n  of RAG requests than those from training crawlers; this content is\n  (unsurprisingly) needed in real-time to respond to prompts.\n\u003c/UserStory\u003e\n\n### 3.1 AI Scraping Levels by Site Category\n\nAI demand for content is unevenly distributed across TollBit partner websites, with certain categories of content subject to markedly higher levels of scraping. When we examine AI bot traffic relative to the level of human traffic — a measure of the relative AI demand for any given content category — we see B2B/professional content receiving the highest level of AI scraping, followed by parenting, sport, consumer technology, and health and wellness.\n\nThis AI-to-human ratio is likely to be an indication of where AI applications present a substitution risk to publisher content; a higher level (particularly if requests are from RAG agents) suggest that AI tools are being used in place of users visiting a publisher website. For example, parenting content was subject to one AI scrape per 67 human visitors in Q1 2025, which has risen to one AI scrape per 23 human visitors in Q2, potentially indicating that users are directing their parenting queries at AI applications instead of visiting publisher websites.\n\n**Figure 3.1. AI scrapes for every 1,000 human requests, Q2 2025**\n\n\u003cImage src=\"/bots/25q2/image11.svg\" alt=\"AI scrapes per human requests\" /\u003e\n\n### 3.2 Growth in AI Scraping by Site Category\n\nWhilst AI requests are growing across almost all content categories, this rate of growth varies substantially. When analysing a cohort of websites that joined the TollBit platform in 2024 we can see that AI requests to parenting content, for example, rose by 333%. Deals and shopping is another outlier, with a +111% increase. These are likely to indicate new or growing AI use-cases with consumers turning to AI to satisfy needs that previously were fulfilled elsewhere.\n\n**Figure 3.2. Percent increase in average scrapes per page — Q1 to Q2 2025**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/bots/25q2/image12.svg\"\n  alt=\"Percent increase in scrapes per page\"\n/\u003e\n\nFurther evidence on these potential shifts in consumer behaviour can be found when comparing changes in human traffic and AI traffic for a given content category. For lifestyle content, human requests declined by 45% whilst those from AI increased by 43%. This suggests that users are turning to AI in place of publisher websites directly. A similar pattern is observed in relation to national news content (AI traffic up 65% and human traffic down 10%). Even where human traffic is growing, this growth rate across many categories is substantially exceeded by the growth in traffic from AI bots (consumer technology, special interest, deals \u0026 shopping, sport, health \u0026 wellness).\n\n**Figure 3.3. Changes in human / AI requests, Q1 vs Q2**\n\n\u003cImage src=\"/bots/25q2/image13.svg\" alt=\"Changes in human vs AI requests\" /\u003e\n\n### 3.3 Real-time/RAG versus Training\n\nComparing the level of demand by RAG bots with that from training bots provides an indication of the types of content that are fetched in real-time.\n\nUnsurprisingly, given the time-sensitive nature of news content, we see almost 5 RAG scrapes per request from a training data collection bot on national news sites.\n\n**Figure 3.4. RAG vs training scrapes, site category, Q2 2025**\n\n\u003cImage src=\"/bots/25q2/image14.png\" alt=\"RAG vs training scrapes\" /\u003e\n\n### 3.4 Scraping Levels Across Jurisdictions\n\nTollBit's footprint of media partners is growing globally. As a result, it is now possible to analyze the level of AI scraping across jurisdictions to identify any patterns or discrepancies. Doing so reveals that APAC websites are, by far, the most scraped domains, particularly by training data crawlers. On average APAC sites received over three times the number of requests in the quarter compared to US sites. One of the drivers for this is likely to be the use of the Robots Exclusion Protocol to disallow access; we find that only 13.5% of APAC sites have disallowed GPTBot (OpenAI's training data crawler), versus 52.5% in the US and 45.8% in Europe (a category which includes the UK).\n\nIt is notable that scraping levels for European websites are lower, with these receiving 27% fewer AI requests than those for US sites owned.\n\n**Figure 3.5. Average scraping levels by website region / jurisdiction, Q2 2025**\n\n\u003cImage src=\"/bots/25q2/image15.svg\" alt=\"Scraping levels by region\" /\u003e\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n\n---\n\n\u003cSection id=\"section-4\"\u003e\n\n## **Section 4: Referral Traffic**\n\n\u003cUserStory\u003e\n  Google referrals are now in decline, both in absolute terms and in terms of\n  share. They have dropped from over 90% of all external visitors in Q2 2024 to\n  84.1% in the same quarter of the following year. AI applications continue to\n  be a tiny source of publisher referrals — 0.102% of referrals in Q2 2025 came\n  from AI apps. Google still delivers 831 human visitors for every human visitor\n  that comes from an AI system. The click-through rate from AI applications\n  remains extremely small; over 91% lower than for the average of the top-10\n  positions on organic search. Referral rates from ChatGPT are markedly stronger\n  for publishers with OpenAI licensing agreements in place.\n\u003c/UserStory\u003e\n\n### 4.1 Aggregate Referrals from AI Applications\n\nWhilst referrals from AI increased from 0.042% of all referrers in Q1 to 0.102% in Q2, this still represents only a tiny fraction of overall traffic to publisher websites with Google delivering 831 visitors for every single visitor from an AI application.\n\n**Figure 4.1. Overall referrals, Q2 2025**\n\n\u003cImage src=\"/bots/25q2/image16.svg\" alt=\"Overall referrals Q2 2025\" /\u003e\n\nGoogle remains the dominant source of external traffic; however, TollBit data indicates this is now in decline on both a relative and absolute basis. When we examine referrals from a cohort of sites that joined TollBit prior to July 2024, we find that visits from Google are down 9.17% between July 2024 and June 2025.\n\n100 crawls from Googlebot resulted in 454 referrals in Q2 2024, yet one year later, in Q2 2025, 100 crawls only resulted in 312 referrals. The same number of crawls now yields 142 fewer referrals.\n\nWhen we analyze Google's share of all external referrals to TollBit partner sites, these have also dropped from over 90% in Q2 2024 to 84.1% in the same quarter of the following year. This decline in traffic from Google is taking place when crawls from Googlebot (its combined user agent) is increasing, and markedly so since the introduction of AI Overviews (see figure 2.6).\n\n**Figure 4.2. Composition of referrals over time**\n\n\u003cImage src=\"/bots/25q2/image17.svg\" alt=\"Composition of referrals over time\" /\u003e\n\n### 4.2 Comparing Referral Rates\n\nWhilst in aggregate publishers are seeing growth in the number of visitors from AI applications, the click-through rate from these interfaces have not meaningfully improved. Whereas Google's organic (non-AI) search delivers, on average across the top-10 results positions, a click 8.6% of the time, for AI applications, this figure remains below 1%. On average it requires 135 real-time scrapes from an AI application for a single visitor to arrive.\n\n**Figure 4.3. AI application click-through rates**\n\n\u003cImage src=\"/bots/25q2/image18.svg\" alt=\"AI application click-through rates\" /\u003e\n\nAlthough the total number of human visitors coming from AI sources remains negligible, it's interesting to note that the distribution of these referrals differs from Google.\n\nOf the few referrals that AI platforms send, the breakdown of referrals by category is quite different from Google search: For example, 45% of AI apps' total referrals are being sent to National News. While across the same set of sites, only 22% of Google's referrals are being sent to National News.\n\nTwo factors may explain this difference:\n\n- Different click-through patterns — AI systems may produce a different rate of click-throughs for similar queries versus Google.\n- Different starting points for information needs — users may be disproportionately turning to AI tools, rather than Google, for certain categories of queries.\n\n**Figure 4.4.1. Google's search referral distributions**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/bots/25q2/image19.svg\"\n  alt=\"Google search referral distributions\"\n/\u003e\n\n**Figure 4.4.2. AI applications' referral distribution**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/bots/25q2/image20.svg\"\n  alt=\"AI applications referral distribution\"\n/\u003e\n\nBy examining the RAG scrape to referral ratio, we can test whether AI applications are more likely to satisfy specific categories of user need and thus depress the click-through rates for that content. This analysis shows that consumer technology, special interest and lifestyle receive far fewer referrals per scrape (likely indicating that AI answers are likely to be sufficient and further information that would lead to a human click-through is not required). Conversely, B2B/professional and entertainment and pop culture content delivers, in proportion, substantially more clicks than scrapes, indicating the opposite.\n\n**Figure 4.5. Scrape-to-Referral Ratio, Q2 2025**\n\n\u003cImage src=\"/bots/25q2/image21.svg\" alt=\"Scrape-to-Referral Ratio Q2 2025\" /\u003e\n\n### 4.3 Shifting Google Value Exchange\n\nAs well as corresponding to an increase in the number of requests to publisher websites (see section 2.3) from its crawler, Google's expansion of AI Overviews in October 2024 also resulted in an increase in the crawl-to-referral ratio by 24.45%. Googlebot is used for a multitude of purposes and Google makes extensive use of offline caches of content so this shift is directional, rather than truly indicative of the number of uses of content that result in traffic delivered. However, it does strongly suggest there is a change to the value exchange, with fewer referrals delivered for each time Google accesses website content.\n\n**Figure 4.6. Scrape-to-referral ratio (Googlebot) after global rollout of AI Overviews**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/bots/25q2/image22.png\"\n  alt=\"Scrape-to-referral ratio after AI Overviews\"\n/\u003e\n\n### 4.4 Effect of Licensing Deals on AI Usage and Referrals\n\nOpenAI has been establishing new partnerships with publishers since it made its first licensing deal with Associated Press in July 2023. At the time of writing, it has almost 40 similar agreements in place with publishers. While limited public information on these deals is available, we have analysed TollBit data to understand whether having a deal corresponds to more scraping or more referrals from ChatGPT.\n\nIn doing so, we find that in the last quarter, publishers with deals saw 88% more scraping on a per-page basis than those without agreements. This rose from a difference of just 10% in Q1 2025.\n\n**Figure 4.7. ChatGPT-User, scrapes per page**\n\n\u003cImage src=\"/bots/25q2/image23.svg\" alt=\"ChatGPT-User scrapes per page\" /\u003e\n\nReferrals, on the other hand, are markedly stronger for publishers with licensing agreements in place. In Q1 2025 the click-through rate was comparable to the average of the top-10 organic search positions. AI companies may be placing logos and names of publishers they have done deals with front and center in AI app citation lists. However, the referrals have dropped in Q2 likely as the number of deals expands and the positions with greater prominence are shared amongst a greater number of publishers.\n\n**Figure 4.8. OpenAI click-through rates**\n\n\u003cImage src=\"/bots/25q2/image24.svg\" alt=\"OpenAI click-through rates\" /\u003e\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n\n---\n\n\u003cSection id=\"section-5\"\u003e\n\n## **Section 5: IP Controls and Errors**\n\n\u003cUserStory\u003e\n  We can observe a growing number of forbidden server errors and redirections\n  being served to AI visitors — likely as a result of bot detection solutions\n  being deployed more extensively across publisher sites. Publishers continue to\n  increase their use of robots.txt to signal that AI bots are not permitted to\n  scrape their content. AI crawlers still routinely ignore website robots.txt\n  signals. Crawlers from ByteDance, OpenAI, Meta, Perplexity, and Amazon all\n  bypass publisher signals at times. Across all AI bots, 13.26% of requests\n  bypassed robots.txt in Q2 2025, this has risen from just 3.3% in Q4 2024.\n\u003c/UserStory\u003e\n\n### 5.1 Server Responses — HTTP 404 Error Rates\n\nEach time a request to a website takes place, the site's server responds with a code which indicates the status of the response, for example, whether the request was successful, blocked or was towards a page that doesn't exist. Monitoring the distribution of these codes — and how they change over time — can provide some insight on the nature of the requests coming from AI bots and how website owners are handling them.\n\n404 errors indicate that the page requested could not be found. In the case of search engines, this is often caused by a broken link or the deletion of a page. AI applications additionally may generate 404 errors by hallucinating URLs, directing users to pages that never existed. Two notable changes in 404 error rates from AI applications have occurred over the last quarter. Firstly, the data indicates a marked increase in OpenAI's figures, rising from 0.3% of responses in Q1 to 3.7% in Q2. Meanwhile, Anthropic's 404 error rate has dropped dramatically from 55% in Q2 2024 (meaning most of the pages requested by users were not found) to 4.8% in Q2 this year. The timing of this improvement corresponds with Claude — Anthropic's chatbot — being given access to the real-time web, allowing it to retrieve and cite live URLs rather than relying on extrapolations from training data.\n\n**Figure 5.1. HTTP 404 error rates, 2024 Q2 to 2025 Q2**\n\n\u003cImage src=\"/bots/25q2/image25.svg\" alt=\"HTTP 404 error rates\" /\u003e\n\n### 5.2 Server Responses — Blocking or Redirecting Error Rates\n\nOver the last year, 302, 402, 403, and 401 response statuses have increased by almost 4x on TollBit partner websites, likely due to publishers deploying security measures (bot detection/WAF) that prevent non-human visitors such as AI bots from accessing content. TollBit provides basic bot enforcement and partners with various cybersecurity companies, including Fastly, DataDome, and HUMAN Security to provide advanced bot blocking options for sites.\n\n**Figure 5.2. All domain quarterly changes in 403 errors**\n\n\u003cImage src=\"/bots/25q2/image26.png\" alt=\"Quarterly changes in 403 errors\" /\u003e\n\n### 5.3 Publisher Use of Disallow Signals\n\nPublishers continue to extend their use of the Robots Exclusion Protocol (AKA 'robots.txt') to signal that AI bots are not permitted to scrape their content. While most publishers implemented changes on the most widely known bots prior to 2025, we can observe a growth in disallow requests as new AI scrapers are released and become known. For example: As of June 2025, 81% of the cohort of sites are blocking CCbot and 73% block GPTBot.\n\n**Figure 5.3. Total AI bot disallow requests**\n\n\u003cImage src=\"/bots/25q2/image27.svg\" alt=\"Total AI bot disallow requests\" /\u003e\n\n**Figure 5.4. Disallowed Bots via Robots.txt, cohort of TollBit partner websites**\n\n\u003cImage src=\"/bots/25q2/image28.svg\" alt=\"Disallowed Bots via Robots.txt\" /\u003e\n\n### 5.4 Robots.txt Compliance\n\nTollBit data shows that many AI crawlers still appear to ignore these publisher robots.txt signals, scraping content even when explicitly requested not to. This behavior varies significantly by bot, with crawlers from ByteDance, OpenAI, Meta, Perplexity and Amazon all bypassing publisher signals at times. Across all AI bots, 13.26% of requests bypassed robots.txt in Q2 2025, this has risen from just 3.3% in Q4 2024.\n\n**Figure 5.5. Requests that bypass robots.txt signals, percent Q2 2025**\n\n\u003cImage src=\"/bots/25q2/image29.svg\" alt=\"Requests that bypass robots.txt\" /\u003e\n\n### 5.5 Redirects to Bot Paywall\n\nThe TollBit platform works with websites' existing tech stacks to redirect bots which are not permitted to access a given site or page to a bot paywall with the price for access set by the publisher. Hits to these paywalls have been growing steeply (360% from Q1 to Q2 2025) as publisher partners deploy this functionality. This delivers an HTTP 402 — Payment Required to the AI bot or agent.\n\n**Figure 5.6. Hits to TollBit bot paywall**\n\n\u003cImage src=\"/bots/25q2/image30.png\" alt=\"Hits to TollBit bot paywall\" /\u003e\n\n_Note: 302 = temporary redirect; 401 = not authenticated; 402 = payment required; 403 = access forbidden/blocked_\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n\n---\n\n\u003cSection id=\"section-6\"\u003e\n\n## **Section 6: Future Trajectory of AI Bot Traffic**\n\n\u003cUserStory\u003e\n  There is evidence that AI apps store \u0026 re-use content, but it appears it's not\n  always optimal for them to cache data for long periods. This highlights the\n  importance of RAG and continued content access. Here's a breakdown of what\n  that caching pattern looks like based on our tests: ChatGPT: cached for 30\n  minutes across user accounts. Gemini: cached for 15 minutes at a user account\n  level. Claude: cached for 16+ days across user accounts.\n\u003c/UserStory\u003e\n\nAI demand for professional content is only set to grow as this technology is deployed in new applications and consumer adoption continues to accelerate. However, identifying this demand by observing the activity on the publisher servers will become harder as AI developers increasingly store and reuse content by making use of offline caches of the content. The TollBit team has been running experiments to understand — and explain — each of the top AI bots usage of caching for our _State of the Bots_ report readers.\n\n**How We Set Up the Experiments**\n\nTo understand how AI applications retrieve web content — and whether they rely on live scraping or cached (aka stored) data — we ran a series of structured experiments across four AI applications: ChatGPT, Gemini, Claude, and Perplexity.\n\n**The Test Website**\n\nWe created one-page websites that showed a jumbled sequence of letters. This \"code\" changes every second and on every load or refresh. Since the site shows a unique code that changes every second, the code returned by the AI application tells us when it accessed the page. If an AI model responded with a code that matched the exact time a prompt was sent, it indicated real-time scraping. If it returned a code from an earlier time period, it suggested the application had cached/stored the content from a previous request.\n\n- The test website did not block any bots in the robots.txt file.\n- The test website wasn't submitted to Google Search Console, so it couldn't be pre-cached.\n\nRepeating this process across hundreds of queries and cross-referencing with server logs let us map cache refresh intervals, identify cross-user caching, and observe whether models behaved consistently across different prompt styles.\n\nWe used the following setup for each AI application/chatbot:\n\n- Ran between 200 to 500 prompts\n- Queried each application using two separate user accounts to test for cross-user caching.\n- Prompt style was designed to trigger RAG behavior. Example: \"Find the latest code displayed on [URL].\"\n- Prompts were spaced at varying time intervals — from a few seconds to a few hours — and made from different user accounts to test both time-based and user-based cache behavior.\n- We cross-referenced each prompt with server logs to verify whether and when the page was accessed by the chatbot's respective AI bots.\n\n### 6.1 OpenAI ChatGPT\n\n**What we tested**\n\nWe prompted ChatGPT (GPT-5 and o4-mini-high) over 500 times using multiple OpenAI accounts.\n\n**What we observed**\n\n- **Cache duration:** The cache duration was approximately 30 minutes. During 30-minute intervals, ChatGPT returned the same cached code regardless of prompt time or user account.\n- **Cross-user caching:** ChatGPT consistently cached content across users, returning the same code even when queried minutes apart from different accounts.\n- **Scraping time:** Real-time scraping occurred only after cache expiry. Once the cache expired, the respective AI bot(s) fetched a fresh version of the code at the time of the prompt.\n- **Citation:** The webpage was cited as a source in the response. Even when the content was cached and not freshly scraped, ChatGPT displayed the website in the citations provided to the user.\n\n**What showed up in our logs**\n\n- **User agent:** `Mozilla/5.0 AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko); compatible; ChatGPT-User/1.0; +https://openai.com/bot`\n- **IP Address:** IP addresses used were part of OpenAI's public list and registered to Microsoft Limited\n- **Additional scraping activity:** We observed OpenAI's SearchBot (OAI-searchbot) accessing /robots.txt multiple times, outside active test windows. This suggests background scanning unrelated to prompt timing.\n\n### 6.2 Google Gemini\n\n**What we tested**\n\nWe prompted Gemini 2.5 Pro over 200 times using three different Google accounts.\n\n**What we observed**\n\n- **Cache duration:** Cache duration was approximately 15 minutes. During 15-minute intervals, repeat prompts returned the same code — the page was not scraped again.\n- **User-level caching:** Caching occurred at the individual user level. Each Google account saw its own cached result. The cache was not shared across users.\n- **Scraping time:** Real-time scraping occurred only after cache expiry. Once the cache expired, the respective AI bot(s) fetched a fresh version of the code at the time of the prompt.\n- **Citation:** Gemini included the test page as a citation, even when the response was served from cache.\n- **Other model behavior:** Gemini 2.5 Flash cache duration did not differ from Gemini 2.5 Pro\n\n**What showed up in our logs**\n\n- **User agent:** `Google` (Note: This user agent string is not listed in Google's official documentation but was consistently used during Gemini's scraping activity.)\n- **IP Address:** Although this IP address is not listed in Google's published IP ranges, a reverse DNS lookup confirmed that it was associated with Google's IP addresses, as per Google's verification guidelines.\n\n### 6.3 Anthropic Claude\n\n**What we tested**\n\nWe prompted Claude (Sonnet 4 and Opus 4.1) over 200 times using multiple Anthropic accounts.\n\n**What we observed**\n\n- **Cache duration:** Even after 16 days of testing and hundreds of queries, Claude continued to return the same stale cached content.\n- **Cross-user caching:** Claude cached responses across users and across time.\n- **Scraping time:** The model never scraped the test page again after the initial prompt, regardless of changes in time gaps between prompts.\n- **Citation:** Claude included the test page as a citation, even when serving cached responses.\n- **Other model behavior:** Both Claude Sonnet 4 and Claude Opus 4.1 showed identical behavior throughout testing.\n\n**What showed up in our logs**\n\n- **User agent:** `Mozilla/5.0 AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko; compatible; Claude-User/1.0; [email withheld by Lighthouse])`\n- **IP Address:** Anthropic doesn't publish IP addresses it uses. The IP addresses were registered to Google Cloud.\n- **Additional scraping activity:** Claude-User checked the /robots.txt page before scraping the website.\n\n_Note: These AI tools are evolving rapidly. The caching behaviors and bot activity we observed may change over time, and results may differ based on prompt wording, account setup, or system updates._\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n\n---\n\n\u003cSection id=\"appendix\"\u003e\n\n## **Appendix: AI User Agent Profiles**\n\nAs our State of the Bots reports chart, the internet is crawled by an ever-growing number of AI user agents. These have different functions for the applications they serve and consequently, behave differently online. Here is a quick overview of the principal forms of AI user agent, what they do, how they work and why they matter to publishers.\n\n### Categories of AI User Agent\n\n**1. Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) Agent**\n\nThese bots retrieve information in real-time to respond to user prompts. They use an index of the web, gathered by an indexing crawler (either proprietary or third-party such as Bing or Google) to locate the relevant content which is then retrieved and synthesized into a response.\n\n_Publisher effects:_ When a RAG agent accesses a site, it's using that content to provide a response to a user's prompt, typically in an AI chatbot (e.g. ChatGPT) or AI search (e.g. Perplexity) application. This might be substitutional to a human visitor.\n\n**2. Training Data Crawling Agent**\n\nLarge language models — such as Llama from Meta or GPT-5 from OpenAI, both of which power a multitude of consumer applications — are trained on vast quantities of data. Training data crawlers move around the web — following links from websites to websites or working through sitemaps — downloading content which is then processed and stored for offline use.\n\n_Publisher effects:_ The inclusion of content in the training data means that a model has this 'knowledge' to answer prompts. Training data typically cuts off several months prior to model release so the substitutional risk is limited for content with a longer shelf-life.\n\n**3. AI Search Indexing Agent**\n\nAI systems with access to the real-time web need an index of the internet. This is used to direct RAG agents to the right sources when collecting data needed for responses to prompts. AI search indexing crawlers build these indexes by systematically navigating the web, collecting and organizing content and metadata.\n\n_Publisher effects:_ These crawlers only ensure that a website's content can be navigated to by a RAG agent, should a relevant prompt require that.\n\n**4. Hybrid Agent**\n\nSome AI developers use a single bot (or at least software which identifies itself with the same user agent metadata) for more than one of these purposes. For example, PerplexityBot appears to act both as an indexing crawler and a RAG agent.\n\n_Publisher effects:_ This makes it hard for publishers to apply granular controls to the access and use of their IP by AI applications.\n\n---\n\n### AI Bot Profiles by Operating Organization\n\n**OpenAI**\n\nDeveloper of ChatGPT, OpenAI's chatbot was first-to-market and still holds a ~60% share with 700M weekly active users as of August 2025. Its foundation models — and therefore user agents — power both ChatGPT and an array of third-party applications, including Microsoft's Co-Pilot and Bing search engine.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **ChatGPT-User** — Accesses websites in real-time and on-demand so that ChatGPT can formulate responses to user prompts based on the live web. It visits websites to gather information then processes, summarises and synthesizes this to provide the output. It is not used to gather data for model training.\n- **OAI-SearchBot** — Used to power ChatGPT's search capabilities. Similar to ChatGPT-User, it accesses the internet in real-time but is optimized for search scenarios, delivering the raw links and search results alongside summaries.\n- **GPTBot** — Gathers data from the web for the training of OpenAI's large language models. It operates continuously in the background and the data it gathers is collected and used offline for model development, rather than real-time responses to user prompts.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ OpenAI respects the signals provided by content owners via robots.txt, allowing them to disallow any or all of its crawlers.\n\n_Publisher partnerships:_ OpenAI has an extensive publisher partnerships program, having signed bilateral deals with ~40 publishers at the time of writing.\n\n\u003cInfoBox title=\"Robots Exclusion Protocol (robots.txt)\"\u003e\n  This index also refers to the Robots Exclusion Protocol or 'robots.txt'. This\n  mechanism allows website owners to give instructions to bots about accessing a\n  website. It uses a machine-readable file (named robots.txt) which specifies —\n  for individual bots or all bots collectively — which pages or sections they\n  can or cannot crawl. Robots.txt operates simply as a signal though and does\n  not actively block access. Not all developers program their bots to comply\n  with these instructions.\n\u003c/InfoBox\u003e\n\n---\n\n**Perplexity**\n\nPerplexity has developed an AI answer engine, effectively AI-powered search that provides users with a natural language response to a prompt alongside a list of links and sources. It has 22 million active users. The standard free product primarily uses a proprietary model, whereas the premium, paid-for service includes access to a range of models including OpenAI's GPT-4 Omni, Claude 3.5 from Anthropic, Llama 3 from Meta and Grok-2 from xAI.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **PerplexityBot** — Gathers data from across the web to index it for Perplexity's search function. It has also appeared to act in real-time, gathering data to respond to specific user queries.\n- **Perplexity-User** — Accesses web pages in real-time to respond to user prompts.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ Perplexity claims that its PerplexityBot user agent respects robots.txt. However, there have been widely-reported complaints from publishers that it has ignored their signals.\n\n_Publisher partnerships:_ In 2024 Perplexity launched a publisher partner program under which it provides a share of advertising revenues generated by responses that are based upon the content of media partners. Around 20 deals have been signed so far.\n\n---\n\n**Anthropic**\n\nFounded by seven former OpenAI employees, Anthropic is an AI developer with a focus on privacy, safety and alignment (with human values). Its foundation models power its Claude chatbot — which has a free and premium version — and a multitude of third-party applications.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **ClaudeBot** — Used to gather data from the internet for AI training.\n- **Claude-SearchBot** — Indexes online content to power Claude's web search. This allows Claude-User to retrieve up-to-date information when responding to user queries.\n- **Claude-User** — Fetches a web page on demand when a user asks a question that requires a live lookup.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ Anthropic's bots respect publisher signals in robots.txt files. Notably they also respond to any disallows for Common Crawl's CCBot.\n\n---\n\n**Google**\n\nGoogle is one of the foremost AI developers with its own proprietary models that have been integrated extensively into its consumer and enterprise applications, including search. It also operates Gemini as a standalone AI chatbot with real-time web access. This has around 14% of the chatbot market with an estimated 47 million active users.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **GoogleBot** — Google's all-purpose search bot. It creates the index of the web that the search engine relies on and evidence suggests it is now also operating in real-time to gather information from websites for both AI Overviews and AI Mode.\n- **Google-Extended** — Used to gather data to train and improve Google's AI models. It operates independently of the crawlers used to power Google's search product.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ Google's bots respect robots.txt signals. However, publishers do not have granular controls over the use of their content in real-time for AI search or Gemini's as these products use the data collected for Google's general search product.\n\n_Publisher partnerships:_ Whilst Google has signed a $60M AI content licensing deal with Reddit, the large number of partnerships it has with publishing businesses are built around its News Showcase product, rather than content for AI.\n\n---\n\n**Meta**\n\nMeta has developed the Llama family of open-source AI models. These are used in Meta's own products and extensively across third-party applications. Whilst historically it has relied on external datasets for model training, it has recently launched a new web crawler to collect data for its LLMs.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **Meta-ExternalAgent** — Described as crawling the web for 'use cases such as training AI models or improving products by indexing content directly'. Launched in summer 2024.\n- **Meta-ExternalFetcher** — Accesses websites in real-time in response to actions by users. Meta is transparent that this crawler may bypass robots.txt on account of being user-'initiated'.\n- **FacebookBot** — Previously described as crawling public web pages to improve language models for speech recognition technology.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ Meta's crawlers respect robots.txt signals although Meta-ExternalFetcher may bypass the protocol because it performs crawls that were user-initiated.\n\n_Publisher partnerships:_ Meta has signed an AI licensing deal with just one publisher — Reuters.\n\n---\n\n**Apple**\n\nApple has been investing in AI — including the development of its own foundation models — to enhance its products, particularly in privacy-centric applications and on-device AI capabilities.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **Applebot** — Acts as both a search indexing crawler and a RAG agent feeding information to power features across Apple's ecosystem, including Siri, Spotlight and Safari.\n- **Applebot-Extended** — A secondary user agent that allows publishers to opt-out of their content being used to train Apple's foundation models. Does not crawl webpages; only determines how Apple can use data crawled by the primary Applebot.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ Apple claims that its bots respect robots.txt directives.\n\n_Publisher partnerships:_ Apple has not publicly announced any AI content licensing deals at time of writing but has reportedly been in negotiations with a number of publishers including Condé Nast and NBC News.\n\n---\n\n**Amazon**\n\nAs well as a strategic partnership with Anthropic, Amazon has developed its own 'Nova' family of AI models which emphasize speed and value. These are offered to AWS clients and have been integrated into Alexa and other Amazon services.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **AmazonBot** — Described as being used to improve services, 'such as enabling Alexa to answer even more questions for customers'.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ AmazonBot respects standard robots.txt rules.\n\n_Publisher partnerships:_ Amazon has three notable AI licensing agreements: a multi-year deal with New York Times, and deals with both Condé Nast and Hearst to supply content for Amazon's AI-powered shopping voice assistant Rufus.\n\n---\n\n**ByteDance**\n\nByteDance — owner of TikTok — has developed both text and video generation models. Most of these are for research purposes although its Doubao text models power its chatbot, only available in China.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **Bytespider** — Has been scraping the web at a high rate since it first appeared in early 2024. ByteDance has published no information on the function it serves or what the data collected is being used for.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ ByteDance has no published robots.txt policy. There are widespread reports of Bytespider ignoring robots.txt signals.\n\n---\n\n**Other Notable AI User Agents**\n\n- **DuckAssistBot** — DuckDuckGo's web crawler that crawls pages in real time to source information for answers by DuckAssist. Data collected is not used to train AI models and it respects robots.txt signals.\n- **Timpibot** — Web crawler for Timpi, a decentralized search index, accessible for a cost to businesses. Data is also available to AI developers for model training.\n- **YouBot** — Crawler for You.com, an AI-powered search engine that integrates AI query responses alongside conventional search links.\n- **Diffbot** — Web crawler focused on extracting data from web pages which it then converts into structured datasets for businesses and developers. Fully respects robots.txt directives.\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"43:T11711,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"var Component=(()=\u003e{var m=Object.create;var h=Object.defineProperty;var b=Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor;var w=Object.getOwnPropertyNames;var v=Object.getPrototypeOf,y=Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty;var A=(s,t)=\u003e()=\u003e{try{return t||s((t={exports:{}}).exports,t),t.exports}catch(i){throw t=0,i}},I=(s,t)=\u003e{for(var i in t)h(s,i,{get:t[i],enumerable:!0})},d=(s,t,i,l)=\u003e{if(t\u0026\u0026typeof t==\"object\"||typeof t==\"function\")for(let r of w(t))!y.call(s,r)\u0026\u0026r!==i\u0026\u0026h(s,r,{get:()=\u003et[r],enumerable:!(l=b(t,r))||l.enumerable});return s};var T=(s,t,i)=\u003e(i=s!=null?m(v(s)):{},d(t||!s||!s.__esModule?h(i,\"default\",{value:s,enumerable:!0}):i,s)),x=s=\u003ed(h({},\"__esModule\",{value:!0}),s);var p=A((C,g)=\u003e{g.exports=_jsx_runtime});var G={};I(G,{default:()=\u003ef,frontmatter:()=\u003ek});var e=T(p()),k={title:\"State of the Bots\",id:\"q2-2025\",quarter:\"q2-2025\",description:\"2025 Q2, Good Net Citizens\",docType:\"report\",sections:[{title:\"Executive Summary\",slug:\"executive-summary\"},{title:\"Section 1: Rise of the Agentic Web\",slug:\"section-1\",subsections:[{title:\"Headless Browsers\",slug:\"headless-browsers\"},{title:\"Future Traffic Patterns\",slug:\"future-traffic\"},{title:\"Agent-to-Web Protocols\",slug:\"agent-protocols\"},{title:\"Regulation \u0026 Policy\",slug:\"regulation\"}]},{title:\"Section 2: Scale of AI Scraping\",slug:\"section-2\",subsections:[{title:\"Aggregate Scraping Levels\",slug:\"aggregate-scraping\"},{title:\"Scraping by Bot Category\",slug:\"bot-category\"},{title:\"AI and Online Search\",slug:\"online-search\"},{title:\"User Agent Scraping Levels\",slug:\"user-agent-levels\"}]},{title:\"Section 3: AI Demand for Content\",slug:\"section-3\",subsections:[{title:\"Scraping by Site Category\",slug:\"site-category\"},{title:\"Growth by Category\",slug:\"growth-category\"},{title:\"RAG vs Training\",slug:\"rag-vs-training\"},{title:\"Scraping by Jurisdiction\",slug:\"jurisdiction\"}]},{title:\"Section 4: Referral Traffic\",slug:\"section-4\",subsections:[{title:\"Aggregate Referrals\",slug:\"aggregate-referrals\"},{title:\"Comparing Referral Rates\",slug:\"comparing-referrals\"},{title:\"Shifting Google Value\",slug:\"google-value\"},{title:\"Licensing Deal Effects\",slug:\"licensing-effects\"}]},{title:\"Section 5: IP Controls \u0026 Errors\",slug:\"section-5\",subsections:[{title:\"HTTP 404 Errors\",slug:\"http-404\"},{title:\"Blocking Errors\",slug:\"blocking-errors\"},{title:\"Publisher Disallow Signals\",slug:\"disallow-signals\"},{title:\"Robots.txt Compliance\",slug:\"robots-compliance\"},{title:\"Bot Paywall Redirects\",slug:\"bot-paywall\"}]},{title:\"Section 6: Future Trajectory\",slug:\"section-6\",subsections:[{title:\"OpenAI ChatGPT\",slug:\"chatgpt-caching\"},{title:\"Google Gemini\",slug:\"gemini-caching\"},{title:\"Anthropic Claude\",slug:\"claude-caching\"}]},{title:\"Appendix: AI User Agent Profiles\",slug:\"appendix\",subsections:[{title:\"Categories of AI User Agent\",slug:\"agent-categories\"},{title:\"Bot Profiles by Organization\",slug:\"bot-profiles\"}]}]};function u(s){let t={code:\"code\",em:\"em\",h2:\"h2\",h3:\"h3\",hr:\"hr\",li:\"li\",p:\"p\",strong:\"strong\",ul:\"ul\",...s.components},{ExecItem:i,ExecSummary:l,Image:r,InfoBox:c,Section:n,UserStory:o}=t;return i||a(\"ExecItem\",!0),l||a(\"ExecSummary\",!0),r||a(\"Image\",!0),c||a(\"InfoBox\",!0),n||a(\"Section\",!0),o||a(\"UserStory\",!0),(0,e.jsxs)(e.Fragment,{children:[(0,e.jsxs)(n,{id:\"executive-summary\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Executive Summary\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(l,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(i,{num:1,children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:`The next wave of AI visitors won't look like bots or agents; they're\nincreasingly looking like humans.`}),` The latest AI browsers like Perplexity\nComet, and devtools like ScrapingBee or Browserless are indistinguishable from\nhumans in site logs: Chrome appears as their user agent and they behave like\nhuman users \\u2014 loading pages, sometimes loading ads, and solving CAPTCHAs. This\nis why it is mission-critical to push for a future where non-human site\ntraffic is required to self-identify.`]})}),(0,e.jsx)(i,{num:2,children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"AI bot activity now surpasses crawls from Bingbot:\"}),` AI bot aggregate\ntraffic has surpassed that of the world's second-largest search engine, Bing \\u2014\nhighlighting the growing scale of AI adoption relative to traditional search\nengines.`]})}),(0,e.jsx)(i,{num:3,children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:`Google's expansion of 'AI Overviews' in October 2024 resulted in 34.8%\nincrease in Googlebot crawls.`}),` At the same time, the number of Google crawls\nneeded to get a human visitor got worse, increasing by 24.4%. The overall\nincrease in AI visitors puts further economic strains on sites, as their CDN\ncosts increase to support this additional bot traffic amidst declining human\nreferrals.`]})}),(0,e.jsx)(i,{num:4,children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Human visitors to sites are falling, while AI bot traffic is rising:\"}),`\nBetween Q1 and Q2 2025, there was a 9.4% decrease in human visitors across\nsites on TollBit. Meanwhile, AI bot/agent traffic is continuing to increase.\nLooking at human and AI visitors across sites, at the start of Q1, 1 out of\nevery 200 visitors was AI. Now it is 1 in every 50, reflecting a 4x increase\nin the relative volume of AI visitors.`]})}),(0,e.jsx)(i,{num:5,children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:`Over the past year, there has been a 336% increase in sites blocking or\nredirecting AI bots/agents,`}),` as evidenced by redirections and HTTP forbidden\nerrors served to AI bots. This is a result of bot detection solutions being\ndeployed more extensively across publisher sites. This corresponds with hits\nto the TollBit Bot/Agent Paywall, which have increased around 360% from Q1 to\nQ2 2025 as websites have increasingly taken active measures to charge AI\ntraffic.`]})}),(0,e.jsx)(i,{num:6,children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Across all AI bots, 13.26% of requests bypassed robots.txt in Q2 2025,\"}),` up\n4x from just 3.3% in Q4 2024.`]})}),(0,e.jsxs)(i,{num:7,children:[(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"There is evidence that AI apps store \u0026 re-use content, but it appears it's not always efficient for them to cache data for long periods.\"}),\" This highlights the importance of RAG and continued content access. Here's a breakdown of what that caching pattern looks like based on our tests:\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"Claude: cached for 16+ days*\"}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"ChatGPT: cached for 30 minutes\"}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"Gemini: cached for 15 minutes\"}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Our testing ended after this time period.\"})})]})]})]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(n,{id:\"section-1\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Section 1: Rise of the Agentic Web\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(o,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`This quarter saw the release of additional AI web browsers and new consumer\nagentic applications. As adoption grows, these look set to radically change\nthe shape of web traffic with profound consequences for digital business\nmodels. New AI applications and capabilities are increasingly being powered by\n'headless' browsers; these are web browsers that are controlled by automated\nsystems. Headless browsers are a more interactive form of web scraping that\nenables AI agents to take actions on behalf of users, including accessing\ndynamic websites, adding items to their cart, or booking a table. There is no\nhuman user controlling the browser, yet the traffic looks like a human visitor\n\\u2014 despite being an agent. TollBit data suggests that human visitors to\nwebsites are beginning to decline, with bots supplanting them.`})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"The second quarter of 2025 saw the mainstream or beta releases of AI web browsers such as OpenAI Agent Mode, Perplexity Comet, Google's Project Mariner. Fortune 1000 companies also turn to platforms like MindStudio or n8n to build agentic systems. These new technologies \\u2014 which further extend the capabilities of automated systems to gather information and perform actions on our behalf \\u2014 foreshadow a transformative change in how consumers engage with the online world.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"While these changes are still nascent, and the signals in the data are only beginning to emerge, we can already see the contours of a future in which interactions with websites look radically different. This shift means traffic will present itself to publishers in different ways with direct implications for revenue, raising fundamental questions about the relationships between website owners, users, and the developers of AI systems that increasingly occupy an intermediary role.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"Headless Browsers and Agentic Systems\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[\"Since the release of chatbots that can access the real-time internet, AI applications have needed systems that interface with websites, retrieving and synthesizing content on behalf of users when required by a prompt. In the first wave of AI this function was served by purpose-built crawlers operated by the AI developer itself. These bots declared themselves via user agent strings such as ChatGPT-User and PerplexityBot. Much of the analysis in TollBit's \",(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"State of the Bots\"}),\" reports has focused on these technologies.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`This landscape is now changing. New AI interfaces and capabilities (chiefly agentic systems and AI browsers) and the widespread adoption of publisher IP controls have led developers to turn to the use of 'headless browsers', often operated by third parties. These are web browsers that are programmatically controlled via automated systems, without a human doing the browsing \\u2014 hence the term, \"headless.\" This tech has been available for many years, but until recently, was almost exclusively used for enterprise functions, such as website performance testing or QA automation.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Three types of AI headless browsers:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Masked web agents:\"}),\" Infrastructure providers allow an AI developer's agent to access websites through headless browsers at scale. Think of this as renting browsers in the cloud, each controlled by the AI developer's agent. These visitors appear to publishers as indistinguishable from ordinary human traffic. Providers of this infrastructure include Browserbase and Hyperbrowser.\"]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Third-party web agents:\"}),\" These services (e.g. BrightData, Apify, Zyte) act as outsourced crawlers. Instead of the AI agent itself opening a browser session, the agent calls an API, and the third-party service performs the crawl. Part of the value prop of these services is their ability to defeat anti-bot tools to scrape high-value sites.\"]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Browser-driven web agents:\"}),\" With the release of the AI-first web browsers \\u2014 \",(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Comet\"}),\" from Perplexity and \",(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Dia\"}),\" from The Browser Company \\u2014 come agents that operate directly inside a consumer-facing browser. From a publisher's perspective, these agents are indistinguishable from normal human browsing.\"]}),`\n`]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 1.1. Perplexity's Comet browser\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image1.png\",alt:\"Perplexity Comet browser\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Headless browsers are being used because they provide a more reliable way to automate web access for AI developers. They execute JavaScript, maintain sessions, and mimic normal user behaviour, enabling agents to complete tasks that simple HTTP-based scrapers cannot. In practice, they can also bypass common bot-mitigation measures.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"In the case of Perplexity Comet, the agent appears to use the user's own desktop to request webpages when automating workflows. This makes the requests appear to come from a user's residential IP address, which can confuse CDN companies into thinking it is legitimate. Comet is also built on the open source Chromium project, which powers Chrome, so the requests also pass along Chrome user agent strings.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"These practices create a new technical challenge for publishers. Some headless browser services can evade detection tools and even solve CAPTCHAs (tests intended to distinguish humans from bots), making automated sessions hard to separate from genuine human visitors.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"Future Traffic Patterns\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Whether a publisher, an ecommerce or an aggregator site, their advertising and subscription revenues depend upon humans engaging with content on properties. When AI systems scrape and summarize (or browse and checkout), they potentially negate the need for a user to visit the site. This corresponds to a lost monetization opportunity for the content creator.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"TollBit data suggests that human traffic to publisher websites is beginning to decline, with bots supplanting it. As the use of headless browsers (or advanced web scrapers) grows, this trend is expected to falsely appear to reverse, with website owners seeing a growth in visitors that present as humans but are in fact agents serving an AI application. These faux human visitors will erode advertiser trust and cannot be monetized with traditional methods.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 1.2. Projected future traffic composition\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image1-1.png\",alt:\"Projected future traffic\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Looking further to the future, as agentic systems grow in complexity and sophistication (which in turn will rely on improvements in reliability and speed), this is likely to result in a dramatic expansion in this faux human traffic \\u2014 far exceeding the levels seen today \\u2014 with multiple agents serving a single user simultaneously; many likely to be operating autonomously and without the need for a user-trigger.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"Agent-to-Web Protocols, Distinguishing Humans from Bots\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Ironically, to maintain the integrity of the open web, it may need to be that a parallel 'agent web' will emerge, optimized for non-human actors. This would negate the need for headless browsers to engage with human websites with all the inefficiencies that entails. This is certainly a possibility with emerging approaches such as the Model Context Protocol (MCP), structured data standards like Schema.org, and TollBit's own agent gateway, creating the means of discrete agent-to-site interfaces.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:'Should agent-to-site protocols become the norm, it would simplify the process of distinguishing between humans and non-humans; the onus of figuring out \"bot or not\" would no longer be an unfair burden placed on website owners in a web that is increasingly trafficked by autonomous visitors.'}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"Regulation and Policy\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"As this section of the report has described, the rise of headless browsers and the blurring of human and non-human traffic pose clear risks for all website owners. For some publishers, value is being eroded by intellectual property leakage. For other sites, resources are diverted into costly detection, mitigation or compute costs. At the same time, these practices by AI developers are stalling the emergence of a fair and liquid content access market.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"While we can already see distinctions between agentic systems, agentic automated systems, user-directed AI browsers, automated AI browsers, etc., it is clear that this taxonomy will evolve, and edge cases that blur the human-bot boundary will continue to appear.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"It is our view at TollBit that this is where regulatory intervention is needed. As a minimum, automated agents should be mandated to identify themselves. There is no legitimate justification for any autonomous visitor on the web to present itself as human; this should be prohibited, requiring disclosure/identification embedded in user agent strings or elsewhere in the HTTP headers.\"})]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(n,{id:\"section-2\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Section 2: Scale of AI Scraping\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(o,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`Traffic from AI bots continue to grow strongly both in absolute terms and as a\nproportion of overall traffic. Out of human \u0026 AI visitor totals, we now see 1\nout of every 50 visitors to a site is an AI visitor. It was 1 out of every 200\nvisitors to a site in Q1. Alongside this growth in AI traffic, the number of\nhuman visitors to websites is beginning to fall, TollBit data saw a 9.4%\nreduction between Q1 and Q2 2025. This suggests that human visitors to\nwebsites are being replaced by AI bots, operating on their behalf. In\naggregate AI bot requests have now surpassed those from Bing, the world's\nsecond-largest search engine that currently accounts for around 4% of the\nmarket.`})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"2.1 Aggregate Scraping Levels\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Over the second quarter of 2025, website traffic from AI bots has continued to grow strongly. When we examine the daily volume of AI bot requests per TollBit partner website since the start of the year we can see a sharp increase, with, on some days, the level exceeding 300% what it was at the beginning of 2025.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.1. Daily per website AI bot scraping level, percent vs 1/1/2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image2.svg\",alt:\"Daily AI bot scraping levels\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"AI bot traffic also continues to grow as a proportion of all web traffic. Looking at human and AI visitors across sites, at the start of 2025, 1 out of every 200 visitors was AI. Now it is 1 in every 50, reflecting a 4x increase in the relative volume of AI visitors.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.2. AI to human ratio, total requests\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image3.svg\",alt:\"AI to human ratio\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Two factors are driving this change in publisher traffic composition. Alongside the growth in AI bot traffic we are now beginning to see human visitors decline. TollBit data saw a 9.4% drop in human requests between Q1 and Q2 2025. Put together with the AI bot traffic growth, these data suggest that substitution could be taking place \\u2014 human visitors to websites being replaced by AI user agents which are operating on their behalf whether that's through a chatbot or an AI search interface. Unless publishers can monetize this AI traffic, it might result in lost revenue.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.3. Human vs AI bot requests, per website (log scale)\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image4.svg\",alt:\"Human vs AI bot requests\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"2.2 Scraping Levels per AI Bot Category\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"This substitutional effect may be driven by consumers using AI applications for information retrieval, likely needs that would previously have been satisfied by the use of a search engine followed by a human visiting a publisher website. When AI applications serve these needs, they often access the web in real-time, using a retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) agent to visit and scrape websites to formulate a response. TollBit data shows that website visits from these AI RAG agents are growing the fastest.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:'These increases in AI Bot traffic hammering sites are concerning for publishers, not only due to the potential loss in human visitors but also because this is driving up their website/CDN costs in order to support this additional traffic. AI tools often \"read\", aka use \u0026 cite more content than a human would to answer a question.'}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.4. AI bot traffic by user-agent type (average, per domain)\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image5.svg\",alt:\"AI bot traffic by user-agent type\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"2.3 AI and Online Search\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"When we compare the level of scraping from these new AI bots to those from the web crawlers operated by conventional search engines, we can see that, in aggregate, AI requests per website have now surpassed those from Bing, the world's second largest search engine, currently holding around 4% of the market.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.5. Requests per site AI bot vs conventional search bots\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image6.png\",alt:\"AI bot vs search bots\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"In response to the growing use of AI chatbots for information retrieval, Google has been introducing AI features into its core web search product. This started with a trial of 'search generative experience' back in May 2023. This feature turned into 'AI Overviews' which were piloted, expanded to all US users then rolled-out extensively \\u2014 to over 100 countries \\u2014 in October 2024.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"This global expansion of AI Overviews coincided with a very substantial increase in the level of requests from Google's crawler on TollBit partner websites. The daily average crawls for the cohort of websites increased by 34.8% after the rollout. This data strongly suggests that Google still needs to visit websites in real-time to power its AI search features.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.6. Googlebot requests and the global rollout of AI Overviews (0 = 10/28/2024)\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image7.png\",alt:\"Googlebot requests and AI Overviews\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"A new user agent from Google?\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"We saw ~45M requests across our publisher network from a user agent string 'Google' and some IP addresses associated with Google. We observe this user agent appearing in server logs when we use Gemini to search the website. However, Google's documentation at the time of publishing this report doesn't mention this crawler.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"While this IP is not listed in Google's published IP ranges, a reverse DNS lookup confirmed the IP addresses were Google, as per Google's verification guidelines.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"We first observed requests from 'Google' user agent in our logs in April 2024, followed by a noticeable uptick in November 2024 and a significant rise in average monthly visits starting April 2025. These increases align with the launch of the Gemini iPhone app in November 2024 and the rollout of AI Mode and Deep Search features in May 2025.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.7. Daily Average Crawls from 'Google' user agent\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image8.svg\",alt:\"Google user agent crawls\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"2.4 User Agent Scraping Levels\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"When we examine the level of scraping by individual AI bots, we can observe a notable increase in activity from OpenAI's web crawlers, both chatgpt-user \\u2014 its RAG agent \\u2014 which has increased by 100% on a per-website basis, and gptbot \\u2014 its training data crawler \\u2014 which has grown by 252%. Notably, aggregate scrapes from Perplexity's user agents (PerplexityBot and Perplexity-User, introduced earlier this year) have decreased by 19% over the quarter. This could be due to alternative data acquisition methods, like third-party web scrapers or disguised user agents.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.8. Monthly Average Scrapes per site by AI Bots \\u2014 All sites from Q4 '24 vs Q1 '25\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image9.svg\",alt:\"Monthly average scrapes by AI bots\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Looking specifically at patterns of activity from training data crawlers, we see periods of intense scraping activity from a particular user agent, followed by relative dormancy. This is likely to be driven by the need to collect data for a specific model training run. In the period from late May to the end of June we saw particularly intensive scraping from gptbot.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.8. Training data crawlers, user agent composition\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image10.svg\",alt:\"Training data crawlers composition\"})]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(n,{id:\"section-3\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Section 3: AI Demand for Content\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(o,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`Certain categories of content are subject to markedly higher levels of AI\nvisitors: Relative to the level of human traffic, B2B/professional, sport,\nparenting and consumer technology content receive the highest level of AI\nscraping, suggesting these are central (publisher-disruptive) AI use cases. AI\nrequests to parenting content (+333%) and deals and shopping content (+111%)\nare growing the fastest across TollBit sites. This is likely to indicate new\nor growing consumer AI use. National news content is subject to 5x the number\nof RAG requests than those from training crawlers; this content is\n(unsurprisingly) needed in real-time to respond to prompts.`})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"3.1 AI Scraping Levels by Site Category\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"AI demand for content is unevenly distributed across TollBit partner websites, with certain categories of content subject to markedly higher levels of scraping. When we examine AI bot traffic relative to the level of human traffic \\u2014 a measure of the relative AI demand for any given content category \\u2014 we see B2B/professional content receiving the highest level of AI scraping, followed by parenting, sport, consumer technology, and health and wellness.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"This AI-to-human ratio is likely to be an indication of where AI applications present a substitution risk to publisher content; a higher level (particularly if requests are from RAG agents) suggest that AI tools are being used in place of users visiting a publisher website. For example, parenting content was subject to one AI scrape per 67 human visitors in Q1 2025, which has risen to one AI scrape per 23 human visitors in Q2, potentially indicating that users are directing their parenting queries at AI applications instead of visiting publisher websites.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.1. AI scrapes for every 1,000 human requests, Q2 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image11.svg\",alt:\"AI scrapes per human requests\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"3.2 Growth in AI Scraping by Site Category\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Whilst AI requests are growing across almost all content categories, this rate of growth varies substantially. When analysing a cohort of websites that joined the TollBit platform in 2024 we can see that AI requests to parenting content, for example, rose by 333%. Deals and shopping is another outlier, with a +111% increase. These are likely to indicate new or growing AI use-cases with consumers turning to AI to satisfy needs that previously were fulfilled elsewhere.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.2. Percent increase in average scrapes per page \\u2014 Q1 to Q2 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image12.svg\",alt:\"Percent increase in scrapes per page\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Further evidence on these potential shifts in consumer behaviour can be found when comparing changes in human traffic and AI traffic for a given content category. For lifestyle content, human requests declined by 45% whilst those from AI increased by 43%. This suggests that users are turning to AI in place of publisher websites directly. A similar pattern is observed in relation to national news content (AI traffic up 65% and human traffic down 10%). Even where human traffic is growing, this growth rate across many categories is substantially exceeded by the growth in traffic from AI bots (consumer technology, special interest, deals \u0026 shopping, sport, health \u0026 wellness).\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.3. Changes in human / AI requests, Q1 vs Q2\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image13.svg\",alt:\"Changes in human vs AI requests\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"3.3 Real-time/RAG versus Training\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Comparing the level of demand by RAG bots with that from training bots provides an indication of the types of content that are fetched in real-time.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Unsurprisingly, given the time-sensitive nature of news content, we see almost 5 RAG scrapes per request from a training data collection bot on national news sites.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.4. RAG vs training scrapes, site category, Q2 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image14.png\",alt:\"RAG vs training scrapes\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"3.4 Scraping Levels Across Jurisdictions\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"TollBit's footprint of media partners is growing globally. As a result, it is now possible to analyze the level of AI scraping across jurisdictions to identify any patterns or discrepancies. Doing so reveals that APAC websites are, by far, the most scraped domains, particularly by training data crawlers. On average APAC sites received over three times the number of requests in the quarter compared to US sites. One of the drivers for this is likely to be the use of the Robots Exclusion Protocol to disallow access; we find that only 13.5% of APAC sites have disallowed GPTBot (OpenAI's training data crawler), versus 52.5% in the US and 45.8% in Europe (a category which includes the UK).\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"It is notable that scraping levels for European websites are lower, with these receiving 27% fewer AI requests than those for US sites owned.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.5. Average scraping levels by website region / jurisdiction, Q2 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image15.svg\",alt:\"Scraping levels by region\"})]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(n,{id:\"section-4\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Section 4: Referral Traffic\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(o,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`Google referrals are now in decline, both in absolute terms and in terms of\nshare. They have dropped from over 90% of all external visitors in Q2 2024 to\n84.1% in the same quarter of the following year. AI applications continue to\nbe a tiny source of publisher referrals \\u2014 0.102% of referrals in Q2 2025 came\nfrom AI apps. Google still delivers 831 human visitors for every human visitor\nthat comes from an AI system. The click-through rate from AI applications\nremains extremely small; over 91% lower than for the average of the top-10\npositions on organic search. Referral rates from ChatGPT are markedly stronger\nfor publishers with OpenAI licensing agreements in place.`})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"4.1 Aggregate Referrals from AI Applications\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Whilst referrals from AI increased from 0.042% of all referrers in Q1 to 0.102% in Q2, this still represents only a tiny fraction of overall traffic to publisher websites with Google delivering 831 visitors for every single visitor from an AI application.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 4.1. Overall referrals, Q2 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image16.svg\",alt:\"Overall referrals Q2 2025\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Google remains the dominant source of external traffic; however, TollBit data indicates this is now in decline on both a relative and absolute basis. When we examine referrals from a cohort of sites that joined TollBit prior to July 2024, we find that visits from Google are down 9.17% between July 2024 and June 2025.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"100 crawls from Googlebot resulted in 454 referrals in Q2 2024, yet one year later, in Q2 2025, 100 crawls only resulted in 312 referrals. The same number of crawls now yields 142 fewer referrals.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"When we analyze Google's share of all external referrals to TollBit partner sites, these have also dropped from over 90% in Q2 2024 to 84.1% in the same quarter of the following year. This decline in traffic from Google is taking place when crawls from Googlebot (its combined user agent) is increasing, and markedly so since the introduction of AI Overviews (see figure 2.6).\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 4.2. Composition of referrals over time\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image17.svg\",alt:\"Composition of referrals over time\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"4.2 Comparing Referral Rates\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Whilst in aggregate publishers are seeing growth in the number of visitors from AI applications, the click-through rate from these interfaces have not meaningfully improved. Whereas Google's organic (non-AI) search delivers, on average across the top-10 results positions, a click 8.6% of the time, for AI applications, this figure remains below 1%. On average it requires 135 real-time scrapes from an AI application for a single visitor to arrive.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 4.3. AI application click-through rates\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image18.svg\",alt:\"AI application click-through rates\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Although the total number of human visitors coming from AI sources remains negligible, it's interesting to note that the distribution of these referrals differs from Google.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Of the few referrals that AI platforms send, the breakdown of referrals by category is quite different from Google search: For example, 45% of AI apps' total referrals are being sent to National News. While across the same set of sites, only 22% of Google's referrals are being sent to National News.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Two factors may explain this difference:\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"Different click-through patterns \\u2014 AI systems may produce a different rate of click-throughs for similar queries versus Google.\"}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"Different starting points for information needs \\u2014 users may be disproportionately turning to AI tools, rather than Google, for certain categories of queries.\"}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 4.4.1. Google's search referral distributions\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image19.svg\",alt:\"Google search referral distributions\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 4.4.2. AI applications' referral distribution\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image20.svg\",alt:\"AI applications referral distribution\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"By examining the RAG scrape to referral ratio, we can test whether AI applications are more likely to satisfy specific categories of user need and thus depress the click-through rates for that content. This analysis shows that consumer technology, special interest and lifestyle receive far fewer referrals per scrape (likely indicating that AI answers are likely to be sufficient and further information that would lead to a human click-through is not required). Conversely, B2B/professional and entertainment and pop culture content delivers, in proportion, substantially more clicks than scrapes, indicating the opposite.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 4.5. Scrape-to-Referral Ratio, Q2 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image21.svg\",alt:\"Scrape-to-Referral Ratio Q2 2025\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"4.3 Shifting Google Value Exchange\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"As well as corresponding to an increase in the number of requests to publisher websites (see section 2.3) from its crawler, Google's expansion of AI Overviews in October 2024 also resulted in an increase in the crawl-to-referral ratio by 24.45%. Googlebot is used for a multitude of purposes and Google makes extensive use of offline caches of content so this shift is directional, rather than truly indicative of the number of uses of content that result in traffic delivered. However, it does strongly suggest there is a change to the value exchange, with fewer referrals delivered for each time Google accesses website content.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 4.6. Scrape-to-referral ratio (Googlebot) after global rollout of AI Overviews\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image22.png\",alt:\"Scrape-to-referral ratio after AI Overviews\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"4.4 Effect of Licensing Deals on AI Usage and Referrals\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"OpenAI has been establishing new partnerships with publishers since it made its first licensing deal with Associated Press in July 2023. At the time of writing, it has almost 40 similar agreements in place with publishers. While limited public information on these deals is available, we have analysed TollBit data to understand whether having a deal corresponds to more scraping or more referrals from ChatGPT.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"In doing so, we find that in the last quarter, publishers with deals saw 88% more scraping on a per-page basis than those without agreements. This rose from a difference of just 10% in Q1 2025.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 4.7. ChatGPT-User, scrapes per page\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image23.svg\",alt:\"ChatGPT-User scrapes per page\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Referrals, on the other hand, are markedly stronger for publishers with licensing agreements in place. In Q1 2025 the click-through rate was comparable to the average of the top-10 organic search positions. AI companies may be placing logos and names of publishers they have done deals with front and center in AI app citation lists. However, the referrals have dropped in Q2 likely as the number of deals expands and the positions with greater prominence are shared amongst a greater number of publishers.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 4.8. OpenAI click-through rates\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image24.svg\",alt:\"OpenAI click-through rates\"})]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(n,{id:\"section-5\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Section 5: IP Controls and Errors\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(o,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`We can observe a growing number of forbidden server errors and redirections\nbeing served to AI visitors \\u2014 likely as a result of bot detection solutions\nbeing deployed more extensively across publisher sites. Publishers continue to\nincrease their use of robots.txt to signal that AI bots are not permitted to\nscrape their content. AI crawlers still routinely ignore website robots.txt\nsignals. Crawlers from ByteDance, OpenAI, Meta, Perplexity, and Amazon all\nbypass publisher signals at times. Across all AI bots, 13.26% of requests\nbypassed robots.txt in Q2 2025, this has risen from just 3.3% in Q4 2024.`})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"5.1 Server Responses \\u2014 HTTP 404 Error Rates\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Each time a request to a website takes place, the site's server responds with a code which indicates the status of the response, for example, whether the request was successful, blocked or was towards a page that doesn't exist. Monitoring the distribution of these codes \\u2014 and how they change over time \\u2014 can provide some insight on the nature of the requests coming from AI bots and how website owners are handling them.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"404 errors indicate that the page requested could not be found. In the case of search engines, this is often caused by a broken link or the deletion of a page. AI applications additionally may generate 404 errors by hallucinating URLs, directing users to pages that never existed. Two notable changes in 404 error rates from AI applications have occurred over the last quarter. Firstly, the data indicates a marked increase in OpenAI's figures, rising from 0.3% of responses in Q1 to 3.7% in Q2. Meanwhile, Anthropic's 404 error rate has dropped dramatically from 55% in Q2 2024 (meaning most of the pages requested by users were not found) to 4.8% in Q2 this year. The timing of this improvement corresponds with Claude \\u2014 Anthropic's chatbot \\u2014 being given access to the real-time web, allowing it to retrieve and cite live URLs rather than relying on extrapolations from training data.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 5.1. HTTP 404 error rates, 2024 Q2 to 2025 Q2\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image25.svg\",alt:\"HTTP 404 error rates\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"5.2 Server Responses \\u2014 Blocking or Redirecting Error Rates\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Over the last year, 302, 402, 403, and 401 response statuses have increased by almost 4x on TollBit partner websites, likely due to publishers deploying security measures (bot detection/WAF) that prevent non-human visitors such as AI bots from accessing content. TollBit provides basic bot enforcement and partners with various cybersecurity companies, including Fastly, DataDome, and HUMAN Security to provide advanced bot blocking options for sites.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 5.2. All domain quarterly changes in 403 errors\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image26.png\",alt:\"Quarterly changes in 403 errors\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"5.3 Publisher Use of Disallow Signals\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Publishers continue to extend their use of the Robots Exclusion Protocol (AKA 'robots.txt') to signal that AI bots are not permitted to scrape their content. While most publishers implemented changes on the most widely known bots prior to 2025, we can observe a growth in disallow requests as new AI scrapers are released and become known. For example: As of June 2025, 81% of the cohort of sites are blocking CCbot and 73% block GPTBot.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 5.3. Total AI bot disallow requests\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image27.svg\",alt:\"Total AI bot disallow requests\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 5.4. Disallowed Bots via Robots.txt, cohort of TollBit partner websites\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image28.svg\",alt:\"Disallowed Bots via Robots.txt\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"5.4 Robots.txt Compliance\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"TollBit data shows that many AI crawlers still appear to ignore these publisher robots.txt signals, scraping content even when explicitly requested not to. This behavior varies significantly by bot, with crawlers from ByteDance, OpenAI, Meta, Perplexity and Amazon all bypassing publisher signals at times. Across all AI bots, 13.26% of requests bypassed robots.txt in Q2 2025, this has risen from just 3.3% in Q4 2024.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 5.5. Requests that bypass robots.txt signals, percent Q2 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image29.svg\",alt:\"Requests that bypass robots.txt\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"5.5 Redirects to Bot Paywall\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"The TollBit platform works with websites' existing tech stacks to redirect bots which are not permitted to access a given site or page to a bot paywall with the price for access set by the publisher. Hits to these paywalls have been growing steeply (360% from Q1 to Q2 2025) as publisher partners deploy this functionality. This delivers an HTTP 402 \\u2014 Payment Required to the AI bot or agent.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 5.6. Hits to TollBit bot paywall\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/bots/25q2/image30.png\",alt:\"Hits to TollBit bot paywall\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Note: 302 = temporary redirect; 401 = not authenticated; 402 = payment required; 403 = access forbidden/blocked\"})})]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(n,{id:\"section-6\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Section 6: Future Trajectory of AI Bot Traffic\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(o,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`There is evidence that AI apps store \u0026 re-use content, but it appears it's not\nalways optimal for them to cache data for long periods. This highlights the\nimportance of RAG and continued content access. Here's a breakdown of what\nthat caching pattern looks like based on our tests: ChatGPT: cached for 30\nminutes across user accounts. Gemini: cached for 15 minutes at a user account\nlevel. Claude: cached for 16+ days across user accounts.`})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[\"AI demand for professional content is only set to grow as this technology is deployed in new applications and consumer adoption continues to accelerate. However, identifying this demand by observing the activity on the publisher servers will become harder as AI developers increasingly store and reuse content by making use of offline caches of the content. The TollBit team has been running experiments to understand \\u2014 and explain \\u2014 each of the top AI bots usage of caching for our \",(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"State of the Bots\"}),\" report readers.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"How We Set Up the Experiments\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"To understand how AI applications retrieve web content \\u2014 and whether they rely on live scraping or cached (aka stored) data \\u2014 we ran a series of structured experiments across four AI applications: ChatGPT, Gemini, Claude, and Perplexity.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"The Test Website\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:'We created one-page websites that showed a jumbled sequence of letters. This \"code\" changes every second and on every load or refresh. Since the site shows a unique code that changes every second, the code returned by the AI application tells us when it accessed the page. If an AI model responded with a code that matched the exact time a prompt was sent, it indicated real-time scraping. If it returned a code from an earlier time period, it suggested the application had cached/stored the content from a previous request.'}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"The test website did not block any bots in the robots.txt file.\"}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"The test website wasn't submitted to Google Search Console, so it couldn't be pre-cached.\"}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Repeating this process across hundreds of queries and cross-referencing with server logs let us map cache refresh intervals, identify cross-user caching, and observe whether models behaved consistently across different prompt styles.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"We used the following setup for each AI application/chatbot:\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"Ran between 200 to 500 prompts\"}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"Queried each application using two separate user accounts to test for cross-user caching.\"}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:'Prompt style was designed to trigger RAG behavior. Example: \"Find the latest code displayed on [URL].\"'}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"Prompts were spaced at varying time intervals \\u2014 from a few seconds to a few hours \\u2014 and made from different user accounts to test both time-based and user-based cache behavior.\"}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"We cross-referenced each prompt with server logs to verify whether and when the page was accessed by the chatbot's respective AI bots.\"}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"6.1 OpenAI ChatGPT\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"What we tested\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"We prompted ChatGPT (GPT-5 and o4-mini-high) over 500 times using multiple OpenAI accounts.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"What we observed\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Cache duration:\"}),\" The cache duration was approximately 30 minutes. During 30-minute intervals, ChatGPT returned the same cached code regardless of prompt time or user account.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Cross-user caching:\"}),\" ChatGPT consistently cached content across users, returning the same code even when queried minutes apart from different accounts.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Scraping time:\"}),\" Real-time scraping occurred only after cache expiry. Once the cache expired, the respective AI bot(s) fetched a fresh version of the code at the time of the prompt.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Citation:\"}),\" The webpage was cited as a source in the response. Even when the content was cached and not freshly scraped, ChatGPT displayed the website in the citations provided to the user.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"What showed up in our logs\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agent:\"}),\" \",(0,e.jsx)(t.code,{children:\"Mozilla/5.0 AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko); compatible; ChatGPT-User/1.0; +https://openai.com/bot\"})]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"IP Address:\"}),\" IP addresses used were part of OpenAI's public list and registered to Microsoft Limited\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Additional scraping activity:\"}),\" We observed OpenAI's SearchBot (OAI-searchbot) accessing /robots.txt multiple times, outside active test windows. This suggests background scanning unrelated to prompt timing.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"6.2 Google Gemini\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"What we tested\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"We prompted Gemini 2.5 Pro over 200 times using three different Google accounts.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"What we observed\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Cache duration:\"}),\" Cache duration was approximately 15 minutes. During 15-minute intervals, repeat prompts returned the same code \\u2014 the page was not scraped again.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User-level caching:\"}),\" Caching occurred at the individual user level. Each Google account saw its own cached result. The cache was not shared across users.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Scraping time:\"}),\" Real-time scraping occurred only after cache expiry. Once the cache expired, the respective AI bot(s) fetched a fresh version of the code at the time of the prompt.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Citation:\"}),\" Gemini included the test page as a citation, even when the response was served from cache.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Other model behavior:\"}),\" Gemini 2.5 Flash cache duration did not differ from Gemini 2.5 Pro\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"What showed up in our logs\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agent:\"}),\" \",(0,e.jsx)(t.code,{children:\"Google\"}),\" (Note: This user agent string is not listed in Google's official documentation but was consistently used during Gemini's scraping activity.)\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"IP Address:\"}),\" Although this IP address is not listed in Google's published IP ranges, a reverse DNS lookup confirmed that it was associated with Google's IP addresses, as per Google's verification guidelines.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"6.3 Anthropic Claude\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"What we tested\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"We prompted Claude (Sonnet 4 and Opus 4.1) over 200 times using multiple Anthropic accounts.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"What we observed\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Cache duration:\"}),\" Even after 16 days of testing and hundreds of queries, Claude continued to return the same stale cached content.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Cross-user caching:\"}),\" Claude cached responses across users and across time.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Scraping time:\"}),\" The model never scraped the test page again after the initial prompt, regardless of changes in time gaps between prompts.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Citation:\"}),\" Claude included the test page as a citation, even when serving cached responses.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Other model behavior:\"}),\" Both Claude Sonnet 4 and Claude Opus 4.1 showed identical behavior throughout testing.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"What showed up in our logs\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agent:\"}),\" \",(0,e.jsx)(t.code,{children:\"Mozilla/5.0 AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko; compatible; Claude-User/1.0; [email withheld by Lighthouse])\"})]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"IP Address:\"}),\" Anthropic doesn't publish IP addresses it uses. The IP addresses were registered to Google Cloud.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Additional scraping activity:\"}),\" Claude-User checked the /robots.txt page before scraping the website.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Note: These AI tools are evolving rapidly. The caching behaviors and bot activity we observed may change over time, and results may differ based on prompt wording, account setup, or system updates.\"})})]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(n,{id:\"appendix\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Appendix: AI User Agent Profiles\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"As our State of the Bots reports chart, the internet is crawled by an ever-growing number of AI user agents. These have different functions for the applications they serve and consequently, behave differently online. Here is a quick overview of the principal forms of AI user agent, what they do, how they work and why they matter to publishers.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"Categories of AI User Agent\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"1. Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) Agent\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"These bots retrieve information in real-time to respond to user prompts. They use an index of the web, gathered by an indexing crawler (either proprietary or third-party such as Bing or Google) to locate the relevant content which is then retrieved and synthesized into a response.\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher effects:\"}),\" When a RAG agent accesses a site, it's using that content to provide a response to a user's prompt, typically in an AI chatbot (e.g. ChatGPT) or AI search (e.g. Perplexity) application. This might be substitutional to a human visitor.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"2. Training Data Crawling Agent\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Large language models \\u2014 such as Llama from Meta or GPT-5 from OpenAI, both of which power a multitude of consumer applications \\u2014 are trained on vast quantities of data. Training data crawlers move around the web \\u2014 following links from websites to websites or working through sitemaps \\u2014 downloading content which is then processed and stored for offline use.\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher effects:\"}),\" The inclusion of content in the training data means that a model has this 'knowledge' to answer prompts. Training data typically cuts off several months prior to model release so the substitutional risk is limited for content with a longer shelf-life.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"3. AI Search Indexing Agent\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"AI systems with access to the real-time web need an index of the internet. This is used to direct RAG agents to the right sources when collecting data needed for responses to prompts. AI search indexing crawlers build these indexes by systematically navigating the web, collecting and organizing content and metadata.\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher effects:\"}),\" These crawlers only ensure that a website's content can be navigated to by a RAG agent, should a relevant prompt require that.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"4. Hybrid Agent\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Some AI developers use a single bot (or at least software which identifies itself with the same user agent metadata) for more than one of these purposes. For example, PerplexityBot appears to act both as an indexing crawler and a RAG agent.\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher effects:\"}),\" This makes it hard for publishers to apply granular controls to the access and use of their IP by AI applications.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"AI Bot Profiles by Operating Organization\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"OpenAI\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Developer of ChatGPT, OpenAI's chatbot was first-to-market and still holds a ~60% share with 700M weekly active users as of August 2025. Its foundation models \\u2014 and therefore user agents \\u2014 power both ChatGPT and an array of third-party applications, including Microsoft's Co-Pilot and Bing search engine.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"ChatGPT-User\"}),\" \\u2014 Accesses websites in real-time and on-demand so that ChatGPT can formulate responses to user prompts based on the live web. It visits websites to gather information then processes, summarises and synthesizes this to provide the output. It is not used to gather data for model training.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"OAI-SearchBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Used to power ChatGPT's search capabilities. Similar to ChatGPT-User, it accesses the internet in real-time but is optimized for search scenarios, delivering the raw links and search results alongside summaries.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"GPTBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Gathers data from the web for the training of OpenAI's large language models. It operates continuously in the background and the data it gathers is collected and used offline for model development, rather than real-time responses to user prompts.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" OpenAI respects the signals provided by content owners via robots.txt, allowing them to disallow any or all of its crawlers.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher partnerships:\"}),\" OpenAI has an extensive publisher partnerships program, having signed bilateral deals with ~40 publishers at the time of writing.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(c,{title:\"Robots Exclusion Protocol (robots.txt)\",children:(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`This index also refers to the Robots Exclusion Protocol or 'robots.txt'. This\nmechanism allows website owners to give instructions to bots about accessing a\nwebsite. It uses a machine-readable file (named robots.txt) which specifies \\u2014\nfor individual bots or all bots collectively \\u2014 which pages or sections they\ncan or cannot crawl. Robots.txt operates simply as a signal though and does\nnot actively block access. Not all developers program their bots to comply\nwith these instructions.`})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Perplexity\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Perplexity has developed an AI answer engine, effectively AI-powered search that provides users with a natural language response to a prompt alongside a list of links and sources. It has 22 million active users. The standard free product primarily uses a proprietary model, whereas the premium, paid-for service includes access to a range of models including OpenAI's GPT-4 Omni, Claude 3.5 from Anthropic, Llama 3 from Meta and Grok-2 from xAI.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"PerplexityBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Gathers data from across the web to index it for Perplexity's search function. It has also appeared to act in real-time, gathering data to respond to specific user queries.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Perplexity-User\"}),\" \\u2014 Accesses web pages in real-time to respond to user prompts.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" Perplexity claims that its PerplexityBot user agent respects robots.txt. However, there have been widely-reported complaints from publishers that it has ignored their signals.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher partnerships:\"}),\" In 2024 Perplexity launched a publisher partner program under which it provides a share of advertising revenues generated by responses that are based upon the content of media partners. Around 20 deals have been signed so far.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Anthropic\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Founded by seven former OpenAI employees, Anthropic is an AI developer with a focus on privacy, safety and alignment (with human values). Its foundation models power its Claude chatbot \\u2014 which has a free and premium version \\u2014 and a multitude of third-party applications.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"ClaudeBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Used to gather data from the internet for AI training.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Claude-SearchBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Indexes online content to power Claude's web search. This allows Claude-User to retrieve up-to-date information when responding to user queries.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Claude-User\"}),\" \\u2014 Fetches a web page on demand when a user asks a question that requires a live lookup.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" Anthropic's bots respect publisher signals in robots.txt files. Notably they also respond to any disallows for Common Crawl's CCBot.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Google\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Google is one of the foremost AI developers with its own proprietary models that have been integrated extensively into its consumer and enterprise applications, including search. It also operates Gemini as a standalone AI chatbot with real-time web access. This has around 14% of the chatbot market with an estimated 47 million active users.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"GoogleBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Google's all-purpose search bot. It creates the index of the web that the search engine relies on and evidence suggests it is now also operating in real-time to gather information from websites for both AI Overviews and AI Mode.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Google-Extended\"}),\" \\u2014 Used to gather data to train and improve Google's AI models. It operates independently of the crawlers used to power Google's search product.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" Google's bots respect robots.txt signals. However, publishers do not have granular controls over the use of their content in real-time for AI search or Gemini's as these products use the data collected for Google's general search product.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher partnerships:\"}),\" Whilst Google has signed a $60M AI content licensing deal with Reddit, the large number of partnerships it has with publishing businesses are built around its News Showcase product, rather than content for AI.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Meta\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Meta has developed the Llama family of open-source AI models. These are used in Meta's own products and extensively across third-party applications. Whilst historically it has relied on external datasets for model training, it has recently launched a new web crawler to collect data for its LLMs.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Meta-ExternalAgent\"}),\" \\u2014 Described as crawling the web for 'use cases such as training AI models or improving products by indexing content directly'. Launched in summer 2024.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Meta-ExternalFetcher\"}),\" \\u2014 Accesses websites in real-time in response to actions by users. Meta is transparent that this crawler may bypass robots.txt on account of being user-'initiated'.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"FacebookBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Previously described as crawling public web pages to improve language models for speech recognition technology.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" Meta's crawlers respect robots.txt signals although Meta-ExternalFetcher may bypass the protocol because it performs crawls that were user-initiated.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher partnerships:\"}),\" Meta has signed an AI licensing deal with just one publisher \\u2014 Reuters.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Apple\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Apple has been investing in AI \\u2014 including the development of its own foundation models \\u2014 to enhance its products, particularly in privacy-centric applications and on-device AI capabilities.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Applebot\"}),\" \\u2014 Acts as both a search indexing crawler and a RAG agent feeding information to power features across Apple's ecosystem, including Siri, Spotlight and Safari.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Applebot-Extended\"}),\" \\u2014 A secondary user agent that allows publishers to opt-out of their content being used to train Apple's foundation models. Does not crawl webpages; only determines how Apple can use data crawled by the primary Applebot.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" Apple claims that its bots respect robots.txt directives.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher partnerships:\"}),\" Apple has not publicly announced any AI content licensing deals at time of writing but has reportedly been in negotiations with a number of publishers including Cond\\xE9 Nast and NBC News.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Amazon\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"As well as a strategic partnership with Anthropic, Amazon has developed its own 'Nova' family of AI models which emphasize speed and value. These are offered to AWS clients and have been integrated into Alexa and other Amazon services.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"AmazonBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Described as being used to improve services, 'such as enabling Alexa to answer even more questions for customers'.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" AmazonBot respects standard robots.txt rules.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher partnerships:\"}),\" Amazon has three notable AI licensing agreements: a multi-year deal with New York Times, and deals with both Cond\\xE9 Nast and Hearst to supply content for Amazon's AI-powered shopping voice assistant Rufus.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"ByteDance\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"ByteDance \\u2014 owner of TikTok \\u2014 has developed both text and video generation models. Most of these are for research purposes although its Doubao text models power its chatbot, only available in China.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Bytespider\"}),\" \\u2014 Has been scraping the web at a high rate since it first appeared in early 2024. ByteDance has published no information on the function it serves or what the data collected is being used for.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" ByteDance has no published robots.txt policy. There are widespread reports of Bytespider ignoring robots.txt signals.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Other Notable AI User Agents\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"DuckAssistBot\"}),\" \\u2014 DuckDuckGo's web crawler that crawls pages in real time to source information for answers by DuckAssist. Data collected is not used to train AI models and it respects robots.txt signals.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Timpibot\"}),\" \\u2014 Web crawler for Timpi, a decentralized search index, accessible for a cost to businesses. Data is also available to AI developers for model training.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"YouBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Crawler for You.com, an AI-powered search engine that integrates AI query responses alongside conventional search links.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Diffbot\"}),\" \\u2014 Web crawler focused on extracting data from web pages which it then converts into structured datasets for businesses and developers. Fully respects robots.txt directives.\"]}),`\n`]})]})]})}function f(s={}){let{wrapper:t}=s.components||{};return t?(0,e.jsx)(t,{...s,children:(0,e.jsx)(u,{...s})}):u(s)}function a(s,t){throw new Error(\"Expected \"+(t?\"component\":\"object\")+\" `\"+s+\"` to be defined: you likely forgot to import, pass, or provide it.\")}return x(G);})();\n;return Component;"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"44:T8513,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\n\u003cSection id=\"executive-summary\"\u003e\n\n## **Executive Summary**\n\n\u003cExecSummary\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={1}\u003e\n  **RAG bot scrapes now exceed Training bot scrapes** across the TollBit\n  network. From Q4 2024 to Q1 2025, RAG bot scrapes per site grew 49%, nearly\n  2.5X the rate of Training bot scrapes (which grew by 18%). This is a clear\n  signal that AI tools require continuous access to content and data for RAG vs\n  for training.\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={2}\u003e\n  **Among websites with TollBit Analytics set up before January 2025, AI Bot\n  traffic volume nearly doubled in Q1, rising by 87%.**\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={3}\u003e\n  **Publishers have attempted to block 4x more AI bots** between January 2024\n  and January 2025 by disallowing them in their robots.txt file. However, AI\n  bots are increasingly ignoring robots.txt. The percentage of AI Bot scrapes\n  that bypassed robots.txt surged from 3.3% in Q4 2024 to 12.9% by the end of Q1\n  2025 (March). In March 2025, over 26M scrapes from AI bots bypassed robots.txt\n  for sites on TollBit.\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={4}\u003e\n  **Bot traffic directed to the TollBit Bot Paywall has increased by 732%** from\n  Q4 2024 to Q1 2025 as publishers explore more proactive defenses that don't\n  depend solely on the honor system of robots.txt.\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={5}\u003e\n  **Some AI companies are now attempting to set a problematic precedent** of\n  treating bot traffic the same as human visitors. Recent updates to numerous\n  major AI companies' terms of service state that their AI bots can act on\n  behalf of user requests, explicitly stating they can ignore robots.txt\n  guidelines when being used for RAG.\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={6}\u003e\n  **On average across TollBit sites, for every 11 scrapes, Bing returns one\n  human visit.** Scrape-to-referral ratios for AI-only apps are: OpenAI 179:1,\n  Perplexity 369:1, and Anthropic 8692:1.\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={7}\u003e\n  **AI apps and agents continue to drive limited traffic back to publishers.**\n  Across the TollBit network, AI apps drove 0.04% of total external referral\n  traffic to sites. Meanwhile, Google drove 85% of external referral traffic.\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003c/ExecSummary\u003e\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n\n---\n\n\u003cSection id=\"section-1\"\u003e\n\n## **Section 1: The Scale of AI Scraping**\n\n### RAG Scrapes Surpass Training Bot Scrapes\n\n**Key Definitions:**\n\n**Retrieval augmented generation (RAG) Agent** — These bots retrieve information in real-time to respond to user prompts on AI tools, such as Perplexity or ChatGPT, by searching the web. The responses they generate often include links or citations to the original sources of the information.\n\n**Training Data Crawler** — These bots collect data to train large language models (LLMs), such as Claude 3.7 Sonnet or Meta's Llama. These bots move around the web — following links, parsing sitemaps, and downloading content — to build massive datasets. The collected data is then used to \"teach\" the model how to generate language.\n\n\u003cUserStory\u003e\n  RAG bot scraping activity has now surpassed the scraping activity of training\n  bots. Monetizing RAG, aka real-time access, is the recurring revenue\n  opportunity for publishers, as AI applications need ongoing access to maintain\n  the utility of their services.\n\u003c/UserStory\u003e\n\nBy analyzing the functions of the bots driving this overall increase in AI web traffic, we see that RAG bot traffic now exceeds Training bot traffic across the TollBit network. From Q4 2024 to Q1 2025, RAG bot traffic per site grew 49%, nearly 2.5X the rate of Training bot traffic (18%).\n\nThis expansion of server requests from RAG agents is a signal of continued AI adoption, particularly in search-like settings. The more that users turn to these services to retrieve information from the real-time web, even if they don't explicitly instruct the AI applications to do so, we would expect this figure to continue increasing. Some of these journeys would previously have resulted in users landing on publisher websites — probably via Google — with monetization opportunities for the content creators that have now been lost.\n\n**Figure 1.1. AI bot traffic by user-agent type (average, per domain)**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Figure 1.1. AI bot traffic by user-agent type (average, per domain).svg\"\n  alt=\"AI bot traffic by user-agent type\"\n/\u003e\n\n### AI User Agent Traffic Continues to Increase\n\n\u003cUserStory\u003e\n  AI bot traffic continues to grow rapidly, making up an ever-larger proportion\n  of overall publisher traffic. OpenAI, Perplexity, and Meta own the most active\n  AI-specific bots.\n\u003c/UserStory\u003e\n\nAI user agent traffic has grown steadily throughout the first quarter of 2025. Among websites that had Analytics set up before January 2025, traffic volume nearly doubled in Q1, rising by 87% during the quarter.\n\n**Figure 1.2. Daily Total AI Bot Traffic — Cohort of websites that onboarded before Jan'25**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Figure 1.2. Daily Total AI Bot Traffic - Cohort of websites that onboarded before Jan’25.svg\"\n  alt=\"Daily Total AI Bot Traffic\"\n/\u003e\n\nActivity levels in the quarter vary widely across AI user agents and between quarters. Average scraping activity across the top 6 AI bots increased by 56% from Q4 to Q1, though individual bot trends varied significantly. Notably, the scraping activity of PerplexityBot — the hybrid RAG agent and AI search indexing crawler for Perplexity — increased markedly (+359%) from the previous quarter.\n\n**Figure 1.3. Monthly Average Scrapes per site by AI Bots — All sites from Q4 '24 vs Q1 '25**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Figure 1.3. Monthly Average Scrapes per site by AI Bots - All sites from Q4 ‘24 vs Q1 ‘25.svg\"\n  alt=\"Monthly Average Scrapes per site by AI Bots\"\n/\u003e\n\nOut of the top 12 AI bot's scraping activities, ChatGPT, META, and Perplexity are the most active, making up a total of about 70% of the top AI Bots scrapes. Looking at the ownership of the most active bots, OpenAI's user agents are three of the top five most active crawlers and collectively account for over 46% of top 12 AI bot scraping across TollBit partner websites in Q1 of 2025.\n\n**Table 1.4 A breakdown of the Top 12 Bots**\n\n| AI Bot             | Q1 Share |\n| :----------------- | :------: |\n| ChatGPT-User       |  28.50%  |\n| Meta-ExternalAgent |  25.45%  |\n| PerplexityBot      |  16.71%  |\n| OAI-SearchBot      |  11.31%  |\n| GPTBot             |  6.92%   |\n| ClaudeBot          |  5.04%   |\n| Bytespider         |  3.14%   |\n| Timpibot           |  1.02%   |\n| DuckAssistBot      |  0.94%   |\n| Perplexity-User    |  0.42%   |\n| CCBot              |  0.37%   |\n| ai2bot-dolma       |  0.18%   |\n\n_Note: There are other players like Google Overviews, Microsoft Co-pilot, and Apple's AI tools who may have larger consumer reach, but because they do not separate their AI user agents from their crawlers, it is not possible to include them in analyses specific to AI bot traffic._\n\n### Scraping Trends by Content Category\n\nAnalyzing scraping trends across different content categories largely shows consistent growth across the board. This analysis focuses on a cohort of websites that onboarded before Q4 2024 and compares how bot scraping has increased by content category.\n\nNotably, the one decline in scraping noted was in \"Deals \u0026 shopping\" category, which spiked up ahead of holiday shopping at the end of 2024. This is likely a great reflection of user queries, and why AI bots would be fetching more content in accordance with what is being searched.\n\n**Figure 1.5 Average scrapes per page for each category — cohort — Q4 '24 vs Q1 '25**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Figure 1.5 Average scrapes per page for each category - cohort- Q4 ‘24 vs Q1 ‘25.svg\"\n  alt=\"Average scrapes per page for each category\"\n/\u003e\n\n### AI Bot Scraping as a Percentage of Googlebot and Bingbot Activity\n\nIn Q2 2024, the scraping activity of the top 6 AI bots was roughly 10% the size of Googlebot's scraping activity. In Q1 2025, AI bot access to sites is now 60.29% that of Bingbot's activity, and 30.55% of Googlebot's total scraping.\n\n| Quarter | Top AI bot scrapes to Google bots | Top AI bot scrapes to Bingbot |\n| :------ | :-------------------------------: | :---------------------------: |\n| 2024 Q2 |               9.89%               |            10.86%             |\n| 2024 Q3 |              17.85%               |            17.36%             |\n| 2024 Q4 |              21.40%               |            42.44%             |\n| 2025 Q1 |            **30.55%**             |          **60.29%**           |\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Chart after both figure 1.5s.svg\"\n  alt=\"AI bot scraping vs Google and Bing\"\n/\u003e\n\nThis data shows the dramatic increase in AI bot market share when compared to Googlebot and Bingbot activity. Google and Microsoft are companies that publishers have obviously had relationships with for 20+ years. In one year they are being hammered by new crawlers where the value exchange is no longer clear, especially if they don't drive traffic back to sites.\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n\n---\n\n\u003cSection id=\"section-2\"\u003e\n\n## **Section 2: AI App Click-through Rates Remain Extremely Low**\n\n\u003cUserStory\u003e\n  Click-through rates from AI applications remain a small fraction of the\n  click-through rate of Google's organic search results.\n\u003c/UserStory\u003e\n\nClick-through rate is calculated by dividing the number of site visits from an AI application by the number of accesses from that AI app's agent, for which we have a strong signal they act in real-time to fetch content on behalf of an end user.\n\nThis is a proxy and in some cases may overstate the actual click-through rate from AI applications as a) content may be held in an offline cache, or b) third-party or masked user agents may be in use. Both of these would have the effect of artificially inflating the click-through rate by obfuscating the true number of times content was accessed.\n\nThe true number is, of course, with the AI companies.\n\n**Figure 2.1. AI application click-through rates vs Google for Q1 2025**\n\n**Figure 2.2. Average Google click-through rates, 1-10 organic positions**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Figure 2.1. AI application click-through rates vs Google for Q1 2025.svg\"\n  alt=\"AI application click-through rates vs Google\"\n/\u003e\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Average AI applications click-through rate vs Average Google click-through rates, 1-10 organic positions.svg\"\n  alt=\"Average Google click-through rates, 1-10 organic positions\"\n/\u003e\n\n### Referral Traffic from AI Apps Is a Tiny Fraction of External Referrals\n\nLooking in aggregate across TollBit's publishing partners, traffic from AI applications represented just 0.04% of all external referrals to sites in Q1 2025. This is insignificant when compared with Google, which delivers around 85% of traceable external visits.\n\nEven as AI usage accelerates — often in settings which have the potential to substitute engagement on a website — the number of referrals from AI applications remains minuscule. The data reinforces what is intuitively the case — these interfaces are simply not designed to require the user to click through to a website. As a consequence, unless some other form of a fair exchange of value occurs, it is hard to make the case for any course of action other than taking all possible measures to prevent AI bots from scraping publisher content.\n\nIn comparison, Google continues to contribute to the bulk of a publisher's total external referral traffic, at 85%. \"Other\" platforms account for the remaining 15% and is comprised of search engines like Bing, Yahoo, and DuckDuckGo as well as social media platforms like LinkedIn and TikTok.\n\nThere are two considerations for the industry as this continues to unfold:\n\n1. Referrals from AI apps seem to have doubled from Q4 2024 to Q1 2025. However, this is likely driven by more adoption of those platforms, not an indication that the platforms are driving more traffic back. This number should be compared to the drop in Google referrals from 90.75% Q2 2024 to 85.056% in Q1 2025. If users are changing their behavior to use more AI tools, this drop from Google referrals is not nearly made up for in referrals by AI search platforms. Part of this drop may also be due to the impact of AI summaries in search.\n\n2. As this trend continues, the web will see increasing amounts of traffic from bots. This incurs growing cost on publisher infrastructure. In the past, the value exchange for crawling was that the platforms would drive traffic. However, this value exchange needs to be reexamined as AI visitors grow if they do not send back traffic to sites.\n\n**Figure 2.3. Sources of referral traffic across publisher sites**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Figure 2.3. Sources of referral traffic across publisher sites.svg\"\n  alt=\"Sources of referral traffic across publisher sites\"\n/\u003e\n\n_Note: The blue line is not representative of 0.04%; this line would not even appear on the chart if accurately displayed._\n\n**Figure 2.4. Sources of external referral traffic across publisher sites (Q2 2024 — Q1 2025)**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Figure 2.4. Sources of external referral traffic across publisher sites (Q2 2024 - Q1 2025).svg\"\n  alt=\"Sources of external referral traffic\"\n/\u003e\n\nA key reminder is that Google referral traffic is also falling each quarter. Referral traffic from AI Bots is still minuscule and nowhere near enough to offset the broader decline.\n\n### Crawl-to-Referral Ratio\n\nIn Q1 2025, on average across TollBit sites, for every 11 crawls, Bing returns one human visitor to sites. This means that Bing's crawl-to-referral ratio is 11:1, up from 8:1 in Q4 2024 and 6:1 in Q3. Crawl-to-referral ratios for AI-only apps are as follows:\n\n- **OpenAI's ratio is 179:1** — improved slightly, dropping from 286:1 in Q4 2024, but still far exceeds Bing's.\n- **Perplexity's ratio is 369:1** — crawling activity surged, increasing from 136:1 in just a quarter.\n- **Anthropic's ratio is 8692:1** — the highest gap, scraping content 8,692 times for every visit it sent, up from 5,880:1 in Q4 2024.\n\nIt is important to note that each AI platform behaves differently, with a UI that is unique. This crawl to referral ratio shows how these changes contribute widely to click throughs from the platforms. It is also worth commending proper use of the user agents for the platforms that separate out crawlers for indexing, training, and retrieval. Many of the up and coming \"agent\" platforms do not discernibly identify themselves, and worse, actively try to pass off as humans by using residential IP addresses or headless browsers.\n\n**Figure 2.5. Number of crawls for each referral by source**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Number of crawls for each referral by source.svg\"\n  alt=\"Number of crawls for each referral by source\"\n/\u003e\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n\n---\n\n\u003cSection id=\"section-3\"\u003e\n\n## **Section 3: Robots Exclusion Protocol — Adoption and Compliance**\n\n### Disallowing RAG Agents on Robots.txt Isn't Enough to Protect Sites\n\n\u003cUserStory\u003e\n  Our data demonstrates that, for certain AI applications, disallowing real-time\n  scraping by RAG bots on Robots.txt has zero impact on the referrals the AI\n  apps deliver. This is likely due to usage of third-party scrapers or masked\n  user agents who continue to scrape sites under the radar.\n\u003c/UserStory\u003e\n\nIn our conversations with publishers, many tell us that they are opting to allow AI bots to scrape their sites in order to ensure that they do not miss out on any referrals that might flow from AI applications. In our first _State of the Bots_ report we demonstrated that the click-through rate from AI platforms is 95.7% lower than for organic results on the first page of Google's search engine results page (we revisit this data for Q1 2025 above).\n\nIn addition to the very low traffic, we also see that, for some AI applications, these external referrals continue to stay consistent even when bots stop scraping sites after being disallowed on robots.txt. This highly likely indicates that some AI apps continue to obtain access to the content even if they appear to be abiding by being disallowed on robots.txt — whether it's by using a third-party scraper or a masked user agent.\n\n**Figure 3.1. PerplexityBot scrape count, cohort of sites that disallow Perplexity on Robots.txt**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Figure 3.1. PerplexityBot scrape count, cohort of sites that disallow Perplexity on Robots.txt.svg\"\n  alt=\"PerplexityBot scrape count for disallowed sites\"\n/\u003e\n\n**Figure 3.2. Perplexity referrals for a cohort of sites that disallow PerplexityBot on Robots.txt**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Figure 3.2. Perplexity referrals for a cohort of sites that disallow PerplexityBot on Robots.txt.svg\"\n  alt=\"Perplexity referrals for disallowed sites\"\n/\u003e\n\n### Publishers Are Trying to Control AI Use of Their Intellectual Property\n\n\u003cUserStory\u003e\n  Over the last year, the number of AI bots that publishers have attempted to\n  block AI bots by disallowing them via their robots.txt files has increased by\n  4x. Website owners are more active in their attempts to restrict unauthorized\n  use of their content. Notably, AI companies have recently begun changing the\n  descriptions of their user agents to specify that they do not always have to\n  abide by restrictions on robots.txt.\n\u003c/UserStory\u003e\n\nTollBit has analysed the robots.txt files of a large cohort of its publishing partners and whether, and how, they have changed from January 2024 to January 2025. We can see that the number of explicit disallow requests for AI bots (i.e. a single site disallowing a single bot would count as one) has increased from 559 to 2,165 (+287%). The average number of AI bots explicitly disallowed per website has grown from 2.2 to 8.6, a 4x increase.\n\n**Figure 3.3. Robots.txt analysis example**\n\n_Example of the evolution of a publisher's robots.txt file from January 2024 to January 2025_\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Figure 3.3. Robots.txt analysis example.svg\"\n  alt=\"Robots.txt analysis example\"\n/\u003e\n\n**Figure 3.4. Total AI bot disallow requests**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Figure 3.4. Total AI bot disallow requests.svg\"\n  alt=\"Total AI bot disallow requests\"\n/\u003e\n\nWhen we look at the specific disallow requests, we can see widespread attempts by publishers to restrict AI bots access to their sites. Interestingly, the number of disallow requests to OpenAI's training data crawler have decreased; this could be due to a combination of OpenAI's partnership program and publishers hoping that referrals from ChatGPT will follow. As we explore in section 2 of this report, the data suggests referrals from AI apps remain extremely low.\n\n**Figure 3.5. Change in Disallowing Bots via Robots.txt Across Websites (2024 vs 2025)**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Figure 3.5. Change in Disallowing Bots via Robots.txt Across Websites (2024 vs 2025).svg\"\n  alt=\"Change in Disallowing Bots via Robots.txt\"\n/\u003e\n\n### AI Companies Changing User Agent Descriptions\n\nSeemingly in response to the increases in websites' attempts to block bots by disallowing them on robots.txt, AI companies are changing the descriptions of their user agents. Some bot descriptions are explicitly stating that, particularly in circumstances where the agent is fetching information in real-time to service a user request, the crawlers will ignore robots.txt instructions.\n\nSince the first _State of the Bots_ report in Q4 2024, Perplexity has added a second user agent, Perplexity-User, which operates as a RAG bot. While this separation is beneficial in that it could help publishers control how their content is used, Perplexity-User is among the bots that does not appear to respect robots.txt instructions.\n\nRobots.txt has been a guideline/handshake agreement that bots agreed to follow since the beginning of the Internet. It sufficed in a world where humans were the primary drivers of economic value on the internet. AI visitors will vastly outnumber human visitors in the future, and we need governance on how agentic visitors identify themselves on the internet.\n\n**Agents and bots should have unique identities on the Internet, separate from human visitors. Bots mimicking humans or being able to take actions as an extension of a human without connected identification to the human sets a dangerous precedent.** This is poised to have massive impacts on advertiser trust, site analytics, and the traffic load to sites, all with limited benefits to the sites being scraped.\n\n**Screenshot 1. Perplexity's developer notes (taken 13th April 2025)**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Screenshot 1. Perplexity’s developer notes (taken 13th April 2025).svg\"\n  alt=\"Perplexity developer notes\"\n/\u003e\n\n**Screenshot 2. Google's developer notes (taken 13th April 2025)**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Screenshot 2. Google’s developer notes (taken 13th April 2025).svg\"\n  alt=\"Google developer notes\"\n/\u003e\n\n**Screenshot 3. Meta's developer notes (taken 13th April 2025)**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Screenshot 3. Meta’s developer notes (taken 13th April 2025).svg\"\n  alt=\"Meta developer notes\"\n/\u003e\n\n### Bot Paywall as an Alternative Defense\n\nInstead of just relying on disallowing bots on Robots.txt, one way websites are forcing AI companies off their sites is by exploring bot-blocking solutions. A cybersecurity tool can be used to detect and redirect AI bot traffic away from the human version of your website. TollBit provides basic cybersecurity measures to its sites and also partners with cybersecurity vendors (like HUMAN Security) to provide a more robust solution to websites on the platform.\n\nThis allows us to build towards the future. Instead of just blocking sites, websites on TollBit can forward the traffic to the tollbit subdomain (e.g. tollbit.time.com). This allows websites to expose parallel infrastructure that is optimized for AI traffic. The number of bots directed to the TollBit Bot Paywall has increased by 732% in Q1 2025 versus Q4 2024.\n\nThis alternative gateway of sanctioned web access helps to:\n\n- Prevent any ads from being inadvertently served to AI visitors\n- Ensure human visitor site metrics don't get impacted by bot traffic\n- Allow AI bots to be presented with an option to pay for access with added benefits\n\n**Figure 3.6. AI bots directed to TollBit Bot Paywall**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Figure 3.6. AI bots directed to TollBit Bot Paywall.svg\"\n  alt=\"AI bots directed to TollBit Bot Paywall\"\n/\u003e\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n\n---\n\n\u003cSection id=\"appendix\"\u003e\n\n## **Appendix: AI User Agent Profiles**\n\n### New User Agents in Q1 2025\n\n**Perplexity-User**\n\nAnnounced at the start of March, Perplexity's new user agent acts in real-time to 'support user actions' within Perplexity. We have therefore categorised Perplexity-User as a RAG agent however, in testing at the start of April 2025, TollBit has found that both PerplexityBot and Perplexity-User will scrape a page in real-time when Perplexity is prompted.\n\n**Claude-User**\n\nAlso announced in March — and coinciding with Claude's new web-access functionality — Claude-User is Anthropic's new RAG agent.\n\n**Claude-SearchBot**\n\nWith details published at the same time as Claude-User, Claude-SearchBot is the search indexing crawler to power Claude's web access.\n\n---\n\n### AI Bot Profiles by Operating Organization\n\n**OpenAI**\n\nDeveloper of ChatGPT, OpenAI's chatbot was first-to-market and still holds a ~60% share with 400M weekly active users as of April 2025. Its foundation models — and therefore user agents — power both ChatGPT and an array of third-party applications, including Microsoft's Co-Pilot and Bing search engine.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **ChatGPT-User** — Accesses websites in real-time and on-demand so that ChatGPT can formulate responses to user prompts based on the live web. It visits websites to gather information then processes, summarizes and synthesizes this to provide the output. It is not used to gather data for model training.\n- **OAI-SearchBot** — An indexing crawler used to power ChatGPT's search capabilities.\n- **GPTBot** — Gathers data from the web for the training of OpenAI's large language models. It operates continuously in the background and the data it gathers is collected and used offline for model development, rather than real-time responses to user prompts.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ OpenAI respects the signals provided by content owners via robots.txt, allowing them to disallow any or all of its crawlers.\n\n_Publisher partnerships:_ OpenAI has an extensive publisher partnerships program, having signed bilateral deals with ~35 publishers at the time of writing.\n\n\u003cInfoBox title=\"Robots Exclusion Protocol (robots.txt)\"\u003e\n  This index also refers to the Robots Exclusion Protocol or 'robots.txt'. This\n  mechanism allows website owners to give instructions to bots about accessing a\n  website. It uses a machine-readable file (named robots.txt) which specifies —\n  for individual bots or all bots collectively — which pages or sections they\n  can or cannot crawl. Robots.txt operates simply as a signal though and does\n  not actively block access. Not all developers program their bots to comply\n  with these instructions.\n\u003c/InfoBox\u003e\n\n---\n\n**Perplexity**\n\nPerplexity has developed an AI answer engine, effectively AI-powered search that provides users with a natural language response to a prompt alongside a list of links and sources. It has 15 million active users. The standard free product primarily uses OpenAI's GPT3.5 model, whereas the premium, paid-for service includes access to a range of models including Claude 3.5 from Anthropic, Llama 3 from Meta and Grok-2 from xAI.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **PerplexityBot** — Gathers data from across the web to index it for Perplexity's search function. It also acts in real-time, gathering data to respond to specific user queries as they are placed.\n- **Perplexity-User** — Announced in March 2025, acts in real-time to 'support user actions' within Perplexity.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ Perplexity claims that its web crawler respects robots.txt. However, there have been widely-reported complaints from publishers that it has ignored their signals, leading to an investigation from Amazon, its cloud provider. In its FAQs, Perplexity explains that 'if a page is blocked, we may still index the domain, headline, and a brief factual summary'.\n\n_Publisher partnerships:_ In 2024 Perplexity launched a publisher partner program under which it provides a share of advertising revenues generated by responses that are based upon the content of media partners. Around 20 deals have been signed so far, mostly (although not exclusively) with smaller or niche publishers. The revenue shared with publishers is reported to be capped at 25%.\n\n---\n\n**Anthropic**\n\nFounded by seven former OpenAI employees, Anthropic is an AI developer with a focus on privacy, safety and alignment (with human values). Its foundation models power its Claude chatbot — which has a free and premium version — and a multitude of third-party applications.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **ClaudeBot** — Used to gather data from the internet for AI training. The Claude chatbot does not have access to the real-time web and has no search functionality; the data ClaudeBot retrieves is only used offline for the development of new large language models.\n- **Claude-User** — Announced in March — and coinciding with Claude's new web-access functionality — Claude-User is Anthropic's RAG agent.\n- **Claude-SearchBot** — The search indexing crawler to power Claude's web access.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ Anthropic's bots respect publisher signals in robots.txt files. Notably they also respond to any disallows for Common Crawl's CCBot.\n\n---\n\n**Google**\n\nGoogle is one of the foremost AI developers with its own proprietary models that have been integrated extensively into its consumer and enterprise applications, including search. It also operates Gemini as a standalone AI chatbot with real-time web access. This has around 14% of the chatbot market with an estimated 42 million active users.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **Google-Extended** — Used to gather data to train and improve Google's AI models. It operates independently of the crawlers used to power Google's search product. It should be noted that both Gemini, when it requires data from the live web, and AI Overviews (the natural language AI response to search queries) do not rely on Google-Extended for real-time data retrieval and therefore disallowing this bot does not control whether a publisher's content is used to inform the outputs of these products.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ Google's bots respect robots.txt signals. However, publishers do not have granular controls over the use of their content in real-time for AI Overviews or Gemini's responses as these products appear to use the data collected for Google's general search product rather than a discrete user agent. In order to prevent content being used for these applications publishers need to use the no-snippet directive or signal Google to stop indexing a page for search entirely. Both of these would have negative effects on prominence and referral traffic.\n\n_Publisher partnerships:_ Whilst Google has signed a $60M AI content licensing deal with Reddit, the large number of partnerships it has with publishing businesses are built around its News Showcase product, rather than content for AI. As competition authorities examine its conduct around Gemini and search, it may soon have to start securing explicit authorization for access to content to fuel these products.\n\n---\n\n**Meta**\n\nMeta has developed the Llama family of open-source AI models. These are used in Meta's own products and extensively across third-party applications. Whilst historically it has relied on external datasets for model training, it has recently launched a new web crawler to collect data for its LLMs.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **Meta-ExternalAgent** — Described as crawling the web for 'use cases such as training AI models or improving products by indexing content directly'. Launched in summer 2024.\n- **Meta-ExternalFetcher** — Accesses websites in real-time in response to actions by users. Meta is transparent that this crawler may bypass robots.txt on account of being user-'initiated'.\n- **FacebookBot** — Previously described as crawling public web pages to improve language models for speech recognition technology. It has since removed this description from its developer site and it is not known whether the bot is now being used for different purposes.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ Meta's crawlers respect robots.txt signals although the Meta-ExternalFetcher bot may bypass the protocol because it performs crawls that were user-initiated.\n\n_Publisher partnerships:_ Meta has signed an AI licensing deal with just one publisher — Reuters.\n\n---\n\n**Apple**\n\nApple has been investing in AI — including the development of its own foundation models — to enhance its products, particularly in privacy-centric applications and on-device AI capabilities.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **Applebot-Extended** — Apple's primary user agent is AppleBot. This is used to collect data to feed into a variety of user products in the Apple ecosystem, including Spotlight, Siri and Safari. Applebot-Extended is a secondary user agent that allows publishers to opt-out of their content being used to train Apple's foundation models. Applebot-Extended does not crawl webpages; it is only used to determine how Apple can use the data crawled by the primary Applebot user agent.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ Applebot-Extended respects robots.txt directives, allowing website owners to control the use of their content for AI training.\n\n_Publisher partnerships:_ Apple has not publicly announced any AI content licensing deals at time of writing but is reportedly in negotiations with a number of publishers including Condé Nast and NBC News.\n\n---\n\n**Amazon**\n\nAs well as a strategic partnership with Anthropic, Amazon has developed its own 'Nova' family of AI models which emphasize speed and value.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **AmazonBot** — Described as being used to improve services, 'such as enabling Alexa to answer even more questions for customers'. There are no published details of how it operates, or what the data it captures is used for.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ AmazonBot respects standard robots.txt rules.\n\n_Publisher partnerships:_ Amazon is reportedly in licensing negotiations with a number of news outlets for access to content that will give a revamped Alexa the ability to answer questions about current events.\n\n---\n\n**ByteDance**\n\nAt the time of writing ByteDance, owner of TikTok, has no live AI applications but is widely reported to be developing its own foundation models.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **Bytespider** — Has been scraping the web at a high rate since it first appeared in early 2024. ByteDance has published no information on the function it serves or what the data collected is being used for.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ ByteDance has no published robots.txt policy. There are widespread reports of Bytespider ignoring robots.txt signals.\n\n---\n\n**Other Notable AI User Agents**\n\n- **DuckAssistBot** — DuckDuckGo's web crawler that crawls pages in real-time to source information for answers by DuckAssist. Data collected is not used to train AI models and it respects robots.txt signals.\n- **Timpibot** — Web crawler for Timpi, a decentralized search index, accessible for a cost to businesses. Data is also available to AI developers for model training.\n- **YouBot** — Crawler for You.com, an AI-powered search engine that integrates AI query responses alongside conventional search links.\n- **Diffbot** — Web crawler focused on extracting data from web pages which it then converts into structured datasets for businesses and developers. Fully respects robots.txt directives.\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"45:Tb7cf,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"var Component=(()=\u003e{var b=Object.create;var c=Object.defineProperty;var m=Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor;var y=Object.getOwnPropertyNames;var w=Object.getPrototypeOf,A=Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty;var v=(i,t)=\u003e()=\u003e{try{return t||i((t={exports:{}}).exports,t),t.exports}catch(o){throw t=0,o}},x=(i,t)=\u003e{for(var o in t)c(i,o,{get:t[o],enumerable:!0})},d=(i,t,o,l)=\u003e{if(t\u0026\u0026typeof t==\"object\"||typeof t==\"function\")for(let r of y(t))!A.call(i,r)\u0026\u0026r!==o\u0026\u0026c(i,r,{get:()=\u003et[r],enumerable:!(l=m(t,r))||l.enumerable});return i};var I=(i,t,o)=\u003e(o=i!=null?b(w(i)):{},d(t||!i||!i.__esModule?c(o,\"default\",{value:i,enumerable:!0}):o,i)),T=i=\u003ed(c({},\"__esModule\",{value:!0}),i);var g=v((G,p)=\u003e{p.exports=_jsx_runtime});var k={};x(k,{default:()=\u003ef,frontmatter:()=\u003eB});var e=I(g()),B={title:\"State of the Bots\",id:\"q1-2025\",quarter:\"q1-2025\",description:\"2025 Q1, The Rise of RAG Bots\",docType:\"report\",sections:[{title:\"Executive Summary\",slug:\"executive-summary\"},{title:\"Section 1: Scale of AI Scraping\",slug:\"section-1\",subsections:[{title:\"RAG vs Training Bots\",slug:\"rag-vs-training\"},{title:\"AI Traffic Growth\",slug:\"traffic-growth\"},{title:\"Scraping by Category\",slug:\"scraping-category\"},{title:\"AI vs Google/Bing\",slug:\"ai-vs-search\"}]},{title:\"Section 2: Click-through Rates\",slug:\"section-2\",subsections:[{title:\"AI vs Google CTR\",slug:\"ai-vs-google-ctr\"},{title:\"Referral Traffic\",slug:\"referral-traffic\"},{title:\"Crawl-to-Referral Ratio\",slug:\"crawl-referral-ratio\"}]},{title:\"Section 3: Robots.txt Compliance\",slug:\"section-3\",subsections:[{title:\"Disallow Effectiveness\",slug:\"disallow-effectiveness\"},{title:\"Publisher IP Controls\",slug:\"ip-controls\"},{title:\"Bot Paywall\",slug:\"bot-paywall\"}]},{title:\"Appendix: AI User Agent Profiles\",slug:\"appendix\",subsections:[{title:\"New User Agents\",slug:\"new-agents\"},{title:\"Bot Profiles by Organization\",slug:\"bot-profiles\"}]}]};function u(i){let t={em:\"em\",h2:\"h2\",h3:\"h3\",hr:\"hr\",li:\"li\",ol:\"ol\",p:\"p\",strong:\"strong\",table:\"table\",tbody:\"tbody\",td:\"td\",th:\"th\",thead:\"thead\",tr:\"tr\",ul:\"ul\",...i.components},{ExecItem:o,ExecSummary:l,Image:r,InfoBox:h,Section:s,UserStory:n}=t;return o||a(\"ExecItem\",!0),l||a(\"ExecSummary\",!0),r||a(\"Image\",!0),h||a(\"InfoBox\",!0),s||a(\"Section\",!0),n||a(\"UserStory\",!0),(0,e.jsxs)(e.Fragment,{children:[(0,e.jsxs)(s,{id:\"executive-summary\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Executive Summary\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(l,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(o,{num:1,children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"RAG bot scrapes now exceed Training bot scrapes\"}),` across the TollBit\nnetwork. From Q4 2024 to Q1 2025, RAG bot scrapes per site grew 49%, nearly\n2.5X the rate of Training bot scrapes (which grew by 18%). This is a clear\nsignal that AI tools require continuous access to content and data for RAG vs\nfor training.`]})}),(0,e.jsx)(o,{num:2,children:(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:`Among websites with TollBit Analytics set up before January 2025, AI Bot\ntraffic volume nearly doubled in Q1, rising by 87%.`})})}),(0,e.jsx)(o,{num:3,children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Publishers have attempted to block 4x more AI bots\"}),` between January 2024\nand January 2025 by disallowing them in their robots.txt file. However, AI\nbots are increasingly ignoring robots.txt. The percentage of AI Bot scrapes\nthat bypassed robots.txt surged from 3.3% in Q4 2024 to 12.9% by the end of Q1\n2025 (March). In March 2025, over 26M scrapes from AI bots bypassed robots.txt\nfor sites on TollBit.`]})}),(0,e.jsx)(o,{num:4,children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Bot traffic directed to the TollBit Bot Paywall has increased by 732%\"}),` from\nQ4 2024 to Q1 2025 as publishers explore more proactive defenses that don't\ndepend solely on the honor system of robots.txt.`]})}),(0,e.jsx)(o,{num:5,children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Some AI companies are now attempting to set a problematic precedent\"}),` of\ntreating bot traffic the same as human visitors. Recent updates to numerous\nmajor AI companies' terms of service state that their AI bots can act on\nbehalf of user requests, explicitly stating they can ignore robots.txt\nguidelines when being used for RAG.`]})}),(0,e.jsx)(o,{num:6,children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:`On average across TollBit sites, for every 11 scrapes, Bing returns one\nhuman visit.`}),` Scrape-to-referral ratios for AI-only apps are: OpenAI 179:1,\nPerplexity 369:1, and Anthropic 8692:1.`]})}),(0,e.jsx)(o,{num:7,children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"AI apps and agents continue to drive limited traffic back to publishers.\"}),`\nAcross the TollBit network, AI apps drove 0.04% of total external referral\ntraffic to sites. Meanwhile, Google drove 85% of external referral traffic.`]})})]})]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(s,{id:\"section-1\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Section 1: The Scale of AI Scraping\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"RAG Scrapes Surpass Training Bot Scrapes\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Key Definitions:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Retrieval augmented generation (RAG) Agent\"}),\" \\u2014 These bots retrieve information in real-time to respond to user prompts on AI tools, such as Perplexity or ChatGPT, by searching the web. The responses they generate often include links or citations to the original sources of the information.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Training Data Crawler\"}),` \\u2014 These bots collect data to train large language models (LLMs), such as Claude 3.7 Sonnet or Meta's Llama. These bots move around the web \\u2014 following links, parsing sitemaps, and downloading content \\u2014 to build massive datasets. The collected data is then used to \"teach\" the model how to generate language.`]}),(0,e.jsx)(n,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`RAG bot scraping activity has now surpassed the scraping activity of training\nbots. Monetizing RAG, aka real-time access, is the recurring revenue\nopportunity for publishers, as AI applications need ongoing access to maintain\nthe utility of their services.`})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"By analyzing the functions of the bots driving this overall increase in AI web traffic, we see that RAG bot traffic now exceeds Training bot traffic across the TollBit network. From Q4 2024 to Q1 2025, RAG bot traffic per site grew 49%, nearly 2.5X the rate of Training bot traffic (18%).\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"This expansion of server requests from RAG agents is a signal of continued AI adoption, particularly in search-like settings. The more that users turn to these services to retrieve information from the real-time web, even if they don't explicitly instruct the AI applications to do so, we would expect this figure to continue increasing. Some of these journeys would previously have resulted in users landing on publisher websites \\u2014 probably via Google \\u2014 with monetization opportunities for the content creators that have now been lost.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 1.1. AI bot traffic by user-agent type (average, per domain)\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Figure 1.1. AI bot traffic by user-agent type (average, per domain).svg\",alt:\"AI bot traffic by user-agent type\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"AI User Agent Traffic Continues to Increase\"}),(0,e.jsx)(n,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`AI bot traffic continues to grow rapidly, making up an ever-larger proportion\nof overall publisher traffic. OpenAI, Perplexity, and Meta own the most active\nAI-specific bots.`})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"AI user agent traffic has grown steadily throughout the first quarter of 2025. Among websites that had Analytics set up before January 2025, traffic volume nearly doubled in Q1, rising by 87% during the quarter.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 1.2. Daily Total AI Bot Traffic \\u2014 Cohort of websites that onboarded before Jan'25\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Figure 1.2. Daily Total AI Bot Traffic - Cohort of websites that onboarded before Jan\\u201925.svg\",alt:\"Daily Total AI Bot Traffic\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Activity levels in the quarter vary widely across AI user agents and between quarters. Average scraping activity across the top 6 AI bots increased by 56% from Q4 to Q1, though individual bot trends varied significantly. Notably, the scraping activity of PerplexityBot \\u2014 the hybrid RAG agent and AI search indexing crawler for Perplexity \\u2014 increased markedly (+359%) from the previous quarter.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 1.3. Monthly Average Scrapes per site by AI Bots \\u2014 All sites from Q4 '24 vs Q1 '25\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Figure 1.3. Monthly Average Scrapes per site by AI Bots - All sites from Q4 \\u201824 vs Q1 \\u201825.svg\",alt:\"Monthly Average Scrapes per site by AI Bots\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Out of the top 12 AI bot's scraping activities, ChatGPT, META, and Perplexity are the most active, making up a total of about 70% of the top AI Bots scrapes. Looking at the ownership of the most active bots, OpenAI's user agents are three of the top five most active crawlers and collectively account for over 46% of top 12 AI bot scraping across TollBit partner websites in Q1 of 2025.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Table 1.4 A breakdown of the Top 12 Bots\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.table,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.thead,{children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.th,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"AI Bot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.th,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"Q1 Share\"})]})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tbody,{children:[(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"ChatGPT-User\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"28.50%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Meta-ExternalAgent\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"25.45%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"PerplexityBot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"16.71%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"OAI-SearchBot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"11.31%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"GPTBot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"6.92%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"ClaudeBot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"5.04%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Bytespider\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"3.14%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Timpibot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"1.02%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"DuckAssistBot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"0.94%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Perplexity-User\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"0.42%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"CCBot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"0.37%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"ai2bot-dolma\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"0.18%\"})]})]})]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Note: There are other players like Google Overviews, Microsoft Co-pilot, and Apple's AI tools who may have larger consumer reach, but because they do not separate their AI user agents from their crawlers, it is not possible to include them in analyses specific to AI bot traffic.\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"Scraping Trends by Content Category\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Analyzing scraping trends across different content categories largely shows consistent growth across the board. This analysis focuses on a cohort of websites that onboarded before Q4 2024 and compares how bot scraping has increased by content category.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:'Notably, the one decline in scraping noted was in \"Deals \u0026 shopping\" category, which spiked up ahead of holiday shopping at the end of 2024. This is likely a great reflection of user queries, and why AI bots would be fetching more content in accordance with what is being searched.'}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 1.5 Average scrapes per page for each category \\u2014 cohort \\u2014 Q4 '24 vs Q1 '25\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Figure 1.5 Average scrapes per page for each category - cohort- Q4 \\u201824 vs Q1 \\u201825.svg\",alt:\"Average scrapes per page for each category\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"AI Bot Scraping as a Percentage of Googlebot and Bingbot Activity\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"In Q2 2024, the scraping activity of the top 6 AI bots was roughly 10% the size of Googlebot's scraping activity. In Q1 2025, AI bot access to sites is now 60.29% that of Bingbot's activity, and 30.55% of Googlebot's total scraping.\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.table,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.thead,{children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.th,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Quarter\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.th,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"Top AI bot scrapes to Google bots\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.th,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"Top AI bot scrapes to Bingbot\"})]})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tbody,{children:[(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"2024 Q2\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"9.89%\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"10.86%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"2024 Q3\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"17.85%\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"17.36%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"2024 Q4\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"21.40%\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"42.44%\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"2025 Q1\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"30.55%\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"60.29%\"})})]})]})]}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Chart after both figure 1.5s.svg\",alt:\"AI bot scraping vs Google and Bing\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"This data shows the dramatic increase in AI bot market share when compared to Googlebot and Bingbot activity. Google and Microsoft are companies that publishers have obviously had relationships with for 20+ years. In one year they are being hammered by new crawlers where the value exchange is no longer clear, especially if they don't drive traffic back to sites.\"})]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(s,{id:\"section-2\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Section 2: AI App Click-through Rates Remain Extremely Low\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(n,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`Click-through rates from AI applications remain a small fraction of the\nclick-through rate of Google's organic search results.`})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Click-through rate is calculated by dividing the number of site visits from an AI application by the number of accesses from that AI app's agent, for which we have a strong signal they act in real-time to fetch content on behalf of an end user.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"This is a proxy and in some cases may overstate the actual click-through rate from AI applications as a) content may be held in an offline cache, or b) third-party or masked user agents may be in use. Both of these would have the effect of artificially inflating the click-through rate by obfuscating the true number of times content was accessed.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"The true number is, of course, with the AI companies.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.1. AI application click-through rates vs Google for Q1 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.2. Average Google click-through rates, 1-10 organic positions\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Figure 2.1. AI application click-through rates vs Google for Q1 2025.svg\",alt:\"AI application click-through rates vs Google\"}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Average AI applications click-through rate vs Average Google click-through rates, 1-10 organic positions.svg\",alt:\"Average Google click-through rates, 1-10 organic positions\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"Referral Traffic from AI Apps Is a Tiny Fraction of External Referrals\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Looking in aggregate across TollBit's publishing partners, traffic from AI applications represented just 0.04% of all external referrals to sites in Q1 2025. This is insignificant when compared with Google, which delivers around 85% of traceable external visits.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Even as AI usage accelerates \\u2014 often in settings which have the potential to substitute engagement on a website \\u2014 the number of referrals from AI applications remains minuscule. The data reinforces what is intuitively the case \\u2014 these interfaces are simply not designed to require the user to click through to a website. As a consequence, unless some other form of a fair exchange of value occurs, it is hard to make the case for any course of action other than taking all possible measures to prevent AI bots from scraping publisher content.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`In comparison, Google continues to contribute to the bulk of a publisher's total external referral traffic, at 85%. \"Other\" platforms account for the remaining 15% and is comprised of search engines like Bing, Yahoo, and DuckDuckGo as well as social media platforms like LinkedIn and TikTok.`}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"There are two considerations for the industry as this continues to unfold:\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ol,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Referrals from AI apps seem to have doubled from Q4 2024 to Q1 2025. However, this is likely driven by more adoption of those platforms, not an indication that the platforms are driving more traffic back. This number should be compared to the drop in Google referrals from 90.75% Q2 2024 to 85.056% in Q1 2025. If users are changing their behavior to use more AI tools, this drop from Google referrals is not nearly made up for in referrals by AI search platforms. Part of this drop may also be due to the impact of AI summaries in search.\"}),`\n`]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"As this trend continues, the web will see increasing amounts of traffic from bots. This incurs growing cost on publisher infrastructure. In the past, the value exchange for crawling was that the platforms would drive traffic. However, this value exchange needs to be reexamined as AI visitors grow if they do not send back traffic to sites.\"}),`\n`]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.3. Sources of referral traffic across publisher sites\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Figure 2.3. Sources of referral traffic across publisher sites.svg\",alt:\"Sources of referral traffic across publisher sites\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Note: The blue line is not representative of 0.04%; this line would not even appear on the chart if accurately displayed.\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.4. Sources of external referral traffic across publisher sites (Q2 2024 \\u2014 Q1 2025)\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Figure 2.4. Sources of external referral traffic across publisher sites (Q2 2024 - Q1 2025).svg\",alt:\"Sources of external referral traffic\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"A key reminder is that Google referral traffic is also falling each quarter. Referral traffic from AI Bots is still minuscule and nowhere near enough to offset the broader decline.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"Crawl-to-Referral Ratio\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"In Q1 2025, on average across TollBit sites, for every 11 crawls, Bing returns one human visitor to sites. This means that Bing's crawl-to-referral ratio is 11:1, up from 8:1 in Q4 2024 and 6:1 in Q3. Crawl-to-referral ratios for AI-only apps are as follows:\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"OpenAI's ratio is 179:1\"}),\" \\u2014 improved slightly, dropping from 286:1 in Q4 2024, but still far exceeds Bing's.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Perplexity's ratio is 369:1\"}),\" \\u2014 crawling activity surged, increasing from 136:1 in just a quarter.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Anthropic's ratio is 8692:1\"}),\" \\u2014 the highest gap, scraping content 8,692 times for every visit it sent, up from 5,880:1 in Q4 2024.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:'It is important to note that each AI platform behaves differently, with a UI that is unique. This crawl to referral ratio shows how these changes contribute widely to click throughs from the platforms. It is also worth commending proper use of the user agents for the platforms that separate out crawlers for indexing, training, and retrieval. Many of the up and coming \"agent\" platforms do not discernibly identify themselves, and worse, actively try to pass off as humans by using residential IP addresses or headless browsers.'}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.5. Number of crawls for each referral by source\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Number of crawls for each referral by source.svg\",alt:\"Number of crawls for each referral by source\"})]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(s,{id:\"section-3\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Section 3: Robots Exclusion Protocol \\u2014 Adoption and Compliance\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"Disallowing RAG Agents on Robots.txt Isn't Enough to Protect Sites\"}),(0,e.jsx)(n,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`Our data demonstrates that, for certain AI applications, disallowing real-time\nscraping by RAG bots on Robots.txt has zero impact on the referrals the AI\napps deliver. This is likely due to usage of third-party scrapers or masked\nuser agents who continue to scrape sites under the radar.`})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[\"In our conversations with publishers, many tell us that they are opting to allow AI bots to scrape their sites in order to ensure that they do not miss out on any referrals that might flow from AI applications. In our first \",(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"State of the Bots\"}),\" report we demonstrated that the click-through rate from AI platforms is 95.7% lower than for organic results on the first page of Google's search engine results page (we revisit this data for Q1 2025 above).\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"In addition to the very low traffic, we also see that, for some AI applications, these external referrals continue to stay consistent even when bots stop scraping sites after being disallowed on robots.txt. This highly likely indicates that some AI apps continue to obtain access to the content even if they appear to be abiding by being disallowed on robots.txt \\u2014 whether it's by using a third-party scraper or a masked user agent.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.1. PerplexityBot scrape count, cohort of sites that disallow Perplexity on Robots.txt\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Figure 3.1. PerplexityBot scrape count, cohort of sites that disallow Perplexity on Robots.txt.svg\",alt:\"PerplexityBot scrape count for disallowed sites\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.2. Perplexity referrals for a cohort of sites that disallow PerplexityBot on Robots.txt\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Figure 3.2. Perplexity referrals for a cohort of sites that disallow PerplexityBot on Robots.txt.svg\",alt:\"Perplexity referrals for disallowed sites\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"Publishers Are Trying to Control AI Use of Their Intellectual Property\"}),(0,e.jsx)(n,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`Over the last year, the number of AI bots that publishers have attempted to\nblock AI bots by disallowing them via their robots.txt files has increased by\n4x. Website owners are more active in their attempts to restrict unauthorized\nuse of their content. Notably, AI companies have recently begun changing the\ndescriptions of their user agents to specify that they do not always have to\nabide by restrictions on robots.txt.`})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"TollBit has analysed the robots.txt files of a large cohort of its publishing partners and whether, and how, they have changed from January 2024 to January 2025. We can see that the number of explicit disallow requests for AI bots (i.e. a single site disallowing a single bot would count as one) has increased from 559 to 2,165 (+287%). The average number of AI bots explicitly disallowed per website has grown from 2.2 to 8.6, a 4x increase.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.3. Robots.txt analysis example\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Example of the evolution of a publisher's robots.txt file from January 2024 to January 2025\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Figure 3.3. Robots.txt analysis example.svg\",alt:\"Robots.txt analysis example\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.4. Total AI bot disallow requests\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Figure 3.4. Total AI bot disallow requests.svg\",alt:\"Total AI bot disallow requests\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"When we look at the specific disallow requests, we can see widespread attempts by publishers to restrict AI bots access to their sites. Interestingly, the number of disallow requests to OpenAI's training data crawler have decreased; this could be due to a combination of OpenAI's partnership program and publishers hoping that referrals from ChatGPT will follow. As we explore in section 2 of this report, the data suggests referrals from AI apps remain extremely low.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.5. Change in Disallowing Bots via Robots.txt Across Websites (2024 vs 2025)\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Figure 3.5. Change in Disallowing Bots via Robots.txt Across Websites (2024 vs 2025).svg\",alt:\"Change in Disallowing Bots via Robots.txt\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"AI Companies Changing User Agent Descriptions\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Seemingly in response to the increases in websites' attempts to block bots by disallowing them on robots.txt, AI companies are changing the descriptions of their user agents. Some bot descriptions are explicitly stating that, particularly in circumstances where the agent is fetching information in real-time to service a user request, the crawlers will ignore robots.txt instructions.\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[\"Since the first \",(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"State of the Bots\"}),\" report in Q4 2024, Perplexity has added a second user agent, Perplexity-User, which operates as a RAG bot. While this separation is beneficial in that it could help publishers control how their content is used, Perplexity-User is among the bots that does not appear to respect robots.txt instructions.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Robots.txt has been a guideline/handshake agreement that bots agreed to follow since the beginning of the Internet. It sufficed in a world where humans were the primary drivers of economic value on the internet. AI visitors will vastly outnumber human visitors in the future, and we need governance on how agentic visitors identify themselves on the internet.\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Agents and bots should have unique identities on the Internet, separate from human visitors. Bots mimicking humans or being able to take actions as an extension of a human without connected identification to the human sets a dangerous precedent.\"}),\" This is poised to have massive impacts on advertiser trust, site analytics, and the traffic load to sites, all with limited benefits to the sites being scraped.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Screenshot 1. Perplexity's developer notes (taken 13th April 2025)\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Screenshot 1. Perplexity\\u2019s developer notes (taken 13th April 2025).svg\",alt:\"Perplexity developer notes\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Screenshot 2. Google's developer notes (taken 13th April 2025)\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Screenshot 2. Google\\u2019s developer notes (taken 13th April 2025).svg\",alt:\"Google developer notes\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Screenshot 3. Meta's developer notes (taken 13th April 2025)\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Screenshot 3. Meta\\u2019s developer notes (taken 13th April 2025).svg\",alt:\"Meta developer notes\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"Bot Paywall as an Alternative Defense\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Instead of just relying on disallowing bots on Robots.txt, one way websites are forcing AI companies off their sites is by exploring bot-blocking solutions. A cybersecurity tool can be used to detect and redirect AI bot traffic away from the human version of your website. TollBit provides basic cybersecurity measures to its sites and also partners with cybersecurity vendors (like HUMAN Security) to provide a more robust solution to websites on the platform.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"This allows us to build towards the future. Instead of just blocking sites, websites on TollBit can forward the traffic to the tollbit subdomain (e.g. tollbit.time.com). This allows websites to expose parallel infrastructure that is optimized for AI traffic. The number of bots directed to the TollBit Bot Paywall has increased by 732% in Q1 2025 versus Q4 2024.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"This alternative gateway of sanctioned web access helps to:\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"Prevent any ads from being inadvertently served to AI visitors\"}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"Ensure human visitor site metrics don't get impacted by bot traffic\"}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.li,{children:\"Allow AI bots to be presented with an option to pay for access with added benefits\"}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.6. AI bots directed to TollBit Bot Paywall\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-2025/Figure 3.6. AI bots directed to TollBit Bot Paywall.svg\",alt:\"AI bots directed to TollBit Bot Paywall\"})]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(s,{id:\"appendix\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Appendix: AI User Agent Profiles\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"New User Agents in Q1 2025\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Perplexity-User\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Announced at the start of March, Perplexity's new user agent acts in real-time to 'support user actions' within Perplexity. We have therefore categorised Perplexity-User as a RAG agent however, in testing at the start of April 2025, TollBit has found that both PerplexityBot and Perplexity-User will scrape a page in real-time when Perplexity is prompted.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Claude-User\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Also announced in March \\u2014 and coinciding with Claude's new web-access functionality \\u2014 Claude-User is Anthropic's new RAG agent.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Claude-SearchBot\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"With details published at the same time as Claude-User, Claude-SearchBot is the search indexing crawler to power Claude's web access.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"AI Bot Profiles by Operating Organization\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"OpenAI\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Developer of ChatGPT, OpenAI's chatbot was first-to-market and still holds a ~60% share with 400M weekly active users as of April 2025. Its foundation models \\u2014 and therefore user agents \\u2014 power both ChatGPT and an array of third-party applications, including Microsoft's Co-Pilot and Bing search engine.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"ChatGPT-User\"}),\" \\u2014 Accesses websites in real-time and on-demand so that ChatGPT can formulate responses to user prompts based on the live web. It visits websites to gather information then processes, summarizes and synthesizes this to provide the output. It is not used to gather data for model training.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"OAI-SearchBot\"}),\" \\u2014 An indexing crawler used to power ChatGPT's search capabilities.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"GPTBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Gathers data from the web for the training of OpenAI's large language models. It operates continuously in the background and the data it gathers is collected and used offline for model development, rather than real-time responses to user prompts.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" OpenAI respects the signals provided by content owners via robots.txt, allowing them to disallow any or all of its crawlers.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher partnerships:\"}),\" OpenAI has an extensive publisher partnerships program, having signed bilateral deals with ~35 publishers at the time of writing.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(h,{title:\"Robots Exclusion Protocol (robots.txt)\",children:(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`This index also refers to the Robots Exclusion Protocol or 'robots.txt'. This\nmechanism allows website owners to give instructions to bots about accessing a\nwebsite. It uses a machine-readable file (named robots.txt) which specifies \\u2014\nfor individual bots or all bots collectively \\u2014 which pages or sections they\ncan or cannot crawl. Robots.txt operates simply as a signal though and does\nnot actively block access. Not all developers program their bots to comply\nwith these instructions.`})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Perplexity\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Perplexity has developed an AI answer engine, effectively AI-powered search that provides users with a natural language response to a prompt alongside a list of links and sources. It has 15 million active users. The standard free product primarily uses OpenAI's GPT3.5 model, whereas the premium, paid-for service includes access to a range of models including Claude 3.5 from Anthropic, Llama 3 from Meta and Grok-2 from xAI.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"PerplexityBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Gathers data from across the web to index it for Perplexity's search function. It also acts in real-time, gathering data to respond to specific user queries as they are placed.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Perplexity-User\"}),\" \\u2014 Announced in March 2025, acts in real-time to 'support user actions' within Perplexity.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" Perplexity claims that its web crawler respects robots.txt. However, there have been widely-reported complaints from publishers that it has ignored their signals, leading to an investigation from Amazon, its cloud provider. In its FAQs, Perplexity explains that 'if a page is blocked, we may still index the domain, headline, and a brief factual summary'.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher partnerships:\"}),\" In 2024 Perplexity launched a publisher partner program under which it provides a share of advertising revenues generated by responses that are based upon the content of media partners. Around 20 deals have been signed so far, mostly (although not exclusively) with smaller or niche publishers. The revenue shared with publishers is reported to be capped at 25%.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Anthropic\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Founded by seven former OpenAI employees, Anthropic is an AI developer with a focus on privacy, safety and alignment (with human values). Its foundation models power its Claude chatbot \\u2014 which has a free and premium version \\u2014 and a multitude of third-party applications.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"ClaudeBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Used to gather data from the internet for AI training. The Claude chatbot does not have access to the real-time web and has no search functionality; the data ClaudeBot retrieves is only used offline for the development of new large language models.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Claude-User\"}),\" \\u2014 Announced in March \\u2014 and coinciding with Claude's new web-access functionality \\u2014 Claude-User is Anthropic's RAG agent.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Claude-SearchBot\"}),\" \\u2014 The search indexing crawler to power Claude's web access.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" Anthropic's bots respect publisher signals in robots.txt files. Notably they also respond to any disallows for Common Crawl's CCBot.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Google\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Google is one of the foremost AI developers with its own proprietary models that have been integrated extensively into its consumer and enterprise applications, including search. It also operates Gemini as a standalone AI chatbot with real-time web access. This has around 14% of the chatbot market with an estimated 42 million active users.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Google-Extended\"}),\" \\u2014 Used to gather data to train and improve Google's AI models. It operates independently of the crawlers used to power Google's search product. It should be noted that both Gemini, when it requires data from the live web, and AI Overviews (the natural language AI response to search queries) do not rely on Google-Extended for real-time data retrieval and therefore disallowing this bot does not control whether a publisher's content is used to inform the outputs of these products.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" Google's bots respect robots.txt signals. However, publishers do not have granular controls over the use of their content in real-time for AI Overviews or Gemini's responses as these products appear to use the data collected for Google's general search product rather than a discrete user agent. In order to prevent content being used for these applications publishers need to use the no-snippet directive or signal Google to stop indexing a page for search entirely. Both of these would have negative effects on prominence and referral traffic.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher partnerships:\"}),\" Whilst Google has signed a $60M AI content licensing deal with Reddit, the large number of partnerships it has with publishing businesses are built around its News Showcase product, rather than content for AI. As competition authorities examine its conduct around Gemini and search, it may soon have to start securing explicit authorization for access to content to fuel these products.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Meta\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Meta has developed the Llama family of open-source AI models. These are used in Meta's own products and extensively across third-party applications. Whilst historically it has relied on external datasets for model training, it has recently launched a new web crawler to collect data for its LLMs.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Meta-ExternalAgent\"}),\" \\u2014 Described as crawling the web for 'use cases such as training AI models or improving products by indexing content directly'. Launched in summer 2024.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Meta-ExternalFetcher\"}),\" \\u2014 Accesses websites in real-time in response to actions by users. Meta is transparent that this crawler may bypass robots.txt on account of being user-'initiated'.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"FacebookBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Previously described as crawling public web pages to improve language models for speech recognition technology. It has since removed this description from its developer site and it is not known whether the bot is now being used for different purposes.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" Meta's crawlers respect robots.txt signals although the Meta-ExternalFetcher bot may bypass the protocol because it performs crawls that were user-initiated.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher partnerships:\"}),\" Meta has signed an AI licensing deal with just one publisher \\u2014 Reuters.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Apple\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Apple has been investing in AI \\u2014 including the development of its own foundation models \\u2014 to enhance its products, particularly in privacy-centric applications and on-device AI capabilities.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Applebot-Extended\"}),\" \\u2014 Apple's primary user agent is AppleBot. This is used to collect data to feed into a variety of user products in the Apple ecosystem, including Spotlight, Siri and Safari. Applebot-Extended is a secondary user agent that allows publishers to opt-out of their content being used to train Apple's foundation models. Applebot-Extended does not crawl webpages; it is only used to determine how Apple can use the data crawled by the primary Applebot user agent.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" Applebot-Extended respects robots.txt directives, allowing website owners to control the use of their content for AI training.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher partnerships:\"}),\" Apple has not publicly announced any AI content licensing deals at time of writing but is reportedly in negotiations with a number of publishers including Cond\\xE9 Nast and NBC News.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Amazon\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"As well as a strategic partnership with Anthropic, Amazon has developed its own 'Nova' family of AI models which emphasize speed and value.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"AmazonBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Described as being used to improve services, 'such as enabling Alexa to answer even more questions for customers'. There are no published details of how it operates, or what the data it captures is used for.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" AmazonBot respects standard robots.txt rules.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher partnerships:\"}),\" Amazon is reportedly in licensing negotiations with a number of news outlets for access to content that will give a revamped Alexa the ability to answer questions about current events.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"ByteDance\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"At the time of writing ByteDance, owner of TikTok, has no live AI applications but is widely reported to be developing its own foundation models.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Bytespider\"}),\" \\u2014 Has been scraping the web at a high rate since it first appeared in early 2024. ByteDance has published no information on the function it serves or what the data collected is being used for.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" ByteDance has no published robots.txt policy. There are widespread reports of Bytespider ignoring robots.txt signals.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Other Notable AI User Agents\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"DuckAssistBot\"}),\" \\u2014 DuckDuckGo's web crawler that crawls pages in real-time to source information for answers by DuckAssist. Data collected is not used to train AI models and it respects robots.txt signals.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Timpibot\"}),\" \\u2014 Web crawler for Timpi, a decentralized search index, accessible for a cost to businesses. Data is also available to AI developers for model training.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"YouBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Crawler for You.com, an AI-powered search engine that integrates AI query responses alongside conventional search links.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Diffbot\"}),\" \\u2014 Web crawler focused on extracting data from web pages which it then converts into structured datasets for businesses and developers. Fully respects robots.txt directives.\"]}),`\n`]})]})]})}function f(i={}){let{wrapper:t}=i.components||{};return t?(0,e.jsx)(t,{...i,children:(0,e.jsx)(u,{...i})}):u(i)}function a(i,t){throw new Error(\"Expected \"+(t?\"component\":\"object\")+\" `\"+i+\"` to be defined: you likely forgot to import, pass, or provide it.\")}return T(k);})();\n;return Component;"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"46:T8e76,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\n\u003cSection id=\"executive-summary\"\u003e\n\n## **Executive Summary**\n\n\u003cExecSummary\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={1}\u003e\n  **Despite AI search companies' claims, TollBit sees that AI bots on average\n  are sending 95.7% less referral traffic than traditional Google search.**\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={2}\u003e\n  **AI bot scraping as a percentage of all traffic to sites more than doubled**\n  (increased by approximately 117%) from Q3 to Q4 2024.\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={3}\u003e\n  **When sites block Perplexity, we see that they continue to send referrals**\n  which means they appear to be continuing to scrape sites under the radar.\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={4}\u003e\n  **AI bot scrapes that bypassed robots.txt grew by over 40%** between Q3 to Q4.\n  Blocking AI bots via robots.txt remains an insufficient mechanism to prevent\n  unwanted scraping.\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003cExecItem num={5}\u003e\n  **Unidentified user agent scraping (aka hidden scraping) is almost equivalent\n  to the average scrapes from identified AI bots** per website for Q4.\n\u003c/ExecItem\u003e\n\n\u003c/ExecSummary\u003e\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n\n---\n\n\u003cSection id=\"foreword\"\u003e\n\n## **Foreword**\n\nWe are excited to be releasing this, the first _TollBit State of the Bots Report_.\n\nThe adoption of artificial intelligence (AI) over the last two years has occurred at a lightning pace. Consumer habits are reforming around this technology which, we believe, carries the potential to make our lives better in a multitude of ways.\n\nOne such area of adoption relates to how we retrieve information. The advent of the internet revolutionized this; at the click of a button we could access practically all the knowledge humankind has created. Artificial intelligence is taking this further. We can now task an AI system with a highly-specific information request and it will do the work for us, sifting through limitless sources and providing us with what we're looking for.\n\nThese information-based AI use cases have the potential to disrupt the current business models of publishers. It looks highly likely that in future we will all spend more time interacting with AI applications and that, in some instances, this will supplant our current interactions with search engines and publisher websites.\n\nWe are optimistic about what this means for content creators though. Our thirst for knowledge is not going to be diminished as a result of this technology. If anything, we believe that the ease with which AI can allow us to access information will create new avenues for our inquisition. Unconstrained by time, screen space and human reading speed, as AI consumes knowledge on our behalf the total volume of information consumed will far eclipse the levels today. These systems are nothing without the materials they were trained on, and access in real-time for RAG (retrieval-augmented generation). Professional media will be more important than ever; the rate at which AI systems are accessing the digital properties of our publishing partners — as is seen in the first _TollBit Artificial Intelligence User Agent Index_ — is evidence of this.\n\nMuch like previous platform shifts — such as that from print to digital, or web 1 to the social internet — in order to succeed in the age of AI, publishers will need to adapt their strategy. As well as delivering deeply engaging experiences on their owned-and-operated platforms, an AI licensing revenue line will allow content businesses to monetize their intellectual property when it is used in an AI setting.\n\nThis is our mission at TollBit. We believe that a vibrant, liquid and fair market for AI access to content needs to develop. And we have a product in market where transactions are flowing between both sides. Our vision is for a world in which professional content businesses can set the price and terms on which they will provide content to AI applications and a frictionless transaction takes place.\n\nThe starting point for this is transparency. We're in the nascent days of AI and the race to build and release models has led to practices that undermine rights holders and threaten the economics of content creation. We're launching this index in an effort to begin creating transparency and, ultimately, to help this licensing market develop. We also want publishers to understand the value of their content to AI systems and the scale of the demand for it.\n\n**Toshit Panigrahi and Olivia Joslin**\n_Co-founders, TollBit_\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n\n---\n\n\u003cSection id=\"section-1\"\u003e\n\n## **Section 1: The AI User Agent Landscape**\n\nSince TollBit's technology went live at the beginning of April 2024, it has been gathering data on the rise of user agents serving artificial intelligence (AI) applications.\n\nIn this, the first _TollBit Artificial Intelligence User Agent Index_, we are providing an overview of the AI user agents that are currently active, what it is believed they do and a short explanation of the user-facing tools they serve.\n\nNote that this only includes those user agents that access websites on behalf of AI applications; not the many other bots that are active across the web serving other purposes, e.g. search indexing. It has also been reported that some AI applications are served by 'unofficial' user agents. These operate opaquely so have not been included individually although are discussed at the end of this section.\n\n\u003cInfoBox title=\"User agents, bots and crawlers\"\u003e\n**User agent** — A user agent is any piece of software that sends requests to websites. This includes browsers (which do so on behalf of the user operating them) and bots, which send requests automatically, typically for indexing or data-mining. Each user agent identifies itself to the website via a 'string' (a piece of metadata which is read by the site's servers), called a 'User-Agent Header'.\n\n**Bot** — A bot is any type of user agent that is not directly instructed by a human to access that website. Bots include AI user agents that operate independently to retrieve information from a website following a human prompt to an AI application. They also include crawlers.\n\n**Crawler** — A crawler is a type of bot that navigates websites in the background to collect data. These are used for indexing by search engines and data mining for research, analysis, or more recently to gather content for training large language models. Crawlers operate autonomously, typically following links across the web to discover and retrieve content at scale.\n\n\u003c/InfoBox\u003e\n\n### 1.1 User Agent Profiles\n\nThe table below lists the AI user agents currently active together with what it is believed their purpose is. There are three primary types of agent, with distinct functions in the development and operation of AI applications:\n\n**Retrieval augmented generation (RAG) agent** — These bots retrieve information in real-time to respond to user prompts. They use an index of the web, gathered by an indexing crawler (either proprietary or third-party such as Bing or Google) to locate the relevant content which is then retrieved and synthesized into a response.\n\n**Training data crawling agent** — Large language models — such as Llama from Meta or GPT-4o from OpenAI, both of which power a multitude of consumer applications — are trained on vast quantities of data. Training data crawlers move around the web — following links from websites to websites or working through sitemaps — downloading content which is then processed and stored for offline use.\n\n**AI search indexing agent** — AI systems with access to the real-time web need an index of the internet. This is used to direct RAG agents to the right sources when collecting data needed for responses to prompts. AI search indexing crawlers build these indexes by systematically navigating the web, collecting and organizing content and metadata.\n\n| User agents          | Operated by | Type                                    |\n| :------------------- | :---------- | :-------------------------------------- |\n| ChatGPT-User         | OpenAI      | RAG agent                               |\n| OAI-SearchBot        | OpenAI      | RAG agent                               |\n| GPTBot               | OpenAI      | Training data crawling agent            |\n| PerplexityBot        | Perplexity  | Hybrid RAG and AI search indexing agent |\n| ClaudeBot            | Anthropic   | Training data crawling agent            |\n| Claude-Web           | Anthropic   | Unknown                                 |\n| anthropic-ai         | Anthropic   | Unknown                                 |\n| Google-Extended      | Google      | Control agent for training data         |\n| Meta-ExternalAgent   | Meta        | Training data crawling agent            |\n| Meta-ExternalFetcher | Meta        | RAG agent                               |\n| FacebookBot          | Meta        | Unknown                                 |\n| Applebot-Extended    | Apple       | Control agent for training data         |\n| AmazonBot            | Amazon      | Training data crawling agent            |\n| Bytespider           | ByteDance   | Training data crawling agent            |\n| DuckAssistBot        | DuckDuckGo  | RAG agent                               |\n| Timpibot             | Timpi       | Training data crawling agent            |\n| YouBot               | You.com     | RAG agent                               |\n| Diffbot              | Diffbot     | Training data crawling agent            |\n\n### 1.2 Third-party and Hidden User Agents\n\nThis index only covers the official, first-party user agents operated by AI developers. There are alternative automated means of accessing information from the internet which are harder for a website publisher to uncover.\n\nFirstly, it is possible for AI developers to obtain data crawled by third-party user agents. Some of these datasets are free and publicly available, such as CommonCrawl, whilst others are available for a fee, such as Timpi's search index. In these instances the end-user is unknown to the website owner at the time of scraping and indeed may only access the data some time after the bot crawled their site.\n\nIt is also possible for AI developers to use hidden or masked user agents which do not make their real operators known through the user agent string. This practice is technically challenging to uncover but it has been reported to be deployed at scale by major AI companies. Cybersecurity tools can assist with detecting such activity. TollBit has partnered with cybersecurity partners to bring these capabilities to its publishing partners.\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n\n---\n\n\u003cSection id=\"section-2\"\u003e\n\n## **Section 2: Scale of AI Scraping**\n\nAs AI models and applications proliferate — and users turn to AI technologies in growing numbers for a wide range of use cases — the scale of AI user agent web access will increase. The level of AI scraping can be seen as a proxy for the rise of AI itself. The _TollBit Artificial Intelligence User Agent Index_ will chart the rise of this activity across publisher properties.\n\nFor this analysis, we looked at the scale of AI scraping across all onboarded TollBit sites in Q4 (section 2.A.) and we looked at a smaller select cohort to see the change in AI bot traffic from Q3 to Q4 (section 2.B.).\n\n### 2.A All Sites Aggregate Scraping Levels\n\nWe analyzed all sites that had fully set up TollBit analytics prior to Q4. On average, these sites on TollBit were scraped over 2 million times in Q4 and the average scraping rate per page was approximately 7 times.\n\n**Table 2.A.1. Average AI user agent scraping rates**\n\n| Metric                            | Q4 2024   |\n| :-------------------------------- | :-------- |\n| Average scraping rate per website | 2,056,658 |\n| Average scraping rate per page    | 7.199     |\n\nDeals and shopping content were scraped most times per page in Q4, followed by national news, consumer tech, and lifestyle content.\n\n**Figure 2.A.2. Scraping levels per page by content category in Q4**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q4-2024/Figure 2.A.2. Scraping levels per page by content category in Q4.svg\"\n  alt=\"Scraping levels per page by content category\"\n/\u003e\n\n### 2.A.3 User Agent Per Page Scraping Levels\n\nThe scale of scraping can also be analyzed at an individual user agent level. A note of caution on this data, however; the use of third-party or hidden user agents is likely to mask the true scale of scraping by some AI developers.\n\n**Table 2.A.3. User agent per page scraping levels for Q4**\n\n| User agent           | Q4 total scrapes / page |\n| :------------------- | :---------------------: |\n| ChatGPT-User         |          64.63          |\n| FacebookBot          |          16.79          |\n| PerplexityBot        |          16.75          |\n| meta-externalagent   |          6.86           |\n| Timpibot             |          5.51           |\n| OAI-SearchBot        |          5.36           |\n| omgili               |          5.05           |\n| Bytespider           |          4.43           |\n| DuckAssistBot        |          4.42           |\n| GPTBot               |          4.24           |\n| meta-externalfetcher |          4.00           |\n| cohere-ai            |          2.80           |\n| CCBot                |          2.66           |\n| ClaudeBot            |          2.59           |\n| anthropic-ai         |          2.25           |\n| Amazonbot            |          1.05           |\n\n### 2.A.4 AI Bot Traffic Breakdown\n\nHere is a breakdown of AI bot traffic distribution, showing which bots contribute the most to total AI activity. ChatGPT-User accounts for the largest share at 15.6% of all AI bot traffic, followed by Bytespider (12.44%) and Meta-ExternalAgent (11.34%).\n\n**Table 2.A.4. AI bot share of total AI traffic**\n\n| AI Bot             | Q4 Share |\n| :----------------- | :------: |\n| ChatGPT-User       |  15.60%  |\n| Bytespider         |  12.44%  |\n| Meta-ExternalAgent |  11.34%  |\n| OAI-SearchBot      |  10.81%  |\n| GPTBot             |  10.32%  |\n| DuckAssistBot      |  9.37%   |\n| ClaudeBot          |  8.62%   |\n| PerplexityBot      |  7.79%   |\n| CCbot              |  4.82%   |\n| Timpibot           |  4.39%   |\n| AmazonBot          |  3.83%   |\n| omgili             |  0.60%   |\n\n**Figure 2.A.5. AI Bot Traffic as a Percentage of Total Traffic to Sites**\n\nAI bot traffic as a percentage of total web traffic shows a clear upward trend over time.\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q4-2024/Figure 2.A.5. AI Bot Traffic as a Percentage of Total Traffic to Sites.svg\"\n  alt=\"AI Bot Traffic as a Percentage of Total Traffic\"\n/\u003e\n\n\u003cInfoBox title=\"Use of unofficial AI user agents — Perplexity\"\u003e\nEvidence of the use of third-party or unidentified AI user agents can be clearly seen in TollBit data. To give an example, the referral rate from Perplexity to multiple domains exceeds the scraping rate. The most plausible explanation for this is that the RAG scrapes that surface the links which generate the referral traffic came from bots that did not identify as 'PerplexityBot' in their user agent string.\n\n**Table 2.A.6. Linkless traffic from Perplexity**\n\n| Domain   | Total scrapes in Q3 and Q4 2024 | Total referrals in Q3 and Q4 2024 |\n| :------- | :-----------------------------: | :-------------------------------: |\n| Domain A |               937               |               7,203               |\n| Domain B |               545               |              10,382               |\n| Domain C |               577               |               1,920               |\n\n\u003c/InfoBox\u003e\n\n\u003cInfoBox title=\"Hidden Scraping Activity\"\u003e\n  Many scrapers don't announce their user agent when scraping the content from\n  the website. The average number of hidden scrapes per website in Q4 was 1.886M\n  (excluding SEO bots). This unidentified scraping is almost equivalent to the\n  average scrapes from AI bots per website for Q4 which was 2M. With hidden\n  scrapers operating at nearly the same scale as identified AI bots, we need to\n  find a monetization model that is a true ecosystem solution and incentivizes\n  AI bots to identify their user agents and pay in scenarios when their scraping\n  directly results in a loss of traffic.\n\u003c/InfoBox\u003e\n\n### 2.B Cohort Analysis of AI Bot Scraping Levels\n\nWe analyzed a cohort of websites that joined TollBit before Q3. These websites were selected because their bot-blocking strategies remained constant during Q3 and Q4 so that we could see the cleanest view of how AI bot traffic evolved over the two quarters. Our analysis found that AI bot scraping activity increased significantly between these quarters in 2024.\n\nOn average, these sites were scraped 5.05 million times per website in Q4, up from 3.13 million in Q3 — an increase of more than 61%. The average number of scrapes per page also rose from 4.2 to 5.0, reflecting a 19% increase in per-page scraping.\n\n**Table 2.B.1. Average AI user agent scraping rates Q3 vs Q4**\n\n| Metric                            |  Q3 2024  |  Q4 2024  |\n| :-------------------------------- | :-------: | :-------: |\n| Average scraping rate per website | 3,127,608 | 5,050,539 |\n| Average scraping rate per page    |    4.2    |    5.0    |\n\n**Figure 2.B.1. Average per-page, per-website AI user agent scraping rates**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q4-2024/Figure 2.B.1. Average per-page, per-website AI user agent scraping rates.svg\"\n  alt=\"Average per-page, per-website AI user agent scraping rates\"\n/\u003e\n\n### 2.B.2 Scraping Levels by Bots\n\nA closer look at AI bot activity within the cohort analysis reveals substantial differences in scraping patterns among various AI user agents. ChatGPT-User exhibited the most aggressive growth, increasing its scraping activity by 6,767.60% quarter-over-quarter. Other notable increases include Timpibot (+1,323.58%), ClaudeBot (+566.79%), and DuckAssistBot (+248.64%).\n\n**Figure 2.B.2. Q3 vs Q4 percentage change in total scraping by AI bots**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q4-2024/Figure 2.B.2. Q3 vs Q4 percentage change in total scraping by AI bots.svg\"\n  alt=\"Q3 vs Q4 percentage change in total scraping by AI bots\"\n/\u003e\n\n### 2.B.3 User Agent Per Page Scraping Levels (Q3 vs Q4)\n\n**Table 2.B.3. User agent per page scraping levels for Q3 vs Q4**\n\n| User agent         | Q3 total scrapes / page | Q4 total scrapes / page | Percentage Change |\n| :----------------- | :---------------------: | :---------------------: | :---------------: |\n| OAI-SearchBot      |           3.2           |          18.0           |      461.65%      |\n| FacebookBot        |           1.3           |           4.0           |      210.32%      |\n| PerplexityBot      |           2.9           |           5.0           |      72.91%       |\n| ChatGPT-User       |          26.6           |          35.5           |      33.63%       |\n| Timpibot           |           2.2           |           2.8           |      30.75%       |\n| omgili             |           4.2           |           5.1           |      22.93%       |\n| DuckAssistBot      |           2.1           |           2.6           |      22.64%       |\n| Ai2Bot             |           1.6           |           1.9           |      22.62%       |\n| ClaudeBot          |           1.9           |           2.2           |      17.99%       |\n| Diffbot            |           2.2           |           2.5           |      14.02%       |\n| CCBot              |           1.2           |           1.3           |       8.01%       |\n| Bytespider         |           9.8           |           8.3           |      -15.38%      |\n| Amazonbot          |           3.3           |           2.6           |      -22.08%      |\n| meta-externalagent |           5.3           |           3.2           |      -39.72%      |\n| GPTBot             |          16.7           |           3.0           |      -82.15%      |\n\nThis user-agent level data suggests that those bots operating in real-time to retrieve information in response to a user prompt are growing the fastest. There is a clear signal of the increase in the use of AI for information retrieval.\n\nThis real-time user agent activity is likely to be substitutional to human access to publisher websites. For example, an AI agent may access five media websites in order to respond to a prompt that satisfies a user's need without them needing to visit another online property. Prior to the advent of AI, this may have resulted in multiple Google searches and visits to websites.\n\nLicensing revenues aside, there are no means of monetizing non-human visitors to a website; advertisements are not served and robots do not purchase subscriptions.\n\n### 2.B.4 AI Bot Traffic as a Percentage of Total Traffic\n\nAI bot traffic from the top 15 AI bots as a percentage of total traffic grew by 117% between Q3 and Q4 2024 for this cohort of websites.\n\n**Figure 2.B.4 Growth in AI Bot Traffic as a Percentage of Total Traffic**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q4-2024/Figure. 2.B.4 Growth in AI Bot Traffic as a Percentage of Total Traffic.svg\"\n  alt=\"Growth in AI Bot Traffic as a Percentage of Total Traffic\"\n/\u003e\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n\n---\n\n\u003cSection id=\"section-3\"\u003e\n\n## **Section 3: Referrals from AI Applications**\n\nAI applications with access to the real-time web such as Perplexity, ChatGPT's browser mode and DuckDuckGo's AI assistant — as well as new entrants into this market, such as ProRata with their Gist product — incorporate a natural language response to a query alongside a list of sources and links which the user can click on if they wish to explore further.\n\nThe level of traffic that publishers can expect from these interfaces is a topic of debate. TollBit has been collecting data on traffic to its publisher partners' properties from AI applications since its technology went live at the beginning of April 2024.\n\nWhilst the total volume of referrals will vary based upon the number of TollBit publisher partners and the usage of AI applications, the referral _rate_ — how many referrals occur each time a piece of content is scraped in real-time to answer a user query — gives a signal of the effectiveness of AI applications in driving traffic to publishers.\n\nThe _TollBit AI User Agent Index_ includes an averaged referral rate across the AI applications that access publisher data in real time and provide traceable traffic back to publishers. In Q4 2024 this referral rate is 0.37%.\n\nThis figure should be considered an absolute maximum as some AI developers use third-party or hidden user agents and each scrape may result in multiple presentations of content and links.\n\n**Table 3.1. Average AI application referral rate**\n\n| Q4 Real-time scrapes | Q4 Total referrals | Q4 Referrals per real-time scrape |\n| :------------------: | :----------------: | :-------------------------------: |\n|     163,365,722      |      605,179       |               0.37%               |\n\n### 3.2 Comparing Referral Rates of AI Interfaces and Conventional Search\n\nThe AI applications with access to the real-time web and therefore capable of driving traffic to a publisher website fall into two broad categories of user interface — general-purpose chatbot and AI search engine. TollBit data finds that AI search engines deliver a superior referral rate of 0.74% per scrape, double the 0.33% rate for chatbots. Again, these figures should be considered a maximum; the use of third-party and hidden user agents may be artificially inflating the rate for AI search products.\n\nThese rates remain extremely low when compared to referrals from a conventional (non-AI) Google search engine results page (SERP). Even when taking the average click-through-rate across the top 10 organic search results (8.63%) AI search engine interfaces deliver 91% fewer referrals and chatbots 96% fewer.\n\n**Figure 3.2. Comparing AI interface and Google SERP referral rates**\n\n\u003cImage\n  src=\"/state-of-the-bots/q4-2024/Figure 3.2. Comparing AI interface and Google SERP referral rates.svg\"\n  alt=\"Comparing AI interface and Google SERP referral rates\"\n/\u003e\n\nThese figures give an indication of the change in traffic levels publishers can expect if user queries move from conventional search to AI applications. They also starkly highlight the difference in the value for publishers when providing their content for use by AI applications versus conventional search. The return cannot be expected to come in the form of traffic from AI applications. The alternative is that it comes from licensing revenues. Our mission at TollBit is to help publishers realize this opportunity.\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n\n---\n\n\u003cSection id=\"section-4\"\u003e\n\n## **Section 4: Compliance with the Robots Exclusion Protocol**\n\nWhilst every AI developer with a published policy claims its crawlers respect the robots exclusion protocol, TollBit data finds that in many instances bots continue scraping despite explicit disallow requests for those user agents in publishers' robots.txt files.\n\nSome of this activity may be accounted for by the delay between a robots.txt file being updated by the publisher and the user agent responding by adapting its activity. Most AI developer notes state that it takes 24-72 hours for changes to be logged and reflected.\n\n**Table 4.1. Aggregate unauthorized scraping levels — all user agents**\n\n| Metric                    | Q4 bypassed scrapes | Q4 total scrapes | Q4 percent of scrapes that bypass |\n| :------------------------ | :-----------------: | :--------------: | :-------------------------------: |\n| Aggregate scraping levels |      9,713,100      |   294,102,061    |               3.3%                |\n\n\u003cInfoBox title=\"Getting your AI user agent data\"\u003e\n  To understand AI demand for your content, TollBit's analytics solution\n  provides granular data on AI user agent activity across your websites. It\n  costs nothing and is simple to implement. To find out more, please visit\n  www.tollbit.com or email us at [email withheld by Lighthouse].\n\u003c/InfoBox\u003e\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n\n---\n\n\u003cSection id=\"appendix\"\u003e\n\n## **Appendix: AI User Agent Profiles**\n\n### AI Bot Profiles by Operating Organization\n\n**OpenAI**\n\nDeveloper of ChatGPT, OpenAI's chatbot was first-to-market and still holds a ~60% share with 300M weekly active users as of December 2024. Its foundation models — and therefore user agents — power both ChatGPT and an array of third-party applications, including Microsoft's Co-Pilot and Bing search engine.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **ChatGPT-User** — Accesses websites in real-time and on-demand so that ChatGPT can formulate responses to user prompts based on the live web. It visits websites to gather information then processes, summarises and synthesizes this to provide the output. It is not used to gather data for model training.\n- **OAI-SearchBot** — Used to power ChatGPT's search capabilities. Similar to ChatGPT-User, it accesses the internet in real-time but is optimized for search scenarios, delivering the raw links and search results alongside summaries.\n- **GPTBot** — Gathers data from the web for the training of OpenAI's large language models. It operates continuously in the background and the data it gathers is collected and used offline for model development, rather than real-time responses to user prompts.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ OpenAI respects the signals provided by content owners via robots.txt, allowing them to disallow any or all of its crawlers.\n\n_Publisher partnerships:_ OpenAI has an extensive publisher partnerships program, having signed bilateral deals with ~35 publishers at the time of writing.\n\n\u003cInfoBox title=\"Robots Exclusion Protocol (robots.txt)\"\u003e\n  This index also refers to the Robots Exclusion Protocol or 'robots.txt'. This\n  mechanism allows website owners to give instructions to bots about accessing a\n  website. It uses a machine-readable file (named robots.txt) which specifies —\n  for individual bots or all bots collectively — which pages or sections they\n  can or cannot crawl. Robots.txt operates simply as a signal though and does\n  not actively block access. Not all developers program their bots to comply\n  with these instructions.\n\u003c/InfoBox\u003e\n\n---\n\n**Perplexity**\n\nPerplexity has developed an AI answer engine, effectively AI-powered search that provides users with a natural language response to a prompt alongside a list of links and sources. It has 15 million active users. The standard free product primarily uses OpenAI's GPT3.5 model, whereas the premium, paid-for service includes access to a range of models including Claude 3.5 from Anthropic, Llama 3 from Meta and Grok-2 from xAI.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **PerplexityBot** — Gathers data from across the web to index it for Perplexity's search function. It also acts in real-time, gathering data to respond to specific user queries as they are placed.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ Perplexity claims that its web crawler respects robots.txt. However, there have been widely-reported complaints from publishers that it has ignored their signals, leading to an investigation from Amazon, its cloud provider. In its FAQs, Perplexity explains that 'if a page is blocked, we may still index the domain, headline, and a brief factual summary'.\n\n_Publisher partnerships:_ In 2024 Perplexity launched a publisher partner program under which it provides a share of advertising revenues generated by responses that are based upon the content of media partners. Around 20 deals have been signed so far, mostly (although not exclusively) with smaller or niche publishers. The revenue shared with publishers is reported to be capped at 25%.\n\n---\n\n**Anthropic**\n\nFounded by seven former OpenAI employees, Anthropic is an AI developer with a focus on privacy, safety and alignment (with human values). Its foundation models power its Claude chatbot — which has a free and premium version — and a multitude of third-party applications.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **ClaudeBot** — Used to gather data from the internet for AI training. The Claude chatbot does not have access to the real-time web and has no search functionality; the data ClaudeBot retrieves is only used offline for the development of new large language models.\n- **Claude-Web** — There is no published information on the function of this bot. It may be experimental or another identifier — used in specific circumstances — for Anthropic's primary user agent ClaudeBot.\n- **anthropic-ai** — As with Claude-Web, there is no published information on this bot or its function.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ Anthropic's bots respect publisher signals in robots.txt files. Notably they also respond to any disallows for Common Crawl's CCBot.\n\n---\n\n**Google**\n\nGoogle is one of the foremost AI developers with its own proprietary models that have been integrated extensively into its consumer and enterprise applications, including search. It also operates Gemini as a standalone AI chatbot with real-time web access. This has around 14% of the chatbot market with an estimated 42 million active users.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **Google-Extended** — Used to gather data to train and improve Google's AI models. It operates independently of the crawlers used to power Google's search product. It should be noted that both Gemini, when it requires data from the live web, and AI Overviews (the natural language AI response to search queries) do not rely on Google-Extended for real-time data retrieval and therefore disallowing this bot does not control whether a publisher's content is used to inform the outputs of these products.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ Google's bots respect robots.txt signals. However, publishers do not have granular controls over the use of their content in real-time for AI Overviews or Gemini's responses as these products appear to use the data collected for Google's general search product rather than a discrete user agent. In order to prevent content being used for these applications publishers need to use the no-snippet directive or signal Google to stop indexing a page for search entirely. Both of these would have negative effects on prominence and referral traffic.\n\n_Publisher partnerships:_ Whilst Google has signed a $60M AI content licensing deal with Reddit, the large number of partnerships it has with publishing businesses are built around its News Showcase product, rather than content for AI.\n\n---\n\n**Meta**\n\nMeta has developed the Llama family of open-source AI models. These are used in Meta's own products and extensively across third-party applications. Whilst historically it has relied on external datasets for model training, it has recently launched a new web crawler to collect data for its LLMs.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **Meta-ExternalAgent** — Described as crawling the web for 'use cases such as training AI models or improving products by indexing content directly'. Launched in summer 2024.\n- **Meta-ExternalFetcher** — Accesses websites in real-time in response to actions by users. Meta is transparent that this crawler may bypass robots.txt on account of being user-'initiated'.\n- **FacebookBot** — Previously described as crawling public web pages to improve language models for speech recognition technology. It has since removed this description from its developer site and it is not known whether the bot is now being used for different purposes.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ Meta's crawlers respect robots.txt signals although Meta-ExternalFetcher may bypass the protocol because it performs crawls that were user-initiated.\n\n_Publisher partnerships:_ Meta has signed an AI licensing deal with just one publisher — Reuters.\n\n---\n\n**Apple**\n\nApple has been investing in AI — including the development of its own foundation models — to enhance its products, particularly in privacy-centric applications and on-device AI capabilities.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **Applebot-Extended** — Apple's primary user agent is AppleBot. This is used to collect data to feed into a variety of user products in the Apple ecosystem, including Spotlight, Siri and Safari. Applebot-Extended is a secondary user agent that allows publishers to opt-out of their content being used to train Apple's foundation models. Applebot-Extended does not crawl webpages; it is only used to determine how Apple can use the data crawled by the primary Applebot user agent.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ Applebot-Extended respects robots.txt directives, allowing website owners to control the use of their content for AI training.\n\n_Publisher partnerships:_ Apple has not publicly announced any AI content licensing deals at time of writing but is reportedly in negotiations with a number of publishers including Condé Nast and NBC News.\n\n---\n\n**Amazon**\n\nAs well as a strategic partnership with Anthropic, Amazon has developed its own 'Nova' family of AI models which emphasize speed and value.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **AmazonBot** — Described as being used to improve services, 'such as enabling Alexa to answer even more questions for customers'. There are no published details of how it operates, or what the data it captures is used for.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ AmazonBot respects standard robots.txt rules.\n\n_Publisher partnerships:_ Amazon is reportedly in licensing negotiations with a number of news outlets for access to content that will give a revamped Alexa the ability to answer questions about current events.\n\n---\n\n**ByteDance**\n\nAt the time of writing ByteDance, owner of TikTok, has no live AI applications but is widely reported to be developing its own foundation models.\n\n**User agents:**\n\n- **Bytespider** — Has been scraping the web at a high rate since it first appeared in early 2024. ByteDance has published no information on the function it serves or what the data collected is being used for.\n\n_Robots.txt policy:_ ByteDance has no published robots.txt policy. There are widespread reports of Bytespider ignoring robots.txt signals.\n\n---\n\n**Other Notable AI User Agents**\n\n- **DuckAssistBot** — DuckDuckGo's web crawler that crawls pages in real-time to source information for answers by DuckAssist. Data collected is not used to train AI models and it respects robots.txt signals.\n- **Timpibot** — Web crawler for Timpi, a decentralized search index, accessible for a cost to businesses. Data is also available to AI developers for model training.\n- **YouBot** — Crawler for You.com, an AI-powered search engine that integrates AI query responses alongside conventional search links.\n- **Diffbot** — Web crawler focused on extracting data from web pages which it then converts into structured datasets for businesses and developers. Fully respects robots.txt directives.\n\n\u003c/Section\u003e\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"47:Te342,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"var Component=(()=\u003e{var u=Object.create;var c=Object.defineProperty;var y=Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor;var A=Object.getOwnPropertyNames;var b=Object.getPrototypeOf,m=Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty;var x=(n,t)=\u003e()=\u003e{try{return t||n((t={exports:{}}).exports,t),t.exports}catch(i){throw t=0,i}},w=(n,t)=\u003e{for(var i in t)c(n,i,{get:t[i],enumerable:!0})},d=(n,t,i,a)=\u003e{if(t\u0026\u0026typeof t==\"object\"||typeof t==\"function\")for(let r of A(t))!m.call(n,r)\u0026\u0026r!==i\u0026\u0026c(n,r,{get:()=\u003et[r],enumerable:!(a=y(t,r))||a.enumerable});return n};var v=(n,t,i)=\u003e(i=n!=null?u(b(n)):{},d(t||!n||!n.__esModule?c(i,\"default\",{value:n,enumerable:!0}):i,n)),I=n=\u003ed(c({},\"__esModule\",{value:!0}),n);var g=x((P,h)=\u003e{h.exports=_jsx_runtime});var B={};w(B,{default:()=\u003ef,frontmatter:()=\u003eT});var e=v(g()),T={title:\"State of the Bots\",id:\"q4-2024\",quarter:\"q4-2024\",description:\"2024 Q4, The First Issue\",docType:\"report\",sections:[{title:\"Executive Summary\",slug:\"executive-summary\"},{title:\"Foreword\",slug:\"foreword\"},{title:\"Section 1: AI User Agent Landscape\",slug:\"section-1\",subsections:[{title:\"User Agent Profiles\",slug:\"user-agent-profiles\"},{title:\"Third-party \u0026 Hidden Agents\",slug:\"hidden-agents\"}]},{title:\"Section 2: Scale of AI Scraping\",slug:\"section-2\",subsections:[{title:\"Aggregate Scraping Levels\",slug:\"aggregate-scraping\"},{title:\"User Agent Scraping Levels\",slug:\"user-agent-scraping\"},{title:\"Cohort Analysis\",slug:\"cohort-analysis\"},{title:\"AI Traffic Percentage\",slug:\"ai-traffic-percentage\"}]},{title:\"Section 3: Referrals from AI\",slug:\"section-3\",subsections:[{title:\"AI Referral Rates\",slug:\"ai-referral-rates\"},{title:\"AI vs Google SERP\",slug:\"ai-vs-google\"}]},{title:\"Section 4: Robots.txt Compliance\",slug:\"section-4\",subsections:[{title:\"Unauthorized Scraping\",slug:\"unauthorized-scraping\"}]},{title:\"Appendix: AI User Agent Profiles\",slug:\"appendix\",subsections:[{title:\"Bot Profiles by Organization\",slug:\"bot-profiles\"}]}]};function p(n){let t={a:\"a\",em:\"em\",h2:\"h2\",h3:\"h3\",hr:\"hr\",li:\"li\",p:\"p\",strong:\"strong\",table:\"table\",tbody:\"tbody\",td:\"td\",th:\"th\",thead:\"thead\",tr:\"tr\",ul:\"ul\",...n.components},{ExecItem:i,ExecSummary:a,Image:r,InfoBox:s,Section:l}=t;return i||o(\"ExecItem\",!0),a||o(\"ExecSummary\",!0),r||o(\"Image\",!0),s||o(\"InfoBox\",!0),l||o(\"Section\",!0),(0,e.jsxs)(e.Fragment,{children:[(0,e.jsxs)(l,{id:\"executive-summary\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Executive Summary\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(a,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(i,{num:1,children:(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:`Despite AI search companies' claims, TollBit sees that AI bots on average\nare sending 95.7% less referral traffic than traditional Google search.`})})}),(0,e.jsx)(i,{num:2,children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"AI bot scraping as a percentage of all traffic to sites more than doubled\"}),`\n(increased by approximately 117%) from Q3 to Q4 2024.`]})}),(0,e.jsx)(i,{num:3,children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"When sites block Perplexity, we see that they continue to send referrals\"}),`\nwhich means they appear to be continuing to scrape sites under the radar.`]})}),(0,e.jsx)(i,{num:4,children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"AI bot scrapes that bypassed robots.txt grew by over 40%\"}),` between Q3 to Q4.\nBlocking AI bots via robots.txt remains an insufficient mechanism to prevent\nunwanted scraping.`]})}),(0,e.jsx)(i,{num:5,children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:`Unidentified user agent scraping (aka hidden scraping) is almost equivalent\nto the average scrapes from identified AI bots`}),\" per website for Q4.\"]})})]})]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(l,{id:\"foreword\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Foreword\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[\"We are excited to be releasing this, the first \",(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"TollBit State of the Bots Report\"}),\".\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"The adoption of artificial intelligence (AI) over the last two years has occurred at a lightning pace. Consumer habits are reforming around this technology which, we believe, carries the potential to make our lives better in a multitude of ways.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"One such area of adoption relates to how we retrieve information. The advent of the internet revolutionized this; at the click of a button we could access practically all the knowledge humankind has created. Artificial intelligence is taking this further. We can now task an AI system with a highly-specific information request and it will do the work for us, sifting through limitless sources and providing us with what we're looking for.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"These information-based AI use cases have the potential to disrupt the current business models of publishers. It looks highly likely that in future we will all spend more time interacting with AI applications and that, in some instances, this will supplant our current interactions with search engines and publisher websites.\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[\"We are optimistic about what this means for content creators though. Our thirst for knowledge is not going to be diminished as a result of this technology. If anything, we believe that the ease with which AI can allow us to access information will create new avenues for our inquisition. Unconstrained by time, screen space and human reading speed, as AI consumes knowledge on our behalf the total volume of information consumed will far eclipse the levels today. These systems are nothing without the materials they were trained on, and access in real-time for RAG (retrieval-augmented generation). Professional media will be more important than ever; the rate at which AI systems are accessing the digital properties of our publishing partners \\u2014 as is seen in the first \",(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"TollBit Artificial Intelligence User Agent Index\"}),\" \\u2014 is evidence of this.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Much like previous platform shifts \\u2014 such as that from print to digital, or web 1 to the social internet \\u2014 in order to succeed in the age of AI, publishers will need to adapt their strategy. As well as delivering deeply engaging experiences on their owned-and-operated platforms, an AI licensing revenue line will allow content businesses to monetize their intellectual property when it is used in an AI setting.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"This is our mission at TollBit. We believe that a vibrant, liquid and fair market for AI access to content needs to develop. And we have a product in market where transactions are flowing between both sides. Our vision is for a world in which professional content businesses can set the price and terms on which they will provide content to AI applications and a frictionless transaction takes place.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"The starting point for this is transparency. We're in the nascent days of AI and the race to build and release models has led to practices that undermine rights holders and threaten the economics of content creation. We're launching this index in an effort to begin creating transparency and, ultimately, to help this licensing market develop. We also want publishers to understand the value of their content to AI systems and the scale of the demand for it.\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Toshit Panigrahi and Olivia Joslin\"}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Co-founders, TollBit\"})]})]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(l,{id:\"section-1\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Section 1: The AI User Agent Landscape\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Since TollBit's technology went live at the beginning of April 2024, it has been gathering data on the rise of user agents serving artificial intelligence (AI) applications.\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[\"In this, the first \",(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"TollBit Artificial Intelligence User Agent Index\"}),\", we are providing an overview of the AI user agents that are currently active, what it is believed they do and a short explanation of the user-facing tools they serve.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Note that this only includes those user agents that access websites on behalf of AI applications; not the many other bots that are active across the web serving other purposes, e.g. search indexing. It has also been reported that some AI applications are served by 'unofficial' user agents. These operate opaquely so have not been included individually although are discussed at the end of this section.\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(s,{title:\"User agents, bots and crawlers\",children:[(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agent\"}),\" \\u2014 A user agent is any piece of software that sends requests to websites. This includes browsers (which do so on behalf of the user operating them) and bots, which send requests automatically, typically for indexing or data-mining. Each user agent identifies itself to the website via a 'string' (a piece of metadata which is read by the site's servers), called a 'User-Agent Header'.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Bot\"}),\" \\u2014 A bot is any type of user agent that is not directly instructed by a human to access that website. Bots include AI user agents that operate independently to retrieve information from a website following a human prompt to an AI application. They also include crawlers.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Crawler\"}),\" \\u2014 A crawler is a type of bot that navigates websites in the background to collect data. These are used for indexing by search engines and data mining for research, analysis, or more recently to gather content for training large language models. Crawlers operate autonomously, typically following links across the web to discover and retrieve content at scale.\"]})]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"1.1 User Agent Profiles\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"The table below lists the AI user agents currently active together with what it is believed their purpose is. There are three primary types of agent, with distinct functions in the development and operation of AI applications:\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Retrieval augmented generation (RAG) agent\"}),\" \\u2014 These bots retrieve information in real-time to respond to user prompts. They use an index of the web, gathered by an indexing crawler (either proprietary or third-party such as Bing or Google) to locate the relevant content which is then retrieved and synthesized into a response.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Training data crawling agent\"}),\" \\u2014 Large language models \\u2014 such as Llama from Meta or GPT-4o from OpenAI, both of which power a multitude of consumer applications \\u2014 are trained on vast quantities of data. Training data crawlers move around the web \\u2014 following links from websites to websites or working through sitemaps \\u2014 downloading content which is then processed and stored for offline use.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"AI search indexing agent\"}),\" \\u2014 AI systems with access to the real-time web need an index of the internet. This is used to direct RAG agents to the right sources when collecting data needed for responses to prompts. AI search indexing crawlers build these indexes by systematically navigating the web, collecting and organizing content and metadata.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.table,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.thead,{children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.th,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"User agents\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.th,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Operated by\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.th,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Type\"})]})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tbody,{children:[(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"ChatGPT-User\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"OpenAI\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"RAG agent\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"OAI-SearchBot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"OpenAI\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"RAG agent\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"GPTBot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"OpenAI\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Training data crawling agent\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"PerplexityBot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Perplexity\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Hybrid RAG and AI search indexing agent\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"ClaudeBot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Anthropic\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Training data crawling agent\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Claude-Web\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Anthropic\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Unknown\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"anthropic-ai\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Anthropic\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Unknown\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Google-Extended\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Google\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Control agent for training data\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Meta-ExternalAgent\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Meta\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Training data crawling agent\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Meta-ExternalFetcher\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Meta\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"RAG agent\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"FacebookBot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Meta\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Unknown\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Applebot-Extended\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Apple\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Control agent for training data\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"AmazonBot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Amazon\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Training data crawling agent\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Bytespider\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"ByteDance\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Training data crawling agent\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"DuckAssistBot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"DuckDuckGo\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"RAG agent\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Timpibot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Timpi\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Training data crawling agent\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"YouBot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"You.com\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"RAG agent\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Diffbot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Diffbot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Training data crawling agent\"})]})]})]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"1.2 Third-party and Hidden User Agents\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"This index only covers the official, first-party user agents operated by AI developers. There are alternative automated means of accessing information from the internet which are harder for a website publisher to uncover.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Firstly, it is possible for AI developers to obtain data crawled by third-party user agents. Some of these datasets are free and publicly available, such as CommonCrawl, whilst others are available for a fee, such as Timpi's search index. In these instances the end-user is unknown to the website owner at the time of scraping and indeed may only access the data some time after the bot crawled their site.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"It is also possible for AI developers to use hidden or masked user agents which do not make their real operators known through the user agent string. This practice is technically challenging to uncover but it has been reported to be deployed at scale by major AI companies. Cybersecurity tools can assist with detecting such activity. TollBit has partnered with cybersecurity partners to bring these capabilities to its publishing partners.\"})]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(l,{id:\"section-2\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Section 2: Scale of AI Scraping\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[\"As AI models and applications proliferate \\u2014 and users turn to AI technologies in growing numbers for a wide range of use cases \\u2014 the scale of AI user agent web access will increase. The level of AI scraping can be seen as a proxy for the rise of AI itself. The \",(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"TollBit Artificial Intelligence User Agent Index\"}),\" will chart the rise of this activity across publisher properties.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"For this analysis, we looked at the scale of AI scraping across all onboarded TollBit sites in Q4 (section 2.A.) and we looked at a smaller select cohort to see the change in AI bot traffic from Q3 to Q4 (section 2.B.).\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"2.A All Sites Aggregate Scraping Levels\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"We analyzed all sites that had fully set up TollBit analytics prior to Q4. On average, these sites on TollBit were scraped over 2 million times in Q4 and the average scraping rate per page was approximately 7 times.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Table 2.A.1. Average AI user agent scraping rates\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.table,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.thead,{children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.th,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Metric\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.th,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Q4 2024\"})]})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tbody,{children:[(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Average scraping rate per website\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"2,056,658\"})]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Average scraping rate per page\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"7.199\"})]})]})]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Deals and shopping content were scraped most times per page in Q4, followed by national news, consumer tech, and lifestyle content.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.A.2. Scraping levels per page by content category in Q4\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q4-2024/Figure 2.A.2. Scraping levels per page by content category in Q4.svg\",alt:\"Scraping levels per page by content category\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"2.A.3 User Agent Per Page Scraping Levels\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"The scale of scraping can also be analyzed at an individual user agent level. A note of caution on this data, however; the use of third-party or hidden user agents is likely to mask the true scale of scraping by some AI developers.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Table 2.A.3. User agent per page scraping levels for Q4\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.table,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.thead,{children:(0,e.jsx