"left\"},children:\"GPTBot\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"16.7\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"3.0\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"-82.15%\"})]})]})]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"This user-agent level data suggests that those bots operating in real-time to retrieve information in response to a user prompt are growing the fastest. There is a clear signal of the increase in the use of AI for information retrieval.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"This real-time user agent activity is likely to be substitutional to human access to publisher websites. For example, an AI agent may access five media websites in order to respond to a prompt that satisfies a user's need without them needing to visit another online property. Prior to the advent of AI, this may have resulted in multiple Google searches and visits to websites.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Licensing revenues aside, there are no means of monetizing non-human visitors to a website; advertisements are not served and robots do not purchase subscriptions.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"2.B.4 AI Bot Traffic as a Percentage of Total Traffic\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"AI bot traffic from the top 15 AI bots as a percentage of total traffic grew by 117% between Q3 and Q4 2024 for this cohort of websites.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 2.B.4 Growth in AI Bot Traffic as a Percentage of Total Traffic\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q4-2024/Figure. 2.B.4 Growth in AI Bot Traffic as a Percentage of Total Traffic.svg\",alt:\"Growth in AI Bot Traffic as a Percentage of Total Traffic\"})]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(l,{id:\"section-3\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Section 3: Referrals from AI Applications\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"AI applications with access to the real-time web such as Perplexity, ChatGPT's browser mode and DuckDuckGo's AI assistant \\u2014 as well as new entrants into this market, such as ProRata with their Gist product \\u2014 incorporate a natural language response to a query alongside a list of sources and links which the user can click on if they wish to explore further.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"The level of traffic that publishers can expect from these interfaces is a topic of debate. TollBit has been collecting data on traffic to its publisher partners' properties from AI applications since its technology went live at the beginning of April 2024.\"}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[\"Whilst the total volume of referrals will vary based upon the number of TollBit publisher partners and the usage of AI applications, the referral \",(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"rate\"}),\" \\u2014 how many referrals occur each time a piece of content is scraped in real-time to answer a user query \\u2014 gives a signal of the effectiveness of AI applications in driving traffic to publishers.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[\"The \",(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"TollBit AI User Agent Index\"}),\" includes an averaged referral rate across the AI applications that access publisher data in real time and provide traceable traffic back to publishers. In Q4 2024 this referral rate is 0.37%.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"This figure should be considered an absolute maximum as some AI developers use third-party or hidden user agents and each scrape may result in multiple presentations of content and links.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Table 3.1. Average AI application referral rate\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.table,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.thead,{children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.th,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"Q4 Real-time scrapes\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.th,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"Q4 Total referrals\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.th,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"Q4 Referrals per real-time scrape\"})]})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.tbody,{children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"163,365,722\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"605,179\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"0.37%\"})]})})]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"3.2 Comparing Referral Rates of AI Interfaces and Conventional Search\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"The AI applications with access to the real-time web and therefore capable of driving traffic to a publisher website fall into two broad categories of user interface \\u2014 general-purpose chatbot and AI search engine. TollBit data finds that AI search engines deliver a superior referral rate of 0.74% per scrape, double the 0.33% rate for chatbots. Again, these figures should be considered a maximum; the use of third-party and hidden user agents may be artificially inflating the rate for AI search products.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"These rates remain extremely low when compared to referrals from a conventional (non-AI) Google search engine results page (SERP). Even when taking the average click-through-rate across the top 10 organic search results (8.63%) AI search engine interfaces deliver 91% fewer referrals and chatbots 96% fewer.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Figure 3.2. Comparing AI interface and Google SERP referral rates\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(r,{src:\"/state-of-the-bots/q4-2024/Figure 3.2. Comparing AI interface and Google SERP referral rates.svg\",alt:\"Comparing AI interface and Google SERP referral rates\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"These figures give an indication of the change in traffic levels publishers can expect if user queries move from conventional search to AI applications. They also starkly highlight the difference in the value for publishers when providing their content for use by AI applications versus conventional search. The return cannot be expected to come in the form of traffic from AI applications. The alternative is that it comes from licensing revenues. Our mission at TollBit is to help publishers realize this opportunity.\"})]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(l,{id:\"section-4\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Section 4: Compliance with the Robots Exclusion Protocol\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Whilst every AI developer with a published policy claims its crawlers respect the robots exclusion protocol, TollBit data finds that in many instances bots continue scraping despite explicit disallow requests for those user agents in publishers' robots.txt files.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Some of this activity may be accounted for by the delay between a robots.txt file being updated by the publisher and the user agent responding by adapting its activity. Most AI developer notes state that it takes 24-72 hours for changes to be logged and reflected.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Table 4.1. Aggregate unauthorized scraping levels \\u2014 all user agents\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.table,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.thead,{children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.th,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Metric\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.th,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"Q4 bypassed scrapes\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.th,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"Q4 total scrapes\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.th,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"Q4 percent of scrapes that bypass\"})]})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.tbody,{children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"left\"},children:\"Aggregate scraping levels\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"9,713,100\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"294,102,061\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.td,{style:{textAlign:\"center\"},children:\"3.3%\"})]})})]}),(0,e.jsx)(s,{title:\"Getting your AI user agent data\",children:(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[`To understand AI demand for your content, TollBit's analytics solution\nprovides granular data on AI user agent activity across your websites. It\ncosts nothing and is simple to implement. To find out more, please visit\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.a,{href:\"http://www.tollbit.com\",children:\"www.tollbit.com\"}),\" or email us at \",(0,e.jsx)(t.a,{href:\"mailto:[email withheld by Lighthouse]\",children:\"[email withheld by Lighthouse]\"}),\".\"]})})]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(l,{id:\"appendix\",children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.h2,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Appendix: AI User Agent Profiles\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.h3,{children:\"AI Bot Profiles by Operating Organization\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"OpenAI\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Developer of ChatGPT, OpenAI's chatbot was first-to-market and still holds a ~60% share with 300M weekly active users as of December 2024. Its foundation models \\u2014 and therefore user agents \\u2014 power both ChatGPT and an array of third-party applications, including Microsoft's Co-Pilot and Bing search engine.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"ChatGPT-User\"}),\" \\u2014 Accesses websites in real-time and on-demand so that ChatGPT can formulate responses to user prompts based on the live web. It visits websites to gather information then processes, summarises and synthesizes this to provide the output. It is not used to gather data for model training.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"OAI-SearchBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Used to power ChatGPT's search capabilities. Similar to ChatGPT-User, it accesses the internet in real-time but is optimized for search scenarios, delivering the raw links and search results alongside summaries.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"GPTBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Gathers data from the web for the training of OpenAI's large language models. It operates continuously in the background and the data it gathers is collected and used offline for model development, rather than real-time responses to user prompts.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" OpenAI respects the signals provided by content owners via robots.txt, allowing them to disallow any or all of its crawlers.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher partnerships:\"}),\" OpenAI has an extensive publisher partnerships program, having signed bilateral deals with ~35 publishers at the time of writing.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(s,{title:\"Robots Exclusion Protocol (robots.txt)\",children:(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:`This index also refers to the Robots Exclusion Protocol or 'robots.txt'. This\nmechanism allows website owners to give instructions to bots about accessing a\nwebsite. It uses a machine-readable file (named robots.txt) which specifies \\u2014\nfor individual bots or all bots collectively \\u2014 which pages or sections they\ncan or cannot crawl. Robots.txt operates simply as a signal though and does\nnot actively block access. Not all developers program their bots to comply\nwith these instructions.`})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Perplexity\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Perplexity has developed an AI answer engine, effectively AI-powered search that provides users with a natural language response to a prompt alongside a list of links and sources. It has 15 million active users. The standard free product primarily uses OpenAI's GPT3.5 model, whereas the premium, paid-for service includes access to a range of models including Claude 3.5 from Anthropic, Llama 3 from Meta and Grok-2 from xAI.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"PerplexityBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Gathers data from across the web to index it for Perplexity's search function. It also acts in real-time, gathering data to respond to specific user queries as they are placed.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" Perplexity claims that its web crawler respects robots.txt. However, there have been widely-reported complaints from publishers that it has ignored their signals, leading to an investigation from Amazon, its cloud provider. In its FAQs, Perplexity explains that 'if a page is blocked, we may still index the domain, headline, and a brief factual summary'.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher partnerships:\"}),\" In 2024 Perplexity launched a publisher partner program under which it provides a share of advertising revenues generated by responses that are based upon the content of media partners. Around 20 deals have been signed so far, mostly (although not exclusively) with smaller or niche publishers. The revenue shared with publishers is reported to be capped at 25%.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Anthropic\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Founded by seven former OpenAI employees, Anthropic is an AI developer with a focus on privacy, safety and alignment (with human values). Its foundation models power its Claude chatbot \\u2014 which has a free and premium version \\u2014 and a multitude of third-party applications.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"ClaudeBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Used to gather data from the internet for AI training. The Claude chatbot does not have access to the real-time web and has no search functionality; the data ClaudeBot retrieves is only used offline for the development of new large language models.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Claude-Web\"}),\" \\u2014 There is no published information on the function of this bot. It may be experimental or another identifier \\u2014 used in specific circumstances \\u2014 for Anthropic's primary user agent ClaudeBot.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"anthropic-ai\"}),\" \\u2014 As with Claude-Web, there is no published information on this bot or its function.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" Anthropic's bots respect publisher signals in robots.txt files. Notably they also respond to any disallows for Common Crawl's CCBot.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Google\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Google is one of the foremost AI developers with its own proprietary models that have been integrated extensively into its consumer and enterprise applications, including search. It also operates Gemini as a standalone AI chatbot with real-time web access. This has around 14% of the chatbot market with an estimated 42 million active users.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Google-Extended\"}),\" \\u2014 Used to gather data to train and improve Google's AI models. It operates independently of the crawlers used to power Google's search product. It should be noted that both Gemini, when it requires data from the live web, and AI Overviews (the natural language AI response to search queries) do not rely on Google-Extended for real-time data retrieval and therefore disallowing this bot does not control whether a publisher's content is used to inform the outputs of these products.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" Google's bots respect robots.txt signals. However, publishers do not have granular controls over the use of their content in real-time for AI Overviews or Gemini's responses as these products appear to use the data collected for Google's general search product rather than a discrete user agent. In order to prevent content being used for these applications publishers need to use the no-snippet directive or signal Google to stop indexing a page for search entirely. Both of these would have negative effects on prominence and referral traffic.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher partnerships:\"}),\" Whilst Google has signed a $60M AI content licensing deal with Reddit, the large number of partnerships it has with publishing businesses are built around its News Showcase product, rather than content for AI.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Meta\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Meta has developed the Llama family of open-source AI models. These are used in Meta's own products and extensively across third-party applications. Whilst historically it has relied on external datasets for model training, it has recently launched a new web crawler to collect data for its LLMs.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Meta-ExternalAgent\"}),\" \\u2014 Described as crawling the web for 'use cases such as training AI models or improving products by indexing content directly'. Launched in summer 2024.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Meta-ExternalFetcher\"}),\" \\u2014 Accesses websites in real-time in response to actions by users. Meta is transparent that this crawler may bypass robots.txt on account of being user-'initiated'.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"FacebookBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Previously described as crawling public web pages to improve language models for speech recognition technology. It has since removed this description from its developer site and it is not known whether the bot is now being used for different purposes.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" Meta's crawlers respect robots.txt signals although Meta-ExternalFetcher may bypass the protocol because it performs crawls that were user-initiated.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher partnerships:\"}),\" Meta has signed an AI licensing deal with just one publisher \\u2014 Reuters.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Apple\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"Apple has been investing in AI \\u2014 including the development of its own foundation models \\u2014 to enhance its products, particularly in privacy-centric applications and on-device AI capabilities.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Applebot-Extended\"}),\" \\u2014 Apple's primary user agent is AppleBot. This is used to collect data to feed into a variety of user products in the Apple ecosystem, including Spotlight, Siri and Safari. Applebot-Extended is a secondary user agent that allows publishers to opt-out of their content being used to train Apple's foundation models. Applebot-Extended does not crawl webpages; it is only used to determine how Apple can use the data crawled by the primary Applebot user agent.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" Applebot-Extended respects robots.txt directives, allowing website owners to control the use of their content for AI training.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher partnerships:\"}),\" Apple has not publicly announced any AI content licensing deals at time of writing but is reportedly in negotiations with a number of publishers including Cond\\xE9 Nast and NBC News.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Amazon\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"As well as a strategic partnership with Anthropic, Amazon has developed its own 'Nova' family of AI models which emphasize speed and value.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"AmazonBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Described as being used to improve services, 'such as enabling Alexa to answer even more questions for customers'. There are no published details of how it operates, or what the data it captures is used for.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" AmazonBot respects standard robots.txt rules.\"]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Publisher partnerships:\"}),\" Amazon is reportedly in licensing negotiations with a number of news outlets for access to content that will give a revamped Alexa the ability to answer questions about current events.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"ByteDance\"})}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:\"At the time of writing ByteDance, owner of TikTok, has no live AI applications but is widely reported to be developing its own foundation models.\"}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"User agents:\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Bytespider\"}),\" \\u2014 Has been scraping the web at a high rate since it first appeared in early 2024. ByteDance has published no information on the function it serves or what the data collected is being used for.\"]}),`\n`]}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.em,{children:\"Robots.txt policy:\"}),\" ByteDance has no published robots.txt policy. There are widespread reports of Bytespider ignoring robots.txt signals.\"]}),(0,e.jsx)(t.hr,{}),(0,e.jsx)(t.p,{children:(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Other Notable AI User Agents\"})}),(0,e.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"DuckAssistBot\"}),\" \\u2014 DuckDuckGo's web crawler that crawls pages in real-time to source information for answers by DuckAssist. Data collected is not used to train AI models and it respects robots.txt signals.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Timpibot\"}),\" \\u2014 Web crawler for Timpi, a decentralized search index, accessible for a cost to businesses. Data is also available to AI developers for model training.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"YouBot\"}),\" \\u2014 Crawler for You.com, an AI-powered search engine that integrates AI query responses alongside conventional search links.\"]}),`\n`,(0,e.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,e.jsx)(t.strong,{children:\"Diffbot\"}),\" \\u2014 Web crawler focused on extracting data from web pages which it then converts into structured datasets for businesses and developers. Fully respects robots.txt directives.\"]}),`\n`]})]})]})}function f(n={}){let{wrapper:t}=n.components||{};return t?(0,e.jsx)(t,{...n,children:(0,e.jsx)(p,{...n})}):p(n)}function o(n,t){throw new Error(\"Expected \"+(t?\"component\":\"object\")+\" `\"+n+\"` to be defined: you likely forgot to import, pass, or provide it.\")}return I(B);})();\n;return Component;"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"2f:[\"$\",\"$L3d\",null,{\"quarters\":[{\"title\":\"State of the Bots\",\"id\":\"q1-q2-2026\",\"quarter\":\"q1-q2-2026\",\"description\":\"2026 Q1 \u0026 Q2, The Bad Bots\",\"docType\":\"report\",\"sections\":[{\"title\":\"Foreword\",\"slug\":\"foreword\"},{\"title\":\"Executive Summary\",\"slug\":\"executive-summary\"},{\"title\":\"Section 1: The Scraper Ecosystem \u0026 Proxy Networks\",\"slug\":\"section-1\",\"subsections\":[{\"title\":\"Scraper Audit Tool \u0026 Results on UK Sites\",\"slug\":\"scraper-audit-uk\"},{\"title\":\"Scraper Audit: Other Observations\",\"slug\":\"scraper-audit-observations\"},{\"title\":\"The Inefficiency and Cost of Web Scraping\",\"slug\":\"scraping-cost\"},{\"title\":\"The Limits of Cybersecurity and the Need for Regulation\",\"slug\":\"cybersecurity-limits\"}]},{\"title\":\"Section 2: The Scale of AI Scraping\",\"slug\":\"section-2\",\"subsections\":[{\"title\":\"Aggregate Scraping Levels\",\"slug\":\"aggregate-scraping\"},{\"title\":\"AI Scraping by Region: North America vs Europe\",\"slug\":\"scraping-region\"},{\"title\":\"The Europe Cohort, Quarter over Quarter\",\"slug\":\"europe-cohort-qoq\"},{\"title\":\"Scraping by AI Company \u0026 by AI Bot\",\"slug\":\"scraping-by-company\"},{\"title\":\"IP Address Investigation\",\"slug\":\"ip-investigation\"}]},{\"title\":\"Section 3: Referral Traffic\",\"slug\":\"section-3\",\"subsections\":[{\"title\":\"Referral Composition\",\"slug\":\"referral-composition\"},{\"title\":\"Scrape-to-Referral Ratio\",\"slug\":\"scrape-referral-ratio\"},{\"title\":\"European Cohort: Referrals \u0026 Ratio Over Time\",\"slug\":\"europe-cohort-referrals\"}]},{\"title\":\"Section 4: Robots.txt and the Bypassing Problem\",\"slug\":\"section-4\",\"subsections\":[{\"title\":\"Disallow Adoption\",\"slug\":\"disallow-adoption\"},{\"title\":\"The Bypass Problem\",\"slug\":\"bypass-problem\"},{\"title\":\"Bypass by AI Bot\",\"slug\":\"bypass-by-bot\"}]}],\"body\":{\"raw\":\"$3e\",\"code\":\"$3f\"},\"_id\":\"state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026.mdx\",\"_raw\":{\"sourceFilePath\":\"state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026.mdx\",\"sourceFileName\":\"q1-q2-2026.mdx\",\"sourceFileDir\":\"state-of-the-bots\",\"contentType\":\"mdx\",\"flattenedPath\":\"state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026\"},\"type\":\"StateOfTheBots\",\"slug\":\"/state-of-the-bots/q1-q2-2026\",\"slugAsParams\":\"q1-q2-2026\"},{\"title\":\"State of the Bots\",\"id\":\"q3-q4-2025\",\"quarter\":\"q3-q4-2025\",\"description\":\"2025 Q3 \u0026 Q4, The Leaky Pipes\",\"docType\":\"report\",\"sections\":[{\"title\":\"Executive Summary\",\"slug\":\"executive-summary\"},{\"title\":\"Section 1: Content Access Complexity\",\"slug\":\"section-1\",\"subsections\":[{\"title\":\"Data Acquisition Stack\",\"slug\":\"data-acquisition\"},{\"title\":\"Google \u0026 